{"raw": {"https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193836": {"title": "Assistant Professor - AI and Policy", "description": "Assistant Professor - AI and Policy. Harris School of Public Policy Studies. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 12, 2026. The Harris School of Public Policy at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions at the Assistant Professor level. The search is open to those working on questions related to AI and Policy. In particular, we welcome applicants who work on developing AI tools to improve public policy outcomes and those who study the behavior and governance of AI and tech systems. Applicants are expected to complete a Ph.D. in Economics, Political Science, Computer Science, or another relevant field by July 31, 2027, and demonstrate strong promise for a scholarly career. To apply for this position, candidates must submit their application through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio academic job board at: apply.interfolio.com/195292 . Applicants are required to submit: 1) curriculum vitae, 2) two letters of recommendation, 3) one writing sample, 4) research statement, 5) teaching statement, and 6) no more than a one-page abstract from their job market paper or dissertation. Applicants may also submit additional optional documents such as: a cover letter, additional writing sample, and/or a third letter of reference. Review of applications will begin on November 11, 2026. No applications will be accepted after May 1, 2027. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Salary Range or Pay Grade: $140,000 - $180,000. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193848": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Geriatric Medicine #MED427a", "description": "Clinical Associates - Geriatric Medicine #MED427a. Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 12, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years in our expanding network. Clinical responsibilities will include seeing patients at UChicago Medicine's Hyde Park campus, inpatient geriatrics consultation, outpatient geriatrics services, and off-site care at affiliated nursing homes. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information on benefit offerings is available in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Geriatric Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195303 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193651": {"title": "Academic Nephrologist - Nephrology #MED439", "description": "Academic Nephrologist - Nephrology #MED439. Section of Nephrology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 8, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Nephrology, is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor. The appointee will provide patient care for patients with chronic kidney disease, end-stage kidney disease, acute kidney injury, and post-transplant general nephrology care at our Hyde Park locations. This appointee\u2019s work will focus on outpatient nephrology and inpatient nephrologic care with a focus on critical care nephrology and will maintain an active and productive clinical portfolio. Other duties include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in nephrology. We especially welcome applicants with experience in inpatient hospital care as well as outpatient in-center dialysis therapies, who may have the opportunity to become a Medical Director or Co-Director of one of our off-site dialysis facilities. To be considered, interested applicants must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195126 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will begin after October 18, 2026. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193777": {"title": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Pulmonary/Critical Care #MED443", "description": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Pulmonary/Critical Care #MED443. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 8, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, is seeking a full-time faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor to join its clinical faculty. The appointee will have clinical responsibilities across the University of Chicago Medicine health system, with a combination of outpatient pulmonary practice and inpatient responsibilities at UChicago Medicine Hyde Park location (CCD) and Ingalls Memorial Hospital and affiliated ambulatory locations. Inpatient responsibilities will primarily include attending coverage in the Medical Intensive Care Unit at UCM Hyde Park and at Ingalls Memorial Hospital and may include pulmonary consult and procedure service based on clinical need. Ambulatory responsibilities will include the evaluation and longitudinal management of patients with a broad range of pulmonary conditions. The appointee will work collaboratively with Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine faculty and clinical teams across the health system. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students and scholarly activity appropriate to the appointee's academic interests and career goals. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine. We especially welcome a physician completing fellowship training in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine who are interested in developing a clinical practice that includes both ambulatory pulmonary medicine and inpatient pulmonary and critical care. To be considered, interested applicants must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195238 . Applicants must upload : a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will begin after October 18, 2026. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193668": {"title": "Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Modern Greek\t", "description": "Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Modern Greek . Department of Classics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 7, 2026. The Department of Classics at the University of Chicago invites applications for a position as an Instructional Professor (open rank) in Modern Greek, with an expected start date of September 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Responsibilities include both teaching and service duties. Teaching normally consists of seven courses across three quarters at all levels of the Modern Greek language. One language course may periodically be replaced by a non-language course in an area of Modern Greek culture or a course in the College Core curriculum . The selected candidate may also contribute to programming in the Center for Hellenic Studies . Service duties may include advising undergraduate theses, creating and overseeing placement exams and graduate language exams, teaching independent studies, or other program-specific duties. Instructional Professors of all ranks are required to engage in regular professional development. The selected candidate will be appointed at the rank of Assistant Instructional Professor, Associate Instructional Professor, or Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications and educational background. The initial appointment is for a minimum of 2 years, with longer initial terms possible depending on qualifications and initial rank. Reappointment in the position is possible after the initial term, contingent upon positive review. Review and progression schedule are determined by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant Instructional Professor: $77,946 - $84,181; Associate Instructional Professor: $84,182 - $91,113; Instructional Professor: $91,114 - $100,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over 12 months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants must have experience teaching at the post-secondary level, with experience teaching Modern Greek at the post-secondary level preferred, and must demonstrate sufficient language proficiency for teaching Modern Greek in a university context, e.g., through an ACTFL-certified result at the Superior level or higher, or through receipt of an advanced degree earned in a modern Greek-language context . An MA in a field related to Modern Greek is required, a PhD is preferred. Candidates with specialized training in second language acquisition, second language pedagogy, and/or assessment are especially encouraged to apply. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/195141 by 10:59pm Central Time/11:59pm Eastern Time on November 9, 2026 : \u00b7 CV \u00b7 Cover letter \u00b7 Teaching statement \u00b7 Sample syllabus from past teaching \u00b7 Teaching evaluations, as available \u00b7 Contact information for three references Longlisted candidates will be asked to provide additional materials following the initial review of applications. Questions may be directed to Anna Hornsby (they/them) at annahornsby@uchicago.edu. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193512": {"title": "Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Chinese\t", "description": "Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Chinese . Department of East Asian Languages & Civilizations. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 6, 2026. The Department of East Asian Languages and Civilizations at the University of Chicago invites applications for a position as an Instructional Professor (open rank) in Chinese, with an expected start date of September 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The selected candidate will be appointed at the rank of Assistant Instructional Professor, Associate Instructional Professor, or Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications and educational background. The initial appointment is for a minimum of 2 years, with longer initial terms possible depending on qualifications and initial rank. Review and progression schedule are determined by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. Responsibilities include both teaching and service duties. Teaching normally consists of six courses across three quarters at all levels of the Chinese language. Service duties may include course coordination, creating and overseeing placement exams and graduate language exams, organizing cultural events and other co-curricular activities, advising undergraduate theses or other program-specific duties. Instructional Professors of all ranks are required to engage in regular professional development. Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant Instructional Professor: $77,946 - $84,181; Associate Instructional Professor: $84,182 - $91,113; Instructional Professor: $91,114 - $100,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants must have previous experience teaching Chinese at the post-secondary level and must demonstrate sufficient language proficiency for teaching Chinese in a university context, e.g., through an ACTFL-certified result at the Superior level or higher, or through receipt of an advanced degree earned in a Chinese-language context . An MA in a field related to Chinese is required, a PhD is preferred. Candidates with specialized training in second language acquisition, second language pedagogy, and/or assessment are especially encouraged to apply. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/194995 by 10:59pm Central Time/11:59pm Eastern Time on November 5, 2026: \u00b7 CV \u00b7 Cover letter \u00b7 Teaching statement \u00b7 2 sample syllabi for different levels of Chinese language courses \u00b7 Contact information for three references Longlisted candidates will be asked to provide additional materials following the initial review of applications, including a video teaching demonstration. Questions may be directed to Dawn Brennan at dbrennan@uchicago.edu . The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190709": {"title": "Senior Research Associate", "description": "Senior Research Associate. Social Sciences Division. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Oct 2, 2026. The University of Chicago, Division of the Social Sciences invites applications as Senior Research Associate in the Knowledge lab for a full time appointment with initial one year term. Reappointment is possible following a successful review. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Senior Research Associate will play a central role in designing and running AI-driven experiments, developing and applying large language models and generative AI methods, building and maintaining large-scale research datasets, and contributing to original research on how artificial intelligence shapes \u2014 and is shaped by \u2014 knowledge production and diffusion. The Senior Research Associate will be expected to take an active role in obtaining and carrying out funded research (they can serve as PI on reseach projects) and may supervise student researchers. There are no teaching responsibilities. The University of Chicago Knowledge Lab (PI Professor James A. Evans) is at the forefront of research at the intersection of artificial intelligence and collective knowledge \u2014 building AI systems that think differently from humans and, in doing so, expand what humanity can discover. Current projects include developing time-aware large language models to predict scientific breakthroughs and direct research funding toward high-impact opportunities, designing AI agents that augment human reasoning across science, policy, and society, and constructing \"virtual laboratories\" that simulate potential futures in science and technology. This work is published in Nature, Science, and PNAS, and has been supported by the NSF, NIH, DARPA, the John F Templeton Foundation, the Novo Nordisk Foundation, among others. Applicants with experience with machine learning, LLMs, and computational social science methods, as well as a demonstrated interest in empirical research on AI, collective intelligence, or scientific discovery are preferred. The successful candidate will work under the supervision of Professor James A. Evans and in collaboration with faculty across the University of Chicago and partner institutions. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess: a Ph.D. in the social sciences (e.g., sociology, economics, political science, communications) or computational sciences (e.g., computer science, informatics/information science, engineering, data science, applied mathematics, statistics, physics), or a related field. Proficiency in Python and experience with large language models and generative AI frameworks (e.g., Hugging Face Transformers, PyTorch) is required. We especially welcome applicants with: Active publication record and engagement with the broader scientific community, ideally in venues at the intersection of AI, computational social science, or science of science. Strong writing skills and experience contributing to research manuscripts, grant proposals, or technical reports. Experience designing and running computational experiments, including hypothesis formation, testing, and iteration at scale. Expertise in large language models, generative modeling, or related deep learning methods; familiarity with fine-tuning, prompt engineering, or model evaluation pipelines is a strong plus. Experience working with large-scale text corpora, bibliometric data, or other unstructured data sources relevant to knowledge and information systems. Familiarity with social and semantic network analysis, embedding models, or other methods for representing and analyzing knowledge structures. Comfort working in Linux/Unix environments and on high-performance computing clusters. Ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative, interdisciplinary environment spanning sociology, computer science, and data science. Strong communication skills, including the ability to translate complex technical methods for diverse audiences. Demonstrated ability to work independently, prioritize across multiple projects, and bring creative, resourceful approaches to research problems. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192473 Applicants must upload: CV/Resume Cover Letter (1-2 pages) Research Statement List of Potential References with name and contact information (3-5) The review of applications will begin after the position has been posted for 30 days and will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193348": {"title": "Consultation-Liaison Psychiatrists #PBN050a", "description": "Consultation-Liaison Psychiatrists #PBN050a. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 2, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will primarily be responsible for evaluating patients, assisting primary teams in the development of treatment plans, and providing ongoing care as indicated. Appointees will be responsible for communicating and collaborating with medical and surgical colleagues to optimize patient care. Appointees will provide clinical leadership in interdisciplinary collaboration with all members of the multidisciplinary team. Outpatient opportunities will include the supervision of a medical-psychiatric clinic. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience will play a key role in expanding clinical services, teaching, research programs and innovative population health initiatives in Consult-Liaison Psychiatry. Building on a strong foundation of clinical excellence, the appointees will join other specialty trained psychiatrists in leading a multidisciplinary team dedicated to providing high-quality, evidence-based consultation to hospitalized patients. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Psychiatry, and 4) have residency training in Adult Psychiatry. We especially welcome applicants with training and experience in Consultation-Liaison Psychiatry. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com /194833. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192762": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition (Pay-Per-Shift) #MED444", "description": "Clinical Associates - Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition (Pay-Per-Shift) #MED444. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 2, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 20% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will primarily assume clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and other offsite locations. This position does not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per hour. These positions are benefits-ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Gastroenterology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses lnterfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/194341 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189370": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Economics (Macroeconomics)", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Economics (Macroeconomics). Macro/International. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business at the University of Chicago is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure track positions in Macroeconomics for the 2027-28 academic year. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s) or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in the relevant field(s) by July 1, 2027 or soon thereafter (we require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent). (We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, job market paper, and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. We will start formally reviewing applications on November 12, 2026 and we will continue to accept applications until March 31, 2027. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190371": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Econometrics and Statistics", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Econometrics and Statistics. Booth - Econometrics/Stats. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions in Econometrics and Statistics. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s) or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in the relevant field(s) by July 1, 2027 or soon thereafter (we require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent). (We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Each candidate should submit a curriculum vita, job market paper, and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193410": {"title": "Faculty (Open Rank) - Medicine and Molecular Engineering", "description": "Faculty (Open Rank) - Medicine and Molecular Engineering. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 1, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) and Biological Sciences Division (BSD) seek applications for a jointly appointed faculty member at any rank. This is a broad area search and invites applicants whose cross-disciplinary research programs seek to drive fundamental and translational advances in medicine and bioengineering. The appointee will hold a primary appointment in either the PME or BSD and a secondary appointment in the BSD or PME. The appointee will be expected to establish and maintain a robust research program and teach at the graduate and undergraduate levels. Academic rank, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. About the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering and the Biological Sciences Division The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering was launched in 2011 with the aim of translating molecular science into solutions with impact in health and immunology, quantum information technology, and environmental sustainability. PME aims to solve important societal problems by creating synergy and collaborations across disciplines. The PME currently has forty-six faculty engaged in both fundamental and applied aspects of molecular engineering and will continue to expand in size over the next decade. PME is organized along three Institutes, where the Immunoengineering and Bioengineering Institute with sixteen faculty work in areas including immunophysiology, vaccine technologies, cell and tissue engineering, systems and computational biology, microbiome engineering, development of high throughput methods (single cell proteomics, microfluidics, high content imaging), quantum biology, protein engineering, automated and autonomous labs, among others. PME faculty interact closely with numerous centers and departments within the BSD as well as in the broader research ecosystem of the Chicagoland area, including Argonne National Laboratory, Chan Zuckerberg Biohub Chicago, and Hyde Park Labs. PME has joint research programs with Divisions across the University of Chicago campus. The Biological Sciences Division includes thirteen research-intensive clinical departments, ten basic science departments, and numerous interdisciplinary and cross-divisional graduate programs, institutes, and centers. It incorporates both the Pritzker School of Medicine and all undergraduate and graduate programs within the biological sciences, forming a single academic and administrative unit to provide a favorable environment for research and the training of researchers through extensive support and encouragement of collaborations between clinical, translational and basic science faculty. The BSD maintains a strong tradition of multidisciplinary and interdepartmental interaction, which augments its educational and research endeavors. The BSD hosts a number of research institutes (e.g., Duchossois Family Institute; Institute for Biophysical Dynamics; Institute for Genomics & Systems Biology; Institute for Integrative Physiology), centers (University of Chicago Comprehensive Cancer Center; Grossman Center for Quantitative Biology & Human Behavior; Chicago Diabetes Research & Training Center) and unique interdepartmental graduate committees structured along programmatic rather than departmental lines. The BSD provides outstanding facilities and an environment for education and research including the Gordon Center for Integrative Science and the Knapp Center for Biomedical Discovery. The unique organization of the Pritzker School of Medicine within the BSD benefits both the students and faculty by offering opportunities for interdisciplinary research and collaboration between the basic science and clinical faculty, and for the faculty to teach in the Division and the medical school. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must hold a doctoral degree or equivalent. We especially welcome applications from candidates with research interests in medicine and engineering. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/194891 . Applicants must upload: A cover letter; A CV including bibliography; A research statement summarizing both past research accomplishments and current / future directions; and Names and contact information for three references. Up to five selected publications may also be included. Review of complete applications will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193286": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in French", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in French. Department of Romance Languages and Literatures. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 1, 2026. The Department of Romance Language and Literatures at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers in French. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in various courses of the French Language curriculum. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching language courses at various levels (elementary, intermediate, and/or advanced), holding regularly scheduled office hours, and the preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the department, the successful candidate may teach between one and four courses per academic year. Teaching for this position will begin January 2027 for the Winter quarter or March 2027 for the Spring quarter. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent in French Language pedagogy and/or a related field, superior proficiency in French and English according to ACTFL guidelines, and experience teaching French at the college or post-secondary level. A master\u2019s degree is preferred. Applications must include: Current CV Cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience and discusses approaches to pedagogy Sample syllabus The names and contact information of three references whose recommendation may be solicited Optional materials: Course evaluations, if available A teaching demonstration video, if available (please include a link to an externally hosted video) The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/194777 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. Review of applications will begin November 2 and continue until the position is filled. In the event that a teaching opportunity becomes available, we will contact applicants with the academic training and experience most appropriate for our available classes. This posting will expire on March 31, 2027. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval. Only complete applications will be considered. For more information about this position, please contact romance-languages@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192446": {"title": "OBGYN Generalist Clinical Associates - #OBG104", "description": "OBGYN Generalist Clinical Associates - #OBG104. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 30, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of General Obstetrics and Gynecology, is searching for full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will be responsible for outpatient clinical care at our Crown Point, Indiana location, and inpatient clinical care at our main campus in Hyde Park. At least 40% of the appointees\u2019 clinical time will be spent in Crown Point, Northwest Indiana. Appointees will be members of the obstetrics inpatient call group and will also participate in gynecology service. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana, and 3) be Board-certified or eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. We especially welcome applicants with training and experience in gynecology (office gynecology, minimally invasive surgery) and obstetrics (vaginal delivery of twins, higher order cesarean sections, and operative vaginal deliveries). To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/194054 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications will continue until positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192911": {"title": "Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Tamil Language", "description": "Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Tamil Language. Department of South Asian Languages and Civilizations. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 28, 2026. The Department of South Asian Languages and Civilizations at the University of Chicago invites applications for a position as an Instructional Professor (open-rank) in Tamil to help grow its existing offerings in South Asian languages. The position begins in the academic year 2027-28 with an expected start date of September 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The selected candidate will be appointed at the rank of Assistant Instructional Professor, Associate Instructional Professor, or Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications and experience. The initial appointment is for a minimum of 2 years, with longer initial terms possible depending on qualifications and initial rank. Review and progression schedule are determined by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. Responsibilities include both teaching and service duties. Teaching normally consists of seven courses across three quarters, six of which are Tamil language courses at all levels of the language. One additional course per year may be a non-language course on a topic related to the culture or history of Tamil-speaking areas, proposed by the Instructional Professor, or a course in the College Core. Service duties may include advising undergraduate theses, creating and overseeing Tamil placement exams and graduate language exams, teaching independent studies, engaging in outreach to build interest in the Tamil program, or other program-specific duties. Instructional Professors of all ranks are required to engage in regular professional development. Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant Instructional Professor: $77,946 - $84,181; Associate Instructional Professor: $84,182 - $91,113; Instructional Professor: $91,114 - $100,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants must have previous experience teaching at the post-secondary level. An MA degree or equivalent is required, with a PhD in a related field preferred. Preferred qualifications include experience teaching Tamil at the post-secondary level, experience teaching at a North American institution, training in second-language pedagogy, and/or familiarity with current approaches to second-language pedagogy. Applicants must have previous experience teaching at the post-secondary level. An MA degree or equivalent is required, with a PhD in a related field preferred. Preferred qualifications include experience teaching Tamil at the post-secondary level, experience teaching at a North American institution, training in second-language pedagogy, and/or familiarity with current approaches to second-language pedagogy. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/194479 by 10:59 PM Central Time/11:59 PM Eastern Time on October 28, 2026: \u00b7 CV \u00b7 Cover letter \u00b7 Teaching statement \u00b7 One sample syllabus for Introductory Tamil \u00b7 One sample syllabus for a non-language course \u00b7 Names and contact information for three references who can supply letters of recommendation upon request Optional: \u00b7 Course evaluations, if available Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following the initial review of applications. Questions may be directed to salc@uchicago.edu . The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192691": {"title": "Instructional Professor (open rank) in Data Science", "description": "Instructional Professor (open rank) in Data Science. Data Science. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 28, 2026. The Data Science Institute invites applications for the position of Instructional Professor (open rank) in the area of Data Science. The selected candidate will be appointed as Assistant Instructional Professor, Associate Instructional Professor, or Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications. Appointments will be made in the Data Science Institute. The appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable. This is a career-track position with potential progression, competitive salary, and benefits. This position is expected to begin on or around September 1, 2027. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University. In addition to teaching (an average teaching load is 2 courses per quarter in the fall, winter, and spring quarters), the candidate will also be expected to keep regular office hours, contribute to non-classroom instructional or service duties and ongoing professional development. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago is implementing an ambitious plan for research and education in Data Science and AI, including new undergraduate and graduate academic programs and new cross-disciplinary research programs. The Data Science Institute ( datascience.uchicago.edu ) is a collaboration among the Department of Computer Science ( cs.uchicago.edu ), the Department of Statistics ( stat.uchicago.edu ), and other units on campus. Topics of interest include safe and trustworthy AI, foundations and applications of artificial intelligence, world models, large language models, scientific AI, data ethics and privacy, data visualization, societal impacts of data and AI, data engineering, and scalable data and AI systems. The Data Science Institute focuses on fundamental advances at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, statistics, and computing in the context of real-world and domain-specific problems. The Committee on Data Science ( codas.uchicago.edu ) is the home of data science education programs. The larger data science community at the University of Chicago includes the Committee on Computational and Applied Mathematics ( cam.uchicago.edu ), the Toyota Technological Institute at Chicago (TTIC), the Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory. Required: By the time of appointment, a PhD in statistics, computer science, or some field of mathematics or science where data science concepts play an important role. At least one year of university-level teaching experience, either as an instructor of record or as a teaching assistant. Preferred: Training, credentials, or experience in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in data science or machine learning. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: https://apply.interfolio.com/194273 . Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae, including teaching experience and publications; syllabi of courses previously taught and/or designed by the candidate or sample syllabi of proposed courses teaching statement, which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching and mentoring activities, teaching philosophy and goals; must include a list of courses the candidate is qualified to teach; three confidential letters of recommendation requested via Interfolio. Additional evidence of pedagogical accomplishment and promise, such as, but not limited to: a teaching portfolio, recent course evaluations, third-party observations of the applicant\u2019s teaching, or other relevant assessments."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192494": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Data Science", "description": "Assistant Professor of Data Science. Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 25, 2026. The University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor in the area of Data Science. Appointments may be made in either the Department of Computer Science or the Department of Statistics, jointly between Statistics and Computer Science, or jointly with another department in the University. The University of Chicago is implementing an ambitious plan for research and education in Data Science and AI, including new undergraduate and graduate academic programs and new cross-disciplinary research programs. The Data Science Institute ( datascience.uchicago.edu ) is a collaboration among the Department of Computer Science ( cs.uchicago.edu ), the Department of Statistics ( stat.uchicago.edu ), and other units on campus. Topics of interest include safe and trustworthy AI, foundations and applications of artificial intelligence, world models, large language models, scientific AI, data ethics and privacy, data visualization, societal impacts of data and AI, data engineering, and scalable data and AI systems. The Data Science Institute focuses on fundamental advances at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, statistics, and computing in the context of real-world and domain-specific problems. The Committee on Data Science (codas.uchicago.edu) is the home of data science education programs. The larger data science community at the University of Chicago includes the Committee on Computational and Applied Mathematics ( cam.uchicago.edu ), the Toyota Technological Institute at Chicago (TTIC), the Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory. Members of the faculty are expected to lead research programs that will produce significant contributions to a field and to teach and mentor at the undergraduate and graduate levels. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in Data Science, AI, Computer Science, Statistics, Applied Mathematics, or a related field is required at the time of appointment. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: https://apply.interfolio.com/194104 . Review of applications will begin on November 1st, 2026, and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: \u25cf cover letter; \u25cf curriculum vitae, including a list of publications; \u25cf statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans; \u25cf description of teaching and mentoring philosophy and experience; \u25cf at least three confidential letters of recommendation requested by the applicant via Interfolio; and \u25cf at least one and up to three publications."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192781": {"title": "Associate Professor of Data Science", "description": "Associate Professor of Data Science. Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 25, 2026. The University of Chicago invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of Associate Professor in the area of Data Science. Appointments may be made in either the Department of Computer Science or the Department of Statistics, jointly between Statistics and Computer Science, or jointly with another department in the University. The University of Chicago is implementing an ambitious plan for research and education in Data Science and AI, including new undergraduate and graduate academic programs and new cross-disciplinary research programs. The Data Science Institute ( datascience.uchicago.edu ) is a collaboration among the Department of Computer Science ( cs.uchicago.edu ), the Department of Statistics ( stat.uchicago.edu ), and other units on campus. Topics of interest include safe and trustworthy AI, foundations and applications of artificial intelligence, world models, large language models, scientific AI, data ethics and privacy, data visualization, societal impacts of data and AI, data engineering, and scalable data and AI systems. The Data Science Institute focuses on fundamental advances at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, statistics, and computing in the context of real-world and domain-specific problems. The Committee on Data Science (codas.uchicago.edu) is the home of data science education programs. The larger data science community at the University of Chicago includes the Committee on Computational and Applied Mathematics ( cam.uchicago.edu ), the Toyota Technological Institute at Chicago (TTIC), the Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory. Members of the faculty are expected to lead research programs that will produce significant contributions to a field, and teach and mentor at the undergraduate and graduate levels. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Required: A PhD in Data Science, AI, Statistics, Computer Science, Applied Mathematics, or related field. Candidates must have evidence of leadership in their field, successful independent research, and a demonstrated track record of mentoring PhD students and/or postdoctoral researchers. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: https://apply.interfolio.com/194358 . Review of applications will begin on November 1st, 2026, and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae, including a list of publications; statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans; and description of teaching and mentoring philosophy and experience. The following materials may be requested: up to three publications and names of three or more people who can provide confidential letters of recommendation."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192722": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science", "description": "Assistant Professor of Political Science. Department of Political Science. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 25, 2026. The Department of Political Science at the University of Chicago invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor specializing in climate politics for an initial 4-year renewable term. We welcome applications from any subfield in Political Science addressing the political challenges of climate mitigation, adaptation, or geoengineering at the local, national, or global level. This position is part of a departmental initiative in the politics of climate in collaboration with the Institute for Climate and Sustainable Growth and the Climate Systems Engineering Initiative at the University of Chicago. This position will begin on or after July 1, 2027. The selected candidate will pursue their own research agenda, collaborate with faculty in the Department of Political Science and other areas of the Division of the Social Sciences and University, and teach in both the undergraduate and graduate curriculum. The teaching load for tenure-track positions is typically four courses per year on the quarter system. Additional responsibilities include advising at all levels of the program and service on departmental and university committees. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . All requirements for receipt of a PhD in a relevant field must be completed prior to the start of the appointment. We seek a colleague with an ambitious research agenda yielding active and innovative scholarship. Successful applicants will contribute to the scholarly community of the department and university. They will have the ability or potential to be an excellent teacher in the university\u2019s core curriculum, in introductory to advanced undergraduate courses in Political Science, and in graduate courses related to their area of expertise. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/194297 Applicants must upload: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Cover Letter Research Statement Teaching Statement One Writing Sample At least 3 Reference Letters The review of applications will begin after the position has been posted for 30 days and will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192742": {"title": "Research Associate Professor, Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics", "description": "Research Associate Professor, Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Sep 24, 2026. The Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at the University of Chicago invites applications for a non-tenure track appointment as a Research Associate Professor, for a three-year renewable term. The selected candidate should have expertise and experience in public economics, labor economics, and microeconomic theory. This is a benefits-eligible position. The Research Associate Professor is primarily an independent researcher conducting original research within economics and is expected to stand alone on their scholarly accomplishments, solo- and co-authoring reputable scientific publications. Appointees are expected to make a substantial contribution to that research and may be part of a research group or in association with a faculty member. Appointees are expected to actively interact with a substantial number of faculty and students and regularly participate in academic conferences and workshops. The Research Associate Professor is expected to have an active role in obtaining and carrying out funded research and serve as a Co-Investigator or Principal Investigator on research grants. Applicants with demonstrated ability to communicate research findings clearly to audiences through oral presentations and written work are strongly preferred. Additionally, the Research Associate Professor will teach one undergraduate class in Economics. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field. Applicants must apply online at the University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board at apply.interfolio.com/194322 . Applications must include three reference letters, curriculum vitae, research statement and up to three published or unpublished research papers. Review of applications will begin 30 days after the posting date and will continue until the position is filled. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192766": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Applied Artificial Intelligence", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Applied Artificial Intelligence. Artificial Intelligence. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 24, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business invites applications for tenure-track positions at the assistant or associate professor levels in Applied AI for the 2026-27 academic year. Candidates should have deep expertise in AI/ML fundamentals, along with a strong interest in empirical work. The empirical work should be in a substantive domain, which could be a subfield of AI/ML (e.g., safety, human-centered AI), in STEM more broadly (e.g., medicine, materials science), or the social sciences (e.g., law, policy). Booth is a research-focused institution doing leading work across a breadth of academic areas, and is seeking candidates with expertise in AI who can enhance and complement the research effort here. Successful candidates will have a track record of publishing in top-tier AI/ML venues (e.g., NeurIPS/ICML/ICLR/CVPR/EMNLP), along with venues specific to their empirical sub-field of choice. Applied AI faculty at Booth are expected to continue publishing in top-tier AI/ML venues and teach AI-related courses at the MBA level. The candidate must have obtained, or expect to obtain shortly, a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in an area relevant to AI\u2014including but not limited to Machine Learning (and related areas in Computer Science), Statistics, Economics, or any Business-related discipline. Relevant industry experience would be considered a valuable part of a candidate's portfolio. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, a sample of written work, and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. We will be reviewing applications on a rolling basis in Fall 2026 and strongly encourage you to complete your application as early as you can. We will continue to accept applications until March 30, 2027 . This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192379": {"title": "Data Science Preceptor", "description": "Data Science Preceptor. Data Science. Position type: Staff. Posted: Sep 23, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Data Science Institute, in partnership with the City Colleges of Chicago (CCC), invites applications for the position of University of Chicago (UChicago) Data Science Preceptorship. The UChicago and CCC Preceptorship program will develop the next generation of teaching academics for data science, engineering, and computer science education. This unique program emphasizes pedagogical best practices and allows Preceptors, from a variety of STEM PhD backgrounds, the opportunity to broaden their classroom skills and enhance their teaching and scholarship portfolios with the support of UChicago and CCC faculty mentorship. The initial appointment is expected to begin August 1, 2027. Preceptors will work side by side with faculty from UChicago and CCC on teaching and developing course materials. Professional development training will be provided by both the UChicago and CCC faculty. Preceptors will gain experience teaching across learning environments with opportunities to engage with UChicago and CCC students in STEM courses. The teaching obligation is expected to be 2 one-quarter courses at UChicago and 2 one-semester courses at CCC per academic year. This role includes dedicated time for research and scholarship activities. This position also includes structured professional development, administrative duties and student mentoring activities. The position is for an initial two-year appointment with a renewal for a third year possible. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Required: A Ph.D. in data science, mathematics, statistics, computer science, biology, physics, astronomy, astrophysics, or other closely related fields to data science. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/193996 Review of applications will begin on December 1st, 2026 and will continue until all available positions are filled. The following materials are required: (a) a cover letter, (b) a curriculum vitae, (c) two or more letters of reference, at least one of which must address teaching ability, (d) teaching statement (description of teaching and mentoring philosophy and experience). Optional materials: course evaluations from courses previously taught."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192457": {"title": "Head of Scholarly Communications", "description": "Head of Scholarly Communications. The Library. Position type: OAA - University Librarian. Posted: Sep 21, 2026. The Head of Scholarly Communications shapes how the University of Chicago shares, preserves, and elevates its research. They will guide a growing portfolio of open scholarship and repository services, lead strategy in a rapidly shifting publishing environment, and serve as a campus-wide voice for sustainable, equitable scholarly communication. This position reports to the Director of Digital Scholarship and works closely with colleagues across the Library and the university. The ideal candidate will bring expertise in open scholarship, copyright, persistent identifiers, and compliance with funder mandates. They will also oversee the institutional repository, UChicago Knowledge, and provide leadership for the staff who run it day to day. Responsibilities: Partner with the Director to set direction for scholarly communication services, ensuring they\u2019re forward-looking, realistic, and aligned with broader Library priorities. Design and deliver inclusive, sustainable services that support open scholarship, long-term preservation, research information management needs, and wide dissemination of University research outputs. Supervise and mentor the Repository Services Manager. Guide policy, development, and operations for UChicago Knowledge. Work directly with faculty, students, and staff to help them manage and share their work, including copyright, PIDs, funder requirements, and more. Provide copyright and scholarly communication expertise across the Library. Collaborate with librarians to create strong outreach, training, and campus engagement. Partner with campus IT and research computing units to evaluate, pilot, and implement technologies that strengthen scholarly communication infrastructure. Participate in national conversations on standards, best practices, and research lifecycle management. Play an active role in Library committees, strategic initiatives, and diversity efforts. Perform other duties as assigned. Required: Master\u2019s degree from an ALA-accredited MLS/MLIS or an advanced degree in a relevant field. At least three years of experience supporting scholarly communication in an academic environment. Experience supervising staff and leading teams through change. Ability to collaborate across disciplines, functions, and diverse communities. Preferred: Experience managing or contributing to institutional repository services, including workflows, metadata practices, or platform administration. Experience supervising staff and leading teams through change. A track record of using technology to evolve services or improve workflows. Evidence of productive, trust-based work with faculty and students. Engagement in professional communities related to scholarly communication. A user-centered mindset, paired with a commitment to continuous improvement and experimentation. To Apply: Submit cover letter, curriculum vitae, and reference contact information online through the University of Chicago's Academic Recruiting website: apply.interfolio.com/194068 . Review of applications will begin after October 21, 2026. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. Salary and Benefits: $72,250- $85,000. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Questions: Contact the University Library Human Resources department at libraryhr@uchicago.edu."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192130": {"title": "Collegiate Assistant Professor - Harper and Schmidt Fellow 2026-27", "description": "Collegiate Assistant Professor - Harper and Schmidt Fellow 2026-27. The College. Position type: Faculty - Instructor/Collegiate Assistant Prof.. Posted: Sep 21, 2026. THE UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO GENERAL EDUCATION TEACHING IN THE HUMANITIES AND SOCIAL SCIENCES The College at the University of Chicago is now accepting applications for four-year postdoctoral teaching appointments as Harper and Schmidt Fellows who hold the rank of Collegiate Assistant Professor. Collegiate Assistant Professors are members of the College Faculty whose primary professional responsibility is to teach in the general education (Core) program . Core courses in the Humanities typically deal with fundamental issues and texts in history, philosophy, and literature. We are inviting applications to all eight sequences into which the Humanities core is organized: Readings in World Literature; Human Being and Citizen; Greece and Rome: Texts, Traditions, Transformations; Philosophical Perspectives on the Humanities; Media Aesthetics; Reading Cultures; Poetry and the Human; and Language and the Human. Core courses in Social Sciences explore, on the basis of significant works or primary source materials, the fundamental concepts and the different modes of inquiry that have defined the social sciences in the modern period. For the 2027-28 cohort of Fellows, we are inviting applications for Collegiate Assistant Professors to teach in three of the year-long sequences into which the Core is organized: Mind ; Power, Identity, Resistance ; and Social Science Inquiry. Other Social Science sequences ( Classics of Social and Political Thought ; Democracy: Equality, Liberty and the Dilemma of Self-Governance ; Global Society ; and Self, Culture and Society ) will also review application files if programmatically necessary. We encourage applicants to spend time reviewing the descriptions and syllabi for Core sequences to understand the distinct topics and approaches and gauge best fit. Descriptions of the Humanities courses are available here: https://college.uchicago.edu/academics/core/humanities-core and the Social Sciences courses here: https://college.uchicago.edu/academics/core/social-sciences-core . In most years, Collegiate Assistant Professors teach two courses (usually of identical preparation) for each of three quarters. In the second through fourth years of residence, each Collegiate Assistant Professor has the opportunity to design up to two independent courses in total, and each is eligible for two quarters of research sabbatical. In the fourth year, there is a possibility of another quarter with a reduced teaching load. The position comes with a salary of $92,875, carries full benefits, and provides a minimum annual professional development fund of $7,000. The effective date for these teaching appointments is September 1, 2027. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The terms and conditions of these positions are described in the collective bargaining agreement between the University and the Service Employees International Union. Online application deadline: 11:59:59 PM EST, Tuesday, December 1, 2026 The positions are open to scholars in all disciplines and areas of specialization who will have completed all requirements for their Ph.D. degree no later than August 31, 2027 . Candidates must demonstrate excellence in teaching as well as in their original scholarship. While many Core sequences feature set reading lists, applicants need not have conventional expertise in the specific texts and topics that are currently taught. Rather, we welcome applicants from a wide variety of disciplinary backgrounds who can apply conceptual and methodological creativity and skill to the teaching of general education courses. Applicants should be prepared 1) to identify and guide students through fundamental questions in a Core sequence and 2) to contribute meaningfully to the instructional community and the curricular evolution for that Core sequence. To complete your application, you will need to upload the following supporting documents via the Interfolio website: apply.interfolio.com/193778 . A curriculum vitae (be sure to list your full legal name) A cover letter describing your past and current research as well as your teaching experience and interests A Teaching Statement, not to exceed 750 words, describing your methods and goals in teaching a text in a general education course. Applicants should write about a specific text of their own choosing, which need not come directly from a Core sequence syllabus but should lend itself to a similar kind of general education course in the chosen program area. NB: Applicants to the MIND sequence should focus their Teaching Statement on the relationship between theory and evidence in understanding the chosen topic . For a description of the sequences, see the College Core Website https://college.uchicago.edu/academics/core-curriculum A research statement, not to exceed 750 words A writing sample of up to 50 pages One or two syllabi from previously taught courses Teaching evaluations Three confidential letters of recommendation, to be received by the application deadline Complete applications include all of the above materials. Only complete applications will be reviewed. Please note that the application deadline is December 1st, 2026 11:59pm EASTERN STANDARD TIME Applications not received by the deadline will not be reviewed. There are no exceptions . Start uploading your application well before the deadline of December 1st, 2026 11:59pm EASTERN STANDARD TIME"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190649": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Nephrology #PED158b", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Nephrology #PED158b. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 21, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Nephrology, and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital seek full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will have experience in the care of all forms of kidney disease in children and will have experience in caring for children with End Stage Renal Disease. Appointees will be expected to participate in patient care at both Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and the Section\u2019s off-site locations in the Chicago area. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is designated as a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and operates a nationally recognized burn unit. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is an integrated children\u2019s hospital that offers a full complement of pediatric services with a total of 172 general pediatric, NICU, and PICU beds. The Department of Pediatrics includes over 170 full-time faculty members and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents, and fellows, our faculty conducts cutting-edge translational and clinical research. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Pediatric Nephrology or equivalent, and 4) have participated in prior clinical, translational, or laboratory-based research. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192916 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192290": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer: Biomedical Innovation #MED319a", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer: Biomedical Innovation #MED319a. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 21, 2026. The Department of Medicine at the University of Chicago seeks a part-time lecturer to develop and teach a course in the Master of Science in Biomedical Sciences (BMS) program ( https://biomed.bsd.uchicago.edu ). The position is part-time and renewable. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits ineligible. The appointee will serve as the primary instructor for the graduate-level course, which should ideally provide students with an overview of key milestones for developing a new biomedical venture, and the value created at each inflection point. The elective course is open to Master\u2019s students in the Biological Sciences Division but may also be open to other students with permission of the instructor. The lecturer will be required to develop (with assistance from a teaching assistant) all course materials, including the development and management of a Canvas Learning Management System course site. Courses run for nine weeks, with the tenth week for a final project or exam. It is preferable that the course meets in the late afternoon either 1 or 2 days a week for a total of 3 hours, although the exact day(s)/time(s) will be determined in consultation with the Lecturer. The class is offered in-person on the University\u2019s main campus in the Hyde Park neighborhood of Chicago, IL. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a graduate level degree in a relevant field. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/193921 . Applicants must upload: 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a proposed syllabus that covers the course topic in sufficient detail, 4) a teaching statement, and 5) names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will end when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187087": {"title": "Ultrasound Director - Emergency Medicine #MED341a", "description": "Ultrasound Director - Emergency Medicine #MED341a. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 21, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking a full-time faculty member at any rank to serve as Ultrasound Director. Responsibilities include ultrasound operations, quality oversight, medical student and resident education, participation in our EM Ultrasound Fellowship Program, and participation in scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. The University of Chicago Emergency Medicine residency program, established in 1972, has a rich history as one of the oldest emergency medicine programs in the country. The appointee will join an academic faculty who are active in research, medical education, simulation, international medicine, EMS/prehospital medicine, and air-medical transport. The University of Chicago Medicine is a STEMI receiving hospital, a comprehensive Stroke Center, a Burn Center, an Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center, and one of the Chicago South EMS regional resource hospitals. The Adult ED has an annual volume of over 65,000 patients, and our Pediatric ED has a volume of 30,000 patients per year. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine, and 4) be ultrasound fellowship trained. We especially welcome applicants who possess leadership skills and a commitment to excellence in patient care, clinical operations, and education. This position will provide the opportunity to join committed faculty members and outstanding residents who are dedicated to academic excellence. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/189273 . Applicants must upload a CV, including a bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit https://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191655": {"title": "Clinical Associates - General Cardiology #MED321b", "description": "Clinical Associates - General Cardiology #MED321b. MED - Section of Cardiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 18, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is seeking full-time clinical cardiologists for appointments as Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. These positions will be primarily focused on outpatient cardiology practice and consultation at our various Chicagoland locations. Appointees will read EKGs, perform exercise stress tests, and read/interpret echocardiograms. There will also be opportunities for inpatient consultation at our main campus in Hyde Park. These positions have opportunities for teaching and scholarly activities, but these are not requirements. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Cardiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/193343 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192217": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor", "description": "Research Assistant Professor. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Sep 17, 2026. The University of Chicago's Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) invites applications for a Research Assistant Professor to join ChemMatCARS. ChemMatCARS, affiliated with the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, the University of Chicago and funded by NSF, is a national synchrotron X-ray user facility located at Sector 15 of the Advanced Photon Source at Argonne National Laboratory. ChemMatCARS provides cutting-edge synchrotron X-ray facilities to support molecular science and engineering research, specializing in advanced chemical and materials crystallography, liquid and soft matter interface science, and anomalous small- and wide-angle scattering. The Research Assistant Professor will serve as a Beamline Scientist and will be expected to originate scientific research and engage in scientific collaborations, develop new techniques, and support existing synchrotron radiation studies at ChemMatCARS. The term of the appointment will be for up to three years with the possibility of renewal. This position is expected to start on or about January 1, 2027. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits guidebook . Minimum Qualifications: PhD in Physics, Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Biophysics, Materials Science, or related field. A minimum of five years, beyond PhD training, conducting independent experimental research at a synchrotron x-ray facility. Must be able to fulfill requirements for access to Argonne National Laboratory and the Advanced Photon Source. Preferred qualifications: E xperience with interfacial X-ray scattering techniques. Experience with interpretation of X-ray reflectivity data. Experience with supported bio-membrane preparation and characterization. The following materials are required: CV Cover letter A list of three references and contact information Research statement Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s academic jobs website: https://apply.interfolio.com/193865"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191922": {"title": "Clinical Chemistry, Assistant Medical Director #PAT061", "description": "Clinical Chemistry, Assistant Medical Director #PAT061. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 17, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pathology, Clinical Chemistry Laboratories, is searching for a full-time Assistant Medical Director at the rank of Assistant Professor. The Clinical Chemistry section has state-of-the-art total laboratory automation and advanced analytical platforms that provide a vast array of clinical diagnostic testing to our medical center, affiliated network, and outreach clients. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. The appointee will be expected to assist the Medical Director in evaluating and implementing new test, providing consultations to both clinicians and laboratory personnel, and overseeing the quality management program. The appointee will also perform review of SPEP/IFE results, participate in the teaching of clinical chemistry fellows in our ComACC-accredited Clinical Chemistry Fellowship Program, as well as pathology residents and medical technologists, and conduct clinical and translational research. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have an MD, DO, or PhD. MD or DO candidates must: 1) be certified or eligible for certification by the American Board of Pathology (ABPath) in Clinical Pathology, 2) be certified or eligible for certification by either the ABPath in Chemical Pathology or the American Board of Clinical Chemistry (ABCC) in Clinical Chemistry, and 3) hold, or be eligible to obtain Illinois and Indiana medical licenses. PhD candidates must be certified or eligible for certification by the ABCC in Clinical Chemistry and must have completed fellowship training. We especially welcome applicants with experience in general diagnostic clinical chemistry, laboratory automation systems, and middleware, preferably in a core laboratory setting. Additional experience in blood gas and point-of-care testing is highly desirable, as is experience with protein electrophoresis services. We also welcome applicants with strong problem-solving, multitasking, and interpersonal skills. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/193589. Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/192022": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics", "description": "Assistant Professor, Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 16, 2026. The Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions in Economics at the Assistant Professor level. This is a full-time, benefits-eligible position expected to begin in the 2027-28 academic year. Applicants in all fields of Economics will be considered. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess a Ph.D. in Economics. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board at: apply.interfolio.com/193682 . Applications are required to include a curriculum vitae, teaching statement, research statement, student evaluations, three letters of recommendation and a job market paper. Additionally, applicants are strongly encouraged to submit published or unpublished research papers. Review of applications will begin on October 19, 2026 and will continue until June 30, 2027, or until the position is filled. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191937": {"title": "Instructional Professor (open rank) in Chemistry", "description": "Instructional Professor (open rank) in Chemistry. Department of Chemistry. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 15, 2026. The Department of Chemistry at the University of Chicago invites applications for a teaching position at the rank of Instructional Professor (open rank). The selected candidate will be appointed as Assistant Instructional Professor, Associate Instructional Professor, or Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications. The appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable. This is a career-track position with potential progression and competitive salary and benefits. Expected start date is academic year 2026-2027. Position responsibilities include: \u00b7 Teach up to six courses per year, including introductory courses in the chemistry major. Collaborative Learning responsibilities will be credited toward and reduce this teaching load. \u00b7 Lead and advance a collaborative learning program to support student success and persistence in STEM. \u00b7 Incorporate innovative, evidence-based pedagogical approaches, including collaborative learning. \u00b7 Contribute to Phoenix STEM program goals, including supporting first-generation and historically underrepresented students. \u00b7 Participate in non-classroom instructional and service activities as needed. \u00b7 Engage in ongoing professional development. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Minimum qualifications: 1. Completion of a Ph.D. degree in Chemistry or a closely related field is required prior to the start of appointment. Preferred qualifications: 1. Teaching experience as an instructor of record in chemistry at the University level. 2. Training in pedagogical innovations. 3. Ability to teach introductory sequences in general chemistry, organic chemistry, or chemistry for non-majors. Applicants must apply online at the University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/193603. Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. A complete application consists of: 1. A cover letter explaining your interest in the position, and relevant teaching experience. 2. A curriculum vitae (CV), including a complete history of courses taught. 3. Syllabi of courses previously taught and/or designed , or sample syllabi for courses you propose to teach. 4. A teaching statement (up to 3 pages) describing your teaching philosophy, instructional methods, commitment to student learning, mentoring experience, and future teaching goals. 5. Additional evidence of pedagogical accomplishment and promise, such as, but not limited to: a teaching portfolio, recent course evaluations, third-party observations of the applicant\u2019s teaching, or other relevant assessments. 6. Three confidential letters of reference requested via Interfolio that specifically address your teaching effectiveness and classroom performance."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191907": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science", "description": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science. Department of Computer Science. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 15, 2026. The Department of Computer Science in the Physical Sciences Division at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor. This is a broad area search and invites applicants from all areas of Computer Science and Engineering, with particular interests in candidates who work on Systems, AI, Robotics / Embodied Intelligence . The Department of Computer Science (cs.uchicago.edu) is the hub of a large, diverse community of researchers focused on advancing the foundations of computing and driving its most advanced applications. The larger computer science community at the University of Chicago includes the Data Science Institute (jointly run by the departments of computer science and statistics), the Toyota Technological Institute at Chicago (TTIC), the Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation, and Argonne National Laboratory. We encourage connections with researchers across the university in such areas as bioinformatics, data science, mathematics, molecular engineering, linguistics, statistics, public policy, and social science, among others: the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. The University of Chicago is in the midst of an ambitious, multi-year effort to significantly expand its computing, data science, and AI activities. This represents a unique opportunity for junior faculty to contribute to the future of the department\u2019s culture and trajectory. The Department of Computer Science is housed in a newly designed, state-of-the-art building that encourages collaboration, experimentation, and interaction. Members of the faculty are expected to lead research programs that will produce significant contributions to a field and teach and mentor at the undergraduate and graduate levels. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in Computer Science or a related field is required at the time of appointment. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/193574 Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: \u00b7 cover letter; \u00b7 curriculum vitae including a list of publications; \u00b7 statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans; \u00b7 description of teaching and mentoring philosophy and experience; \u00b7 applicants are required to request at least three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio. The following materials are optional: \u00b7 up to three sample publications."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191813": {"title": "INAUGURAL CHAIR AND PROFESSOR: DEPARTMENT OF EMERGENCY MEDICINE  #BSD045", "description": "INAUGURAL CHAIR AND PROFESSOR: DEPARTMENT OF EMERGENCY MEDICINE #BSD045. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Sep 14, 2026. The University of Chicago's Division of Biological Sciences and Pritzker School of Medicine, one of the country's pre-eminent medical schools, seeks nominations and applications for an outstanding academic, visionary emergency medicine leader to serve as the Inaugural Chair and Professor, Department of Emergency Medicine. The appointee will join the University of Chicago faculty at an exciting time in the School of Medicine's long history. The appointee will be expected to take full advantage of the extraordinary resources of one of the world's most remarkable academic medical centers, with a strong commitment to clinical, research, and educational endeavors. Reporting to the Dean of the Division of Biological Sciences and the Pritzker School of Medicine, the Chair of the Department of Emergency Medicine will have overall responsibility for strategy, clinical and educational program development, and operations within Emergency Medicine. The new Department builds on a Section of Emergency Medicine with decades of institutional strength \u2014 a thriving residency among the nation's oldest, five fellowship programs, and a faculty of clinician-scholars with a remarkable track record of producing leaders in the field. The Inaugural Chair inherits a strong foundation and the institution's full support to build something extraordinary. The Chair will oversee the growth and development of the department's outstanding clinical programs and will support ground-breaking basic, clinical, and translational research. They will be a transformative leader with the vision and drive to establish a top-tier academic emergency medicine program and to position the University of Chicago as a national leader in the field. The Inaugural Chair will be responsible for: \u2022 Expanding faculty and supporting robust residency and fellowship training programs \u2022 Fostering a culture of innovation and scholarship \u2022 Cultivating key partnerships across the University's clinical enterprise and broader academic community \u2022 Engaging with regional and national emergency medicine organizations \u2022 Serving as a champion for high-quality emergency care for the communities served by the University of Chicago Medicine Please find here an expanded position specification, including contact information for inquiries and nominations. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: \u2022 Have a medical doctorate or equivalent \u2022 Hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois \u2022 Hold Board certification by the American Board of Emergency Medicine or comparable credentials \u2022 Qualify for appointment at senior faculty rank The appointee will have a faculty appointment. Academic rank, track, tenure status, and compensation depend on qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about benefit offerings is available in the Benefits Guidebook . To be considered, applicants must apply through the University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/193491 . Applicants must upload: \u2022 A cover letter \u2022 A CV, including bibliography \u2022 A brief bullet-point summary of administrative roles held and their accomplishments in each We will continue reviewing complete applications until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191465": {"title": "Pediatric Psychologists - GI #PBN086", "description": "Pediatric Psychologists - GI #PBN086. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 11, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide assessments and brief, evidence-based interventions for pediatric patients in gastroenterology (GI), interact with team members to inform shared treatment plans, and deliver clinical and consultative services to GI providers. Responsibilities include supporting clinics including neurogastroenterology (e.g., IBS, gastroparesis, abdominal pain), celiac, and obesity. Appointees will join a multidisciplinary team aiming to initiate and advance the reach and effectiveness of the integrated behavioral health program in pediatric GI. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a PhD or PsyD in Clinical Psychology or Counseling Psychology from an APA-accredited university-based doctoral program, 2) hold or be eligible for an Illinois license in Clinical Psychology, and 3) have completed an internship at an APA-accredited site. We especially welcome applicants with training and experience in pediatric psychology (with an emphasis in GI preferred), behavioral medicine principles, evidence-based treatment for medical and psychiatric conditions, and/or competency in cognitive-behavior therapy, including third wave treatments and motivational enhancement. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/193172 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191566": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Chemistry", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Chemistry. Department of Chemistry. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 9, 2026. The Department of Chemistry in the Physical Sciences Division at the University of Chicago invites applications for a part-time lecturer position. This position is expected to begin on or after September 28, 2026. The appointment will be for autumn quarter 2026, with the possibility of renewal for future terms and potential progression. The position responsibilities include teaching laboratory and discussion sections of general and/or organic chemistry and may involve non-classroom instructional or service duties as necessary. This position is part-time and is not benefits eligible. This is not a salaried position; compensation is paid on a per-course basis (or per course-equivalent) in the teaching quarter(s). Applicants must currently be authorized to work in the U.S. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University. Required qualifications: By the time of hire, completion of all requirements for a degree (M.S.) in Chemistry or a related field. At least one year of university-level teaching experience, either as an instructor of record or as a teaching assistant. Preferred qualifications: A Ph.D. in Chemistry or a related field Training, credentials, or experience in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in organic chemistry. Applicants must apply online at apply.interfolio.com/193269 and upload the following required materials: Cover letter Current curriculum vitae Syllabi of courses previously taught and/or designed by the candidate or sample syllabi of proposed courses A teaching statement Names and contact information of three references whose recommendation letters may be solicited Additional evidence of pedagogical accomplishment and promise, such as, but not limited to: a teaching portfolio, recent course evaluations, third-party observations of the applicant\u2019s teaching, or other relevant assessments Review of applications will begin on September 21, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190883": {"title": "General Urologists, Clinical Associates #SUR157a", "description": "General Urologists, Clinical Associates #SUR157a. Section of Urology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 9, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Urology, is searching for full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will have inpatient and outpatient responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Urology at the Hyde Park location and affiliated sites depending on the department\u2019s needs. Clinical duties include direct patient care through office visits, ambulatory procedures, state-of-the-art surgery, and call. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Urology excels in robotic and laparoscopic treatments for both cancerous and non-cancerous urologic conditions, with national expertise in bladder cancer, prostate issues, reconstructive surgery, kidney stones, and pediatric urology. The program includes practice activities on the main campus in Hyde Park as well as at Orland Park and the South Loop; outpatient facilities are located in River East; the Ingalls Health System, which has integrated into our network; a multispecialty care facility in Northwest Indiana; and affiliated clinics at Advent Health. Appointees may be assigned to one or more of these locations. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate (MD) or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or eligible with the American Board of Urology or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192632 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after September 19, 2026. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191138": {"title": "Neurotologist Clinical Associates, Part-time (50% FTE) #SUR196", "description": "Neurotologist Clinical Associates, Part-time (50% FTE) #SUR196. Section of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 8, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Otolaryngology\u2013Head & Neck Surgery is seeking part-time Neurotology Clinical Associates at 50 % effort for renewable terms of up to 2 year s . Appointees will focus on the diagnosis and treatment of a broad range of ear-related disorders and will share our mission of providing the highest quality patient care. Appointees will support and complement faculty in Otolaryngology\u2013Head and Neck Surgery at the Hyde Park campus and affiliated satellites. Duties will include patient care in the ambulatory and operative settings. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible . The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery has a rich history, matched only by our ongoing commitment to excellence in medical education, research, patient care, and service to our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible by the American Board of Otolaryngology or equivalent, and 4) have completed an accredited residency or equivalent training in Otolaryngology\u2013Head and Neck Surgery . To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192873 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191144": {"title": "Neurotologist Clinical Associates, Part-time (Pay-Per Shift) #SUR195 ", "description": "Neurotologist Clinical Associates, Part-time (Pay-Per Shift) #SUR195 . Section of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 8, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Otolaryngology\u2013Head & Neck Surgery is seeking part-time Neurotology Clinical Associates at 10-30% effort for renewable terms of up to 1 year. Appointees will focus on the diagnosis and treatment of a broad range of ear-related disorders and will share our mission of providing the highest quality patient care. Appointees will support and complement faculty in Otolaryngology\u2013Head and Neck Surgery at the Hyde Park campus and affiliated satellites. Duties will include patient care in the ambulatory and operative settings. Compensation is paid per shift. These positions are benefits ineligible. The Department of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery has a rich history, matched only by our ongoing commitment to excellence in medical education, research, patient care, and service to our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible by the American Board of Otolaryngology or equivalent, and 4) have completed an accredited residency or equivalent training in Otolaryngology\u2013Head and Neck Surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192878 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190427": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry", "description": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry. Department of Chemistry. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 7, 2026. The Department of Chemistry at The University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track Assistant Professor of Chemistry, in any area of Chemistry broadly defined. Secondary appointments with other departments are possible. Review of applications will begin November 15th, 2026, and will continue until all positions are filled or the search is closed. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance and housing support. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. At the time of hire the successful candidate must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D., or the foreign equivalent, in chemistry or a related field. Applicants must apply online at apply.interfolio.com/192208 and upload the following required materials: a cover letter a curriculum vitae with a list of publications a succinct outline of research plans a one-page teaching statement applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation through Interfolio"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191123": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Department of Statistics", "description": "Assistant Professor, Department of Statistics. Department of Statistics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 7, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Statistics invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. The search is open to any area in the field of Statistics, broadly defined. The University of Chicago has the highest standards for scholarship and faculty quality, is dedicated to fundamental research, and has an inherently interdisciplinary structure that is very supportive of newly emerging research opportunities. Minimum qualifications: Completion of all requirements for the PhD in statistics or some field of computer science, mathematics, or other areas where statistical concepts or methods play an important role. Preferred qualifications: At least one year of university-level teaching experience, such as, but not limited to, a TA or instructor of record; At least two years of experience as a postdoctoral researcher. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/192854 . Review of applications will begin on November 1 st , 2026 and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae including a list of publications; statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans; a teaching statement three confidential letters of reference. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191236": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics", "description": "Assistant Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics. Department of Astronomy and Astrophysics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 4, 2026. The Department of Astronomy & Astrophysics at the University of Chicago invites applications for an Assistant Professor. We are interested in outstanding scientists who use theoretical, computational, and/or observational approaches to study any aspect of the \u201cExtreme Universe\u201d, including multi-messenger/time-domain Astronomy and high-energy/plasma Astrophysics. The ideal candidate would pursue an original research program in the Department, possibly complementing our existing strengths in cosmology, observational astronomy, galaxy formation, and exoplanets. Computational astrophysics is supported by access to high-performance computing through the Research Computing Center at the University and through connections to Fermilab and Argonne National Laboratory, which hosts the Aurora exascale supercomputer. Department members are also involved in the Kavli Institute for Cosmological Physics, the Enrico Fermi Institute, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory; they play leading roles in a wide range of research projects, including Rubin Observatory's Legacy Survey of Space and Time (LSST), Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory (LIGO), the South Pole Telescope (SPT), the Simons Observatory; X-Ray Imaging and Spectroscopy Mission (XRISM), high-energy neutrino experiments such as RNO-G and PUEO, and many others; more details can be found at the Department website. The University of Chicago is a founding partner of the Giant Magellan Telescope, and members of the Department have access to the twin 6.5-m Magellan Telescopes. All University departments and institutes are charged with building a faculty from a diversity of backgrounds and with diverse viewpoints, to cultivate an inclusive community that values freedom of expression, and to welcome and support all their members. The successful candidate will conduct an independent research program and teach and advise undergraduate and graduate students. The salary range for this position is $120,000- $130,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for housing support and a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. All requirements for a Ph.D. in Physics, Astrophysics or a closely related field must be completed prior to the start of the appointment, expected to be July 1, 2027 or later . Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago academic careers website: apply.interfolio.com/192963 Required materials are: \u2014 a cover letter, \u2014 a curriculum vitae, \u2014 a list of publications, \u2014 a statement of research, \u2014 a teaching statement (which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching and mentoring activities, teaching philosophy and goals, and should also, where possible, include a description of the candidate\u2019s experience in teaching and mentoring students, research or teaching assistants, or postdoctoral fellows, and in fostering their learning and ability to do research). Applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin on Monday, November 16 and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191238": {"title": "Associate Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics", "description": "Associate Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics. Department of Astronomy and Astrophysics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 4, 2026. The Department of Astronomy & Astrophysics at the University of Chicago invites applications for an Associate Professor. We are interested in outstanding scientists who use theoretical, computational, and/or observational approaches to study any aspect of the \u201cExtreme Universe\u201d, including multi-messenger/time-domain Astronomy and high-energy/plasma Astrophysics. The ideal candidate would pursue an original research program in the Department, possibly complementing our existing strengths in cosmology, observational astronomy, galaxy formation, and exoplanets. Computational astrophysics is supported by access to high-performance computing through the Research Computing Center at the University and through connections to Fermilab and Argonne National Laboratory, which hosts the Aurora exascale supercomputer. Department members are also involved in the Kavli Institute for Cosmological Physics, the Enrico Fermi Institute, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory; they play leading roles in a wide range of research projects, including the Rubin Observatory's Legacy Survey of Space and Time (LSST), Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory (LIGO), the South Pole Telescope (SPT), the Simons Observatory, the X-Ray Imaging and Spectroscopy Mission (XRISM), high-energy neutrino experiments such as RNO-G and PUEO, and many others; more details can be found at the Department website. The University of Chicago is a founding partner of the Giant Magellan Telescope, and members of the Department have access to the twin 6.5-m Magellan Telescopes. All University departments and institutes are charged with building a faculty from a diversity of backgrounds and with diverse viewpoints, to cultivate an inclusive community that values freedom of expression, and to welcome and support all their members. The successful candidate will conduct an independent research program and teach and advise undergraduate and graduate students. The salary range for this position is $135,000 - $150,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for housing support and a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. A Ph.D. in astrophysics or a closely related field is required. Additionally, evidence of leadership in the field and a successful independent research record is required. Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago academic careers website: apply.interfolio.com/192965 Required materials are: \u25cf a cover letter, \u25cf a curriculum vitae, \u25cf a list of publications, \u25cf a statement of research, \u25cf and a teaching statement (which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching and mentoring activities, teaching philosophy and goals, and should also, where possible, include a description of the candidate\u2019s experience in teaching and mentoring students, research or teaching assistants, or postdoctoral fellows, and in fostering their learning and ability to do research; the teaching statement may also include relevant experience in teaching and mentoring students from a diversity of backgrounds, if applicable). Contact information for three references is also required. Review of applications will begin on Monday, November 16 and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191245": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics (Origins of Life)", "description": "Assistant Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics (Origins of Life). Department of Astronomy and Astrophysics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 4, 2026. The Department of Astronomy & Astrophysics at The University of Chicago invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in observational astrophysics relevant to the Origins of Life, broadly defined. The Department is seeking candidates who would strengthen the Department\u2019s connection to the Chicago Center for the Origins of Life, which supports multidisciplinary research focused on understanding how life developed on Earth and the potential for life elsewhere. The Center brings together scientists across the Physical Sciences Division of the University of Chicago, primarily from the Departments of Astronomy and Astrophysics, Chemistry, the Geophysical Sciences, and Physics, to promote, encourage, and support fundamental research in this area, train early-career scientists in cross-disciplinary scientific exploration, and foster a community of innovative thinkers that will push traditional disciplinary boundaries in studying the history and future of life in the universe. The University of Chicago is a founding partner of the Giant Magellan Telescope, and members of the Department have access to the twin 6.5-m Magellan Telescopes. Computational astrophysics is supported by access to high-performance computing through the Research Computing Center at the University and through connections to Argonne National Laboratory and Fermilab. The University of Chicago has an inherently crosscutting and interdisciplinary structure that is very supportive of newly emerging research opportunities and is open to the possibility of appointments with other departments. Department members are also involved in the Kavli Institute for Cosmological Physics, the Enrico Fermi Institute, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory. The successful candidate will conduct an independent research program, and teach and advise undergraduate and graduate students. The salary range for this position is $120,000- $130,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for housing support and a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in Physics, Astrophysics, or a closely related field at the time of hire, expected to be July 1, 2027 or later. Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago academic careers website at apply.interfolio.com/192968 and upload the following required materials: \u2022 A cover letter. \u2022 A curriculum vitae. \u2022 A list of publications. \u2022 A research statement. \u2022 A teaching statement (which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching and mentoring activities, teaching philosophy and goals, and should also, where possible, include a description of the candidate\u2019s experience in teaching and mentoring students, research or teaching assistants, or postdoctoral fellows, and in fostering their learning and ability to do research; the teaching statement may also include relevant experience in teaching and mentoring students from a diversity of backgrounds, if applicable). \u2022 Applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio. Review of applications will begin on Monday, November 16 and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191246": {"title": "Associate Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics (Origins of Life)", "description": "Associate Professor in Astronomy & Astrophysics (Origins of Life). Department of Astronomy and Astrophysics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 4, 2026. The Department of Astronomy & Astrophysics at The University of Chicago invites applications for an Associate Professor in observational astrophysics relevant to the Origins of Life, broadly defined. The Department is seeking candidates who would strengthen the Department\u2019s connection to the Chicago Center for the Origins of Life, which supports multidisciplinary research focused on understanding how life developed on Earth and the potential for life elsewhere. The Center brings together scientists across the Physical Sciences Division of the University of Chicago, primarily from the Departments of Astronomy and Astrophysics, Chemistry, the Geophysical Sciences, and Physics, to promote, encourage, and support fundamental research in this area, train early-career scientists in cross-disciplinary scientific exploration, and foster a community of innovative thinkers that will push traditional disciplinary boundaries in studying the history and future of life in the universe. The University of Chicago is a founding partner of the Giant Magellan Telescope, and members of the Department have access to the twin 6.5-m Magellan Telescopes. Computational astrophysics is supported by access to high-performance computing through the Research Computing Center at the University and through connections to Argonne National Laboratory and Fermilab. The University of Chicago has an inherently crosscutting and interdisciplinary structure that is very supportive of newly emerging research opportunities and is open to the possibility of appointments with other departments. Department members are also involved in the Kavli Institute for Cosmological Physics, the Enrico Fermi Institute, Fermilab, and Argonne National Laboratory. The successful candidate will conduct an independent research program, and teach and advise undergraduate and graduate students. The salary range for this position is $135,000-$150,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for housing support and a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. A Ph.D. in Astrophysics or a closely related field is required. Additionally, evidence of leadership in the field and a successful independent research record is required. Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago academic careers website at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/192971 and upload the following required materials: \u25cf a cover letter, \u25cf a curriculum vitae, \u25cf a list of publications, \u25cf a statement of research, \u25cf and a teaching statement (which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching and mentoring activities, teaching philosophy and goals, and should also, where possible, include a description of the candidate\u2019s experience in teaching and mentoring students, research or teaching assistants, or postdoctoral fellows, and in fostering their learning and ability to do research; the teaching statement may also include relevant experience in teaching and mentoring students from a diversity of backgrounds, if applicable). Contact information for three references is also required. Review of applications will begin on Monday, November 16 and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191319": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Theater and Performance Studies", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer in Theater and Performance Studies. Committee on Theater and Performance Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 4, 2026. The Committee on Theater and Performance Studies at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers during the 2026-27 academic year. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in the Theater and Performance Studies program. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching seminar- or workshop-style courses, holding regularly scheduled office hours, and preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the department, the successful candidate may teach between one and four courses per academic year. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a Bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent in Theater or a related discipline; a Master\u2019s degree or PhD is strongly preferred. Applicants must have a minimum of three years of professional theater experience. Applications must include: A current curriculum vitae, A cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience, And the names and contact information of three references whose recommendation letters may be solicited. Optional materials: Course evaluations, if available The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/193042 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. Review of applications will begin on September 8, 2026, and continue until March 31, 2027, or until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190996": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Migration Studies", "description": "Assistant Professor of Migration Studies. Department of Race, Diaspora, and Indigeneity. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 4, 2026. The University of Chicago, Division of the Social Sciences, Department of Race, Diaspora, and Indigeneity invites applications for a tenure track position at the rank of assistant professor for an initial 4 year renewable term. This is a full-time position with an expected start date on or after July 1, 2027. We intend to appoint a scholar in the humanities or social sciences whose research examines historical or contemporary migrations, displacements, and diasporas. The scope of this search is resolutely global, and we encourage applications from scholars who examine the contours of race, Indigeneity, and migration in or between the broader Americas, Mediterranean, Asia, Pacific, Indian Ocean, or the African continent. We welcome applicants from a range of methodological traditions including qualitative and quantitative social sciences, archival and oral historical approaches, literary and performance studies. Successful candidates will maintain a research and teaching agenda that centers questions of race and/or Indigeneity as foundational, rather than ancillary, to the study of migration and the intellectual project of the Department of Race, Diaspora, and Indigeneity. The selected candidate will pursue their own research agenda, collaborate with faculty in the Department of Race, Diaspora, and Indigeneity and other areas of the Division of the Social Sciences and University, and teach in both the undergraduate and graduate curriculum. The teaching load for tenure-track positions is typically four courses per year on the quarter system, including in the University\u2019s College Core curriculum . Additional responsibilities include advising at all levels of the program, and service on departmental and university committees. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . About the Department Founded in 2022, the Department of Race, Diaspora, and Indigeneity (RDI) is home to an interdisciplinary group of scholars whose work engages the conceptual pillars of the unit. RDI is housed in the Social Sciences at the University but is committed to working across the social sciences, humanities, sciences, professional schools, and the arts to build a truly interdisciplinary, outward-facing department. RDI values community-engaged and informed work. We currently offer a robust undergraduate curriculum (including a major and a minor) and a graduate certificate. We are preparing to launch a PhD program in the coming years. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess a terminal Degree in hand by the start of the appointment in a relevant field. We welcome applications from scholars trained in all disciplines in the social sciences, humanities, sciences, professional schools, and the arts, as well as those trained in interdisciplinary departments or programs. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192729. Applicants must upload:1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a research statement in which you state your intellectual vision and trajectory, including a brief description of any public-facing work you have done and that you may hope to do in the future, 4) a teaching statement, which includes what you hope to accomplish as a teacher and mentor in the classroom and beyond as well as your vision of diversity, equity, and inclusion, 5) writing sample which is either a published piece from a book, edited volume or journal, or if unpublished, a work or pieces that is no more than 10,000 words. 6)Provide a list of three references that may be contacted at a later date. The review of applications will begin after the position has been posted for 30 days and will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189725": {"title": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in Global Studies", "description": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in Global Studies. Social Sciences Collegiate Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Social Sciences Collegiate Division at the University of Chicago invites applications for an open-rank Instructional Professor position in the undergraduate Global Studies program. This is a full-time, career-track teaching position. The initial appointment term will be from two to five years, depending on rank, and renewable with opportunity for promotion within the Instructional Professor track based on performance review and College recommendation. The expected start date is negotiable between March 1 and September 1, 2027. Global Studies is an interdisciplinary, undergraduate program focused on fostering a critical understanding of global institutions, structures, networks, and flows and preparing students for a wide range of internationally focused careers. The Instructional Professor will teach up to six quarter-length courses each academic year on themes central to the Global Studies curriculum, including the core Global Studies sequence and/or BA thesis seminar, as well as methods or topics courses in the interdisciplinary social sciences. The appointee will also provide administrative support for the undergraduate curriculum, programming, and events and academic advising for Global Studies majors, including linking students to relevant research, fellowship, and internship opportunities. This base pay range of $75,676\u2013$100,000 is dependent on rank (assistant, associate, or full) for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position includes support for professional development and may also offer a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. The terms and conditions of this position are described in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Chicago and the Service Employees International Union. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants must have a Ph.D. from a relevant field in hand by the start of the appointment. We are open to all interdisciplinary scholars from the social sciences but will especially welcome applicants with expertise in global institutions, global history, or science and technology. Skill in teaching archival or digital methods is also a plus, as is specialization in East Asia, Africa, or Latin America. To apply for this position, you must submit a complete application through the University of Chicago\u2018 s Interfolio website: apply.interfolio.com/191567. Complete applications include the following: Cover letter describing your interest in the position, teaching experience in global-focused methods and topics, and academic background Curriculum vitae Teaching statement Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations, performance review, or other relevant assessments) Syllabi of courses taught Confidential letters of recommendation from three references A brief (chapter or article-length) sample of the applicant's academic work Review of applications will begin on October 5 , 2026 , and continue until the position is filled or the search is closed."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190967": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Assistant Professor. Department of Physics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Department of Physics at the University of Chicago, together with research institutes including the Enrico Fermi Institute and the James Franck Institute, is conducting a search at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor. The search is open to all subfields of theoretical physics, including, but not limited to, high-energy theory, gravity, condensed matter theory, quantum information, and physics of learning and computation. The Department members cover a wide range of research areas; details can be found at the Department website ( https://physics.uchicago.edu/research/ ). The newly founded Leinweber Institute for Theoretical Physics fosters cross-disciplinary collaboration among theorists working on a wide range of problems. Research connected to the Kavli Institute for Cosmological Physics, Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory, and Argonne National Laboratory is encouraged. Duties include conducting an independent research program, and teaching and advising undergraduate and graduate students. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in physics or a closely related field at the time of hire. Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago Academic Careers website at apply.interfolio.com/192707 Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae including list of publications; research statement; description of teaching and mentoring philosophy and experience; applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190976": {"title": "Associate Professor", "description": "Associate Professor. Department of Physics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Department of Physics at the University of Chicago, together with research institutes including the Enrico Fermi Institute and the James Franck Institute, is conducting a search at the rank of tenured Associate Professor. The search is open to all subfields of theoretical physics, including, but not limited to, high-energy theory, gravity, condensed matter theory, quantum information, and physics of learning and computation. The Department members cover a wide range of research areas; details can be found at the Department website ( https://physics.uchicago.edu/research/ ). The newly founded Leinweber Institute for Theoretical Physics fosters cross-disciplinary collaboration among theorists working on a wide range of problems. Research connected to the Kavli Institute for Cosmological Physics, Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory, and Argonne National Laboratory is encouraged. Duties include conducting an independent research program, and teaching and advising undergraduate and graduate students. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in physics or a closely related field at the time of hire. Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago Academic Careers website at apply.interfolio.com/192713 Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae including list of publications; research statement; description of teaching and mentoring philosophy and experience; a list of three references, including name, title, institution, and contact information"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191153": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences - Earth and Planetary Materials", "description": "Assistant Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences - Earth and Planetary Materials. Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor. For this search, we welcome applications from all candidates whose research complements or expands the department\u2019s strengths in the broad areas of geochemistry, cosmochemistry, solid-earth geophysics, planetary evolution or those whose work is aligned with the efforts of the University\u2019s Center for the Origins of Life . We seek individuals who will lead world-class research programs and enhance the intellectual endeavors of the department and the university. Ongoing research activities can be found on the department website. Note: the Department is carrying out a parallel search for an Assistant Professor across all areas of Earth and Planetary Sciences. Candidates are encouraged to also apply for that position. The posting can be found here: apply.interfolio.com/192886 Completion of all requirements for the PhD in Earth or planetary sciences or a related field prior to appointment is required. Required materials are: \u00b7 a cover letter, \u00b7 a curriculum vitae, \u00b7 a statement of research, \u00b7 a statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy; \u00b7 and three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio. Applications must be submitted through the University of Chicago academic careers website: apply.interfolio.com/192883 Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. Base salary range is $120,000 - $150,000 The base salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191154": {"title": "Associate Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences - Earth and Planetary Materials", "description": "Associate Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences - Earth and Planetary Materials. Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of Associate Professor. For this search, we welcome applications from all candidates whose research complements or expands the department\u2019s strengths in the broad areas of geochemistry, cosmochemistry, solid-earth geophysics, planetary evolution or those whose work is aligned with the efforts of the University\u2019s Center for the Origins of Life . We seek individuals who will lead world-class research programs and enhance the intellectual endeavors of the department and the university. Ongoing research activities can be found on the department website. Completion of all requirements for the PhD in Earth or planetary sciences or a related field prior to appointment is required. Additionally, evidence of leadership in the field and successful independent research record is required. Required materials are: \u00b7 a cover letter, \u00b7 a curriculum vitae, \u00b7 a statement of research, \u00b7 and a statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy. Applicants may be asked to provide the names and contact information of three individuals who can provide confidential letters of recommendation. Applications must be submitted through the University of Chicago academic careers website: apply.interfolio.com/192884 Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. Base salary range is $130,000 - $165,000 The base salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191155": {"title": "Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences - Earth and Planetary Materials", "description": "Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences - Earth and Planetary Materials. Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of Professor. For this search, we welcome applications from all candidates whose research complements or expands the department\u2019s strengths in the broad areas of geochemistry, cosmochemistry, solid-earth geophysics, planetary evolution or those whose work is aligned with the efforts of the University\u2019s Center for the Origins of Life . We seek individuals who will lead world-class research programs and enhance the intellectual endeavors of the department and the university. Ongoing research activities can be found on the department website. Completion of all requirements for the PhD in Earth or planetary sciences or a related field prior to appointment is required. Additionally, evidence of leadership in the field and successful independent research record is required. Required materials are: \u00b7 a cover letter, \u00b7 a curriculum vitae, \u00b7 a statement of research, \u00b7 and a statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy. Applicants may be asked to provide the names and contact information of three individuals who can provide confidential letters of recommendation. Applications must be submitted through the University of Chicago academic careers website: apply.interfolio.com/192885 Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. Base salary range is $155,000 - $210,000 The base salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191157": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences", "description": "Assistant Professor, Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor across all areas of Earth and Planetary Science. We seek individuals who will lead world-class research programs and enhance the intellectual endeavors of the department and the university. Ongoing research interests and activities can be found on the department website. Note: The Department is carrying out a parallel search in the general area of Earth and Planetary Materials for candidates whose research focuses on geochemistry, geophysics, cosmochemistry, planetary evolution, or related fields. That search is at all ranks, including Assistant Professor. Applicants whose research aligns with that search are encouraged to also apply for that position. The job posting can be found here: apply.interfolio.com/192883 Completion of all requirements for the PhD in Earth or planetary sciences or a related field prior to appointment is required. Required materials are: \u00b7 a cover letter, \u00b7 a curriculum vitae, \u00b7 a statement of research, \u00b7 a statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy, \u00b7 and three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio. Applications must be submitted through the University of Chicago academic careers website: apply.interfolio.com/192886 Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Base salary range for this position is: Assistant Professor: $120,000 - $150,000 The base salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190640": {"title": "Human Genetics Tenure-Track Faculty HGD011", "description": "Human Genetics Tenure-Track Faculty HGD011. Department of Human Genetics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 2, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Human Genetics in the Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time faculty members. Academic rank, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Competitive start-up funds and generous research space, including access to state-of-the-art core facilities, will be available, as well as graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs within the biological and physical sciences. The Department has broad research interests and existing strengths in the genetics of complex traits and common diseases, statistical and population genetics, and functional genomics of human disease. In this search, we seek scientists that would strengthen and complement our current research in a range of experimental or computational areas. The University is a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation. The Biological Sciences Division, Physical Sciences Division, and the University of Chicago Medical Center, are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park neighborhood. This structure facilitates opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration, notably in the context of two newly launched programs in Computational Biology and (Epi)genomic Networks[CO1], as well as for interactions with our affiliates, Argonne National Laboratory and the Marine Biological Laboratory. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. We especially welcome applications from candidates whose interests include mechanisms of gene expression and chromatin regulation, genome function, gene regulatory networks, the interplay of genetics and environmental factors, and the development and application of high throughput experimental tools. We also seek candidates interested in developing and applying computational, statistical, and machine learning/AI methods to address these and other fundamental questions in human genetics and genomics. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board which uses Interfolio to accept applications https://apply.interfolio.com/192404 . Applicants must upload a cover letter, CV including bibliography, research statement, teaching statement and contact information for 3 references. The research statement should summarize past research accomplishments and future research plans. Review of complete applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190702": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine \u2013 PED190a", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine \u2013 PED190a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 2, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine, seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will focus on care of children in inpatient and ambulatory settings at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital ambulatory clinics and other offsite ambulatory locations affiliated with the University of Chicago. Appointees will have leadership potential. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a 155-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offers world-class medical care to children from around the Chicagoland area and across the globe. These positions require collaborative physician leaders who will be positioned to succeed in a busy, dynamic environment and advance the clinical, educational, and scholarly goals of the Section of Pulmonology. The Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine program at the University of Chicago works closely with Pediatric Otolaryngology and Maxillofacial surgery to care for children with complex neonatal craniofacial deformities. Appointees will attend in the Pediatric Sleep outpatient clinics, interpret pediatric polysomnograms (from neonates to adolescents) and provide clinical coverage for inpatient sleep consultation services. The institution has an ACGME-accredited residency program as well as a Sleep Medicine Fellowship with 3-4 clinical fellows annually. Appointees will have responsibility for teaching students, residents and fellows in both the outpatient and inpatient settings at the Comer Children's Hospital and other practice plan sites and have a scholarly interest. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent degree, 2) have completed fellowship training in Sleep Medicine, 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Sleep Medicine, and 4) have or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois. In addition to Sleep Medicine, expertise in Pediatric Pulmonology, Pediatric Critical Care, Pediatric Neurology or other subspecialties complementary to Sleep Medicine are preferred. Additionally, expertise in nocturnal ventilation management and caring for complex ventilatory disorders is preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192462 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191004": {"title": "Research Assistant Professors \u2013 Hematology/Oncology #MED379b", "description": "Research Assistant Professors \u2013 Hematology/Oncology #MED379b. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Sep 2, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, seeks Research Assistant Professors who will conduct academic research in association with a faculty member or team. The appointees will contribute to our translational research program in Developmental Therapeutics and Immuno-Oncology by leading projects aimed at developing therapeutic strategies that enhance innate immunity against cancers that are refractory to immunotherapy. The appointees will be expected to build internal and external collaborative partnerships with other researchers as appropriate, participate in the preparation and writing of grant applications and reports, and write scientific manuscripts. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Biological Sciences Division, other academic units of the University of Chicago, and UChicago Medicine are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park. The appointees will join a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation with ample opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration, both within and outside the Biological Sciences Division. Prior to the start of employment, applicants must: 1) hold a Ph.D. degree in cancer biology, immunology, or a related field and 2) have completed postdoctoral training. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192737 . Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188782": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Accounting", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Accounting. Accounting. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions in Accounting beginning in the 2027-28 academic year. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s) or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in accounting by July 1, 2027 or soon thereafter. (We require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent. We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Applicants should be interested in contributing to a program that welcomes, respects, and supports a diverse community of scholars. Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vita, job market paper, and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. We will begin reviewing applications on October 1, 2026 , and strongly encourage you to submit your application by November 9, 2026 . We will continue to accept applications until April 1, 2027 . This position is benefits eligible - for information on benefits, please consult the University of Chicago Benefits Guide: https://mybenefits.nfp.com/UChicago/benefits-guide/. The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189301": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Finance", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Finance. Finance. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions in Finance for the 2027-28 academic year. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in finance by July 1, 2027 or soon thereafter. (We require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent. We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, a sample of written work, and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. We will start formally reviewing applications on November 10, 2026, and strongly encourage you to complete your application by then. We will continue to accept applications until March 31, 2027. This position is benefits eligible - for information on benefits, please consult the University of Chicago Benefits Guide: https://mybenefits.nfp.com/UChicago/benefits-guide/. The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190478": {"title": "William H. Kruskal Instructor of Statistics", "description": "William H. Kruskal Instructor of Statistics. Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Statistics invites applications for the position of William H. Kruskal Instructor. We seek recent or expected Ph.D. graduates doing advanced research with a connection to statistical theory, methodology and applications, probability theory, machine learning, or data science. Kruskal Instructor responsibilities include engagement in independent research and teaching up to three quarter-long courses per year in statistics or related fields. The appointment is for a term of up to three years. The University of Chicago has the highest standards for scholarship and academic quality, is dedicated to fundamental research, and has an inherently inter-disciplinary structure that is very supportive of newly emerging research opportunities. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Required: Completion of all requirements for the Ph.D. in statistics, mathematics, computer science, or a related field by the time of hire. Preferred: Research interests related to those of faculty in the Department of Statistics https://stat.uchicago.edu/people/#faculty . Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/192259 . Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae including a list of publications; statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans; teaching statement; three confidential letters of reference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190497": {"title": "William H. Kruskal Instructor of Statistics, Computational and Applied Mathematics", "description": "William H. Kruskal Instructor of Statistics, Computational and Applied Mathematics. Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Statistics invites applications for the position of William H. Kruskal Instructor. We seek recent or expected Ph.D. graduates doing advanced research with a connection to applied mathematics, computational mathematics, or machine learning. Kruskal Instructor responsibilities include engagement in independent research and teaching up to three quarter-long courses per year. The appointment is for a term of up to three years. The University of Chicago has the highest standards for scholarship and academic quality, is dedicated to fundamental research, and has an inherently inter-disciplinary structure that is very supportive of newly emerging research opportunities. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Required : Completion of all requirements for the Ph.D. in mathematics, statistics, computer science, or a related field by the time of hire. Preferred: research interests related to those of faculty in the Committee on Computational and Applied Mathematics https://cam.uchicago.edu/people/#faculty . Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/192274. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: cover letter; curriculum vitae including a list of publications; statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans; teaching statement; three confidential letters of reference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/191005": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer. Harris School of Public Policy Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The Harris School of Public Policy at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time lecturer positions for the 2026-2027 academic year to teach in our programs. We seek excellent teachers and experienced practitioners to teach in a variety of policy-related areas that enrich the Harris School\u2019s already robust curriculum. Candidates must have at least a tentative idea for a course or seminar to be offered, as well as expertise and experience directly relevant to the course or seminar being proposed. Qualified candidates must have a graduate level degree and/or professional experience in the relevant field; a PhD is preferred. Candidates must have relevant or policy-related experience; prior experience teaching students is strongly preferred. We will review applications on an on-going basis throughout the year, beginning on October 2, 2026, but will stop accepting applications for the 2026-2027 academic year on April 1, 2027. Each candidate must submit a curriculum vitae or resume and a course proposal. Optionally, prior teaching course evaluations and other materials relevant to your candidacy may be included. Interested candidates must submit their application through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio job boards at: apply.interfolio.com/192736 The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-2027 will range from $9,193 - $13,000. This position is not benefits eligible. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190076": {"title": "Assistant Instructional Professor", "description": "Assistant Instructional Professor. Department of Mathematics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Mathematics invites applications for a position at the rank of Assistant Instructional Professor. This is a non-tenure-track appointment, eligible for reappointment and promotion through the Instructional Professor ranks. Initial appointments at this rank are for a term of up to three years. A collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University governs the terms and conditions of employment for this position. The expected start date is September 1, 2027. Position responsibilities typically include teaching six one-quarter courses per year, sustaining a professional development program that supports the educational mission of the Department, and contributing to the University's teaching mission through non-classroom instructional duties, service duties, or both. Salary range: $93,500\u2013$97,500 for a nine-month academic appointment, paid over twelve months. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Additional information is available in the Benefits Guidebook . Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. Minimum Qualifications Completion of a PhD in Mathematics or a closely related field by the start date Preferred Qualifications Evidence of effective teaching in a range of undergraduate mathematics courses, including single-variable calculus, multivariable calculus, and linear algebra Applicants must apply online at the University of Chicago's Academic Jobs website at apply.interfolio.com/191893 A complete application consists of: A cover letter; An up-to-date curriculum vitae; Syllabi of courses previously taught or designed by the applicant, or sample syllabi of proposed courses; A teaching statement, including a summary of teaching activities, a detailed explanation of teaching philosophy and goals, and a list of courses previously taught, if applicable; Three letters of recommendation, at least two of which must address teaching ability; Additional evidence of pedagogical accomplishment, such as, but not limited to, a teaching portfolio, recent course evaluations, third-party observations of the applicant's teaching, or other relevant assessments. Questions about this search may be directed to Emily Caliendo at ecaliendo@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190599": {"title": "L.E. Dickson Instructor", "description": "L.E. Dickson Instructor. Department of Mathematics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Mathematics invites applications for the position of L.E. Dickson Instructor. This is open to mathematicians who have recently completed or will soon complete a doctorate in mathematics or a closely related field, and whose work shows remarkable promise in mathematical research. The initial appointment is for a term of up to three years. The teaching obligation is generally four one-quarter courses per year. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants are strongly encouraged to include additional information related to their teaching experience, such as evaluations from courses previously taught, as well as an AMS cover sheet. If you have applied for an NSF Mathematical Sciences Postdoctoral Fellowship, please include that information in your application, and let us know how you plan to use it if awarded. Questions may be directed to ecaliendo@uchicago.edu. We will begin screening applications on November 1, 2026. Screening will continue until all available positions are filled. Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in mathematics or a closely related field is required at the time of appointment. Required materials are: (a) a cover letter, (b) a curriculum vitae, (c) three or more letters of reference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability, (d) a description of previous research and plans for future mathematical research and (e) a teaching statement. Applications must be submitted online through Mathjobs https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28778 ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190611": {"title": "Assistant Professor", "description": "Assistant Professor. Department of Mathematics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 1, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Mathematics invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor. Successful candidates are typically two to four years past the Ph.D. These positions are intended for mathematicians whose work has been of outstandingly high caliber. Appointees are expected to have the potential to become leading figures in their fields. The appointment is generally for four years, with the possibility for renewal. The teaching obligation is up to three one-quarter courses per year. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants are strongly encouraged to include additional information related to their teaching experience, such as evaluations from courses previously taught, as well as an AMS cover sheet. Questions may be directed to ecaliendo@uchicago.edu. We will begin screening applications on October 1, 2026. Screening will continue until all available positions are filled. Completion of a Ph.D. in mathematics or a closely related field is required at the time of appointment. Required materials are: (a) a cover letter, (b) a curriculum vitae, (c) three or more letters of reference, at least one of which addresses teaching ability, (d) a description of previous research and plans for future mathematical research and (e) a teaching statement. Applications must be submitted online through Mathjobs https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/28780 ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190304": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition #MED438", "description": "Clinical Associates - Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition #MED438. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Aug 28, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to 3 years. Appointees will primarily assume clinical responsibilities at our Northbrook offsite location. This position does not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Gastroenterology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses lnterfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192088 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190348": {"title": "Assistant Professors in Ecology \u2013 Tenure Track Faculty \u2013 ECE009 ", "description": "Assistant Professors in Ecology \u2013 Tenure Track Faculty \u2013 ECE009 . Department of Ecology and Evolution. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 27, 2026. The Department of Ecology and Evolution at the University of Chicago is searching for full-time tenure-track faculty members working in ecology, to be appointed at the rank of Assistant Professor. The University is an exciting center of scientific discovery and innovation, and opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration and participation as graduate student trainers abound. Most university divisions and centers are contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s vibrant Hyde Park neighborhood, where many faculty members and students live. Competitive research space and start-up funding will be available, and new faculty will work with outstanding colleagues and graduate students in a variety of programs at the University and at our National Lab affiliates, Argonne National Laboratory and Fermilab . The Department manages the greenhouse facilities on campus, and the Warren Woods Ecological Field Station in Three Oaks, Michigan. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. We especially encourage applications from researchers who address fundamental questions in ecology using field research and/or computational, mathematical, and statistical models interrogating empirical data. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/192136 . A pplicants must upload a cover letter, a CV with bibliography, a research statement, a teaching statement, and contact information for three references. Applications received by October 1, 2026, are guaranteed full consideration; however, applications will be accepted until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190612": {"title": "Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) - Chemistry", "description": "Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) - Chemistry. Department of Chemistry. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Aug 27, 2026. The Department of Chemistry at the University of Chicago invites applications for a Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) position in Organic Chemistry. The candidate will be appointed as Assistant Senior Instructional Professor, Associate Senior Instructional Professor, or Senior Instructional Professor depending on qualifications and years of experience in a similar role. Appointments at this rank are for a term of up to five years with the possibility of renewal. This is a career-track position with potential progression and competitive salary and benefits. Expected start date is academic year 2026-2027. Responsibilities will include: Serve as Assistant Director of Undergraduate Studies for Organic Chemistry, contributing to undergraduate advising, curriculum development, pedagogical innovation, and program coordination. Oversee undergraduate organic chemistry laboratories, including development of experiments and instructional materials, management of laboratory spaces and equipment, safety coordination, and laboratory operations. Teach undergraduate organic chemistry and serve as secondary instructor for the organic chemistry sequence. Coordinate course operations, instructional resources, assessments, accommodations, and academic administration. Hire, supervise, train, and mentor teaching assistants, graders, and instructional staff within the organic chemistry undergraduate program, ensuring consistency and instructional quality. Manage student enrollment, academic progress, and course-related issues in coordination with faculty, advisors, and University partners. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Minimum qualifications: Completion of a Ph.D. degree in Organic Chemistry, Chemistry, or a closely related field is required prior to the start of appointment. Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated excellence in teaching undergraduate organic chemistry. Experience teaching and coordinating undergraduate organic chemistry laboratories. Experience in curriculum development, instructional innovation, and evidence-based pedagogy. Experience mentoring and supervising teaching assistants or instructional staff. Demonstrated organizational and administrative skills in managing large undergraduate courses. Experience with learning management systems and instructional technologies. Applicants must apply online at the University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/192378. Review of applications will begin September 27, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. A complete application consists of: A cover letter explaining your interest in the position, relevant teaching and management experience, and how your expertise aligns with the Department's educational mission. A curriculum vitae (CV). Evidence of successful management of programs (or evidence of potential to do so). Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as course evaluations, performance review(s), or other relevant assessments. A teaching statement (up to 3 pages) describing your teaching philosophy, instructional methods, mentoring experience, and future educational goals. Syllabi from courses previously taught and/or developed. Three confidential letters of recommendation requested via Interfolio."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190260": {"title": "Faculty in Neuro-oncology Research #NSU024", "description": "Faculty in Neuro-oncology Research #NSU024. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurological Surgery is rapidly growing and expanding its focus on brain tumor research and treatment. We invite applications for full-time faculty members at any rank to oversee a research program in any area of basic and/or translational research in neuro-oncology. Appointees will be based on the University of Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park campus. Appointees will teach at the undergraduate and/or graduate level and may participate in clinical care if appropriate. Academic rank, track (BSD tenure-track or School of Medicine track), and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Neurological Surgery is engaged in cutting-edge neurosurgical research and development, focusing on pioneering new treatments for a range of neurological conditions. This includes ongoing investigations into treatments for brain tumors, epilepsy, CNS congenital anomalies, and neurovascular disease. While the primary faculty appointment will be in the Department of Neurological Surgery, appointees may have an opportunity for secondary appointments in other departments of the Biological Sciences Division (BSD) dependent upon interests and qualifications. There will be multiple opportunities for appointees to collaborate with faculty in the following programs: the Ben May Department for Cancer Research, the Ludwig Center of Chicago, the Department of Medicine\u2019s Section of Hematology/Oncology, and The University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center and its Abbvie Foundation Cancer Pavilion (opening in 2027), and the University of Chicago Neuroscience Institute. The University of Chicago is a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation. The Biological Sciences Division, other academic units of the University, and the University of Chicago Medical Center are all contained within one compact campus in the Hyde Park neighborhood of Chicago. Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art core facilities, outstanding colleagues, and graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs within and outside the Biological Sciences. Faculty in the BSD collaborate widely across their respective Departments, with other Divisions, Schools, and Institutes at the University of Chicago, and with researchers at the Argonne National Laboratory and the Marine Biological Laboratory. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) hold a PhD or MD/PhD in Neuroscience, Cancer Biology, Neurobiology, Bioengineering, Immunology, Cell Biology, Anatomy or a related field, and 2) have at least five years\u2019 experience overseeing a research laboratory in an academic or industry setting. We especially welcome established principal investigators with funded brain tumor research programs. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192056 . Applicants must upload: CV with bibliography, cover letter, research statement, teaching statement, up to five selected publications, and contact information for three references. Review of complete applications ends when positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190294": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Child Abuse Pediatricians #PED143a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Child Abuse Pediatricians #PED143a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 25, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Academic Pediatrics, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank in Child Abuse Medicine. Appointees will provide child abuse consultation within an established interdisciplinary process and participate in court-related activities as needed; support the Multidisciplinary Pediatric Education and Evaluation Consortium (MPEEC) program by providing real-time expert medical consultation to child welfare and law enforcement for children reported for serious harm; train pediatric residents and other leaners in the evaluation, diagnosis, and management of suspected child maltreatment; provide education to trainees and other clinical leaders in Child Abuse Medicine; and participate in activities that support the clinical and academic mission of Child Abuse Medicine. These activities will occur at the University of Chicago\u2019s Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and affiliated sites. Other duties will include teaching, supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is designated as a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and operates a nationally recognized burn unit. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is an integrated children\u2019s hospital that offers a full complement of pediatric services with a total of 172 general pediatric, NICU, and PICU beds. The Department of Pediatrics includes over 170 full-time faculty members and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents, and fellows, our faculty conducts cutting-edge translational and clinical research. The Comer Children\u2019s Child Advocacy and Protective Services (CAPS) program, established in 1991, has a national reputation as a leader in the medical care of abused and neglected children. CAPS is primarily a hospital-based consultation service. The core CAPS team includes three board-certified physicians, a physician assistant, and a clinical social worker. More information about the CAPS program can be found at https://www.uchicagomedicine.org/comer/conditions-services/emergency-medicine/child-protective-services . MPEEC is a national landmark program that has received state support for over 18 years and is managed by CAPS staff, with case coordination supported by a full-time investigations coordinator. More information about MPEEC can be found at: https://mpeec.uchicago.edu/mpeec-medical-consortium/ . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Child Abuse Pediatrics. We especially welcome applicants with a strong commitment to advancing scholarship and clinical excellence in child abuse pediatrics. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192083 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190319": {"title": "Pediatric General Surgeons #SUR161a", "description": "Pediatric General Surgeons #SUR161a. Section of Pediatric Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 24, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Pediatric Surgery, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will have inpatient and outpatient responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Pediatric Surgery. Responsibilities will include patient care and teaching at The University of Chicago - Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and at affiliated off-site locations within the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Surgery (or equivalent) in Pediatric Surgery, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in Pediatric Surgery. We especially welcome applicants with diverse backgrounds and an interest in academic medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/192107 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189412": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science", "description": "Assistant Professor of Political Science. Department of Political Science. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 24, 2026. The University of Chicago, Division of the Social Sciences, Department of Political Science invites applications for a tenure track position at the rank of assistant professor of American Politics for an initial 4 year renewable term. We welcome applications from the entire subfield of American politics. This is a full-time position with an expected start date on or after July 1, 2027. The selected candidate will pursue their own research agenda, collaborate with faculty in the Department of Political Science and other areas of the Division of the Social Sciences and University, and teach in both the undergraduate and graduate curriculum. The teaching load for tenure-track positions is typically four courses per year on the quarter system. Additional responsibilities include advising at all levels of the program, and service on departmental and university committees. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . All requirements for receipt of a PhD in a relevant field must be completed prior to the start of the appointment. We seek applicants with an ambitious research agenda yielding active and innovative scholarship. Successful applicants will contribute to the scholarly community of the department and university. They will have the ability or potential to be an excellent teacher in the university\u2019s core curriculum, in introductory to advanced undergraduate courses in Political Science, and in graduate courses related to their area of expertise. We especially welcome applicants with a demonstrated strong commitment to teaching and advising. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/191666 . Applications must include a 1) cover letter, 2) CV, 3) teaching statement, 4) research statement, 5) one example of scholarly writing, and 6) at least three confidential letters of recommendation. Consideration of applications will begin thirty days after the position has been posted and continue until the position is filled or the search has ended."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190180": {"title": "Biochemistry and Biophysics Tenure Track Faculty - BMB005a", "description": "Biochemistry and Biophysics Tenure Track Faculty - BMB005a. Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 21, 2026. The Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology at the University of Chicago is searching for full-time tenure-track faculty members at the rank of assistant professor. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology is home to world class mechanistic research in many fields. Situated within the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), the Department\u2019s work is enriched by a highly collaborative University environment within a compact campus centered in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park neighborhood. Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art core facilities providing cryoEM & ET as well as other microscopy, cutting-edge next generation sequencing and genomics, flow cytometry and an endowed, world-class plant research facility. New faculty will work with outstanding colleagues and graduate students in a variety of programs at the University and at our National Lab affiliates, Argonne National Laboratory and Fermilab. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or its equivalent. We especially welcome applicants who are studying macromolecular dynamics and structure, mechanisms of molecular function, and related areas. Topics of interest are: proteins; nucleic acids and their assemblies; membrane proteins; cellular processes in proteostasis, condensates, and immunology; biomolecular design; chemical biology, and computational analysis. Applicants with experience in research incorporating Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning or Quantum technologies are especially encouraged to apply. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:/ apply.interfolio.com/191981 . Applicants must upload a cover letter, a CV with bibliography, a research statement, a teaching statement, and arrange for 3 confidential reference letters to be submitted via Interfolio. Review of applications is expected to begin on September 21, 2026, and end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/190026": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Modern Greek", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Modern Greek. Department of Classics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Aug 19, 2026. The Department of Classics at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers in Modern Greek. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in various courses of the Modern Greek Language curriculum. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching language courses at various levels (elementary, intermediate, and/or advanced), holding regularly scheduled office hours, and the preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the department, the successful candidate may teach between one and four courses per academic year. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a Master\u2019s degree or equivalent in Modern Greek language pedagogy and/or a related field, superior proficiency in Modern Greek and English according to ACTFL guidelines, and experience teaching Modern Greek at the college or post-secondary level. Applications must include: Current CV Cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience and discusses approaches to pedagogy Sample syllabus The names and contact information of three references whose recommendation may be solicited Optional Materials: Course evaluations, if available A teaching demonstration video, if available (please include a link to an externally hosted video) The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/191848 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. Review of application materials will begin August 26, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. In the event that a teaching opportunity becomes available, we will contact applicants with the academic training and experience most appropriate for our available classes. This posting will expire on March 31, 2027. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval. Only complete applications will be considered. For more information about this position, please contact Anna Hornsby (they/them), annahornsby@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189741": {"title": "Senior Research Associate", "description": "Senior Research Associate. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Aug 19, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Development Innovation Lab (DIL) and the Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics invites applications for a non-tenure track Senior Research Associate position, with a focus on Development Economics and Mathematics. The position will be a two-year, renewable appointment that will begin on October 1, 2026, or soon thereafter. The Development Innovation Lab at the University of Chicago uses the tools of economics to develop innovations with the potential to benefit millions of people in low- and middle-income countries. It was founded and is led by Nobel laureate Michael Kremer. The Senior Research Associate (SRA) will be the research lead for weather forecasting at DIL. They will work closely with the faculty Director (Michael Kremer) on large-scale human centered weather forecasting initiatives. The SRA will help lead the Lab\u2019s work to develop, adapt, and validate scalable weather forecasting innovations to inform agricultural decision-making. Examples of research projects might include Human-Centered Weather Forecasting in Indonesia, India, and Ethiopia. The SRA will also help apply for grants and secure funds for research and will be responsible for both fundraising and for overseeing project budgets. The SRA might be responsible for overseeing junior staff. The position is based in Chicago. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Review of applications will begin 30 days after the posting date and will continue until the position is filled. Only shortlisted candidates will be contacted. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess a Ph.D. in mathematics or related field. We especially welcome applicants with: A strong early-career research record in development economics and weather forecasting, including publications and working papers in progress Postdoctoral or equivalent research experience developing and validating statistical and/or forecasting methodology Experience applying quantitative methods to development contexts, and translating them into applied forecasting work with policy or field partners Demonstrated ability to communicate research findings clearly to technical and non-technical audiences through oral presentations and written work Experience designing and overseeing large-scale forecasting efforts, including methods deployed in operational or real-world settings A record of methodological contributions to the design and evaluation of weather forecasts Demonstrated success developing models that have been adopted by government agencies or international organizations To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/191581. Applications must include a curriculum vitae, cover letter, contact information for three references, and up to three published or unpublished research papers."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189839": {"title": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates \u2013 Geriatrics & Palliative Medicine #MED440", "description": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates \u2013 Geriatrics & Palliative Medicine #MED440. Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Aug 19, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Geriatrics & Palliative Medicine, is searching for part-time Clinical Associates at 20% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide direct patient care responsibilities in the section of Geriatrics and Palliative Care within the UChicago Medicine Health System. These positions do not require formal teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per hour. These positions are benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Geriatrics or Palliative Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/191676 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189810": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Operations Management", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Operations Management. Operations. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 17, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business invites applications for tenure-track positions at the assistant or associate professor levels in Operations Management for the 2027-28 academic year. Successful candidates will have outstanding research abilities and will be committed to achieving excellence in teaching operations management at the MBA level. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in Operations Management, Operations Research, Management Science, or Industrial Engineering or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in the relevant field(s) by July 1, 2027, or soon thereafter. (We require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent. We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) We will begin formally reviewing applications on November 13 . You must have submitted all materials by that date to ensure full and timely consideration. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189891": {"title": "Lecturer, Graham School", "description": "Lecturer, Graham School. Graham School of Continuing Liberal and Professional Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Aug 17, 2026. The Graham School of Continuing Liberal Arts Education is currently seeking qualified applicants for a part-time position to teach in our Master of Liberal Arts program. Current University faculty and staff, as well as professionals working in relevant positions outside of the University, are eligible to apply. Courses are held online or in person at our Chicago campus. The program currently operates on a quarterly schedule with classes held weekly, 8 to 10 weeks of instruction per quarter. Responsibilities Develop syllabus including weekly class schedule, discussion topics, learning objectives, reading assignments, and assessment plans using grading rubrics. Develop Canvas Learning Management System course site. Develop and deliver in-class content (lectures, activities, breakout group work). Develop asynchronous content which supplements the in-class learning experience (recorded lectures, discussion board content, project, and research work). Grade assignments and return comments and grades to students in timely manner. Attend all weekly in-person and/or remote synchronous sessions. Attend instructor meetings coordinated and hosted by program staff. Work with the program director and other program instructors to ensure consistency across class sections and participate in curriculum reviews. Supplemental asynchronous content and remote synchronous instruction methods must be developed and planned in conjunction with Graham School online instructional design and/or teaching and learning teams and approved by the program. After initial review of applications, candidates may be requested to submit a course proposal. Qualified applicants must have a graduate-level degree in a relevant field, PhD preferred. Candidates with prior teaching experience and/or relevant practical experience are strongly preferred. CV or Resume Cover Letter (optional) References upon request"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189775": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Syntax", "description": "Assistant Professor in Syntax. Department of Linguistics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 17, 2026. The Department of Linguistics at the University of Chicago invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Syntax, with an expected start date of July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The selected candidate will pursue their own research agenda, collaborate with faculty in the Department of Linguistics and other areas of the Division of the Arts & Humanities and University, and teach in both the undergraduate and graduate curriculum. The teaching load for tenure-track positions is typically four courses per year on the quarter system. Additional responsibilities include advising at all levels of the program, and service on departmental and university committees. Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant Professor: $90,000-$120,000 ; This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The successful candidate will have an active research agenda resulting in publication in leading venues in their field or the potential to develop such a publication record, as well as the ability or potential to be an excellent teacher and mentor at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Applicants must specialize in syntax, broadly construed, but will ideally have wide intellectual engagement in linguistics. The department welcomes additional research interests, skills, or secondary expertise in an area that complements or enhances existing strengths in the department. Applicants must be able to teach in the University\u2019s core curriculum , as well as general linguistics courses and courses at all levels in syntax, including seminars, and provide research mentoring. All requirements for receipt of a PhD in a relevant field must be completed prior to the start of the appointment. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/191616 by 10:59 PM Central Time/11:59 PM Eastern Time on October 18, 2026: \u25cf CV \u25cf Cover letter \u25cf Research statement \u25cf Teaching statement \u25cf Two publications or writing samples representative of work in your area(s) of expertise \u25cf The names and contact information of three recommenders who may be asked to provide letters on behalf of the applicant Applicants will be asked to provide other additional materials following the initial review of applications. Questions may be directed to Tracy L. Davis at tracyd@uchicago.edu . This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189390": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Behavioral Neurologists #NEU070", "description": "Academic Physicians - Behavioral Neurologists #NEU070. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 14, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank to serve as behavioral neurologists. Appointees will join the team within the Center for Comprehensive Care and Research on Memory Disorders (The Memory Center) and collaborate closely with the Healthy Aging & Alzheimer\u2019s Research Care (HAARC) Center, a multidisciplinary hub for research in aging, Alzheimer\u2019s disease, and related dementias (ADRD). The HAARC Center is dedicated to advancing discovery in neurobiologic, cognitive, genetic, lifestyle, psychosocial, and related factors that promote resilience, resistance, and health span. The appointees will join a multidisciplinary team that includes neurology, psychiatry, geriatrics, nursing, and social work. The primary role for the appointees will be to establish a strong clinical presence and to develop or extend the focus of scholarship. Teaching and mentorship of medical students, residents, fellows, and faculty are core expectations. These positions may require some ambulatory practice at UChicago Medicine offsite practices. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Scholarly and research activities may align with and enhance existing strengths within the Biological Sciences Division, including neurobiology, computational neuroscience, genetics, neuroimaging, and immunology. Appointees will have opportunities to collaborate with the Neuroscience Institute, the Institute for Population and Precision Health, the Aging Research Program within the Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine, the Center for Neurocognitive Outcomes Improvement Research, the Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience, and other programs and institutes across the University. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neurology, and 4) have completed at least one year of postdoctoral training in behavioral neurology/cognitive disorders or related areas such as neuropsychiatry, preventive neurology, or vascular cognitive neurology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/191246 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189281": {"title": "CSEi Research Fellow", "description": "CSEi Research Fellow. Climate and Energy Institute. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Aug 14, 2026. The Climate Systems Engineering initiative (CSEi) at the University of Chicago invites applications for the 2027-29 cohort of CSEi Research Fellows, to be appointed as Postdoctoral Researcher at the Rank of Research Associate . We seek outstanding early career scholars interested in any aspect of climate systems engineering. The University of Chicago is building a substantial research cluster, including the anticipated addition of ten new faculty lines, on the science, technology, and public policy of Climate Systems Engineering. Our goal is to advance understanding of the benefits, risks, and governance of technologies that might reduce the impacts of accumulated greenhouse gases, and to educate students who will face the challenges of managing industrial civilization on a fragile planet. The CSEi\u2019s topical scope includes open-system carbon removal such as enhanced rock weathering, ocean alkalinity enhancement, solar geoengineering, and interventions to limit the loss of glacial ice. The CSEi welcomes research from across fields including the sciences, humanities, social sciences, public policy, and law. The CSEi Research Fellows program aims to develop the next generation of research leaders who will shape the future of climate systems engineering for the benefit of people and the planet. CSEi Research Fellows will work closely with UChicago faculty mentors, but their research and funding are independent. The program will help them develop strong research networks by facilitating collaboration among the Research Fellows cohort and by ensuring that they have ample opportunities to interact with the whole community of CSEi-affiliated faculty and research leaders. Securing a faculty mentor in advance is strongly preferred, and applicants are encouraged to reach out early to engage a mentor. UChicago faculty members from any discipline may potentially serve as mentors, regardless of whether they have prior experience with climate systems engineering. To offer a sense of the range of opportunities for collaboration, leading faculty from a variety of fields have contributed statements about research interests and possibilities for collaboration. These statements, available on the CSEi website , are examples of how faculty research interests might overlap with climate systems engineering. Applicants may reach out to any UChicago faculty member about these or other topics. This is a two-year position, starting between August 3 \u2013 September 16, 2027 , that offers a $95K salary per year, dedicated workspace, and research and travel funding of $10,000 per year. This position is benefits eligible - for information on benefits, please consult the University of Chicago Benefits Guide : https://mybenefits.nfp.com/UChicago/benefits-guide/ . Additionally, the University will provide a one-time payment of $4,000 for relocation assistance, payable at the end of the first month of employment at the University. Please note that this payment is taxable. Teaching is not required, though an optional teaching opportunity during the second year of the fellowship may be available, subject to need and requisite approval. In-person interaction is a crucial part of the program, and Research Fellows are expected to participate actively in the life of the CSEi. Required: Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. or other terminal degree appropriate to the field is required at the time of appointment. Preferred: 0-3 years professional experience. Pre-matching with a faculty mentor and enthusiastic recommendation from the mentor. Academic and professional success in the early career stage (typically graduate school and the first three years after completion of degree) and the potential to make an important contribution to the field of climate systems engineering. Residency: The fellowship requires residency in the Chicago area. For UChicago applicants: Applicants who are currently postdocs at UChicago or who received terminal degrees from UChicago are eligible, provided their research and mentor arrangements are distinct from their PhD or current postdoctoral research, taking them in new directions and forging new connections. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/191139 . The following materials are required: C.V. Statement of research objectives that explains how the research will advance understanding of climate systems engineering, and how the research relates to the applicant\u2019s prior work and skills. (maximum 2 pages). Writing sample consisting of a chapter, essay, or article. Two letters of reference: please provide contact information for two letter writers. Name of UChicago faculty member who has agreed to serve as mentor. If applying without a pre-matched mentor, please provide the names of faculty members whom you want to work with and an explanation of why you were not able to secure a mentor in advance. Complete applications must be submitted via Interfolio by 11:59 PM Eastern Time on November 2, 2026."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189674": {"title": "Associate Director for Writing in the Core", "description": "Associate Director for Writing in the Core. New Collegiate Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Aug 13, 2026. The Chicago Center for Writing at the University of Chicago invites applications for an Associate Director for Writing in the Core. Reporting to the Executive Director of the Chicago Center for Writing and the Master of the New Collegiate Division, the Associate Director will serve as a key member of the Center\u2019s leadership team during its launch phase and beyond. The selected candidate will be appointed at the rank of Assistant Senior Instructional Professor beginning January 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter, for an initial term of three years, renewable with the possibility for promotion. This is a 12-month, benefits-eligible position. For information on benefits, please consult the University of Chicago Benefits Guide . Launching in Autumn 2026, the Chicago Center for Writing will serve as a faculty-led, intellectually ambitious hub for writing instruction, pedagogy, and support across the University of Chicago. Housed in the New Collegiate Division and working in close partnership with academic departments and campus units, the Center advances an integrated, multi-year approach to writing education that spans foundational Core instruction, discipline-specific writing, public and professional communication, and individualized support through a vibrant writing lab. The Associate Director for Writing in the Core will provide intellectual, curricular, and administrative leadership for writing instruction across the Core, with primary responsibility for coordinating the College\u2019s Core writing requirement, Inquiry, Conversation, Argument (WRIT 10100), and supporting writing instruction in adjacent Core contexts. Additionally, the successful candidate will provide sustained, expert guidance to instructors teaching with writing; develop adaptable pedagogical resources; support coherent co-curricular programming; and help articulate the intellectual identity of the Chicago Center for Writing during a period of institutional growth. The primary areas of responsibility for the Associate Director for Writing in the Core include: Strategic leadership for writing in the Core Instructor supervision, mentoring, and evaluation Curriculum and pedagogical resource development Cross-Core collaboration and co-curricular support Writing technologies (including generative AI) and academic integrity Evidence-informed program development and public engagement Teaching up to four quarter-length courses per academic year Minimum Qualifications Doctorate or equivalent terminal degree completed by the position start date Demonstrated record of excellence in college-level teaching, including substantial experience teaching courses in which writing is central to student learning Evidence of successful experience in academic program management, curriculum development, and instructor mentoring, or evidence of strong potential to do so Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, instructors, staff, and students across disciplines Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching or administering first-year writing, Core/general-education writing, writing-in-the-disciplines, or interdisciplinary writing curricula Experience supervising, mentoring, observing, evaluating, or providing professional development for instructional faculty or other academic appointees Experience developing shared curricular resources, assignment sequences, instructor-facing materials, or student-facing writing resources Experience with assessment or evidence-informed program development in writing instruction Demonstrated reflective engagement with technologies of writing (including generative AI) and digital literacy as they relate to collegiate writing pedagogy Strong interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills, including the ability to build trust with faculty and instructors across departments and programs Intellectual curiosity, broad disciplinary fluency, and a demonstrated commitment to the University of Chicago\u2019s values of rigorous inquiry, free expression, and serious intellectual exchange Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website: apply.interfolio.com/191519 . A complete application must include the following materials in PDF format: Curriculum vitae Cover letter describing your interest in the position, experience in writing pedagogy and program leadership, approach to mentoring and supervising instructors, and vision for supporting writing in the Core Teaching statement to complement what you discuss in your cover letter, not to exceed 750 words Evidence of teaching effectiveness, including syllabi for two courses previously taught Evidence of successful management of programs, or evidence of potential to do so Contact information for three references Review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026 , and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189380": {"title": "Scholarly Services Librarian", "description": "Scholarly Services Librarian. D'Angelo Law Library. Position type: OAA - University Librarian. Posted: Aug 12, 2026. The D'Angelo Law Library contributes to the success of the University of Chicago and Law School communities and is a valued partner in the Law School's scholarship, teaching, and learning. D'Angelo is a part of the University of Chicago Library, working cooperatively to ensure rapid access to collections and resources. D'Angelo team members foster andmaintain a collaborative, inclusive, and supportive workplace culture to provide exceptional and innovative service to the University of Chicago community. The D'Angelo Law Library is seeking a collaborative, service-oriented Scholarly Services Librarian to join our User Services team. This position provides high-quality reference and faculty research support to the University of Chicago Law School community, with the opportunity to grow the Library's faculty-facing scholarly services \u2014 helping faculty and student-edited journals navigate publishing, visibility, and emerging developments in legal scholarship such as empirical research and generative AI. We welcome applicants at any career stage. This role is responsible for providing excellent reference and faculty research support and building the Law Library's scholarly services for the Law School community. If you are newer to the profession, you will be mentored and supported as you develop these services; if you are more experienced, you will have room to lead and shape this work from the start. Responsibilities Reference & Research Support Provide in-person and virtual reference services to Law School faculty, students, and other researchers as part of a rotating schedule. Respond to faculty research requests and support legal and interdisciplinary research needs using print and digital resources. Create and maintain research guides, tutorials, and other research tools. Contribute to library instruction, orientations, and workshops for students, faculty, and staff. Scholarly Services Provide faculty-facing guidance on scholarly visibility, publishing, and academic identity (e.g., scholarly profiles, ORCID, SSRN). Serve as a point of contact for the Law School's research centers. Help faculty and student-edited journals understand how developments such as empirical legal research, interdisciplinary work, and generative AI may affect research and publishing decisions, connecting them to relevant campus expertise when appropriate. Contribute to the ongoing development of scholarly services based on faculty needs and observed trends. Professional Engagement & Service Participate in library and University committees. Engage in professional development, including opportunities to participate in law librarianship organizations at the regional or national level. Perform other duties as assigned. Required A graduate degree from an ALA-accredited library school or a J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school (degree must be completed by the start date of employment). Experience using legal sources, databases, and research tools, as evidenced by relevant coursework, work experience, or other related experience. Excellent communication, organizational, and planning skills, with a demonstrated ability to work effectively with faculty, students, and staff. Evidence of initiative, a strong user-service philosophy, and a commitment to collaboration and continuous improvement. Preferred Both a graduate degree from an ALA-accredited library school and a Juris Doctor (J.D.) from an ABA-accredited law school. Previous experience working in an academic or law library. Knowledge of data research tools, statistical software, or empirical research methodologies in law or the social sciences. Familiarity with current trends in legal scholarship, academic publishing, or the impact of generative AI on the research life cycle. Teaching or training experience. Knowledge of one or more non-English languages. To Apply: Submit cover letter, curriculum vitae, and reference contact information online through the University of Chicago's Academic Recruiting website: apply.interfolio.com/191245 . Review of applications will begin after September 10, 2026. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. Salary and Benefits: $72,250- $85,000. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Questions: Contact the University Library Human Resources department at libraryhr@uchicago.edu."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189208": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Behavioral Science", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Behavioral Science. Behavioral. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 10, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions in Behavioral Science beginning in the 2027-28 academic year. We will consider candidates with interests in the areas of decision-making, social psychology and organizations, all broadly defined. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD in the relevant field(s) by July 1, 2027 or soon thereafter (we require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent). (We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which point you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Each candidate should submit a curriculum vita, job market paper, a research statement and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. Applications will be accepted online at this link: We will start formally reviewing applications on September 15, 2026, and we will continue to accept applications until March 31, 2027. This position is benefits eligible - for information on benefits, please consult the University of Chicago Benefits Guide: https://mybenefits.nfp.com/UChicago/benefits-guide/. The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188915": {"title": "Japanese Studies Librarian ", "description": "Japanese Studies Librarian . The Library. Position type: OAA - University Librarian. Posted: Aug 10, 2026. General Summary: The University of Chicago Library empowers users with deep and rich collections, extensive expertise, innovative programs, and diverse spaces. We are committed to cultivating an inclusive community, enhancing access to scholarly resources, advancing digital scholarship, engaging locally and globally, and excelling in a changing environment. Home to one of the most comprehensive and dynamic East Asian collections, the University of Chicago is an active partner and collaborator in both the Big Ten Academic Alliance and the Ivy Plus Libraries. The Library is seeking a collaborative, service-oriented, and forward-thinking individual for the position of Japanese Studies Librarian. The incumbent of this position manages the Japanese Studies collection and develops and delivers research services to a wide range of users across the University, as well as engages in community, to support new methods and innovations in research. The ideal candidate possesses skills and abilities to help transform collections, instruction, outreach, and reference and is creative in solving problems in a collaborative environment. Responsibilities: Reporting to the Director of the East Asian Collection, the Japanese Studies Librarian will perform the following duties, but not limited to: Serve as the Library\u2019s primary expert and liaison for Japanese Studies, independently cultivating and sustaining relationships with students and faculty across departments, professional schools, and programs. Lead the development, promotion, preservation, and assessment of distinctive, interdisciplinary, multi-format, and intersectional Japanese studies collections that serve the wide-ranging needs of students and faculty. Design, deliver and assess a comprehensive program of research services, instruction, and outreach for Japanese Studies adapting to the shifting pedagogical and research needs across the university. Manage the Japanese Studies acquisition budget, making strategic allocation decisions and maintaining accountability for expenditures and vendor relationships Serve as the primary representative for the Library on Japanese Studies in the Big Ten Academic Alliance and the Ivy Plus Libraries, actively contributing to collaborative collection development initiatives and services innovations. Collaborate with subject librarians and digital scholarship units on complex, cross-functional initiatives involving Japanese language materials, contributing subject expertise and taking ownership of the Japanese Studies dimensions of those projects. Perform original and complex copy cataloging for Japanese language materials using national and local standards and serve as a local resource for cataloging questions involving Japanese language content. Actively participate in departmental, divisional and library-wide projects, initiatives, committees and task forces. R epresent the Library externally through active engagement with professional organizations, consortia, and scholarly communities, and contribute meaningfully to the advancement of the profession through publications, presentations or leadership roles in relevant professional associations. Perform other duties as assigned. Required: Master\u2019s degree from an ALA - accredited institution of library and information science or advanced degree in a relevant academic discipline. Demonstrated native or bilingual proficiency in written and spoken Japanese and excellent oral and written communication skills in English. Demonstrated ability to work effectively, as part of a team and independently, in a culturally diverse and constantly changing environment. Demonstrated user-centered commitment to public service, including outreach and service excellence. Advanced knowledge of Japanese history, literature, and culture. Minimum 3 years of experience working in an academic or other type of research library or in a scholarly research setting. Preferred: MA or PhD in Japanese studies Knowledge of Japanese publishing industry, book trade, and latest developments in Japanese resources. Knowledge of classical Japanese and/or other East Asian languages. Knowledge of current relevant library trends and issues. Experience working in an academic library. Experience working with cataloging and/or acquisitions modules in a library management system. Experience using digital scholarship methodologies and tools. Demonstrated commitment to continuing education and active involvement in the library profession. To Apply: Submit cover letter, curriculum vitae, and reference contact information online through the University of Chicago's Academic Recruiting website: http://apply.interfolio.com/191060 . Review of applications will begin after October 9, 2026. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. Salary and Benefits: $72,250- $85,000. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Questions: Contact the University Library Human Resources department at libraryhr@uchicago.edu."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189157": {"title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Direct Social Work Practice) in the Crown Family School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice", "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Direct Social Work Practice) in the Crown Family School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice. Crown Family School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 10, 2026. The University of Chicago Crown School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice (Crown Family School) invites applications for one or more faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor (tenure-track) or Associate Professor (tenured) from scholars whose work advances the science of direct social work practice. Successful applicants will be engaged in theoretically grounded, empirically driven, and methodologically rigorous scholarship that advances knowledge relevant to social work practice. The successful applicant\u2019s research agenda may focus on prevention, intervention, implementation, service delivery, community-based practice, or related approaches. We welcome applicants whose scholarship addresses a broad range of substantive areas, practice settings and populations, particularly those that complement the School\u2019s curricular pathways . The successful applicant\u2019s scholarship should be relevant to the mission of the School and the social work profession, with the potential for securing research funding commensurate with their scholarly agenda. Successful applicants will be prepared to teach direct practice courses and mentor students at all levels, primarily at the master\u2019s and doctoral levels. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or after. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Crown Family School builds upon a legacy of more than a century of scholarship, education and leadership in social work and social welfare, blending a distinctive interdisciplinary tradition with applied problem solving to foster innovative solutions to some of society\u2019s most pressing social problems. For more information about the Crown School, please visit our website at: https://crownschool.uchicago.edu/ . Applicants must possess a doctorate (or have a doctorate by the date of appointment) in social work or a related field. An MSW degree is preferred. At least two years of post-master's direct practice experience is also preferred. Successful candidates will have an interest in and ability to teach direct practice courses. Candidates should submit a cover letter (no longer than two pages), a Curriculum Vitae, a statement that details research and teaching interests, and two writing samples that best represent the applicant\u2019s approach. Applicants at the dissertation stage must include part of their dissertation as one writing sample. Candidates should indicate which rank (Assistant or Associate) they are applying to within the cover letter. Contact information for at least three references must also be included; references will be contacted to request a letter of reference after an initial review of submitted materials, and candidates will be notified prior to contact. Consideration of applications will begin on September 10, 2026, and continue until the position is filled or the search is closed; early submission is encouraged. Please apply online at the Academic Recruitment web page at the University of Chicago website using the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191033"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188863": {"title": "OBGYN Hospitalist Faculty \u2013 #OBG097", "description": "OBGYN Hospitalist Faculty \u2013 #OBG097. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 10, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time OBGYN Hospitalist faculty members at the rank of Assistant Professor. Appointees will be members of the obstetrics and gynecology call group and will contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship, including teaching and supervision of trainees and medical students. Appointees will be mostly responsible for inpatient clinical care at our Hyde Park campus. They will belong to the Section of General Obstetrics & Gynecology and will collaborate with affiliated midwives, Maternal-Fetal Medicine, Neonatology, Anesthesiology providers and physicians from Sections of Complex Gynecologic Surgery, Comprehensive Gynecology, and Gynecologic Oncology. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications and positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University of Chicago Family Birth Center is a high-complexity, high-acuity unit with a Level III NICU, and OB Emergency Department with an in-house OB hospitalist model (most of the time, 2 physicians in-house 24/7). We deliver over 2,800 babies annually. The OB shifts are either 12 or 24 hours. Additionally, the University of Chicago\u2019s inpatient Gynecology Hospitalist Service covers weekdays in-house and night calls from home with the expectation of coming in for emergency Gynecology surgeries. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. We especially welcome applicants with training and experience in gynecologic surgery, inpatient obstetrics (vaginal delivery of twins, higher-order cesarean sections, and operative vaginal deliveries), and experience in resident and/or medical student teaching. Candidates that prefer to focus on Obstetrics or Gynecology only will also be considered. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190762 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188968": {"title": "Breast Imaging Radiologists #RAD101", "description": "Breast Imaging Radiologists #RAD101. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank to serve as breast imaging radiologists on the School of Medicine track. This is an exceptional opportunity to join a world-class academic medical center that values both professional excellence and work-life balance. Remote clinical days are available weekly, and scheduling is thoughtfully designed to support personal well-being alongside clinical and academic success. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . There are also opportunities to earn additional income through a productivity-based incentive model are readily available. Clinical responsibilities include the full spectrum of breast imaging, including screening and diagnostic mammography, tomosynthesis, breast ultrasound, breast MRI, and image-guided breast procedures. Appointees will work closely with surgeons, oncologists, pathologists, technologists, nurses, and referring clinicians in multidisciplinary breast care and will contribute to timely, high-quality patient evaluation and communication. The broader UChicago environment includes an $815 million state-of-the-art cancer center opening in 2027. This transformational project includes a comprehensive, fully integrated breast center that will significantly expand imaging volumes, services, and technology. Faculty responsibilities also include teaching and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows, as well as participation in conferences, case review, and other educational activities. Appointees will be expected to contribute to the academic mission of the Department through scholarly activity, which may include clinical research, educational work, publications, abstracts, presentations, or quality improvement projects. The Department of Radiology at the University of Chicago is a collegial, collaborative, and academically active environment with more than 80 full-time faculty members and a long tradition of excellence in patient care, education, and research. The department offers a strong institutional commitment to academic development and the opportunity to work within one of the nation\u2019s leading academic medical centers, providing the foundation for a fulfilling career as an academic radiologist. We also offer a strong culture of mentorship and professional development, with opportunities for academic advancement, leadership growth, and career development at all stages of their careers. Learn more at radiology.uchicago.edu . Located on the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park campus\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our location offers the best of city living in a dynamic and diverse neighborhood. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be American Board certified or Board eligible in Radiology, and 4) completed fellowship training in Breast Imaging or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190860 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189095": {"title": "Assistant Professor in American Cinema and/or Television  ", "description": "Assistant Professor in American Cinema and/or Television . Department of Cinema and Media Studies. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 10, 2026. The Department of Cinema and Media Studies at the University of Chicago invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in American Cinema and/or Television, with an expected start date of July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Areas of expertise can be mainstream, independent, experimental, animated, and nonfiction cinema and/or television from any time period, from the silent era to the present. Research topics may include but are not limited to form, style, and genre; labor and industrial practices; race, gender, and ethnicity; and networks, platforms, and data. The selected candidate will pursue their own research agenda, collaborate with faculty in the Department of Cinema and Media Studies and other areas of the Division of the Arts & Humanities and University, and teach in both the undergraduate and graduate curriculum. The teaching load for tenure-track positions is typically four courses per year on the quarter system. Additional responsibilities include advising at all levels of the program and service on departmental and university committees. Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant Professor: $90,000-$120,000 ; this base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Candidate should have an active research agenda resulting in publication in leading venues in their field or the potential to develop such a publication record. They should have the ability or potential to be an excellent teacher in introductory and required courses in cinema and media theory and history, as well as in the University\u2019s Core curriculum , and to direct doctoral theses. A ll requirements for receipt of a PhD in a relevant field must be completed prior to the start of the appointment. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/190975 by 10:59 PM Central Time/11:59 PM Eastern Time on October 19, 2026. Only complete applications can be considered. \u00b7 Cover letter \u00b7 CV \u00b7 A writing sample of article or chapter length \u00b7 Research statement \u00b7 Teaching statement \u00b7 The names and contact information of three recommenders Following the initial review of applications, selected applicants will be asked to provide additional materials. Questions may be directed to Kina Thornton at smthornton@uchicago.edu with the subject line ASSISTANT PROFESSOR SEARCH. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/189104": {"title": "Professor of Marketing", "description": "Professor of Marketing. Marketing. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 7, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenured positions in Marketing beginning in the 2027-28 academic year. We are seeking the best possible candidates without regard to subfield of specialization. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s). Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment. Senior candidates will be judged on their established scholarship, teaching and collegiality. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, course evaluations from prior teaching, at least two samples of written work, a research statement, and the names of five scholars willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, research, and teaching. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188854": {"title": "Physician-Scientists: All Health-Related Research Areas - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD044", "description": "Physician-Scientists: All Health-Related Research Areas - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD044. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 6, 2026. Scope and Opportunity The University of Chicago's Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time, outstanding physician-scientists in any area of health-related research who are interested in joining a premier academic medical center committed to building the next generation of integrated clinical and biomedical research programs. This search is open to physician-scientists with relevant degrees (i.e., MD, MD/PhD or equivalent) working in any health-related research area basic, translational, clinical, computational, population, implementation, outcomes, public health and health services research. Academic rank, track, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. Primary appointments will be in appropriate departments or units in the Biological Sciences Division (BSD) with possible secondary appointments in other relevant units or Divisions depending on the candidates' research. Positions will include clinical activity, typically 0.1-0.25 of clinical full-time employment (FTE), which is negotiable on an individual basis. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a highly collaborative academic ecosystem with outstanding basic and translational science and clinical departments housed within a compact urban campus, state-of-the-art core facilities, exceptional graduate and medical students, and opportunities to engage with new multidisciplinary research and commercialization initiatives, including Hyde Park Labs , a new hybrid facility for academic research, science-based companies and the UChicago Science Incubator. Research Priorities The goal of this search is to recruit physician-scientists who will build ambitious, disease-relevant research programs and strengthen the connections among clinical care, basic discovery, translational science, and the training of future biomedical investigators. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Mechanistic studies rooted in human disease Clinical and translational research linked to patient populations, biospecimens, imaging, or longitudinal cohorts Precision medicine, biomarkers, diagnostics, and therapeutic response Early-phase trials, experimental therapeutics, and investigator-initiated clinical studies Health outcomes, health services research, implementation science, and population health Data-driven medicine, AI-enabled clinical research, and learning health systems Human genetics, genomics, immunology, metabolism, neuroscience, cancer, infectious disease, inflammatory disease, and other areas of human biology Programs that bridge clinical departments with basic science, engineering, computation, entrepreneurship, or therapeutic development Selection Process The initial phase of the search is led by a committee composed of faculty from multiple departments rather than by a single hiring department. This phase is designed to identify leading candidates across the breadth of the search area and to consider how their scholarship could contribute to, and be advanced by, the scientific and clinical programs, and intellectual environment, of our academic medical center. During an initial campus visit, candidates will meet faculty from multiple departments, institutes, and programs. A follow-up visit will be organized by the departmental affiliation(s) best suited to support each candidate\u2019s work and long-term success. Additional information about the selection principles and the broader division-wide recruitment strategy is available here: BSD Faculty Recruitment . A Division-Wide Recruitment Initiative This search is one of five coordinated, open-rank searches through which the Biological Sciences Division and UChicago Medicine, in collaboration with the Physical Sciences Division and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, are recruiting a cohort of exceptional faculty to expand the scale, disciplinary breadth, and human impact of biomedical research at UChicago. Candidates whose scope is solely research (i.e., no clinical FTE) whose work spans more than one search area are encouraged to apply to multiple searches. Cancer Biology and Immunology Chemical Biology and Therapeutics Translational Neuroscience AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine Physician-Scientists\u2014All Health-Related Areas (this ad) Application Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have an MD, MD/PhD, or an equivalent degree. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190751 . Applicants must upload: a cover letter CV including bibliography research statement Review of complete applications will end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188843": {"title": "Cancer Biology and Immunology - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search  #BSD041", "description": "Cancer Biology and Immunology - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD041. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 5, 2026. Scope and Opportunity The University of Chicago's Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time scientists , physician-scientists, and engineers whose work will help define the next generation of cancer biology and immunology . We are especially interested in candidates whose research illuminates the dynamic biology of malignant cells, immune cells, tissue environments, host physiology, microbes, metabolism, and therapeutic intervention in cancer. Researchers in immune-related diseases, such as autoimmunity, infection biology, transplantation immunology, immune tolerance, and inflammation are also encouraged to apply. Academic rank, track, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. P rimary appointments will be in appropriate departments or units, most likely in the Biological Sciences Division, though potentially in the Physical Sciences Division, or the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, with possible secondary appointments in other relevant units depending on the candidates' research. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a highly collaborative academic ecosystem in which outstanding basic science and clinical departments are co-located on a compact urban campus, with state-of-the-art core facilities, exceptional graduate and medical students, and opportunities to participate in multidisciplinary research, translation, commercialization, and entrepreneurship, including Hyde Park Labs , a new hybrid facility for academic research, science-based companies and the UChicago Science Incubator. Research Priorities The goal of this search is to recruit faculty who will deepen and expand the University of Chicago\u2019s strengths in cancer biology, immunology, or immuno-oncology, while building new connections among basic scientists, clinicians, engineers, computational biologists, public health and translational investigators. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: \u00b7 Tumor microenvironment and cancer\u2013immune ecology \u00b7 Cancer metabolism or immunometabolism \u00b7 T-cell, NK-cell, B-cell, and myeloid-cell biology \u00b7 Microbiome, virome, and host\u2013microbe interactions \u00b7 Tissue immunity in inflammation and/or tumor biology \u00b7 Cancer initiation, progression, metastasis, dormancy, recurrence, and immune escape \u00b7 Aging and immunosenescence in cancer \u00b7 Neuroimmune regulation in cancer and immunity \u00b7 Spatial, systems, and computational cancer or immunology \u00b7 Single-cell, functional-genomic, proteomic, metabolomic, or imaging approaches to cancer and/or immunity \u00b7 Immune monitoring, biomarker discovery, and mechanisms of therapeutic response or resistance \u00b7 Vaccines, antigen discovery, antigen presentation, and immune recognition \u00b7 Engineered immunity, synthetic immunology, and cell-based therapies, including CAR, TCR, NK-cell, and gene-edited immune-cell platforms \u00b7 Antibody therapeutics, bispecifics, immune checkpoints, and other immune-modulating strategies Candidates with programs oriented toward fundamental discovery, mechanistic cancer biology, immunology, or tumor ecology are encouraged to apply through this search. Candidates whose work is primarily focused on drug discovery, therapeutic chemistry, biologics, platform development, or later-stage therapeutic translation may also wish to consider the Chemical Biology and Therapeutics search. Selection Process The initial phase of the search is led by a committee composed of faculty from multiple departments and divisions rather than by a single hiring department. This phase is designed to identify leading candidates across the breadth of the search area and to consider how their scholarship could contribute to, and be advanced by, the University\u2019s scientific programs and intellectual environment. During an initial campus visit, candidates will meet faculty from multiple departments, institutes, and programs. A follow-up visit will be organized by the departmental affiliation(s) best suited to support each candidate\u2019s work and long-term success . Additional information about the selection principles and the broader division-wide recruitment strategy is available here: BSD Faculty Recruitment . A Division-Wide Recruitment Initiative This search is one of five coordinated, open-rank searches through which the Biological Sciences Division and UChicago Medicine, in collaboration with the Physical Sciences Division and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, are recruiting a cohort of exceptional faculty to expand the scale, disciplinary breadth, and human impact of biomedical research at UChicago. Candidates whose work spans more than one search area are encouraged to apply to multiple searches. Cancer Biology and Immunology (this ad) Chemical Biology and Therapeutics Translational Neuroscience AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine Physician-Scientists\u2014All Health-Related Areas Application Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a PhD, MD, or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190755 . Applicants must upload : a cover letter CV including bibliography research statement teaching statement names and contact information for three references Review of complete applications will start on September 30, 2026, and end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188846": {"title": "Translational Neuroscience - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search  #BSD040", "description": "Translational Neuroscience - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD040. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 5, 2026. Scope and Opportunity The University of Chicago's Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time scientists , physician-scientists, and engineers whose work will advance understanding of the nervous system and accelerate the translation of discovery into new diagnostic, preventive, or therapeutic strategies for neurological, psychiatric, developmental, sensory, and neurodegenerative disorders . This search builds on a highly successful, decade-long focus on building systems-level experimental, computational, and theoretical neuroscience at the University of Chicago. Academic rank, track, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. P rimary appointments will be in appropriate departments or units, most likely in the Biological Sciences, though potentially in the Physical Sciences Division, or the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, with possible secondary appointments in other relevant units depending on the candidates' research. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a highly collaborative academic medical center with outstanding basic science and clinical departments, state-of-the-art core facilities, exceptional undergraduate, graduate and medical students, and opportunities to engage with new multidisciplinary research and commercialization initiatives, including Hyde Park Labs , a new hybrid facility for academic research, science-based companies and the UChicago Science Incubator. Research Priorities The current search is intended to capture a broad range of neuroscience, including molecular, cellular, circuit, systems, computational, behavioral, clinical, and population-level approaches. We seek to recruit faculty who will strengthen connections among basic neuroscience, clinical neuroscience, human genetics, computational biology, engineering, and therapeutic development, with the aim of creating transformative programs in human neuroscience and neurological disease. For the coming year, we are particularly interested in researchers whose work advances restorative neuroscience and circuit interventions, as well as researchers studying neuroimmunology, neuroinflammation and related brain-body mechanisms. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Restorative neuroscience and circuit interventions Neuroimmunology, neuroinflammation, and brain\u2013body interactions Neurodegeneration, aging, and mechanisms of neuronal vulnerability Synaptic, circuit, systems, and behavioral neuroscience Computational neuroscience, neural data science, and AI-enabled neurobiology Neurogenetics, genomics, and molecular mechanisms of neurological disease Developmental neuroscience and neurodevelopmental disorders Neurovascular biology, blood\u2013brain barrier biology, and brain metabolism Neuromodulation, gene therapy, cell therapy, and precision neuroscience Selection Process The initial phase of the search is led by a committee composed of faculty from multiple departments and divisions rather than by a single hiring department. This phase is designed to identify leading candidates across the breadth of the search area and to consider how their scholarship could contribute to, and be advanced by, the University\u2019s scientific programs and intellectual environment. During an initial campus visit, candidates will meet faculty from multiple departments, institutes, and programs. A follow-up visit will be organized by the departmental affiliation(s) best suited to support each candidate\u2019s work and long-term success . Additional information about the selection principles and the broader division-wide recruitment strategy is available here: BSD Faculty Recruitment . A Division-Wide Recruitment Initiative This search is one of five coordinated, open-rank searches through which the Biological Sciences Division and UChicago Medicine, in collaboration with the Physical Sciences Division and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, are recruiting a cohort of exceptional faculty to expand the scale, disciplinary breadth, and human impact of biomedical research at UChicago. Candidates whose work spans more than one search area are encouraged to apply to multiple searches. Cancer Biology and Immunology Chemical Biology and Therapeutics Translational Neuroscience ( this ad) AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine Physician-Scientists\u2014All Health-Related Areas Application Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent and have completed postdoctoral training. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190756 . Applicants must upload : a cover letter CV including bibliography research statement teaching statement names and contact information for three references Review of complete applications will start on September 15, 2026, and end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188855": {"title": "Chemical Biology and Therapeutics - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD042", "description": "Chemical Biology and Therapeutics - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD042. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 5, 2026. Scope and Opportunity The University of Chicago's Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time scientists , physician-scientists, and engineers whose work will advance the discovery, design, development, translation, and mechanistic understanding of new therapeutic strategies . This search is intended to capture the full continuum from basic chemical and molecular discovery to translational therapeutics, including programs that bridge chemistry, biology, engineering, computation, pharmacology, and medicine . We are particularly interested in candidates who develop innovative approaches to interrogate biological mechanisms, modulate disease-relevant pathways, discover new therapeutic targets, or create transformative therapeutic platforms. Academic rank, track, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. P rimary appointments will be in appropriate departments or units, most likely in the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), though potentially in the Physical Sciences Division (PSD) or the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME), with possible secondary appointments in other relevant units depending on the candidates' research. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a highly collaborative academic ecosystem in which outstanding basic science, engineering, and clinical departments are co-located on a compact urban campus, with state-of-the-art core facilities, exceptional graduate and medical students, and opportunities to participate in multidisciplinary research, translation, commercialization, and entrepreneurship, including Hyde Park Labs , a new hybrid facility for academic research, science-based companies and the UChicago Science Incubator, and the newly established Therapeutic Discovery Institute. Research Priorities The goal of this search is to recruit faculty who will help build an integrated therapeutic discovery and development ecosystem at the University of Chicago, connecting fundamental biological discovery with chemical, computational, engineering, and clinical approaches to developing new medicines. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Chemical biology and molecular mechanisms of disease Small-molecule drug discovery, medicinal chemistry, and therapeutic design Protein-, peptide-, antibody-, RNA-, gene-, genome engineering-, and cell-based therapeutics Targeted protein degradation and molecular glues Structural biology, structure-based and computational drug discovery High-throughput screening, functional genomics, and chemical-genetic approaches Drug delivery, biomaterials, nanomedicine, and tissue-targeted therapeutics Synthetic biology and engineered therapeutic platforms Translational pharmacology, biomarker-guided therapeutics, and early-stage clinical translation Therapeutic development at the interface of academia, industry, and entrepreneurship Selection Process The initial phase of the search is led by a committee composed of faculty from multiple departments and divisions rather than by a single hiring department. This phase is designed to identify leading candidates across the breadth of the search area and to consider how their scholarship could contribute to, and be advanced by, the University\u2019s scientific programs and intellectual environment. During an initial campus visit, candidates will meet faculty from multiple departments, institutes, and programs. A follow-up visit will be organized by the departmental affiliation(s) best suited to support each candidate\u2019s work and long-term success . Additional information about the selection principles and the broader division-wide recruitment strategy is available here: BSD Faculty Recruitment . A Division-Wide Recruitment Initiative This search is one of five coordinated, open-rank searches through which the Biological Sciences Division and UChicago Medicine, in collaboration with the Physical Sciences Division and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, are recruiting a cohort of exceptional faculty to expand the scale, disciplinary breadth, and human impact of biomedical research at UChicago. Candidates whose work spans more than one search area are encouraged to apply to multiple searches. Cancer Biology and Immunology Chemical Biology and Therapeutics (this ad) Translational Neuroscience AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine Physician-Scientists\u2014All Health-Related Areas Application Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190752 . Applicants must upload : a cover letter CV including bibliography research statement teaching statement names and contact information for three references Review of complete applications will start on September 30, 2026, and end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188858": {"title": "AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine  - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search  #BSD043", "description": "AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine - A Division-wide, Open-rank Faculty Search #BSD043. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 5, 2026. Scope and Opportunity The University of Chicago's Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time scientists , physician-scientists, and engineers who bring innovative experimental, computational, and theoretical approaches to bear on fundamental questions in biology, medicine, and public health . This search is focused on realizing the power and promise of AI and statistical modeling approaches for biological sciences, capturing the full continuum from basic biological discovery to clinical translation and public health, including programs that bridge biophysics, computation, systems biology, bioengineering, and medicine. We especially welcome applicants who develop innovative approaches to investigate biological complexity and emergent properties at any scale\u2014from biomolecular design, molecular systems, and cellular physiology to cellular communities in tissues, organisms, and ecosystems. We welcome applicants who use quantitative and AI-driven methods to generate mechanistic insight, develop interpretable and predictive models, advance therapeutic and translational strategies for disease diagnosis, treatment, and management, or to develop new theory for biology. Academic rank, track, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. P rimary appointments will be in appropriate departments or units, most likely in the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), though potentially in the Physical Sciences Division (PSD) or Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME), with possible secondary appointments in other relevant units depending on the candidates' research. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a highly collaborative academic ecosystem in which outstanding basic science and clinical departments are co-located on a compact urban campus, with state-of-the-art core facilities, exceptional graduate and medical students, and opportunities to participate in multidisciplinary research, translation, commercialization, and entrepreneurship, including Hyde Park Labs , a new hybrid facility for academic research, science-based companies and the UChicago Science Incubator. Research Priorities The goal of this search is to recruit a cluster of faculty who will establish a distinctive community in AI-Enabled biology and medicine, forging new conceptual frameworks that predict, explain, and engineer biology across scales and with transformative consequences for both fundamental discoveries and applications to human health. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Computational and experimental approaches that yield interpretable, mechanistic models of living systems, from molecules to cell signaling networks, to host-microbiome symbioses, organismal behavior and ecosystems Research aimed to discover the evolutionary origins of functional biological systems at any scale. Spatial and temporal biology through interpretable, multimodal representations of biological organization and dynamics across scales Digital twins - Generative, predictive representations of cells, networks, and tissues that forecast dynamics such as morphogenesis, tumor growth, neural computation, organismal behavior and cognition, immune-microbiome interactions, and regeneration Machine-learning foundations for multimodal integration of data across genomics, metagenomics, proteomics, metabolomics, imaging, clinical records, and other data modes Developing biologically inspired approaches for AI/ML Enabling measurement and instrumentation, including multiplexed and multispectral imaging and molecular profiling that resolve cellular features in tissues Experimental design and autonomous discovery to generate the information-rich datasets needed to build and test predictive models of biological function AI-enabled therapeutic development for human disease Computational pathology, radiology, and imaging, and clinically informed models of disease trajectory, patient stratification, and therapeutic response Trustworthy, interpretable, and equitable AI in biomedical research and medicine Selection Process The initial phase of the search is led by a committee composed of faculty from multiple departments and divisions rather than by a single hiring department. This phase is designed to identify leading candidates across the breadth of the search area and to consider how their scholarship could contribute to, and be advanced by, the University\u2019s scientific programs and intellectual environment. During an initial campus visit, candidates will meet faculty from multiple departments, institutes, and programs. A follow-up visit will be organized by the departmental affiliation(s) best suited to support each candidate\u2019s work and long-term success . Additional information about the selection principles and the broader division-wide recruitment strategy is available here: BSD Faculty Recruitment . A Division-Wide Recruitment Initiative This search is one of five coordinated, open-rank searches through which the Biological Sciences Division and UChicago Medicine, in collaboration with the Physical Sciences Division and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering, are recruiting a cohort of exceptional faculty to expand the scale, disciplinary breadth, and human impact of biomedical research at UChicago. Candidates whose work spans more than one search area are encouraged to apply to multiple searches. Cancer Biology and Immunology Chemical Biology and Therapeutics Translational Neuroscience AI-Enabled Biology and Medicine (this ad) Physician-Scientists\u2014All Health-Related Areas Application Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190757 . Applicants must upload : a cover letter CV including bibliography research statement teaching statement names and contact information for three references Review of complete applications will start on September 30, 2026, and end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188699": {"title": "Comprehensive Gynecology Clinical Associates - #OBG101", "description": "Comprehensive Gynecology Clinical Associates - #OBG101. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Aug 5, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Comprehensive Gynecology, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will devote their clinical effort to outpatient gynecologic care at UChicago Medicine practices in Northbrook and River East and Labor & Delivery on-call services at our Hyde Park campus. Appointees will provide comprehensive outpatient gynecologic care including (but not limited to) abnormal uterine bleeding, uterine fibroids, endometriosis, pelvic pain, menopause-related care, contraception, cervical dysplasia, vulvovaginal disorders, sexual health concerns, and other complex benign gynecologic issues. These positions offer the opportunity to practice within a nationally recognized academic health system committed to innovation, interdisciplinary collaboration, and advancing women's health across the lifespan. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications and experience. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Clinical responsibilities will include providing comprehensive gynecologic care in the ambulatory setting, participating in Labor & Delivery on-call duties in Hyde Park, performing office-based gynecologic procedures, including endometrial biopsy, colposcopy, cervical and endometrial procedures, intrauterine device and contraceptive implant insertion and removal, vulvar biopsy and other minor procedures and performing common outpatient surgical procedures. Additionally, appointees will collaborate with multidisciplinary teams to deliver coordinated evidence-based care. Prior to the start of employment, qualified individuals must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. We especially welcome applicants who are dedicated with expertise in the full spectrum of benign gynecologic conditions and a commitment to providing high-quality, patient-centered care within a leading academic medical center. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190609. Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187378": {"title": "Bigelow Fellow/Lecturer", "description": "Bigelow Fellow/Lecturer. Law School. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Aug 4, 2026. THE UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO LAW SCHOOL. We seek to appoint Bigelow Fellows at the rank of Lecturer to teach legal research and writing, effective July 1, 2027. The appointment is for one year with possible reappointment for a second term. We seek candidates who have a law degree, demonstrate promise of distinguished legal scholarship and teaching and who bring diverse perspectives and ideas that enrich student experiences and the Law School\u2019s intellectual culture. The Bigelow Program has succeeded in placing its fellows in tenure-track academic positions. For further information, visit http://www.law.uchicago.edu/bigelow/howtoapply . This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applications are accepted only at apply.interfolio.com/189492 . You will be required to furnish a cv, law school transcript, research agenda, writing sample, and the names of at least two references . Applications accepted until all positions are filled or until 6/30/2027."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187379": {"title": "Law School Faculty", "description": "Law School Faculty. Law School. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Aug 4, 2026. THE UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO LAW SCHOOL seeks to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions starting July 1, 2027. We seek the best candidates without regard to specialization. Faculty members are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to excel as teachers, and to exhibit the highest level of engagement in the life of the Law School. All candidates will be judged on their potential to perform at this level, taking into account factors that include publications and other scholarship, grades, recommendations, experience, and ability. In assessing potential contributions to the life of the Law School, we value candidates who will contribute diverse perspectives and ideas that will enrich and improve student experiences and the Law School\u2019s intellectual culture. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . To be considered, candidates must register on-line at apply.interfolio.com/189493 . Please upload a r\u00e9sum\u00e9, the names of at least two qualified scholars willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching, copies of published or in-progress scholarship, and a law school transcript. No applications will be accepted after 6/30/2027."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188568": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in the Biological Sciences ", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer in the Biological Sciences . Biological Sciences Collegiate Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 31, 2026. The Biological Sciences Collegiate Division may have part-time teaching opportunities during the Autumn, Winter, and Spring quarters of the 2026-2027 academic year. Responsibilities: \u00b7 Teach a 10-week undergraduate course on campus or a 3-week intensive course at the Marine Biological Laboratory \u00b7 Prepare course materials, including assignments and exams \u00b7 Grade coursework and maintain academic records \u00b7 Hold regularly scheduled office hours \u00b7 Applicants must have a Ph.D. in a biological science \u00b7 Prior teaching experience at the university or four-year college level. Applicants must submit a curriculum vitae and complete an online application through the University of Chicago's Interfolio jobs board at apply.interfolio.com/190496 If a teaching opportunity becomes available, qualified applicants will be contacted based on their academic training and experience. These positions are part of the Service Employees International Union (SEIU)."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188339": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Pre-Modern History of the Islamicate Middle East or Central Asia", "description": "Assistant Professor in Pre-Modern History of the Islamicate Middle East or Central Asia. Department of Middle Eastern Studies. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Jul 31, 2026. The Department of Middle Eastern Studies at the University of Chicago invites applications for a tenure-track position, at the rank of Assistant Professor, in the Pre-Modern History of the Islamicate Middle East or Central Asia, with an expected start date of July 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. We invite applications from historians whose research focuses on the social, political, economic, or intellectual history of Muslim/Islamic societies within the Late Antique to Early Modern Periods (approx. 600\u20131700, C.E.). The teaching load for tenure-track positions is typically four courses per year on the quarter system, with courses ranging from introductory surveys to advanced seminars in the department and including courses in the University of Chicago's Core curriculum . Additional responsibilities include advising at all levels of the program and service on departmental and university committees. Salary Range: Assistant Professor: $90,000-$120,000; this base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The successful candidate should have a demonstrated ability and/or potential to carry out original independent research in the areas of the search, resulting in publications in leading venues which make or have the potential to make significant contributions to the relevant scholarly field(s). The successful candidate will also have demonstrated ability or potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching, and ability to teach a wide range of courses successfully. An excellent command of either Classical Arabic or Persian is required; research competency in an additional Middle Eastern/Central Asian language is highly desirable ( Arabic, Perisan, Turkic languages, or other relevant language). All requirements for receipt of a PhD in a relevant field completed prior to the start of the appointment. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/190288 by 10:59 PM Central Time/11:59 PM Eastern Time on October 19, 2026: \u00b7 CV \u00b7 Cover letter \u00b7 Research statement \u00b7 Writing sample \u00b7 The names and contact information of three recommenders who may be asked to provide letters on behalf of the applicant Applicants will be asked to provide a teaching statement and other additional materials following the initial review of applications. Questions may be directed to Salma Abdulkader at sabdulkader@uchicago.edu . This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188510": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer \u2013 Financial Mathematics Program", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer \u2013 Financial Mathematics Program. Department of Mathematics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 31, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Mathematics invites applications for the position of Part-Time Lecturer to teach in the In-person Master of Science in Financial Mathematics (MSFM) program and/or the Online MSFM program, in one of the following areas: Commodities Crypto and Smart Contracts Financial Computing Fixed Income Machine Learning Options and Derivatives Risk Management Trading and Market-Making Artificial Intelligence (AI) The program and class(es) that selected candidates will teach during their first term will be determined as part of the interview process, and taking into consideration the candidate\u2019s background, qualifications, and preferences. Courses are held for five (50-unit) or nine (100-unit) weeks during each academic quarter, with the sixth or tenth week for a final project or exam. For the In-person program, instruction is expected to be primarily in person, with some limited opportunities to offer remote or hybrid sessions. Courses can meet in the daytime (meeting twice a week for a total of three hours) or in the evening (meeting once a week for a total of three hours). For the Online program, instruction is delivered remotely. Courses meet in the evening (once a week for a total of three hours). The exact day(s)/time(s) for either modality is determined in consultation with the part-time lecturer. This position is part-time and is not benefits eligible. Applicants must currently be authorized to work in the U.S. Minimum qualifications: One of the following: a master\u2019s degree in a related field a bachelor's degree and 3 years of professional experience in the specific course area Preferred qualifications: a doctorate in a related field five years of teaching experience, as an instructor of record, in a professional graduate degree program at a University five years of professional experience in the specific course area Instructors of remotely taught courses must be in the United States when teaching for the program, specifically in California, Colorado, District of Columbia, Illinois, Indiana, Massachusetts, Michigan, New Mexico, New York, Oregon, Utah, Virginia, or Wisconsin for tax purposes. Remote instructors must be able to perform their responsibilities from a remote location, and their remote work arrangements are their responsibility. This includes, but is not limited to, an adequate internet connection and an adequate working space. Apply online at the University of Chicago Academic Careers website at apply.interfolio.com/190444 . Review of applications will begin on August 31, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Required materials: CV cover letter list of three references sample course syllabus Optional materials: teaching evaluations from past courses taught."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188093": {"title": "General Otolaryngologists, Clinical Associates (60% Effort) #SUR194", "description": "General Otolaryngologists, Clinical Associates (60% Effort) #SUR194. Section of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 30, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Otolaryngology\u2013Head & Neck Surgery, seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 60% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will focus on the diagnosis and treatment of a broad range of ear, nose, throat, and related disorders and will share our mission of providing the highest quality patient care. Appointees will support and complement faculty in Otolaryngology\u2013Head and Neck Surgery at the Hyde Park campus and affiliated satellites. Duties will include patient care. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery has a rich history, matched only by our ongoing commitment to excellence in medical education, research, patient care, and service to our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois, 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Otolaryngology or equivalent, and 4) have completed an accredited residency or equivalent training in Otolaryngology\u2013Head and Neck Surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/190066 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188290": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Chinese", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Chinese. Department of East Asian Languages & Civilizations. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 29, 2026. The Department of East Asian Languages and Civilizations at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers in Chinese. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in various courses of the Chinese Language curriculum. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching language courses at various levels (elementary, intermediate, and/or advanced), holding regularly scheduled office hours, and the preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the department, the successful candidate may teach between one and four courses per academic year. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a Master\u2019s degree or equivalent in a field related to Chinese Language pedagogy, superior proficiency in Chinese and English according to ACTFL guidelines, and experience teaching Chinese at the college or post-secondary level. Applications must include: Current CV Cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience and discusses approaches to pedagogy Sample syllabus The names and contact information of two references whose recommendation may be solicited Optional materials: Course evaluations, if available A teaching demonstration video, if available (please include a link to an externally hosted video) The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/190241 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. Review of application materials will begin August 28, 2026, and continue until the position is filled or the posting is closed. In the event that a teaching opportunity becomes available, we will contact applicants with the academic training and experience most appropriate for our available classes. This posting will expire on March 31, 2027. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval. Only complete applications will be considered. For more information about this position, please contact Dawn Brennan, dbrennan@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188437": {"title": "Research Associate - Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics", "description": "Research Associate - Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Jul 29, 2026. The Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at the University of Chicago invites applications for a full-time Research Associate for a three-year term starting in the 2026-2027 academic year. About the TMW Center Co-directed by Dana Suskind (Biological Sciences Division), and John List (Economics), the TMW Center for Early Learning + Public Health aspires to create a population-level shift in the knowledge and behavior of parents and caregivers to optimize the foundational brain development in children, birth to five years of age, particularly those born into poverty. The TMW Center has conducted 12 randomized control, quasi-experimental and implementation trials to date and plans to continue its active randomized control and implementation trials, including ongoing data collection and analysis in the coming years. As part of these research projects, the Center is continuing to adapt and validate two state-of-the-art tools: (1) the SPEAK - a computer-adaptive tool designed to assess caretaker expectations and knowledge of early childhood cognitive and language development, and (2) the Luet \u2013 a wearable device paired with an app that uses machine learning to measure early child development through language and physical movement. Job Summary: The Research Associate will work, in partnership with Co-Director Suskind and Senior Research Associate Pernaudet, to design and execute TMW Center research projects. Core responsibilities include conducting rigorous quantitative analyses, writing and publishing findings, presenting research, and leading grant applications in collaboration with Professors List and Suskind. The position entails collaboration on new and ongoing research projects, with a highly interdisciplinary team of economists, psychologists, engineers, app developers, and more. A primary focus of the work is applying economic models and machine learning techniques to empirical analysis of data generated from TMW Center\u2019s research projects, including via Luet and the SPEAK. The position will also allow the Research Associate to be part of the vibrant research environment of the TMW Center for Early Learning + Public Health and the Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at The University of Chicago. This position will report to the TMW Center Co-Directors, Dana Suskind and John List with additional reporting to the Center COO for matters outside of research. This position will require a commitment of at least 3 years. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Responsibilities will include, but are not limited to: In collaboration with Co-Director, develop research designs for TMW Center projects, including formulating testable research questions, selecting appropriate measurements, and ensuring clean identification of outcomes. At the direction of the Co-Director, write, submit, and publish findings in peer-reviewed journals. Annual goal is, on average, to have at least four papers in progress and at least two submitted for publication. Disseminate TMW research findings to broader audiences by presenting in select conferences and meetings of early childhood researchers, psychologists, and economists. Maintain existing TMW Center research collaborations with faculty at the University of Chicago, as well as other national and international faculty with whom the Center is partnering on projects. Participate in and contribute to grant preparation process in a timely manner in close collaboration with the Co-Directors, potential Co-PIs, collaborators and consultants and the Grants and Contracts office. Participate in regularly scheduled TMW Center team meetings, lab meetings, and \u2018papers\u2019 meeting with Co-Director Suskind and weekly Econ meeting with Co-Director List. Competencies: Excellent organizational, quantitative, and analytical skills with high-level of accuracy and sharp attention to detail required. Superior interpersonal skills and the ability to work effectively with a wide range of individuals and constituencies in a diverse professional environment required. Ability to effectively manage a heavy workload, competing priorities, and tight deadlines required. A quick, self-learner with ability to initiate, organize, prioritize and complete multiple, high-level projects; meet tight deadlines; and work effectively with minimal supervision in a fast-paced environment required. Strong computer skills including programming, ability to work with data sets and data visualization ability required. Strong written and verbal communication skills required. Ability to work both independently and as a team member required. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Economics. Two years postdoctoral experience and research within field of economics. A microeconomist with experience in the experimental and behavioral economics is preferred Strong expertise in education and health policy. Demonstrated ability to communicate research findings clearly to multiple audiences through oral presentations and written work. Experience in writing grant proposals, and a strong commitment to research preferred. We especially welcome applicants with technical knowledge or skills in: Knowledge of Stata, Python, Matlab, and/or R preferred. Knowledge of LaTeX required. Applicants must apply online through the University of Chicago Acdemic Recruitment job board at https://apply.interfolio.com/190379. Applicants are required to submit: Resume/CV Cover Letter (include earliest available start date) Research statement Additionally, applicants are strongly encouraged to submit, as optional, up to three published or unpublished research papers. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188394": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Law", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer in Law. Law School. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 28, 2026. The University of Chicago Law School invites applications for part-time lecturer positions for the 2026-27 academic year. We seek excellent teachers and experienced practitioners to teach in a variety of law, law-practice, and law-related areas that enrich the Law School's already robust curriculum. (Candidates are invited to consult the Law School's current curricular offerings at: https://www.law.uchicago.edu/prospective/curriculum ). Candidates must have at least a tentative idea for a course or seminar to be offered, as well as expertise and experience directly relevant to the course or seminar being proposed. Qualified candidates must have a graduate level degree; J.D. or appropriate terminal degree preferred. Candidates must have relevant or law-related experience; prior experience teaching law students is strongly preferred. Among other considerations, we value candidates who will contribute diverse perspectives and ideas that will enrich and improve student experiences and the Law School's intellectual culture. We will review applications on an on-going basis throughout the year but will stop accepting applications for the 2026-27 academic year on March 26, 2027. Each candidate must submit a curriculum vitae or resume and a course proposal. In addition, we would prefer to receive teaching evaluations if available from any prior teaching experience. Other material relevant to your candidacy may be included. Interested parties must submit materials online at this website."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188063": {"title": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) - Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics", "description": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) - Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 28, 2026. The Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at the University of Chicago invites applications for appointment as Instructional Professor at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full. This is a full-time, career-track teaching position to begin on or after July 1, 2027. The initial appointment term is from three to five years depending on rank and is renewable with opportunity for promotion within the Instructional Professor track based on performance review. The successful candidate will teach up to six courses in the undergraduate and master\u2019s curriculum annually. We are interested in candidates with expertise in either macroeconomics and its applied fields, OR statistics, data science, casual inference, and econometrics. In addition to teaching, the IP will advise and mentor undergraduate and masters students, organize student activities, contribute to recruitment and admissions of MA students and oversee and plan the curriculum under the direction of the Director of Undergraduate Studies and the Director of MA programs. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees Union (SEIU) and the University. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Economics. Teaching experience with courses in applied economics, particularly pertaining to macroeconomics, OR statistics, data science, casual inference, and econometrics, with an emphasis on practical applications of the tools taught in these courses is required. Consideration for the hiring level will be granted conditional on professional qualifications. The successful candidate will have outstanding in-person, written, and virtual communication skills and the ability to teach audiences with diverse backgrounds and with varying levels of knowledge about economics. Candidates should be comfortable teaching from introductory courses through courses at the master\u2019s level. Applicants must apply online at the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio academic job board at: apply.interfolio.com/190053 . Applicants are required to submit: 1) curriculum vitae, 2) teaching statement, 3) student evaluations, 4) two published or unpublished research papers, and 5) three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled or the search has ended."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188079": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer - Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer - Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 28, 2026. The Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at the University of Chicago invites applications for a part-time, renewable Lecturer 1B position for spring quarter of the 2026-2027 academic year. Depending on the curricular needs of the department, the successful candidate/s will teach between one and three courses per academic year. Additional duties include holding regularly scheduled office hours, and the preparation and grading of assignments and testing materials. A demonstrated record of teaching experience in empirical industrial organization, financial market design, international finance and microstructure or related fields is strongly preferred. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees Union (SEIU) and the University. This is a part time, non-benefits eligible position. Applicants must have a completed Ph.D. in Economics or Finance and a record of teaching at a university or four-year college level. Applicants must apply online on the University of Chicago's Interfolio academic job board at: apply.interfolio.com/190056 . Applicants are required to submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, teaching evaluations, course proposal or sample syllabus, and contact information for at least two references. Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188258": {"title": "Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) in Data Science", "description": "Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) in Data Science. Data Science. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 28, 2026. The Data Science Institute at the University of Chicago invites applications for the position of Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) to lead the Data Science Clinic. The Data Science Clinic is an experiential project-based course where students work in teams as data scientists with real-world clients from industry, academia, and social impact organizations. You can view more information about the DSI Clinic on our website here . The candidate will be appointed as Assistant Senior Instructional Professor, Associate Senior Instructional Professor, or Senior Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications and years of experience in a similar role. The appointment will be made in the Data Science Institute. The appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable. This is a career-track position with competitive salary and benefits. This position is expected to begin on or around December 1st, 2026. Responsibilities include leading and teaching three quarters of the data science clinic courses as well as up to one additional course in data science. Additional responsibilities include hiring, evaluation, supervision, and mentoring of academic and staff appointees in the DSI Clinic program. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago is implementing an ambitious plan for research and education in Data Science and AI, including new academic programs at the undergraduate and graduate levels and new cross-disciplinary research programs. The Data Science Institute ( datascience.uchicago.edu ) is a collaboration among the Department of Computer Science ( cs.uchicago.edu ), the Department of Statistics ( stat.uchicago.edu ), and other units on campus. Topics of interest include foundations and applications of artificial intelligence, machine learning, large language models, scientific AI and machine learning, data ethics and privacy, data visualization, societal impact of data and AI, safe and trustworthy AI, data engineering, scalable data systems, and high-dimensional statistical inference. We encourage applications from all researchers focused on developing the foundations and practice of Data Science as an emerging field. The Data Science Institute focuses on research that advances fundamental theories at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, statistics, and computing in the context of real-world and domain-specific problems. The Committee on Data Science ( codas.uchicago.edu ) is the home of data science education programs. The larger data science community at the University of Chicago includes the Committee on Computational and Applied Mathematics (cam.uchicago.edu), the Toyota Technological Institute at Chicago (TTIC), the Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation, Fermilab and Argonne National Laboratory. Required: A Ph.D. in a related field At least two years of experience teaching in a college or university as an instructor of record Preferred: Training and professional experience in one or more of the following areas: data science, machine learning, artificial intelligence, databases, and applied statistics. Experience in management of experiential learning academic programs and personnel And 3 years of industry experience with demonstrated quantitative, analytic, data science, and/or AI experience. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/190212 . Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until all positions are filled. The following materials are required: curriculum vitae cover letter A teaching statement including description of teaching philosophy, curricular development experience, and ability to interact with a diverse group of students Evidence of successful management of programs (or evidence of potential to do so) Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations, performance review, or other relevant assessments), syllabi of courses previously designed and taught three confidential letters of recommendation requested via Interfolio"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187341": {"title": "Senior Instructional Professor (Open-Rank)/Associate Academic Director MS-CAPP", "description": "Senior Instructional Professor (Open-Rank)/Associate Academic Director MS-CAPP. Physical Sciences Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 27, 2026. The Department of Computer Science at the University of Chicago invites applications for a Senior Instructional Professor (SIP) position that would hold a concurrent appointment as the Associate Academic Director for the Master of Science in Computational Analysis and Public Policy (MS-CAPP). The selected candidate will be appointed as Assistant Senior Instructional Professor, Associate Senior Instructional Professor, or (Full) Senior Instructional Professor, with rank determined by qualifications. The appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable. This is a career-track position with competitive salary and benefits. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . MS-CAPP is a joint program between the Department of Computer Science and the Harris School of Public Policy in which students learn both fundamental concepts and applied skills in computer science, data science, statistics, and public policy. MS-CAPP graduates take on roles as data scientists, data engineers, software engineers, policy professionals, etc in a wide variety of private sector and public sector organizations. The selected candidate will be an important part of the MS-CAPP leadership team. The person in this role will work with the Director on revising the curriculum in response to rapid changes in technology and on developing new courses as needed. The person in this role is responsible for working with instructors who teach within the program to ensure they are delivering high quality courses that are relevant to the students\u2019 career aspirations, and for recruiting and managing professional staff. In addition, this role includes day-to-day management of the program staff, overseeing admissions, overseeing enrollment and faculty support, helping students with career planning, and other duties based on program needs. The person hired into this role will teach four courses for MS-CAPP and, possibly, one for the Masters Program in Computer Science. Minimum Qualifications: \u25cf One of the following: A doctorate in Computer Science or a related field at the time of appointment; or A master's degree and 4 years of relevant professional experience; or A bachelor\u2019s degree and 10 years of relevant professional experience. \u25cf At least two years\u2019 experience teaching in a college or university as an instructor of record \u25cf Experience in management of programs, personnel, and/or budgets. Preferred Qualifications: \u25cf Professional experience in public interest technology/civic technology and/or public policy. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago Jobs website: apply.interfolio.com/189510. Review of applications will begin on August 26th and continue until the position is filled. The following materials are required: - Curriculum vitae - Cover letter, which must include a description of the applicant\u2019s management experience. - Statement of teaching philosophy and curricular development experience - A list of three references, including name, title, employer/school and email address - Sample syllabus for a class the applicant has taught before, or would be interested in teaching - Teaching evaluations from past teaching at the university level - Evidence of successful management of programs (or evidence of ability to do so)"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/188115": {"title": "Part-Time Lecturer in the Humanities Core", "description": "Part-Time Lecturer in the Humanities Core. Humanities Collegiate Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 24, 2026. The Arts and Humanities Collegiate Division at the University of Chicago is now accepting applications for part-time Lecturers who will teach up to four courses in the Humanities Core during the 2026\u201327 academic year. Part of every first-year student\u2019s course schedule, the Humanities Core is the foundation of Chicago\u2019s general education curriculum. It is instrumental in facilitating the transition from high school to college by helping students become independent thinkers. In small discussion-based seminars, students learn and practice the art of oral argument and dialogue. To learn more about the sequence and the Humanities Core, see the College\u2019s online catalog: http://collegecatalog.uchicago.edu/thecollege/humanities/ Responsibilities include preparing and teaching seminar-style courses, holding regularly scheduled office hours, preparing and grading assignments, and attending staff meetings. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University. This position is part-time and not benefits-eligible. The position requires a Ph.D. in a relevant Humanities discipline. The position is open to applicants who will have completed all requirements for their Ph.D. no later than August 31, 2026. Experience teaching relevant coursework at the college level is required. To be considered for the position, you must submit your application through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio site at: apply.interfolio.com/190086. Applicants are required to submit the following: A cover letter An up-to-date Curriculum Vitae A teaching statement Syllabi of courses previously taught and/or designed by the candidate or sample syllabi of proposed courses Additional evidence of pedagogical accomplishment and promise: such as a teaching portfolio, recent course evaluations, third-party observations of the applicant\u2019s teaching if available, or other relevant assessments. The review of applications will begin on August 7, 2026, and continue until the position is filled or the search is closed."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187817": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Genitourinary Oncology Program #MED355a", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Genitourinary Oncology Program #MED355a. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 22, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank on the School of Medicine track. Appointees will be expected to deliver outstanding patient care and collaborate well within a multidisciplinary team. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and at network locations. Appointees are also expected to develop a patient-oriented clinical research program in their area of interest that will complement our existing GU research pipeline. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. We especially welcome applicants who specialize in the treatment of genitourinary cancers, with a preference for those with expertise in renal (kidney) malignancies. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/189853 . Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187409": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 OB/GYN Generalists - #OBG102", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 OB/GYN Generalists - #OBG102. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 21, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will be responsible for providing direct patient care, including coverage in the Labor and Delivery Unit at UChicago Medicine Ingalls Memorial Hospital (IMH), supporting the gynecology inpatient service, and providing outpatient care at the Ingalls ambulatory clinics or associated Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs). The appointees will be members of the Obstetrics and Gynecology call groups. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. We especially welcome applicants with training and experience in general obstetrics and gynecology and a special interest in building a high-quality obstetrics and gynecology program in a community hospital setting. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/189522 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187791": {"title": "Academic Vitreoretinal Specialists #OVS028a", "description": "Academic Vitreoretinal Specialists #OVS028a. Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 21, 2026. The Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science at the University of Chicago is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide patient care focused on medical and surgical retina services within the University of Chicago Medical Center. Additional responsibilities will include teaching residents and overseeing trainees, which encompasses medical students, residents, and retina fellows, along with engaging in scholarly activities. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs for eligible employees, including healthcare, retirement and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will focus on the management of patients experiencing medical and surgical vitreoretinal conditions. Appointees will become part of a dynamic and esteemed community of faculty members and students at UChicago Medicine, within the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago, and its associated institutions. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible in ophthalmology, 4) have completed an ACGME-approved ophthalmology residency or its equivalent, and 5) have completed a two-year vitreoretinal fellowship. We especially welcome applicants who have an interest in Retina Clinical Trials and research on Macular Degeneration. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications at https://apply.interfolio.com/189836. Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187579": {"title": "Faculty Radiation Oncologists #RON037", "description": "Faculty Radiation Oncologists #RON037. Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 20, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will provide radiation oncology services at department locations and may lead a practice comprised of one to three radiation oncologists as well as nursing, physics, dosimetry, and therapy staff. The clinical work will primarily be based at University Medical Center at Hyde Park and/or other Chicago-area network sites as needed. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be Board certified or eligible in radiation oncology, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited residency or equivalent in radiation oncology. We especially welcome applicants who have formerly served as an attending radiation oncologist in an academic institution, as well as those who have experience participating in clinical trials. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/189661. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/187315": {"title": "Senior Assistant Instructional Professor - Master of Science in Genetic Counseling Program #MED435", "description": "Senior Assistant Instructional Professor - Master of Science in Genetic Counseling Program #MED435. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 15, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine invites applications for a Senior Assistant Instructional Professor in the Master of Science Program in Genetic Counseling. This is a full-time career-track teaching position, a two-year program designed to provide foundational training in genetic counseling. The core curriculum focuses on the applications and innovations in genetics, genomics, counseling, and applied research. Our current course offerings cover fundamentals of medical genetics, genetic counseling, ethics, and statistics. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The initial three-year appointment will begin in Academic Year 2026-2027 and is renewable. The appointee will be appointed as Program Director and initially teach 3 courses per academic year in addition to substantial mentoring and service responsibilities. Their courses likely will include a core requirement in health communication & counseling techniques, medical genetics, as well as being the course director for fieldwork rotations based on professional expertise of the candidate. This course load is subject to change over time based on administrative and service duties. In addition, the appointee will advise students on their independent research projects and quarterly course selection, serving as first and second readers on projects, and providing broad academic and professional guidance to a cohort of master\u2019s students in weekly office hours. They also closely advise students and alumni for job searches. Other non-classroom duties include participating in admissions and recruiting as well as other co-curricular academic programming. They hire and manage course instructors and contribute to program operations, participate in pedagogy workshops, and the annual student research symposium. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: 1) a master\u2019s degree in the discipline of genetic counseling, 2) current certification in genetic counseling by the ABGC or the American Board of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ABMGG), 3) university level teaching experience in relevant course(s), and 4) at least five years of experience as a certified genetic counselor, a minimum of three of which must be in a patient-facing role (clinic or research). To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/189440 . Applicants are required to upload the following materials with their application: 1) cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae including bibliography; 3) contact information for 3 references, 4) teaching statement, 5) sample syllabi of courses previously taught as primary instructor and/or of proposed course, and 6) teaching evaluations from the past two years. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184843": {"title": "Assistant Professor School of Medicine Track \u2013 Ben May Department for Cancer Research #BMD011", "description": "Assistant Professor School of Medicine Track \u2013 Ben May Department for Cancer Research #BMD011. Ben May Department for Cancer Research. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 8, 2026. The University of Chicago's Ben May Department for Cancer Research seeks full-time faculty members at the rank of Assistant Professor. The Department has an illustrious history of discovery in cancer biology in a dynamic academic setting. Research in the department is focused on mechanistic insights into tumor biology with an emphasis on signaling, metabolism, and immunology and a goal to translate scientific discovery into the clinic. Appointees are expected to direct a proteomics core facility and establish an independent cutting-edge research program in cancer biology that complements and extends the research conducted by the Department. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent in Chemistry, Biochemistry, or a closely related field. To be considered, those interested must apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications at https://apply.interfolio.com/187595 . Applicants must upload: a cover letter, curriculum vitae with bibliography, a research statement, a teaching statement, and contact information for three references. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186660": {"title": "Part-time Clinical Associates - Pediatric Critical Care #PED178a", "description": "Part-time Clinical Associates - Pediatric Critical Care #PED178a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 8, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Critical Care Medicine, is seeking Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to two years. Clinical responsibilities include providing coverage for the Comer Children\u2019s Hospital Pediatric Intensive Care Unit (PICU) and Sedation Services. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per shift. These positions are benefits ineligible. The Section of Pediatric Critical Care has a fully accredited fellowship program, and a 30 bed PICU, in addition to the Pediatric Sedation and Palliative Care Services. Faculty members within the Section are actively involved in research, quality improvement, and medical education initiatives. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is a designated Level I Pediatric Trauma Center, a Magnet hospital recognized for nursing excellence, and an ELSO Gold Level Center of Excellence for extracorporeal life support. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Critical Care Medicine . We especially welcome applicants with experience caring for complex and critically ill infants, children, and adolescents during the past five years. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/188933 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186541": {"title": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED434", "description": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED434. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 1, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to two years. Clinical responsibilities include evaluating and treating patients at the newly built Emergency Department at Crown Point Hospital, IN. The Crown Point Emergency Department comprises an 8-bed unit with a projected annual volume of approximately 8,000 patient encounters in its first year. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per hour. These positions are benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Indiana, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/188837 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186530": {"title": "Research Assistant/Associate Professor in Nuclear Medicine Instrumentation #RAD081a", "description": "Research Assistant/Associate Professor in Nuclear Medicine Instrumentation #RAD081a. Department of Radiology. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Jun 30, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Radiology is searching for a Research Assistant Professor or Research Associate Professor to join the nuclear medicine and molecular imaging instrumentation laboratory in the Molecular Imaging and Theranostics (MI&T) group. The MI&T group has a long history of developing novel imaging instruments and devices. The appointee will initially conduct academic research in collaboration with a faculty member or team, participating in one or more of the ongoing research projects on PET, SPECT and/or multi-modality imaging systems, but will be expected, over time, to develop their own independent research agenda and grant portfolio. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: 1) a doctoral degree or equivalent in physical sciences, engineering, or related field and 2) completed postdoctoral training. We especially welcome applicants with expertise and experience in radiation detection technologies, PET and/or SPECT instrumentation, as well as related physics and computing methodologies, and image-guided therapy applications. Assessment of applications will consider publication record, experience with collaborative research, track record, and potential to obtain extramural funding. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/188833 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186435": {"title": "Clinical Instructors, Neurological Surgery #NSU022", "description": "Clinical Instructors, Neurological Surgery #NSU022. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Instructor. Posted: Jun 30, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurological Surgery seeks full-time Clinical Instructors for renewable terms of one year. The successful appointees will be physicians with subspecialty interest in neurosurgery. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, and 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois. We especially welcome applicants who have completed additional fellowship training in functional neurosurgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/188753 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184855": {"title": "Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Physicians \u2013 Region 11 #MED431", "description": "Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Physicians \u2013 Region 11 #MED431. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 29, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank to serve within Region 11 EMS and support the University of Chicago Medicine\u2019s role as an Illinois Department of Public Health (IDPH) Resource Hospital. Since its designation in 1976, UChicago Medicine has provided medical oversight, protocol development, and administrative leadership for the Chicago South EMS System as part of Region 11 EMS. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Region 11 EMS Medical Directors Consortium (MDC) is comprised of four Resource Hospitals that collaborate to ensure high-quality, coordinated emergency medical services across the city of Chicago. UChicago Medicine is a Level I Adult Trauma Center, Level I Pediatric Trauma Center, Comprehensive Stroke Center, STEMI Center, Burn Center, VAD Center, and Level III Perinatal Center. Appointees will join a highly collaborative academic faculty with active programs in emergency medicine research, medical education, simulation, international emergency medicine, EMS and prehospital medicine, and air-medical transport. These positions offer the opportunity to contribute meaningfully to regional EMS leadership and may evolve into a future Assistant EMS Medical Director role within Region 11 EMS. Clinical responsibilities will include direct attending coverage within the Emergency Department at the University of Chicago Medical Center Hyde Park campus, with additional responsibilities in resident and trainee education, EMS quality improvement initiatives, protocol development, systems collaboration, and engagement with regional EMS agencies, fire departments, and hospital leadership. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine, and 4) complete an accredited EMS fellowship or meet specific practice pathways and pass the EMS subspecialty exam. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187605 . Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186434": {"title": "Endovascular Neurosurgeons #NSU025", "description": "Endovascular Neurosurgeons #NSU025. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 29, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurological Surgery seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. This is an exciting opportunity to be a member of our growing neurosurgical practice. Appointees will provide clinical care in the inpatient and outpatient setting as a neurosurgeon, including an active neurosurgical clinical practice at our offsite locations, including but not limited to Advent Hinsdale. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation and academic rank are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University also has a robust Neuroscience Institute, which brings together more than 90 neuroscientists based in academic and clinical departments across the campus into a cohesive program. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) have or be eligible for permanent medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana, 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible by The American Board of Neurological Surgery, and 4) have additional sub-specialty fellowship training in endovascular neurosurgery. Candidates with dual training in skull base or cerebrovascular neurosurgery in addition to endovascular fellowship are particularly encouraged. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/188752 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186135": {"title": "Pediatric Infectious Diseases \u2013 Part-Time Clinical Associates #PED238", "description": "Pediatric Infectious Diseases \u2013 Part-Time Clinical Associates #PED238. Section of Pediatric Infectious Diseases. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pediatric Infectious Diseases, seeks part-time Clinical Associates to join our expanding Pediatric Infectious Disease group at Comer Children's Hospital at 10% effort. Applicants will have a strong background in pediatric infectious diseases. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits ineligible. The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior and compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide an education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. The department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. UChicago Medicine Comer Children's Hospital is a free standing 165 bed children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Pediatric Infectious Diseases. We welcome applicants dedicated to working as part of a dynamic and exceptional team of pediatric specialists. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/188515 . Applicants must upload: CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186219": {"title": "Gender & Reconstructive Plastic Surgeons #SUR193", "description": "Gender & Reconstructive Plastic Surgeons #SUR193. Section of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Plastic & Reconstructive Surgery, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide gender affirmation and general plastic and reconstructive care to patients. Appointees will help to increase transgender patient volume and provide support to the greater Chicagoland transgender patient population. Also, appointees will develop offsite practices to improve our geographic footprint in the South Suburbs and River East markets. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Plastic & Reconstructive Surgery is committed to medical education, research, patient care, and serving our community. These surgeons will support transgender surgery in addition to general reconstruction and aesthetic/contouring surgery. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or eligible for Board certification in Plastic Surgery. We especially welcome applicants who have completed a microsurgery fellowship and have experience in transgender surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/188583 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/186019": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia - Full-time #ACC082", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia - Full-time #ACC082. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 25, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including, but not limited to Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and or main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Clinical Associates will have access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care resources including, but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. A separate search is underway for similar part-time appointees. We encourage applications to one or both positions: 50% - 80% effort Clinical Associate: https://apply.interfolio.com/187953 Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications at https://apply.interfolio.com/188434 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/185884": {"title": "Director of Adult Cardiac Imaging #MED430", "description": "Director of Adult Cardiac Imaging #MED430. MED - Section of Cardiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 18, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is searching for a full-time Associate Professor or Professor to serve as the Director of Adult Cardiac Imaging. The appointee will join and lead an established group of nationally and internationally renowned physicians, researchers, and educators in the field of cardiac imaging. In conjunction with other leaders in the Section of Cardiology and the Heart and Vascular Center, the Director is expected to expand clinical research and to develop strategies for increasing patient referrals and procedure volumes. The Director will provide administrative functions necessary for the efficient and fiscally responsible management of the program. The appointee will also coordinate the strategic direction, operational efficiency, and clinical quality of the program, which includes the delivery of exceptional patient care, optimizing imaging resource utilization, and driving innovation in cardiac imaging technology. Academic rank and compensation are dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Cardiac Imaging Laboratory at the University of Chicago has a long-standing tradition of excellence in a full range of diagnostic and therapeutic procedures, including transthoracic and transesophageal imaging, as well as guidance of structural heart disease repair. Clinical responsibilities include review and interpretation of cardiac CT/MRI/ECHO (multimodality) images, outpatient cardiology clinic, inpatient services and consults, as well as supervising activities of satellite locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of fellows and students, as well as scholarly activities. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate (MD) or equivalent degree, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified in internal medicine and in cardiovascular disease, and 4) have experience and understanding of other cardiac imaging modalities, namely Echo, MRI, CT, and PET. We especially welcome applicants with clinical multimodality imaging (CT/MRI/ECHO) experience, leadership experience in managing complex clinical operations, and a track record of clinical research in cardiac imaging. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/188329 . Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/185321": {"title": "Clinical Professor, Open-Rank (50% - 80% effort) #ACC080", "description": "Clinical Professor, Open-Rank (50% - 80% effort) #ACC080. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Track Professor. Posted: Jun 16, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care is searching for part-time clinical academic anesthesiologists from 50% - 80% effort for appointment at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Professor. Clinical responsibilities will include providing perioperative care in operating room and non-operating room environments. Covered locations include the University of Chicago Medical Center main campus in Hyde Park, and possibly system network sites including Ingalls Memorial Hospital, our location in Crown Point Indiana, and other sites to be added in the future. Other duties include teaching and supervision of fellows, residents, and medical students. Scholarly activity is not required but is supported. Academic rank, track and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. Clinical Academics will have access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care resources, including but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and/or Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications at http://apply.interfolio.com/187956. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/185222": {"title": "Emergency Radiologists #RAD096", "description": "Emergency Radiologists #RAD096. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 16, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiology seeks full-time faculty members at 100% effort in the School of Medicine track for renewable terms. Appointees will have responsibilities that include interpretation of all aspects of emergency radiology diagnostic examinations and will join our team of emergency radiologists, extending an existing evening shift. This is a fully remote position, with clinical responsibilities performed via teleradiology. Education and scholarly activities are expected. Work shifts will be primarily nights and weekends. The anticipated clinical schedule is 7 days on clinical service and 14 days off clinical service. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. As well as an anticipated annual incentive payment between $30,000 to $75,000 based on work schedule. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will contribute primarily to clinical care in a high-acuity emergency radiology setting, with additional participation in education and scholarly activity consistent with the School of Medicine track. Educational contributions may include supervision and teaching of residents, fellows, and medical students in the clinical environment. Scholarly activity may include quality improvement initiatives, clinical protocol development, educational innovation, multidisciplinary collaboration, or participation in clinical research, with mentorship and institutional support available to foster growth in these areas. A substantial prior record of funded research is not required. The University of Chicago Medical Center is a designated Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and includes a nationally recognized burn unit. The Department of Radiology comprises more than 80 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents, and fellows, faculty engage in clinical, translational, and educational scholarship within a highly collaborative academic environment. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be American Board certified or Board eligible in Radiology. We especially welcome applicants with prior experience as an emergency radiology attending in high-volume, high-acuity settings, emergency radiology fellowship training or equivalent experience. Basic pediatric emergency radiology skills are also preferred. The Department is especially interested in candidates who demonstrate: \u00b7 A strong commitment to high-quality, efficient clinical care in a fast-paced emergency radiology environment \u00b7 Engagement in resident and medical student education, particularly through case-based and clinical teaching \u00b7 Interest in developing focused academic or programmatic contributions aligned with emergency radiology (e.g., quality improvement, protocol development, curriculum design, or multidisciplinary collaboration) To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187892. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/185312": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia Part Time (50% - 80%) #ACC081", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia Part Time (50% - 80%) #ACC081. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 12, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care seeks Clinical Associates from 50% - 80% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include providing perioperative care in operating room and non-operating room environments. Covered locations include the University of Chicago Medical Center main campus in Hyde Park, and possibly system network sites including Ingalls Memorial Hospital, our location in Crown Point Indiana, and other sites to be added in the future. These positions do not require formal teaching or scholarly activity, compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. Clinical Associates will have full access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care, including but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications at https://apply.interfolio.com/187953 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184772": {"title": "Comprehensive Gynecology Faculty - #OBG100", "description": "Comprehensive Gynecology Faculty - #OBG100. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 11, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Comprehensive Gynecology (GC), is searching for full-time faculty members at the rank of associate or assistant professor. Appointees will be responsible for comprehensive, patient-centered gynecologic care across the lifespan and will have expertise in minimally invasive gynecologic surgery, office-based procedures, hormonal and non-hormonal therapies, and preventive women\u2019s health care. Appointees will be afforded opportunities to expand their clinical skills through career development focusing on menopause and perimenopause management, weight management/obesity care, bone health/osteoporosis prevention and treatment, and related medical and surgical therapies. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees\u2019 primary responsibilities will be dedicated to supporting the Comprehensive Gynecology section\u2019s practices in Flossmoor, IL, and Crown Point, IN. Appointees will operate at our ambulatory surgical center in Crown Point, IN, and our main campus in Hyde Park, IL. Appointees\u2019 clinical efforts, including call duties in Hyde Park (Gynecology call), will emphasize individualized, compassionate care for women across the lifespan, addressing comprehensive benign gynecologic needs in a collaborative academic environment. Faculty with relevant experience will have opportunities to contribute to program development, quality improvement initiatives, and clinical leadership within the Section of Comprehensive Gynecology and the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified individuals must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187550 . Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/185220": {"title": "Neuroradiology Section Chief #RAD097", "description": "Neuroradiology Section Chief #RAD097. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiology invites applications for Section Chief of Neuroradiology at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor on the School of Medicine track. This is a unique leadership opportunity to shape the future of neuroimaging at a world-class academic institution, as we launch Chicago\u2019s first freestanding facility dedicated solely to cancer care and research\u2014an $815 million state-of-the-art cancer center opening in 2027. The incoming Section Chief will play a leading role in defining the service line and developing it into a national hub for innovation and clinical excellence. The Section Chief will have the opportunity to: \u2022 Build and lead a high-performing clinical team within a supportive and collegial department. \u2022 Shape the strategic direction of neuroradiology across UChicago Medicine\u2019s expanding clinical enterprise, including regional sites and community partnerships. \u2022 Drive clinical innovation in advanced neuroimaging, including stroke imaging, functional and quantitative MRI, and image-guided spine and head & neck procedures, supported by state-of-the-art technology and strong institutional investment. \u2022 Partner closely with neurointerventional, neurology, and neurosurgery teams to further integrate imaging into complex care pathways and expand collaborative clinical programs. \u2022 Collaborate with internationally recognized programs in stroke, neuro-oncology, epilepsy, and neurodegenerative disease to advance multidisciplinary clinical and research initiatives. \u2022 Leverage strong institutional resources in data science and artificial intelligence to develop and implement advanced imaging analytics and quantitative imaging programs. \u2022 Recruit, mentor, and develop faculty while building subspecialty expertise across key areas of neuroradiology. \u2022 Integrate into a culture of shared discovery alongside our PhD Basic Sciences members. \u2022 Enhance education and mentorship in one of the nation\u2019s premier radiology training programs, with opportunities to shape curricula and foster trainee development. The Section Chief will participate in strategic planning, budgeting, faculty recruitment, and operational oversight for the Section; nurture relationships with referring providers; and contribute to institutional outreach. Clinical responsibilities include the full spectrum of neuroradiology, including brain, spine, and head & neck imaging. Protected administrative time is provided to support leadership activities. Weekly remote reading days are available, and the position includes teaching, supervision of trainees, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. Opportunities to earn additional income through a productivity-based incentive model are readily available. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is a Comprehensive Stroke Center and a Level 1 Trauma Center, with nationally recognized programs in neurology, neurosurgery, and neuro-oncology. The institution supports advanced neuroimaging across acute stroke care, epilepsy, neurodegenerative disease, and brain tumor management, with strong integration into multidisciplinary clinical care pathways. With over 80 full-time faculty, the Department of Radiology fosters innovation in basic, translational, and clinical research in a collegial and collaborative environment. A tradition of excellence in patient care, education, and research, combined with the strengths of a leading academic institution, provides the foundation for a fulfilling career as an academic neuroradiologist. Learn more at radiology.uchicago.edu. Located in the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park neighborhood\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our campus offers the best of city living in a vibrant, diverse community. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and unparalleled connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana 3) be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible, and 4) have completed Fellowship training in Neuroradiology or equivalent. We especially encourage applicants with experience in clinical operations leadership and a demonstrated ability to advance clinical, academic, and outreach missions. The University seeks a visionary leader with a collaborative spirit and a passion for patient-centered care and academic discovery To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187890 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/185165": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Spanish", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Spanish. Department of Romance Languages and Literatures. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jun 10, 2026. The Department of Romance Language and Literatures at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers in Spanish. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in various courses of the Spanish Language curriculum. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching language courses at various levels (elementary, intermediate, and/or advanced), holding regularly scheduled office hours, and the preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the department, the successful candidate may teach between one and four courses per academic year. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a Master\u2019s degree or equivalent in Spanish Language pedagogy and/or a related field, superior proficiency in Spanish and English according to ACTFL guidelines, and experience teaching Spanish at the college or post-secondary level. Applications must include: Current CV Cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience and discusses approaches to pedagogy Sample syllabus The names and contact information of three references whose recommendation may be solicited Optional Materials: Course evaluations, if available A teaching demonstration video, if available (please include a link to an externally hosted video) The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/187851 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. Review of application materials will begin July 10, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. In the event that a teaching opportunity becomes available, we will contact applicants with the academic training and experience most appropriate for our available classes. This posting will expire on March 31, 2027. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval. Only complete applications will be considered. For more information about this position, please contact Callie Manick, cpbrown@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184162": {"title": "Pediatric Cardiologist Clinical Associates #PED239", "description": "Pediatric Cardiologist Clinical Associates #PED239. Section of Pediatric Cardiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 9, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Pediatrics, Section of Cardiology, and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital are seeking part-time Clinical Associates up to 10% effort for renewable terms of up to one year. Responsibilities include outpatient clinics, inpatient consultations, procedures, and reading cardio-diagnostic testing as applies to the specialty field, as needed. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per hour. These positions are benefits ineligible. The Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance\u2019s Cardiovascular service line is among the nation\u2019s top Children\u2019s heart programs in the country. Our multidisciplinary team includes cardiologists, fellows, 3 board congenital heart surgeons, and a dedicated cardiac anesthesia service. Our cardiac surgeons perform over 400 cardiac operations per year and achieve outcomes that rank among the best in the nation (STS-CHS-Database). We also have one of the highest ambulatory clinic volumes in the country with clinic sites throughout Chicago, the Chicago suburbs, Northern Illinois, and Northern Indiana. Our cardiac program is one of the largest in the Midwest and provides expertise in echocardiography, advanced cardiac imaging CT/MRI, electrophysiology, interventional cardiac catheterization, fetal cardiology, adult congenital heart disease, Marfan Syndrome, Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy, cardiac critical care, and preventative and rehabilitative cardiology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, (2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the states of Illinois and Indiana, (3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Cardiology, and (4) have completed a fellowship in pediatric cardiology or pediatric cardiology subspecialty. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/187127 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184902": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Global Environmental Change ", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Global Environmental Change . Committee on Environment, Geography, and Urbanization. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jun 9, 2026. The University of Chicago, Division of the Social Sciences is searching for a Part-Time Lecturer in the Committee on Environment, Geography and Urbanization (CEGU) to teach in the 2027 Winter Quarter. The appointee will teach one section of Global Environmental Change, a CEGU foundations course in environmental science/critical physical geography. The appointee is expected to design their own course syllabus in consultation with CEGU\u2019s Director of Undergraduate Studies. The course will be taught on the Hyde Park Campus, twice a week at a day and time that fits both the lecturer\u2019s and program\u2019s schedule. This is a part time, non-benefits eligible position. The appointment may be renewed depending on funding and programmatic need. The course considers the genealogy of environmental transformations that shape structural inequalities in society and introduces students to the major elements of earth system science and the study of global land-use change, with particular attention to key theoretical paradigms, methodological approaches, and forms of environmental and spatial data. Instructors for this course will have expertise and teaching experience in environmental or earth science, physical geography, or related fields with attention to socio-environmental relationships. Based in the Division of Social Sciences at the University of Chicago, the Committee on Environment, Geography and Urbanization (CEGU) is an interdisciplinary platform for critical thinking, advanced research, and innovative pedagogy on the societal and spatial dimensions of climate change, biodiversity loss, and other kinds of environmental transformation. CEGU seeks to investigate and respond to the environmental challenges of our time not only through research and pedagogy on climate change but also by actively centering contemporary planetary environmental emergencies in all aspects of social research and humanistic inquiry. Key fields of research and pedagogy include urban environmental studies and sustainable urbanism; energy histories and geographies; environmental humanities; spatial and environmental media; and environmental policy, design and practice. CEGU houses the undergraduate major and minor, a new MA certificate, and a Ph.D. certificate in Environment, Geography and Urbanization. More about CEGU and the undergraduate major are available at https://cegu.uchicago.edu/undergraduate-studies/ . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess: A Ph.D. in a field of environmental science, earth science, physical geography, or a related field. We especially welcome applicants with: Experience teaching relevant coursework at the college level is required, and experience developing and teaching foundational curricula and/or electives on environmental themes. A broad theoretical and methodological foundation in environmental science, physical geography, land change science, climate science, earth system science, environmental engineering, or a closely related field, with attention to critical perspectives on the historical relationships between environmental transformation and society To be considered, applicants must apply through The University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/187936 Applicants must upload a CV, Cover Letter, Teaching Statement, Example Syllabi from Previous Courses Taught, and Contact Information for at least 3 references. The review of applications will begin 30 days after the position is open and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184724": {"title": "Assistant Professors \u2013 Cytopathologists #PAT060", "description": "Assistant Professors \u2013 Cytopathologists #PAT060. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 4, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pathology invites applications for full-time Cytopathologists at the rank of Assistant Professor. Appointees will work in the Cytopathology service and will allow for cross-coverage of other services, including the surgical/anatomic pathology subspecialty. Appointees will be based on the University of Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park campus and will report to the Vice Chair for Anatomic Pathology and the Director of Cytopathology. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of residents, fellows, and medical students. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. The Department of Pathology at the University of Chicago has a long history of excellence. Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners and will have the opportunity to collaborate widely across the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), UChicago Medicine, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. We pride ourselves on career development and provide resources and mentoring support to ensure the success of our faculty members. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate degree or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be a board-certified or board-eligible Cytopathologist. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187519 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184182": {"title": "Part-Time Clinical Associates \u2013 Maternal-Fetal Medicine \u2013 #OBG098", "description": "Part-Time Clinical Associates \u2013 Maternal-Fetal Medicine \u2013 #OBG098. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 3, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Maternal-Fetal Medicine (MFM), is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 40% effort for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will provide on campus ambulatory work only to include clinic and ultrasound coverage at Hyde Park campus. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited residency in OB/GYN, 4) have completed an accredited fellowship in MFM, and 5) be board certified or eligible in MFM by ABOG. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187142 . Applicants must upload: CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184334": {"title": "Assistant Director, Transplant Immunology and Immunogenetics Laboratory and Faculty #PAT059", "description": "Assistant Director, Transplant Immunology and Immunogenetics Laboratory and Faculty #PAT059. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 1, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pathology is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of assistant professor to serve as the Assistant Director of the Transplant Immunology and Immunogenetics Laboratory Section. The appointee will work with the laboratory director, administrative director and manager, as well technical teams to facilitate laboratory testing (e.g., troubleshooting of unexpected results); review molecular typing, antibody testing, and cross-matching results; create reports and evaluate pre-transplant and post-transplant recipient immunological risk. The Assistant Director will participate in consultation with physicians, clinical transplant meetings, and clinical consultation with the on-call team; test validations; QA and QC processes and procedures; and, among others, updates of standard operating procedures and policies. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Transplant Immunology and Immunogenetics Laboratory Section serves both the solid organ (renal, pancreas, liver, heart, and lung) as well as the hematopoietic cell transplant programs. This Laboratory is accredited by ASHI and CAP. The Transplant Immunology Laboratory Section at the University of Chicago, under the director of Dr. Yanping Huang, holds an ASHI-Approved Fellowship Program in Histocompatibility & Immunogenetics. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or PhD, 2) if MD, hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana 3) have completed a Transplant Immunology and Immunogenetics fellowship, and 4) be Board certified or eligible by the American College of Histocompatibility and Immunogenetics (ACHI). We especially welcome applicants with solid experience in donor selection, proficiency in troubleshooting in all areas of testing (next-generation sequencing, other molecular methods, antibody, and crossmatching), strong translational research interest, and relevant publications in histocompatibility, immunogenetics, and/or immunology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/187230. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, cover letter, and three references. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/184100": {"title": "Part-time (10%) Clinical Associates - Pulmonary Critical Care #MED429", "description": "Part-time (10%) Clinical Associates - Pulmonary Critical Care #MED429. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 29, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care, is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will be primarily based at Ingalls Memorial Hospital in Harvey, IL, with potential expansion of coverage to affiliated sites in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL, Orland Park, IL, and Calumet City, IL, providing inpatient and outpatient clinical care during daytime or nocturnal shifts. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per hour. These positions are benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) hold a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/187099 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183907": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Sleep Neurologists #NEU056a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Sleep Neurologists #NEU056a. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 21, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology seeks full-time Sleep Neurology faculty members at any rank. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a growing neurosciences service line, which includes the opportunity to practice at University of Chicago\u2019s expanding clinical locations both in and outside of our main campus in Hyde Park. Appointees will participate in clinical, educational, and research activities including outpatient clinic, inpatient service (may be optional if a pure outpatient position is desired), polysomnogram and home sleep study interpretations, and involvement with fellow, resident, and medical student education. Administrative and leadership roles may be available. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME accredited residency in neurology, 4) have completed an ACGME accredited sleep medicine fellowship, and 5) be board certified in neurology and board certified or board eligible in sleep medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/186942 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182988": {"title": "Academic Physicians, Pediatric Infectious Diseases PED237", "description": "Academic Physicians, Pediatric Infectious Diseases PED237. Section of Pediatric Infectious Diseases. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 20, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pediatric Infectious Diseases seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. As full-time faculty members, the appointees will contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship, with responsibilities that include clinical coverage of the inpatient consultation and teaching service, outpatient care, and mentoring of trainees. Clinical duties include providing consultative inpatient infectious disease care, managing patients with general and complex pediatric infectious diseases, and contributing to the development of clinical protocols. Limited off-hours and occasional weekend coverage are expected. The appointees will work collaboratively with other sections within the Department and across the University of Chicago Medicine system, which includes multiple inpatient and ambulatory sites. Primary clinical placement will be at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital in the Hyde Park neighborhood of Chicago, with responsibilities that may extend to other affiliated locations. Other duties include teaching and supervision of trainees and medical students, as well as participation in scholarly activity. The Section is home to a robust Pediatric Infectious Diseases fellowship program, as well as a Med-Peds Infectious Diseases fellowship, offering additional opportunities for teaching, mentorship, and participation in trainee education and career development. Opportunities are available for involvement in clinical research, clinical trials, quality improvement initiatives, and other academic pursuits. The appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners across UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is a full-service children\u2019s hospital located in the heart of Chicago and serves as the primary site for pediatric care within the University of Chicago Medicine system. The Department of Pediatrics includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers and is committed to providing superior, compassionate healthcare while fostering the next generation of leaders in child health. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have an MD or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or board eligible in Pediatric Infectious Diseases. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/186068 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183854": {"title": "Orthopaedic Hand Surgeons: Clinical Associates #ORT047", "description": "Orthopaedic Hand Surgeons: Clinical Associates #ORT047. Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 18, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Medicine is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. The appointees will have inpatient and outpatient responsibilities in the context of a career in orthopaedic hand surgery and trauma reconstruction of musculoskeletal injuries at the Hyde Park location and affiliated off sites in Crown Point, IN, Harvey, IL, Tinley Park, IL, and other sites as determined by the section. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Orthopaedic Surgery, and 4) have completed an ACGME-approved orthopaedic surgery residency or its equivalent. We especially welcome applicants who have completed an ACGME-approved fellowship or its equivalent in orthopaedic hand surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications https://apply.interfolio.com/186888 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183759": {"title": "Senior Instructional Professor (Open Rank) \u2013 Quantum Engineering", "description": "Senior Instructional Professor (Open Rank) \u2013 Quantum Engineering. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: May 14, 2026. The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering at the University of Chicago invites applications for Senior Instructional Professor (open rank). This is a full-time, career-track position beginning September 2026, and is renewable with the opportunity for progression. The selected candidate will be appointed as Assistant Senior Instructional Professor, Associate Senior Instructional Professor, or Senior Instructional Professor, with rank determined by qualifications. Depending on the rank, the appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable with the possibility for promotion. The selected candidate will be responsible for leading and providing instructional oversight of the Quantum Engineering track of the Master of Engineering (MEng) program. Instructional responsibilities will include the delivery of up to six courses during the academic year, with the courses having the primary formats of lectures, laboratories, or engineering design projects. This instructional responsibility may include up to three quarters per academic year of the MENG 26200/36200 QuantumLab laboratory course, the undergraduate Engineering Design course, and the development and delivery of new courses, depending on programmatic needs for the MEng, undergraduate, and/or PhD programs. The candidate will also be expected to keep regular office hours, contribute to non-classroom instructional or service duties, and ongoing professional development. The candidate will manage the Quantum Educational Lab (\u201cQuantumLab\u201d), a pioneering space delivering hands-on instruction in quantum systems and engineering. The candidate will be responsible for managing the capital and operating budgets of the facility, the portfolio of experiments and laboratory equipment, and the access to and utilization of the physical laboratory space. They will also be responsible for staffing and management of the technical staff that design, build, and maintain experiments for the facility. More broadly, the selected candidate will be expected to work with the PME faculty, leadership, and staff to support its graduate and undergraduate programs. Additional responsibilities beyond the classroom will include, but not be limited to: advising students on the Quantum Engineering track of the Master of Engineering program; advising College students majoring and minoring in Molecular Engineering; engaging in recruitment activities for the PME academic programs; developing, participating in, and contributing to program operations, admissions, student recruitment, alumni engagement; and/or delivering summer programming for the PME. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . A PhD in Physics, Engineering, or related field is required by the start of appointment. The following materials must be submitted through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/186802 : Cover letter, outlining the applicant\u2019s prior experience in their discipline and prior teaching or mentoring experience; Curriculum Vitae; At least one course syllabus of a course designed and taught; Course evaluations or other evidence of teaching effectiveness; Evidence of successful management of programs (or evidence of potential to do so); Teaching statement; and Contact information for at least three references. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183642": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Dance", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Dance. Committee on Theater and Performance Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: May 14, 2026. The Theater and Performance Studies program at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in the Theater and Performance Studies program, in the area of Dance Studies. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching seminar- or workshop-style courses, holding regularly scheduled office hours, and preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the program, the successful candidate may teach between one and four courses per academic year. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent in Dance, Theater, or a related discipline; a master\u2019s degree or Ph.D. is strongly preferred. Applicants must have a minimum of three years of professional experience. Applications must include: Current CV Cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience Sample syllabi The names and contact information of three references whose recommendation letters may be solicited Optional materials: Course evaluations, if available The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/186698 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. In the event that a teaching opportunity becomes available, we will contact applicants with the academic training and experience most appropriate for our available classes. This posting will expire on March 31, 2027. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval. Only complete applications will be considered. For more information about this position, please contact Vicki Walden, Theater & Performance Studies, vwalden@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183675": {"title": "Part-time Lecturer in Sound Design", "description": "Part-time Lecturer in Sound Design. Committee on Theater and Performance Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: May 14, 2026. The Theater and Performance Studies program at the University of Chicago invites applications for part-time Lecturers. The selected candidate will have part-time teaching opportunities in the Theater and Performance Studies program, in the area of Sound Design. Responsibilities include preparing and teaching seminar- or workshop-style courses, holding regularly scheduled office hours, and preparing and grading of assignments and testing materials. Depending on the curricular needs of the program, the successful candidate may teach between one and two courses per academic year. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026-27 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits eligible. Applicants must hold a bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent in Theater, Design, or a related discipline; a master\u2019s degree or Ph.D. is strongly preferred. Applicants must have a minimum of three years of professional experience. Applications must include: Current CV Cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience Sample syllabi The names and contact information of three references whose recommendation letters may be solicited Optional materials: Course evaluations, if available The materials listed above must be submitted through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment site at the following link: apply.interfolio.com/186728 . Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following initial review. In the event that a teaching opportunity becomes available, we will contact applicants with the academic training and experience most appropriate for our available classes. This posting will expire on March 31, 2027. This position is contingent upon budgetary approval. Only complete applications will be considered. For more information about this position, please contact Vicki Walden, Theater & Performance Studies, vwalden@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183441": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor - Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition #MED428", "description": "Research Assistant Professor - Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition #MED428. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: May 13, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition seeks a Research Assistant Professor who will participate in multidisciplinary collaborative research initiatives where they are expected to make significant intellectual and technical contributions and publish original work in peer review journals. They will also contribute to grant development, mentor trainees and junior investigators, and support translational research programs aimed at improving patient outcomes. The appointee will contribute to research programs in genomics/epigenomics, cancer biomarker discovery, digestive disease mechanisms, and translational clinical research. Current research will focus on digestive disease mechanisms, with the goal of improving early detection, risk stratification, and therapeutic development to enhance patient outcomes. The appointee will join a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation. Opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration are abundant, both within and outside the Biological Sciences Division. The Biological Sciences Division, other academic units of the University of Chicago, and UChicago Medicine are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a doctoral degree or equivalent degree in biochemistry, molecular biology, cancer biology, or a related discipline and 2) have completed postdoctoral training. We especially welcome applicants with expertise including translational oncology, liquid biopsy technologies, transcriptomics, epigenomics, proteomics, or organoid culture models. Publication record and ability to obtain extramural grants will be considered. To be considered, interested individuals must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/186522 . Applicants must upload: Cover letter, Curriculum vitae including bibliography, and Research statement. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183555": {"title": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine   #MED203b", "description": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED203b. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 13, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years at 10% effort. Appointees will provide direct patient care responsibilities in the Emergency Department within the UChicago Medicine Health System. These positions do not require formal teaching or scholarly activity. These positions are benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/186614 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182539": {"title": "Medical Physicists  #RON036", "description": "Medical Physicists #RON036. Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 12, 2026. The Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology in the Biological Sciences Division at the University of Chicago is searching for full-time Medical Physicist faculty members at any rank. The Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology is a growing, multi-institutional academic program with partnerships across the University of Chicago health system, including collaborations with the University of Illinois Chicago and multiple suburban and regional sites. The department continues to expand its clinical and academic footprint, supported by major investments in cancer care, research, and innovation. The Section offers a highly collaborative research environment with strong connections to Radiology, Cancer Biology, Physics, Computer Science, Biomedical Engineering, and translational oncology. The Medical Physics Section includes 20 faculty and staff physicists, along with 10 dosimetrists and support personnel, supporting a comprehensive clinical program across multiple sites. Clinical operations treat approximately 200 patients per day using a wide range of advanced and established technologies, including IMRT, IGRT, framed and frameless SRS, SBRT, HDR brachytherapy, and prostate seed implants. Equipment resources include 10 Varian TrueBeams systems, Ethos with HyperSight, Brainlab ExacTrac with 6D couch, CT simulators, Bravos HDR afterloaders, and the Eclipse treatment planning system. Appointees responsibilities include clinical service, independent and collaborative research, teaching, and mentorship of trainees and students, along with other scholarly activities. Faculty are actively engaged in the NIH T32-supported Graduate Program in Medical Physics and CAMPEP-accredited residency program. Research within the Medical Physics Section focuses on AI-enabled imaging and treatment planning, including fast and low-dose imaging, adaptive radiotherapy, motion management, auto-planning, brachytherapy innovation, and treatment response prediction, with the overarching goal of improving clinical outcomes, efficiency, and the therapeutic ratio of radiation therapy. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) Have a doctoral degree in medical physics or a closely related field, such as physics or engineering, and 2) Be eligible for American Board of Radiology certification in Therapeutic Medical Physics. We are particularly interested in candidates who will contribute to our expanding strengths in AI, advanced imaging, and computational radiation oncology. Areas of interest include but are not limited to: 1) machine learning and deep learning; 2) medical imaging, image-guided radiotherapy and adaptive radiotherapy; 3) big data analytics and computational medical physics; 4) treatment planning, dosimetry, and translational radiotherapy research. This is an outstanding opportunity to join a leading academic radiation oncology department with a strong tradition of discovery, an expanding system-wide clinical presence, and growing investment in AI, imaging, adaptive radiotherapy, and translational research. We are seeking exceptional candidates who are motivated to build innovative research programs while contributing to excellence in clinical care and education, and who are eager to help shape the future of medical physics through leadership in AI- and imaging-driven radiation oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/186567. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/183432": {"title": "Neonatology Clinical Associates #PED231", "description": "Neonatology Clinical Associates #PED231. Section of Pediatric Neonatology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 11, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide neonatal care primarily at Methodist Hospital Northlake, and Methodist Hospital Southlake, with the possibility of additional off-site community hospitals. Responsibilities include attendance at deliveries, resuscitations, codes, stabilization for transport, and care for all enhanced Level 2 and tertiary level NICU patients. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Indiana and, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. We especially welcome applicants who have cared for newborns requiring intensive care within the past five years. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/186510 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182873": {"title": "Section Chief, Behavioral Neurology #NEU068", "description": "Section Chief, Behavioral Neurology #NEU068. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 7, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurology is seeking a full-time Behavioral Neurologist at any rank to serve as Section Chief of Behavioral Neurology. The Section Chief will lead the clinical team within the Section and the Center for Comprehensive Care and Research on Memory Disorders (The Memory Center) and collaborate closely with the Healthy Aging & Alzheimer\u2019s Research Care (HAARC) Center, a multidisciplinary hub for research in aging, Alzheimer\u2019s disease, and related dementias (ADRD). The HAARC Center is dedicated to advancing discovery in neurobiologic, cognitive, genetic, lifestyle, psychosocial, and related factors that promote resilience, resistance, and health span. The Section Chief will lead a multidisciplinary team that includes neurology, psychiatry, geriatrics, nursing, and social work, while maintaining an active clinical practice. The Section Chief will guide the strategic growth of the Section that spans UChicago Medicine and NorthShore hospitals and clinics and their community programs, expand participation in ADRD-related clinical trials, recruit and mentor faculty, clinical associates, and advanced practice providers, and develop or extend a program of scholarship aligned with his/her expertise. Teaching and mentorship of medical students, residents, fellows, and faculty are core expectations of this role. This position offers an exciting leadership opportunity for an experienced physician with demonstrated strengths in clinical care, research, practice management, teaching, and faculty mentorship. We seek a dynamic, collaborative, and inclusive leader to support a highly engaged clinical team and to continue advancing high-quality, team-based care. This position may require some ambulatory practice at UChicago Medicine offsite practices. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . . Scholarly and research activities may align with and enhance existing strengths within the Biological Sciences Division, including neurobiology, computational neuroscience, genetics, neuroimaging, and immunology. The appointee will have opportunities to collaborate with the Neuroscience Institute, the Institute for Population and Precision Health, the Aging Research Program within the Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine, the Center for Neurocognitive Outcomes Improvement Research, the Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience, and other programs and institutes across the University. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neurology, and 4) have completed a least one year of postdoctoral training in behavioral neurology/cognitive disorders or related areas such as neuropsychiatry, preventive neurology, or vascular cognitive neurology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185962 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182455": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor or Research Associate Professor or Research Professor - #OBG099", "description": "Research Assistant Professor or Research Associate Professor or Research Professor - #OBG099. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: May 6, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology/Section of Gynecologic Oncology and Dr. Lengyel\u2019s Translational Research Laboratory seeks a research appointee at any rank (Research Assistant Professor or Research Associate Professor or Research Professor) whose primary activity will be experimental research in association with a faculty member or team. The appointee will contribute to research projects exploring various aspects of the biology of ovarian cancer. The applicants\u2019 publication record in experimental tumor biology and their ability to obtain extramural funding will be considered in the selection process. Academic rank and compensation are dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a PhD or MD degree in tumor biology or a related discipline 2) have completed postdoctoral training and have at least 3 years of postdoctoral experience. We especially welcome applicants with expertise in tumor biology, metastasis research, proteomics, or organoid cultures, as Dr. Lengyel\u2019s research laboratory in the Center for Integrative Science focuses on the investigation of new therapies for the treatment of ovarian cancer. His research is funded by the National Institutes of Health (NIH). In addition to clinical and research activities, Dr. Lengyel mentors residents and postdoctoral scholars in the Department of Obstetrics/Gynecology and has been recognized with Outstanding Investigator Award by the NIH. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a academic recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/185581 Applicants must upload: 1) CV including bibliography, 2) cover letter and 3) research statement. Review of complete applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182798": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Marketing ", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Marketing . Marketing. Position type: Faculty. Posted: May 4, 2026. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions in Marketing beginning in the 2027-28 academic year. We are seeking the best possible candidates without regard to subfield of specialization. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s) or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in the relevant field(s) by July 1, 2027, or soon thereafter (we require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent). (We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment. Junior candidates will be judged on potential, supported by the sum total of materials submitted with the job application. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, job market paper, and at least two letters of reference from scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate's ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. We will begin formally reviewing applications on July 7, 2026, and strongly encourage you to complete your application by then. We will continue to accept applications until April 1, 2027 . Salary Range: $225,000 - $245,000; 9 month salary This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182800": {"title": "Open Rank Full-Time Clinical Pathology Informatician and Medical Director #PAT049a", "description": "Open Rank Full-Time Clinical Pathology Informatician and Medical Director #PAT049a. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty. Posted: May 4, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pathology seeks an academic clinical pathologist and informatician to join its faculty at any rank. The appointee will be based on the University of Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park campus but may also cover off-site outpatient laboratories. Additionally, the appointee will have clinical responsibilities in clinical pathology. The appointee will work under the direct supervision of the Vice Chair, Clinical Pathology. Academic rank, track, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . We are seeking a results-driven clinical informaticist to evaluate clinical, laboratory, and pathologic data to improve Pathology departmental operations as well as help target and monitor laboratory and pathology test utilization improvements and help to focus and monitor departmental and interdepartmental quality initiatives. The appointee will be gathering and evaluating operational and testing data, developing effective departmental information dashboards, and through data, enabling the department to collaborate on targeted healthcare service improvements. Other duties may include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. In addition to critical operational and quality initiatives, there is the possibility that machine learning and predictive analytics might be developed with this data to contribute to optimized health services and quality patient care. CLINICAL INFORMATICIST RESPONSIBILITIES: Analyzing and interpreting data to identify areas that need improvement Enhancing health information systems that support the collection, sharing, standardization, and integration of departmental healthcare data Collaborating on improving standards of care, policies, and procedures Training departmental staff on the use of any developed data analytics and displays Providing technical support, optimizing security, and maintaining departmental databases as appropriate Ensuring compliance in healthcare information management regulations Documenting processes, maintaining records, and preparing reports Keeping abreast of advancements in the field of clinical informatics Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for a medical license in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) have board certification in AP/CP or CP only (with or without an additional subspecialty) through the American Board of Pathology, and 4) have the ability to carry and maintain CLIA licensure. We especially welcome applicants who are board certified or eligible in clinical informatics or have substantial data science experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185896 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182617": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor\u201a Healthy Aging, Alzheimer\u2019s, & Related Dementias Researcher, Healthy Aging & Alzheimer\u2019s Research Care (HAARC) Center #NEU065a", "description": "Research Assistant Professor\u201a Healthy Aging, Alzheimer\u2019s, & Related Dementias Researcher, Healthy Aging & Alzheimer\u2019s Research Care (HAARC) Center #NEU065a. Department of Neurology. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Apr 30, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology in the Biological Sciences Division is searching for a Research Assistant Professor to join the Healthy Aging & Alzheimer\u2019s Research Care (HAARC) Center . The HAARC Center seeks to serve as an aging, Alzheimer\u2019s disease, and related dementia (ADRD) research hub dedicated to the discovery of neurobiological, cognitive, genetic, lifestyle, psychosocial, and related factors that promote resilience, resistance, and extended health span. The Center plans to achieve these goals through multidisciplinary research, community engagement, education, and the development of new evidence-based interventions. The primary activity will be academic research in association with a faculty member or team. The appointee will develop an independent research program focused on the neurobiological and neuropathologic mechanisms underlying cognitive resilience, resistance, heterogeneity, and selective vulnerability across healthy aging and the Alzheimer\u2019s disease and related dementias (ADRD) spectrum, including within the SuperAging framework. This work will leverage existing infrastructure within the HAARC Center and may include the development and application of biomarker, molecular, quantitative, and digital neuropathology approaches, with integration of multimodal phenotypic data. The appointee will also contribute to multidisciplinary, team-based research by integrating neuropathologic data with clinical, cognitive, imaging, genetic, and biomarker measures across longitudinal cohorts. This includes participation in biospecimen-based research and efforts to harmonize tissue and laboratory protocols to enable rigorous, cross-cohort discovery. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a doctoral degree or equivalent in Biological or Biomedical Sciences or related disciplines, and 2) have completed postdoctoral training. We especially welcome applicants with expertise in neuropathologic analysis, tissue processing workflows, quality assurance procedures, and integration of neuropathologic data with other data streams. Publication record and ability to obtain extramural grants will be considered. To be considered, interested individuals must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/185722 . Applicants must upload: 1) Cover letter, 2) Curriculum vitae (including bibliography), 3) Research statement (two pages maximum), and 4) Contact information for three references. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182432": {"title": "Medical Director and Clinical Associate Position #PAT048a", "description": "Medical Director and Clinical Associate Position #PAT048a. Department of Pathology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 30, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pathology is searching for a Clinical Associate at 100% effort for a renewable term of up to two-years. The appointee will be based on the University of Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park campus but may also cover off-site outpatient laboratories and will have clinical responsibilities in clinical pathology. The position reports to the Vice Chair for Clinical Pathology. The position does not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The department of Pathology at the University of Chicago has a long history of excellence. The appointee will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners and will have the opportunity to collaborate widely across the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), UChicago Medicine, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. We pride ourselves on career development and provide resources and mentoring support to ensure the success of our faculty members. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible to hold a medical license in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) have board certification in Anatomic Pathology/Clinical Pathology (with or without an additional subspecialty) through the American Board of Pathology, and 4) have the ability to carry and maintain CLIA licensure. We especially welcome applicants with good communication skills and the ability to work as a team. Applicants with experience in administration, quality improvement, and lab operations preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185559 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182063": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor #NSU023", "description": "Research Assistant Professor #NSU023. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Apr 29, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurological Surgery in the Biological Sciences Division is searching for a Research Assistant Professor. The appointee will contribute to the scientific direction of a faculty-approved research program and lead the development and implementation of projects that advance the laboratory\u2019s translational and clinical research mission. Appointees should have the technical knowledge, expertise, experience and accomplishment in research to make meaningful, original, intellectual contributions to research programs. The appointee will lead the design, execution, and completion of approved research projects, including study design, data acquisition, statistical analysis, interpretation, and manuscript preparation. In collaboration with the principal investigator and research team, the appointee will help conceptualize and advance new research initiatives from idea generation through implementation and dissemination. The role will include conducting statistical and clinical outcomes analyses using multi-institutional and national registry datasets, identifying and developing novel research questions aligned with the laboratory\u2019s scientific priorities, and performing feasibility assessments using available datasets and resources. Appointees are expected to contribute to successful grant applications and are encouraged to apply for and develop their own funding sources in time. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The appointee will integrate large clinical datasets and registry data to support translational and clinical research, including data availability assessments and the generation of site-specific reports. Additional responsibilities will include participating in multidisciplinary laboratory meetings, initiating and maintaining research collaborations with other faculty at the University of Chicago, troubleshooting research challenges and implementing new strategies, and providing input on the organization, data handling, and interpretation of current and upcoming clinical trials. The appointee will serve as a resource for data collection and analysis; develop laboratory protocols and training on new techniques; manage, analyze, and make recommendations on complex datasets; create first drafts of scientific writing and publications, including protocols and grants and train and mentor laboratory personnel. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a doctoral degree in related field, and 2) have completed postdoctoral training. To be considered, applicants must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185207 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of complete applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182314": {"title": "Academic Dermatologists - Dermatology #MED296a", "description": "Academic Dermatologists - Dermatology #MED296a. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 27, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Dermatology, is searching for full-time Assistant Professors. Appointees will have a mix of outpatient clinical responsibilities focused on general and complex medical or cosmetic dermatology, and inpatient service at our Hyde Park campus and affiliated sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate (MD) or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or board-eligible in dermatology. We especially welcome applicants with interests in medical education and/or the ability to direct and implement a specialty program in dermatology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185446 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181958": {"title": "Clinical Professor (Part-Time) #SUR191", "description": "Clinical Professor (Part-Time) #SUR191. Department of Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Track Professor. Posted: Apr 24, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of General Surgery, is searching for a part-time (20% effort) Clinical Professor. The appointee will build a practice with a primary focus in wound care management, use of multilayer compression wraps, skin substitute grafting, negative pressure wound therapy, wound debridement and limb salvage. The practice location for this position will primarily be at UChicago Medicine Ingalls Memorial Hospital. Additionally, the appointee will focus on improving hyperbaric medicine treatments as needed. We do not expect this appointee to take calls. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible with the American Board of General Surgery or equivalent. We especially welcome applicants who have completed an accredited fellowship training in wound care and hyperbaric medicine, prior experience with complex wound management, and experience with hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT). To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185117 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/182200": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Pediatric Pulmonology #PED236", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Pediatric Pulmonology #PED236. Section of Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 22, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pulmonology, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years to join our expanding Pulmonology group. The location of services will be oriented to Hyde Park and the University of Chicago Medical Center's offsite locations in the greater Chicagoland area. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited Pulmonology training program, and 4) be Board certified or Board eligible in Pulmonology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185334 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179991": {"title": "#RAD094 Emergency Radiologists - Clinical Associates", "description": "#RAD094 Emergency Radiologists - Clinical Associates. Department of Radiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 20, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiology seeks Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will have responsibilities that include interpretation of all aspects of emergency radiology diagnostic examinations and will be joining our team of six emergency radiologists, extending an existing evening shift. Work shifts will be primarily nights and weekends; arrangements are flexible and may be fully remote. The anticipated clinical schedule is 7 days on clinical service and 14 days off clinical service. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation depends on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible. We especially welcome applicants with emergency radiology fellowship training or equivalent experience. Basic pediatric ER radiology skills are also sought. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183396 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181851": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Gynecologic Oncology Program   #MED265b", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Gynecologic Oncology Program #MED265b. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 20, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who specialize in the treatment of gynecologic cancers and related clinical research. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and at network locations. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/185030 . Applicants must upload: CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181309": {"title": "Academic Trauma Surgeons #SUR190", "description": "Academic Trauma Surgeons #SUR190. Level 1 Trauma and Acute Care Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 20, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Trauma and Acute Care Surgery, seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students in both surgical critical care and trauma fellowships, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications . These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . They will join a vibrant, diverse, and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Science Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. A tradition of excellence in patient care, basic, clinical, and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions, provides the foundation for a career as an academic physician. UChicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. Plans are underway for an additional 150-bed patient tower. New ambulatory facilities are located in Orland Park and the South Loop, and the Ingalls Health System has recently integrated into the network. The University of Chicago has established level 1 adult trauma services on its Hyde Park medical campus, supplementing the existing level 1 pediatric trauma services and the burn and complex wound center. The University of Chicago is now up for level 1 verification status for both the adult and pediatric trauma services within the American College of Surgeons (ACS) in 2027. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in surgical critical care and general surgery (or equivalent), and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship or equivalent training in trauma/critical care. We especially welcome applicants with trauma and acute care surgery experience and an interest in leadership in trauma and surgical critical care. We invite applicants committed to working in academic medicine with interests in research related to injury response, trauma care, trauma prevention, healthcare disparities, innovative approaches to urban healthcare delivery and/or other broad areas related to the mission of an academic level 1 trauma center. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/184589 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181151": {"title": "Part-time Neuro Oncology Clinical Associates #NEU066", "description": "Part-time Neuro Oncology Clinical Associates #NEU066. Department of Neurology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 17, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology is searching for part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to one year. Appointees will be based at UChicago Medicine Hyde Park and will have clinical responsibilities in Neuro-Oncology. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per clinic day. These positions are benefits ineligible. The Department of Neurology at the University of Chicago has a long history of excellence. Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty, physicians, and learners and will have the opportunity to collaborate widely across the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), UChicago Medicine, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. Appointees will join a multidisciplinary team in brain tumor therapeutics comprised of established clinicians in neurology, neurosurgery, radiation oncology, and medical oncology. Clinical responsibilities will span outpatient clinic and inpatient consult service. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited residency in neurology, 4) be board certified or board eligible in neurology, and 5) have completed a neuro-oncology fellowship and be UCNS neuro-oncology eligible or certified. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/184437. Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181294": {"title": "Part-time Clinical Associates \u2013 OB/GYN Generalist \u2013 OBG096", "description": "Part-time Clinical Associates \u2013 OB/GYN Generalist \u2013 OBG096. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 17, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of General Obstetrics and Gynecology, is searching for part-time Clinical Associates at 60% effort for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will devote their clinical time to outpatient responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and satellite sites. Clinical activities will focus on the evaluation and management of patients, minor in-office procedures, and the schedule may vary week to week based on the section\u2019s needs. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications and experience. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Before the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board-certified or eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/184574 . Applicants must upload CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications will continue until positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180503": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA) Oncology #MED426", "description": "Academic Physicians - Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA) Oncology #MED426. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 17, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and network locations. Other duties will include a focus on related clinical research and the teaching and supervision of trainees and students. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Hematology and/or Oncology. We especially welcome applicants who specialize in the care of Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA) patients with hematologic and oncologic conditions. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183853. Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180605": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Multiple Myeloma Program #MED208c", "description": "Academic Physicians - Multiple Myeloma Program #MED208c. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 17, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who specialize in the treatment of multiple myeloma. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and network locations. Other duties will include a focus on related clinical research and the teaching and supervision of trainees and students. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Hematology and/or Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183947 . Applicants must upload a cover letter and CV, including bibliography. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181503": {"title": "Faculty Neuroradiologists #RAD095", "description": "Faculty Neuroradiologists #RAD095. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 16, 2026. The Department of Radiology at the University of Chicago is expanding and seeking full-time faculty at any rank to join our Neuroradiology section. Appointees will have responsibilities in adult and pediatric neuroimaging, including brain, head and neck, and spine. Opportunities to perform and supervise minor procedures are also available. Remote clinical days are available weekly, and scheduling is designed to support personal well-being alongside clinical and academic success. Protected academic time is also provided. These roles include participation in interdisciplinary conferences and teaching of medical students, residents, and fellows. Numerous opportunities exist for collaborative research within the University and with community partners. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will be part of a vibrant academic community at UChicago Medicine, one of the world\u2019s top academic institutions. UChicago Medicine is a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center, a Comprehensive Stroke Center, and a regional referral hub for complex neurologic and ENT disorders. With over 80 full-time faculty, the Department of Radiology fosters innovation in basic, translational, and clinical research in a collegial and collaborative environment. A tradition of excellence in patient care, education, and research, combined with the strengths of a leading academic institution, provides the foundation for a fulfilling career as an academic radiologist. Learn more at radiology.uchicago.edu . Located on the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park campus\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our location offers the best of city living in a dynamic and diverse neighborhood. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Radiology, and 4) have completed a Radiology Fellowship in Neuroradiology or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/184751 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180530": {"title": "Adult Psychologists - #PBN066", "description": "Adult Psychologists - #PBN066. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank for the Adult Psychology program. Appointees will provide assessments and evidence-based interventions for adult patients presenting with psychological disorders. These positions support the evaluation and treatment of adult patients referred for behavioral health concerns, including mood and anxiety disorders, obsessive compulsive and related disorders, trauma and stressor related disorders, and other related conditions including medical conditions. Appointees will maintain an ambulatory clinical practice focused on the evidence-based assessment, diagnosis, and management of psychological disorders in an adult patient population. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a PhD or PsyD in Clinical Psychology or Counseling Psychology from an APA-accredited university-based doctoral program, 2) hold or be eligible for an Illinois license in Clinical Psychology, and 3) have completed an internship at an APA-accredited site. We especially welcome applicants with postdoctoral training and experience in evidence-based psychological treatment of adults. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/183874 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180305": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Neuroradiologists #RAD090a", "description": "Clinical Associates - Neuroradiologists #RAD090a. Department of Radiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiology is expanding and searching for full-time Clinical Associates at 100% effort for up to three-year renewable terms. Appointees will have responsibilities in adult and pediatric neuroradiology including brain, head and neck, and spine, as well as have supervisory roles in other minor procedures. Appointees will have access to a full complement of advanced imaging procedures. These positions include weekly remote workdays, with fully remote arrangements available. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine is a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center, a Comprehensive Stroke Care Center, and a referral center for numerous neurologic and otolaryngologic disorders. A more complete overview of the department is available at http://radiology.uchicago.edu . Located on the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park campus\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our location offers the best of city living in a dynamic and diverse neighborhood. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Radiology, and 4) have completed a Radiology Fellowship in Neuroradiology or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183689. Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181205": {"title": "Pediatric Cardiologist Clinical Associates #PED232", "description": "Pediatric Cardiologist Clinical Associates #PED232. Section of Pediatric Cardiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 10, 2026. The University of Chicago Department of Pediatrics, Section of Cardiology, and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital are seeking full-time Clinical Associates at 100% effort for up to one-year renewable terms. Responsibilities include outpatient clinics, inpatient consultations, procedures, and reading cardio-diagnostic testing as applies to the specialty field. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. The Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance\u2019s Cardiovascular service line is among the nation\u2019s top Children\u2019s heart programs in the country. Our multidisciplinary team includes cardiologists, fellows, 3 board congenital heart surgeons, and a dedicated cardiac anesthesia service. Our cardiac surgeons perform over 400 cardiac operations per year and achieve outcomes that rank among the best in the nation (STS-CHS-Database). We also have one of the highest ambulatory clinic volumes in the country with clinic sites throughout Chicago, the Chicago suburbs, Northern Illinois, and Northern Indiana. Our cardiac program is one of the largest in the Midwest and provides expertise in echocardiography, advanced cardiac imaging CT/MRI, electrophysiology, interventional cardiac catheterization, fetal cardiology, adult congenital heart disease, Marfan Syndrome, Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy, cardiac critical care, and preventative and rehabilitative cardiology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, (2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the states of Illinois and Indiana, (3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Cardiology, and (4) have completed a fellowship in pediatric cardiology or pediatric cardiology subspecialty. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/184491 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181241": {"title": "Neurological Surgery - Clinical Associates #NSU021", "description": "Neurological Surgery - Clinical Associates #NSU021. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurological Surgery is searching for full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to 2 years. This is an exciting opportunity to be a member of our growing neurosurgical program led by our Inaugural Chair of Neurological Surgery, Dr. Mohamad Bydon. Appointees will provide clinical care in the inpatient and outpatient setting as a neurosurgeon, including an active neurosurgical clinical practice at one of our offsite locations, including but not limited to: Crown Point, Ingalls, and Advent Hinsdale. These roles do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University also has a robust Neuroscience Institute, which brings together more than 90 neuroscientists based in academic and clinical departments across the campus into a cohesive program. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) have or be eligible for permanent medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana; and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible by The American Board of Neurological Surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/184528 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179990": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Genetics & Genomics PED180a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Genetics & Genomics PED180a. PEDS - Section of Genetics and Genomics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 10, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Genetics and Genomics, seek full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will focus on care of children in inpatient and ambulatory settings at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital and Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance. Other opportunities will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a 155-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offers world-class medical care to children from around the Chicagoland area and across the globe. Appointees will join a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation, and opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration are abundant, both within and outside the Biological Sciences Division. These positions require collaborative physician leaders who will be positioned to succeed in a dynamic environment and advance the clinical, educational, and scholarly goals of the growing Section of Genetics and Genomics. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have an MD, MD PhD, or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) Board certified or Board Eligible in Clinical Genetics and Genomics. We especially welcome applicants with experience as clinical educators, those engaged in translational research in the field of Pediatric Genetics, and/or expertise in metabolic disorders. Consideration will also be given to those who have completed an ACGME-accredited Pediatric Residency in addition to Medical Genetics and Genomics Residency. To be considered, interested individuals must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which utilizes Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183397 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, cover letter, contact information for 3 references, and a personal statement that includes any projects you have worked on and your vision for an academic career. This may include research, education, and/or community involvement. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/181385": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor, Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics", "description": "Research Assistant Professor, Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Apr 9, 2026. The Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics at the University of Chicago invites applications for non tenure track appointment as Research Assistant Professor, for a three year renewable term. The selected candidate should have expertise and experience in theoretical models in labor and public economics as well as in microeconometrics and programming. This is a benefits-eligible position. The Research Assistant Professor is primarily an independent researcher conducting original research within economics and is expected to stand alone on their scholarly accomplishments, solo- and co-authoring reputable scientific publications. Appointees are expected to make a substantial contribution to that research and may be part of a research group or in association with a faculty member. Appointees are expected to actively interact with a substantial number of faculty and students and regularly participate in academic conferences and workshops. The Research Assistant Professor is expected to have an active role in obtaining and carrying out funded research and serve as a Co-Investigator or Principal Investigator on research grants. Applicants with demonstrated ability to communicate research findings clearly to audiences through oral presentations and written work are strongly preferred. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field. Applicants must apply online at the University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board at apply.interfolio.com/184650. Applications must include three reference letters, curriculum vitae, research statement and up to three published or unpublished research papers. Review of applications will begin 30 days after the posting date and will continue until the position is filled. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180279": {"title": "Assistant Professors\u201a Biomedical Informatics #PED230", "description": "Assistant Professors\u201a Biomedical Informatics #PED230. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 3, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Biomedical Informatics, is seeking full-time faculty members at the rank of assistant professor to advance the department\u2019s clinical quality improvement, operational analytics, and clinical research informatics programs. Appointees will collaborate with clinicians, quality leaders, researchers, and operational teams to develop reproducible analytic pipelines; design patient cohorts, registries, and quality metrics; and translate EHR, laboratory, and operational data into actionable insights that improve clinical care and support the department\u2019s strategic goals. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Faculty will play a key role in developing and optimizing clinical quality metrics. They will also construct and maintain sustainable informatics infrastructure, as well as design integrated reporting solutions that leverage Epic\u2019s ecosystem (e.g., Reporting Workbench, Registries, Radar dashboards, and Clarity reporting). Responsibilities will include creating and maintaining data pipelines for internal quality monitoring and external reporting requirements (such as eCQMs, accreditation metrics, and U.S. News & World Report submissions); analyzing workflows, resource utilization, throughput, and follow-up adherence; and integrating diverse clinical data sources to support decision-making at both the point-of-care and leadership levels. Additional responsibilities include teaching and supervising students, trainees, and fellows; providing methodological support for departmental research projects; and contributing to the design and evaluation of informatics tools used across the clinical enterprise. Faculty will be expected to develop and sustain a scholarly research program aligned with the missions of the Section of Biomedical Informatics and the Department of Pediatrics. Areas of focus may include biomedical informatics, clinical data modeling, interoperability standards, and methods for transforming EHR and operational data into actionable knowledge. Appointees may pursue methodological work in semantic data modeling, HL7/FHIR standard development, clinical terminology harmonization, or scalable approaches to data interoperability, as well as applied research stemming from clinical quality initiatives, operational analytics, or data-driven workflow improvement. Opportunities exist to collaborate with institutional partners including the Biological Sciences Division, the Center for Research Informatics (CRI), the Health Data Science Institute (HDSI), the Center for Personalized Therapeutics, the Pediatric Cancer Data Commons (PCDC), the Section of Genomics and Data Science within Pediatrics, and other University research initiatives such as the Institute for Translational Medicine (ITM) and the Institute for Population and Precision Health (IPPH). Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) a doctoral degree (or equivalent) in biostatistics, statistics, biomedical informatics, computer science, applied mathematics, data science, or a related field by the start of appointment and, 2) Completed postdoctoral training prior to the start of employment. We welcome applicants with experience in informatics, clinical quality analytics, advanced data modeling, EHR data extraction, or applied biostatistics. Candidates with experience developing informatics tools, EHR integrations, FHIR or interoperability solutions, and clinical-decision support workflows are strongly encouraged to apply. Preference will be given to candidates with demonstrated expertise in one or more of the following areas: analysis of large clinical datasets; development of clinical quality or operational metrics; building Epic-based clinical analytics solutions; interoperability standards (e.g., HL7, FHIR); biomarker or phenotype modeling; Bayesian or predictive modeling; or the analysis of genomics or other omics-scale data. Experience supporting clinical workflows and quality improvement initiatives are desirable. To be considered, applicants must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183666 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180472": {"title": "General Pediatricians - Clinical Associates #PED233", "description": "General Pediatricians - Clinical Associates #PED233. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 3, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Academic Pediatrics, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will have strong primary care skills and possess excellent communication skills. Direct patient care responsibilities include managing patients with general and complex pediatric needs. Limited off-hours and occasional weekend coverage are expected. Primary clinical placement is anticipated to be at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and UChicago Flossmoor Clinics and may include other locations as needed. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. The Department of Pediatrics includes more than 170 full-time faculty, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Pediatrics. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183822 . Applicants must upload: a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180511": {"title": "Reproductive Psychiatrists  #PBN069", "description": "Reproductive Psychiatrists #PBN069. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 1, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will contribute to psychiatric care of the Maternal and Women's Health program, including providing specialized care for mental health conditions associated with reproductive life stages, including pregnancy, postpartum, infertility, pregnancy loss, and menopause. Appointees will deliver medication management and, when appropriate, psychotherapy for patients experiencing mood and anxiety disorders that may be influenced or intensified by hormonal changes. These roles work closely with OB/GYN clinicians to offer coordinated, holistic care that supports maternal and reproductive mental health. They will also contribute to the continued growth of the Reproductive Psychiatry program. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Psychiatry, and 4) have residency training in Adult Psychiatry. We especially welcome applicants with experience who are also familiar with being an integral part of a multidisciplinary care team who have experience contributing to the growth and development of Women\u2019s Health Programs. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/183860 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180253": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Allergy and Immunology #PED216", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Allergy and Immunology #PED216. Section of Pediatric Allergy and Immunology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Mar 31, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Allergy and Immunology, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years to join our expanding Allergy and Immunology group. The location of services will be oriented to Hyde Park and the University of Chicago Medical Center's offsite locations in the greater Chicagoland area. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited Allergy/Immunology training program, and 4) be Board certified or Board eligible in Allergy/Immunology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/183639 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179883": {"title": "Part-time (10%) Clinical Associates \u2013 General Internal Medicine #MED425", "description": "Part-time (10%) Clinical Associates \u2013 General Internal Medicine #MED425. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Mar 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of General Internal Medicine, is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms up to three years to join an established group of General Internists. Clinical responsibilities include establishing a cancer survivorship clinic, being the primary care physician to a panel of internal medicine patients by seeing patients in our academic primary care clinic, and/or affiliated sites, as well as opportunities to attend on the inpatient general internal medicine teaching service, consult services, and/or other clinical programs. Scholarly activity and teaching are not required. These positions are benefits ineligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or eligible in Internal Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183299 . Applicants must upload a CV . Review of applications ends when these positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180117": {"title": "Instructional Professor (open rank) in Quantitative Biology MGC003c", "description": "Instructional Professor (open rank) in Quantitative Biology MGC003c. Department of Molecular Genetics and Cell Biology. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Mar 24, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Molecular Genetics and Cell Biology is accepting applications for a full-time instructional professor at any rank. The appointee will play a key role in implementing new instruction in graduate-level quantitative biology, serving the Molecular and Cell Biology (MCB) and Genetic Mechanisms and Evolution (GME) training programs, as well as other National Institutes of Health T32 training programs and associated graduate programs. The appointee will partner with MCB and GME faculty to develop and co-teach: (a) graduate-level courses that cover the fundamentals of scientific programming, quantitative data analysis, and statistics; and (b) modular short courses and workshops that cover more advanced topics in data analysis, mathematical modeling/computer simulation, and best practices in rigorous and reproducible science. The appointee will play a central role in developing, evaluating, and refining the practical hands-on components of these courses and workshops, and introducing similar practical components into existing graduate courses. Through all these roles, the appointee will be expected to provide mentorship and guidance to graduate students seeking to establish core competency in programming, computation, and statistics, or seeking advanced knowledge and mastery over quantitative methods and approaches specific to their thesis research. . The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a PhD or equivalent degree in quantitative biology or a related field, and 2) have prior university level teaching experience, either as the primary instructor or as a teaching assistant. We especially welcome applicants who have served as the primary instructor for an undergraduate or graduate course. We prefer applicants with experience in quantitative biology and who can begin in the spring/summer of 2026. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183517 . Applicants must upload: 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a teaching statement describing interests and past experience in teaching, mentoring students, developing courses or course materials, and providing instruction in quantitative approaches to biology, and 4) three letters of reference. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/180177": {"title": "Neuro-Oncologist Faculty #NEU054a", "description": "Neuro-Oncologist Faculty #NEU054a. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 23, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology is searching for full-time neuro-oncologist faculty at any rank. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a growing neurosciences service line with the opportunity to practice across University of Chicago's expanding clinical network. Practice locations will include Hyde Park \u2014 home to our new free-standing Cancer Hospital \u2014 as well as ambulatory care sites in Northwest Indiana, the south suburbs of Chicago, and downtown Chicago. In addition to clinical responsibilities spanning outpatient clinic and inpatient service, appointees will actively participate in educational and research activities, including the training of fellows, residents, and medical students. Appointees will join a multidisciplinary team in brain tumor therapeutics comprised of established clinicians in neurology, neurosurgery, radiation oncology, and medical oncology and collaborate with existing institutional expertise in oncology drug development, clinical pharmacology, immunology, and pharmacogenomics. The University is also home to the Ludwig Center for Metastasis Research and the Neurosciences Institute and the Grossman Center for Quantitative Biology and Human Behavior. In addition, appointees may also participate in other research activity such as clinical trials. Appointees will have experience in neuro-oncology clinical trials and leverage the available infrastructure and resources in the Department of Neurology and UChicago Cancer Center to successfully launch and conduct innovative clinical trials in neuro-oncology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, 3) have completed an ACGME accredited residency in neurology, 4) be board certified or board eligible in neurology, and 5) have completed a neuro-oncology fellowship and be UCNS neuro-oncology eligible or certified. We especially welcome candidates with an interest and background in neuro-oncology research. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/183634 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179251": {"title": "Part-time (10%) Clinical Associates \u2013 Endocrinology #MED424", "description": "Part-time (10%) Clinical Associates \u2013 Endocrinology #MED424. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Mar 19, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Adult and Pediatric Endocrinology, Diabetes, and Metabolism, seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to three years to join an established group of nationally known endocrinologists. The appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities primarily at our Hyde Park campus and at our network and affiliate sites. Inpatient activities will be on the endocrinology consultation service and/or the General Medicine inpatient service. These positions are not benefits eligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Endocrinology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/182742 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175383": {"title": "Neurological Surgery Faculty Position - Spine Neurosurgeon #NSU013a", "description": "Neurological Surgery Faculty Position - Spine Neurosurgeon #NSU013a. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 18, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurological Surgery is searching for a full-time faculty member at any rank. This is an exciting opportunity to be a member of our growing neurosurgical program as we welcome our Inaugural Chair, Dr. Mohamad Bydon. The appointee will provide clinical care in the inpatient and outpatient setting as a neurosurgeon specializing in the management of spinal disorders. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The appointee will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University also has a robust Neuroscience Institute, which brings together more than 90 neuroscientists based in academic and clinical departments across the campus into a cohesive program. The Department of Neurological Surgery currently has 12 full-time attending neurosurgeons with plans to expand in the coming years. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) have or be eligible for permanent medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana; 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible by The American Board of Neurological Surgery; and 4) have additional fellowship training in spine surgery or equivalent experience. We especially welcome applicants with an academic interest and/or potential for research-based program in a field relevant to clinical practice, medical education, and/or quality improvement. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179286 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179784": {"title": "Professor with Tenure - Political Science, American Politics", "description": "Professor with Tenure - Political Science, American Politics. Department of Political Science. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Mar 13, 2026. The Social Sciences Division, Department of Political Science at the University of Chicago invites applications for a tenured faculty appointment in American Politics at the rank of Professor. Candidates must have distinguished records of research accomplishment in the study of American political institutions. Applicants must also demonstrate their effectiveness in teaching and advising at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The successful candidate will also demonstrate their commitment to providing intellectual leadership to the Department and University community. This position will begin on or after July 1, 2026. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Candidates must have completed all requirements for the PhD degree prior to the start of the appointment. Applicants must apply at the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website at https://apply.interfolio.com/183202 . Applications must include: 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) teaching statement, and 4) research statement. Consideration of applications will begin on April 13, 2026 and continue until the position is filled or the search has ended."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179490": {"title": "Chief Quality Officer \u2013 Hyde Park - #BSD039", "description": "Chief Quality Officer \u2013 Hyde Park - #BSD039. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Biological Sciences Division (BSD) and the University of Chicago Medical Center (UCM) seek applicants for the role of Chief Quality Officer (CQO) for The University of Chicago Medical Center (UCMC) - Hyde Park. The appointee will serve as the UCMC Physician Patient Safety Officer who in collaboration with the Departmental Clinical Chairs ensures excellence in clinical care, quality, safety and experience outcomes. Working as a dyad partner with the UCM Vice President, Clinical Performance Excellence (CPE) this role is the physician responsible for the overall strategy, direction, and execution of clinical excellence (e.g. stewardship, outcome measures, risk reduction) for UCMC Hyde Park. The CQO will drive a culture of continuous improvement and clinical excellence, ensuring the highest standards of patient care consistent with BSD/UCM\u2019s Mission, Vision, and Values. The CQO will have an appointment in an academic department of the Biological Sciences Division at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The CQO will lead transformational change, driving internal and external performance improvements with speed and impact. The CQO will provide executive leadership in strengthening a clinical excellence culture where everyone is engaged to improve the patient experience and impact. The CQO will advance accountability and effectiveness by applying rigorous analytic, evidence-based approaches while supporting discovery, scholarship, and innovation. Working through departmental and service line quality chiefs and other designated leaders, the CQO ensures that physicians\u2019 efforts in clinical quality, safety, access, patient experience, and health equity are pursued with the highest standards of rigor and accountability. The CQO will also champion innovation in quality and safety practices and partner closely with patient experience leaders to keep patient-centered care at the core of the organization\u2019s work. In a rapidly evolving healthcare environment where quality, safety, and patient experience directly shape an organization\u2019s reputation, identity, and reimbursement, the CQO will drive improvement by strengthening performance through thoughtful risk-benefit decision-making, benchmarking against leading academic health systems and integrated delivery systems and implementing best-in-class solutions to improve quality, efficiency, and productivity. The CQO will provide visionary oversight for departmental and service line quality chiefs and the antimicrobial stewardship program, reporting to UCMC Chief Medical Officer Dr. Tipu Puri and Krista Curell, President of UCMC Hyde Park and UChicago Crown Point, and Executive Vice President and System COO. The full description of the primary functions and qualifications can be found here . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate, DO, or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be board-certified or board-eligible through the appropriate specialty board. Experience operating within a dyad physician/administrator leadership structure, with 3\u20135 years of progressive formal leadership experience in clinical quality, patient safety, or performance improvement in a large, complex academic medical center or teaching hospital is preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/182964 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a statement of interest (< 2 pages, highlighting the applicant\u2019s personal vision and relevant leadership experience). Review of complete applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/179278": {"title": "Academic Trauma Surgeons #ORT045", "description": "Academic Trauma Surgeons #ORT045. Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 10, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Medicine is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank to lead our busy Level 1 orthopaedic trauma service. The appointees will have inpatient and outpatient responsibilities in the context of a career in academic orthopaedics. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits- eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Orthopaedic Surgery, and 4) have completed an ACGME-approved orthopaedic surgery residency or its equivalent. We especially welcome applicants who have completed an ACGME-approved fellowship or its equivalent in orthopaedic traumatology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications https://apply.interfolio.com/182766 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/178661": {"title": "Director of the Duchossois Family Institute \u2013 #BSD038  ", "description": "Director of the Duchossois Family Institute \u2013 #BSD038 . Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Mar 5, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Biological Sciences Division (BSD) invites applications for a visionary leader in microbiome research to continue the legacy of scientific discovery and pioneering therapeutic applications at the Duchossois Family Institute ( henceforth referred to as \u201cThe Institute\u201d ). Leveraging the vibrant research programs, robust core platforms, and comprehensively annotated commensal strain bank developed at the Institute, the successful candidate will implement a vision for the next phase of fundamental research and therapeutic applications for this nationally eminent Institute. With 22 affiliated faculty and world-class core facilities, The Institute has emerged as a national leader for microbiome research and the first academic entity with an FDA-approved facility for the manufacture of microbiome-derived therapeutics for human use. Academic rank will be commensurate with accomplishments, with a primary faculty appointment in any of several departments within the BSD, and options for secondary appointments throughout the University of Chicago. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Institute is supported by state-of-the-art technology platforms, including gnotobiotics, metabolomics, metagenomics, and a new current Good Manufacturing Practices (cGMP) facility to manufacture live biotherapeutic products for clinical trials of microbiome optimization. The Director will articulate a vision for the Institute and lead a comprehensive investigation of the interactions among the metagenome, microbiome, immune system, and human health that builds upon these research platforms and aims to translate laboratory discoveries into novel FDA-approved clinical therapies. The Institute combines the advantages of a major academic medical center with expertise in venture creation and technology commercialization at the Polsky Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation ( https://polsky.uchicago.edu/page/about ). Experienced staff from the Polsky Center help connect the latest scientific findings with leaders in business, potential investors, and entrepreneurs. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, or PhD or equivalent, and for clinicians 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and 3) be board-certified or board-eligible through the appropriate specialty board. We seek a PhD, MD, MD/PhD or equivalent scientist with a record of creative and impactful research, visionary leadership, and experience leading academic programs and commercial partnerships. We especially encourage applications from mid-career and senior-level investigators with demonstrated leadership skills and experience leading team efforts or building programs. Likewise, scientists or physician-scientists with industry experience, or experience in venture creation or industrial partnerships are encouraged to apply. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, or PhD or equivalent, and for clinicians 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and 3) be board-certified or board-eligible through the appropriate specialty board. To be considered, interested candidates must apply online through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which utilizes Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/182203 . Applicants must upload: 1) Curriculum vitae including bibliography; 2) Statement of research interests and vision for the Institute. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/177445": {"title": "Part-time Lecturers \u2013 Biomedical Informatics #PED226", "description": "Part-time Lecturers \u2013 Biomedical Informatics #PED226. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Feb 26, 2026. The Department of Pediatrics at the University of Chicago seeks part-time lecturers to develop and teach courses in the Master of Science in Biomedical Informatics ( https://bmi.bsd.uchicago.edu ) program. These positions are renewable. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits ineligible. Demand for more sophisticated health information systems and medical research methodologies is rapidly growing due to advances in technology, volume of available health data, and policy initiatives. With a high premium placed on improved delivery of care, therapeutics and outcomes, the skills students acquire in the Master of Science in Biomedical Informatics prepare them to master the foundation of informatics and develop solutions to address these growing needs and current healthcare challenges. The program operates remotely and on a quarterly schedule, with 9 or 10 weeks of instruction per quarter. Appointees will teach from the following list of courses: \u00b7 Introduction to Biostatistics \u00b7 Introduction to R Programming \u00b7 Overview of Clinical Care Systems \u00b7 Introduction to Biomedical Informatics \u00b7 Leadership and Innovation for Informatics \u00b7 Concepts in Computer Programming \u00b7 Ethics and Policy Questions: Genomics, Healthcare and Big Data \u00b7 Introduction to Applied Data Analysis \u00b7 Advanced Concepts in Computer Programming \u00b7 Intermediate Applied Data Analysis \u00b7 HIT Integration, Interoperability Standards \u00b7 Decision Support Systems & Health Care \u00b7 Introduction to Bioinformatics \u00b7 Advanced Bioinformatics: Genome Analysis \u00b7 Geographic Information Systems and Health Information \u00b7 Population Health Informatics \u00b7 Machine Learning for Biomedical Informatics \u00b7 Capstone Proposal and Research Methods \u00b7 Capstone Implementation, Writing and Presentation Appointees will be required to develop the syllabus, synchronous class content for remote sessions, asynchronous content, assignments, and provide timely assignment feedback and grade submission(s). They will also need to manage the Canvas course site, attend all class sessions, hold weekly office hours, attend quarterly instructor meetings, participate in the capstone project process and co-curricular programming. Prior to starting employment, qualified applicants must have 1) a graduate-level degree in a relevant field and 2) have relevant experience in biomedical informatics, clinical care, medical research, or practical experience in health care related fields. Candidates with prior teaching experience and/or relevant practical experience are strongly preferred. Additionally, candidates with a PhD and/or MD are preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/181100 . Applicants must upload 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae or resume, 3) a proposed syllabus that covers the course topic in sufficient detail, 4) a teaching statement, and 5) teaching evaluations. Review of applications will end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/178338": {"title": "Director, Adult Cystic Fibrosis Program #MED416a", "description": "Director, Adult Cystic Fibrosis Program #MED416a. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 26, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary & Critical Care Medicine, is searching for a full-time faculty member to serve as the Director, Adult Cystic Fibrosis Program at any rank. The Director will have both clinical and administrative responsibilities within our established CF program, overseeing the continued clinical and research growth of the program. Clinical responsibilities will include outpatient clinic for CF at the Hyde Park campus and inpatient care in the MICU, procedure, and/or pulmonary consult services. The Director will also be responsible for mentoring junior faculty in clinical care and research in CFF and will work closely with our colleagues in bronchiectasis, lung transplantation, pediatrics, infectious disease, and other clinical departments at the University of Chicago. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical degree or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Pulmonary and Critical Care medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/181901 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the available position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/177688": {"title": "Section Chief of Musculoskeletal Radiology #RAD093", "description": "Section Chief of Musculoskeletal Radiology #RAD093. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 25, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiology invites applications for a Section Chief of Musculoskeletal Imaging at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor on the School of Medicine track. This is a unique opportunity to lead and grow a thriving Musculoskeletal Imaging program at a world-class academic institution. The University seeks a visionary leader with a collaborative spirit and a passion for patient-centered care, academic excellence, and innovation. The Section Chief will have the opportunity to: Build and lead a high-performing clinical team within a supportive and collegial department. Shape the strategic direction of MSK imaging across UChicago Medicine\u2019s expanding clinical enterprise, including regional sites and community partnerships. Drive clinical innovation, including advanced MSK imaging and image-guided interventions, supported by state-of-the-art technology and strong institutional investment. Collaborate with internationally recognized basic, clinical, and translational researchers to launch and grow academic initiatives. Enhance education and mentorship in one of the nation\u2019s premier radiology training programs, with opportunities to shape curricula and foster trainee development. The Section Chief will participate in strategic planning, budgeting, faculty recruitment, and operational oversight for the Section; nurture relationships with referring providers; and contribute to institutional outreach. Clinical responsibilities include the full spectrum of MSK imaging and procedures. Protected administrative time is provided to support leadership activities. Weekly remote reading days are available , and the position includes teaching, supervision of trainees, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. Opportunities to earn additional income through a productivity-based incentive model are readily available. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is a Level 1 Trauma Center and home to nationally recognized sports medicine, rheumatology, and orthopedic oncology programs. With over 80 full-time faculty, the Department of Radiology fosters innovation in basic, translational, and clinical research in a collegial and collaborative environment. A tradition of excellence in patient care, education, and research, combined with the strengths of a leading academic institution, provides the foundation for a fulfilling career as an academic radiologist. Learn more at radiology.uchicago.edu . Located in the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park neighborhood\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our campus offers the best of city living in a vibrant, diverse community. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and unparalleled connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible, and 4) have completed fellowship training in Musculoskeletal Radiology or equivalent. We especially encourage applicants with experience in clinical operations leadership and a demonstrated ability to advance clinical, academic, and outreach missions. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/181320 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/177728": {"title": "Part-time Lecturers \u2013 Threat and Response Management #MED423", "description": "Part-time Lecturers \u2013 Threat and Response Management #MED423. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Feb 25, 2026. The Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine at the University of Chicago seeks part-time lecturers to develop and teach courses in the Master of Science in Threat and Response Management program ( https://threatresponse.bsd.uchicago.edu/ ). These positions are renewable for up to three year terms. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits ineligible. Combining rigorous academic inquiry with emergency management practice, the program is designed to help students prepare for, respond to, and recover from crises and disaster. The face of emergency management is changing rapidly. Alongside traditional threats and disasters, governments, businesses, and communities are facing emerging challenges due to environmental threats, infrastructure protection and cybersecurity risk. The program operates in an executive, monthly, hybrid format and includes nine (9) hours of instruction one three-day weekend each month on Thursday, Friday, Saturday. Classes are scheduled during morning, afternoon, and evening hours. Courses meet for three consecutive months during each 9 or 10-week quarter for a total of 27 instructional hours. In general, the first weekend of classes each quarter will be held in person at our Hyde Park campus, and the last two weekends of each quarter will be remote via Zoom. Appointees will teach the following courses: Core Courses \u00b7 Introduction to Homeland Security and Emergency Management \u00b7 TRM Introductions: An Evolving Threat Landscape \u00b7 Intelligence Driven Decision Making \u00b7 Bureaucracy, Politics & Ethics in a Digital Age \u00b7 Threat and Response Planning & Communication \u00b7 Threat and Response Leadership Case Studies \u00b7 VUCA Risks: Infectious Disease Appointees are required to develop course syllabi, in-class and asynchronous content, assignments, and provide timely assignment feedback and submission of course grades. They are also required to manage the Canvas course site, attend all monthly in-person and remote synchronous sessions, host office hours, attend quarterly instructor meetings, participate in capstone project process, and other co-curricular programming and recruiting events. Prior to starting employment, qualified applicants must have 1) a graduate-level degree in a relevant field (PhD and/or MD preferred) and 2) have relevant experience in emergency management, homeland or national security, cybersecurity, environmental security, social sciences, public policy, public health, legal and ethical issues, or related field. Candidates with prior teaching experience and/or relevant practical experience are strongly preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/181350 . Applicants must upload: 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae or resume, 3) a proposed syllabus that covers the course topic in sufficient detail, 4) a teaching statement, and 5) recent teaching evaluations. Review of applications will end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/178593": {"title": "Primary Instructors for Summer 2026 Immersion & Summer Online Programs", "description": "Primary Instructors for Summer 2026 Immersion & Summer Online Programs. The College. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Feb 24, 2026. Primary instructors have full responsibility for teaching part or all of a course in the Immersion or Summer Online programs for pre-college (academically advanced high school) students, developed in collaboration with the Summer Session Office. These are graded courses designed specifically for academically advanced high school students ready to tackle UChicago undergraduate-level material and assignments, and feature both traditional (e.g., lecture, discussion) and experiential (e.g., labs, field trips) learning activities. Courses are taught in one of two formats: An intensive three-week format (June 15\u2013July 2 or July 7\u201323, 2026) that meets in-person Monday through Friday, from 9am to 3pm, with a lunch break from 11:30am to 1pm. No classes meet on June 19 (Juneteenth), which is a university holiday. An intensive three-week format (July 7\u201323, 2026) that is remotely taught, with synchronous class sessions held Monday through Friday, for two hours in the morning or evening, with asynchronous materials such as pre-recorded lectures made available via Canvas. Our instructional employment webpage contains the list of course offerings in which we have openings, as well as more information about our policies and processes: https://summer.uchicago.edu/summer-employment/ Your cover letter should address a specific course that is listed on the Instructional Employment page as seeking instructors, as well as your qualifications to teach that course; without this, your application will not be considered. Courses will be listed and the page will be updated, as needed. The links below provide descriptions of the Immersion and Summer Online programs; please see individual course listings for course dates, meeting times, and other details. https://summer.uchicago.edu/pre-college/pre-college/3-week-immersion/ https://summer.uchicago.edu/pre-college/pre-college/summer-online/ Bachelor\u2019s degree required; Master\u2019s or Doctoral degree preferred Prior teaching experience at the university or four-year college level required; familiarity with UChicago pedagogical practices, style, and curriculum preferred Previous experience working with high school students or in an intensive summer program preferred but not required Candidates whose University of Chicago affiliation will end before the start of summer quarter (such as graduate students planning to graduate in the spring) will need to apply as external applicants; contact summersession@uchicago.edu for more information. All hires must be approved by the Offices of the College Dean and the Provost, are conditional on having authorization to work in the United States, and are also contingent on successful completion of a background check. All pre-college instructors must complete mandatory training on Safety of Minors on Campus compliance. Instructors of remotely taught courses must be in the United States when teaching for the program, specifically in California, Colorado, District of Columbia, Illinois, Indiana, Massachusetts, Michigan, New Mexico, New York, Oregon, Utah, Virginia, or Wisconsin for tax purposes. Remote instructors must be able to perform their responsibilities from a remote location, and their remote work arrangements are their responsibility. This includes, but is not limited to, an adequate internet connection and an adequate working space. External candidates hired for this position will hold an appropriate title based on their status and affiliation at the time of appointment. For candidates hired as lecturers, t he terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026\u201327 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits-eligible. Visit our Summer Session Pre-College page to learn more about the programs and their current course offerings, particularly those in which we have openings, as listed on our instructional employment page . Make sure that you are available to teach during the scheduled dates and times posted for the course before you apply. Submit your application through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website : upload your cover letter, CV, and reference contact information for a current or previous teaching supervisor and/or department chair. Review of applications will begin March 26, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/178598": {"title": "Primary Instructors for 2026 Summer Quarter and September Term Undergraduate Course Offerings", "description": "Primary Instructors for 2026 Summer Quarter and September Term Undergraduate Course Offerings. The College. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Feb 24, 2026. Primary instructors have full responsibility for teaching an established course that is part of the regular undergraduate curriculum, developed in collaboration with the home department and Summer Session, which is offered based on departmental and student needs. Summer Quarter and September Term courses are drawn from the undergraduate curriculum of the College. They are presented in an intensive, condensed format, for a total of 27 contact hours over the course of three to eight weeks. There is no reading or exam period for summer quarter courses; final exams are given during the last class session. Classes are not held on June 19 (Juneteenth), July 3 (observance of Independence Day), or September 7 (Labor Day), all of which are University holidays. All instructors must coordinate with their co-instructors and teaching assistants, where applicable. Our instructional employment webpage contains the list of course offerings for which we have openings, as well as more information about our policies and processes: https://summer.uchicago.edu/summer-employment/ Your cover letter should address a specific course that is listed on the Instructional Employment page as seeking instructors, as well as your qualifications to teach that course; without this, your application will not be considered. Courses will be listed and the page will be updated, as needed. The links below provide descriptions of the Summer Quarter and September Term programs; please see individual course listings for course dates, meeting times, and other details. https://summer.uchicago.edu/undergraduates/make-the-most-of-summer/explore-undergraduate-courses/ Bachelor\u2019s degree required; Master\u2019s or Doctoral degree preferred Prior teaching experience at the university or four-year college level required; familiarity with UChicago pedagogical practices, style, and curriculum preferred Previous experience working in an intensive summer program preferred, but not required Candidates whose University of Chicago affiliation will end before the start of summer quarter (such as graduate students planning to graduate in the spring) will need to apply as external applicants; contact summersession@uchicago.edu for more information. All hires must be approved by the Offices of the College Dean and Provost, are conditional on having authorization to work in the United States, and are also contingent on successful completion of a background check. Instructors of remotely taught courses must be in the United States when teaching for the program, specifically in California, Colorado, District of Columbia, Illinois, Indiana, Massachusetts, Michigan, New Mexico, New York, Oregon, Utah, Virginia, or Wisconsin for tax purposes. Remote instructors must be able to perform their responsibilities from a remote location, and their remote work arrangements are their responsibility. This includes, but is not limited to, an adequate internet connection and an adequate working space. External candidates hired for this position will hold an appropriate title based on their status and affiliation at the time of appointment. For candidates hired as lecturers, t he terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the University and Service Employees International Union. The per-course salary for academic year 2026\u201327 will be at least $9,193. This position is not benefits-eligible. Visit our Summer Quarter page to learn more about the programs and their current course offerings, particularly those in which we have openings, as listed on our instructional employment page . Make sure that you are available to teach during the scheduled dates and times posted for the course before you apply. Submit your application through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website : upload your cover letter, CV, and reference contact information for a current or previous teaching supervisor and/or department chair. Review of applications will begin March 26, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/178052": {"title": "#RAD091a Academic Pediatric Radiologists ", "description": "#RAD091a Academic Pediatric Radiologists . Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 23, 2026. The Department of Radiology, Section of Pediatric Radiology at the University of Chicago is seeking full-time faculty at any rank. Appointees should have experience with all aspects of Pediatric Radiology (conventional radiography, fluoroscopy, ultrasound, CT, and MRI). Remote clinical days are available weekly, and scheduling is thoughtfully designed to support personal well-being alongside clinical and academic success. Appointees will participate in interdisciplinary conferences and teaching of medical students, residents, and fellows, and engage in scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. Opportunities to earn additional income through a productivity-based incentive model are available. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will be part of a vibrant academic community at UChicago Medicine that values both professional excellence and work-life balance. The University of Chicago Medical Center is designated as both a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and operates a nationally recognized burn unit. Comer Children's Hospital is an integrated children\u2019s hospital that offers a full complement of pediatric services with a total of 172 general pediatric, NICU and PICU beds. With over 80 full-time faculty, the Department of Radiology fosters innovation in basic, translational, and clinical research in a collegial and collaborative environment. Numerous opportunities exist for collaborative research within the University and with community partners. A tradition of excellence in patient care, education, and research, combined with the strengths of a leading academic institution, provides the foundation for a fulfilling career as an academic radiologist. Learn more at radiology.uchicago.edu . Located on the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park campus\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our location offers the best of city living in a dynamic and diverse neighborhood. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible, and 4) Fellowship training in Pediatric Radiology or equivalent To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/181640 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/178064": {"title": "Chest Imaging Radiologists #RAD092a", "description": "Chest Imaging Radiologists #RAD092a. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 23, 2026. The Department of Radiology at the University of Chicago is expanding and seeking full-time faculty at any rank to join our Cardiothoracic Radiology Section. Appointees will have responsibilities encompassing the full gamut of adult cardiothoracic imaging. This is an exceptional opportunity to join a world-class academic medical center that values both professional excellence and work-life balance. Remote clinical days are available weekly, and scheduling is thoughtfully designed to support personal well-being alongside clinical and academic success. Protected academic time and competitive base compensation are provided. Opportunities to earn additional income through a productivity-based incentive model are readily available. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will be part of a vibrant academic community at UChicago Medicine, one of the world\u2019s top academic institutions. Duties will include teaching and supervising trainees and students, as well as scholarly activity. The role includes participation in interdisciplinary conferences and teaching of medical students, residents, and fellows. Numerous opportunities exist for collaborative research within the University and with community partners, including active programs in artificial intelligence, quantitative imaging, and imaging informatics with direct clinical translation. UChicago Medicine serves as a Level 1 Trauma Center and a national referral destination for complex cardiothoracic conditions, including thoracic oncology, transplantation, and interstitial lung diseases. We are home to a highly respected lung cancer screening program. With over 80 full-time faculty, the Department of Radiology fosters innovation in basic, translational, and clinical research in a collegial and collaborative environment. A tradition of excellence in patient care, education, and research, combined with the strengths of a leading academic institution, provides the foundation for a fulfilling career as an academic radiologist. Learn more at radiology.uchicago.edu . Located on the University\u2019s historic Hyde Park campus\u2014just south of downtown Chicago and steps from Lake Michigan\u2014our location offers the best of city living in a dynamic and diverse neighborhood. Chicago is a world-class city known for its culture, cuisine, and connectivity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Radiology, and 4) have completed a Fellowship in Cardiothoracic Imaging or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/181652 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/177380": {"title": "Assistant Professors in Family Medicine #FMD020", "description": "Assistant Professors in Family Medicine #FMD020. Department of Family Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 20, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Family Medicine is seeking full-time faculty members at the rank of assistant professor. Clinical responsibilities include seeing patients in our UChicago Medicine Cottage Grove primary care clinic, as well as opportunities with other clinical programs. Educational responsibilities will include teaching students from the University of Chicago Pritzker School of Medicine and residents from the University of Chicago Family Medicine residency program. Scholarly activity can be either self-identified or aligned with established departmental interests and priorities. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or eligible in Family Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/181047 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/177250": {"title": "Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility Physicians \u2013 OBG094", "description": "Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility Physicians \u2013 OBG094. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 18, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Reproductive Endocrinology Infertility (REI), is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will be responsible for outpatient clinical care at satellite locations, including in vitro fertilization (IVF) coverage and availability to perform reproductive surgeries. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . We deliver high-quality fertility services to diverse populations. In addition to providing the full breadth of reproductive medicine and assisted reproductive technology (ART) services, we encourage and support faculty members to develop a niche expertise within the REI field. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited fellowship in REI or have equivalent experience, and 4) be Board certified or eligible in REI. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180927 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176876": {"title": "Clinical Associates Offsite \u2013 General Neurology #NEU034b", "description": "Clinical Associates Offsite \u2013 General Neurology #NEU034b. Department of Neurology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Feb 9, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology seeks General Neurology Clinical Associates with 50-100% effort for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will have primarily outpatient responsibilities for patients requiring neurological care, including evaluation, diagnosis, treatment, and longitudinal management of general neurology conditions. Clinical Associates will be expected to maintain an independent outpatient clinic and collaborate with multidisciplinary teams as appropriate. The location of services will be oriented to any of University of Chicago Medical Center's offsite locations in the greater Chicagoland area. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neurology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180604 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176331": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Palliative Medicine #MED417", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Palliative Medicine #MED417. Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 9, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Geriatrics & Palliative Medicine, is searching for full-time assistant professors to serve as an Academic Palliative Medicine Physician. Appointees will manage patients on our inpatient palliative medicine consultation service and collaborate in multidisciplinary patient care within our embedded palliative medicine clinics at our main campus or other Chicagoland locations. They may also participate in leading interdisciplinary team meetings and conduct home hospice visits. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Hospice & Palliative Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180135 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/177399": {"title": "Instructional Professors (Open Rank) in General Biology and Physiology", "description": "Instructional Professors (Open Rank) in General Biology and Physiology. Biological Sciences Collegiate Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Feb 6, 2026. The Biological Sciences Collegiate Division at the University of Chicago is accepting applications for three open-rank Instructional Professor positions . These are full-time teaching positions beginning on September 1, 2026, or as soon as possible thereafter. The appointment will be for an initial term of at least two years, with reappointment or progression possible following review. Appointment at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Instructional Professor will be considered based on the candidate\u2019s experience. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses, some designed for majors and some for non-majors, in the areas of general biology, cell biology, genetics, developmental biology, and/or physiology. Some courses include both lecture and lab components, and some are taught in partnership with university faculty. Therefore, the Instructional Professor must be able to teach a course independently and to function well as a member of a teaching team in both a lecture and laboratory teaching setting. Additional duties include hiring, training, and supervising teaching assistants. Instructional Professors of all ranks are required to engage in regular professional development. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant: $86,297-$93,200; Associate: $93,201-$100,620; Full: $100,621-$107,000 Applicants must have a PhD in Biology or a related field in hand before the start date and at least one year of teaching university-level lecture and/or laboratory courses. To apply for this position, candidates must submit their application through the University of Chicago's Interfolio job board at apply.interfolio.com/181059 . Applicants must upload the following materials with their application: 1) a cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a teaching statement which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching philosophy and goals; 4) syllabi of courses previously taught or sample syllabus of a proposed course; 5) course evaluations as the instructor of record or evidence of past teaching performance; 6) the names and contact information (including email addresses) for three references whose letters of recommendation may be solicited Review of applications will begin on March 12, 2026 , and will continue until the positions are filled or the search is closed. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176260": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor ", "description": "Research Assistant Professor . Department of Chemistry. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Feb 3, 2026. The Department of Chemistry at the University of Chicago invites applications for a Research Assistant Professor appointment. The Research Assistant Professor will conduct research in collaboration with a faculty member, their research group, and interdisciplinary partners on projects involving sleep-dependent memory consolidation, glial biology, lysosomal signaling, and advanced imaging techniques. The position will include work on C. elegans and zebrafish models, integrating AI-driven behavioral analysis, optogenetics, and spectroscopic imaging to study brain plasticity. The successful candidate is expected to develop and secure funding for an independent research program aligned with these themes and contribute to collaborative projects within the department. The term of the appointment will be for up to three years with the possibility of renewal. The position is expected to begin on or around Spring Quarter 2026. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits guidebook . Minimum qualifications: 1. A doctoral degree in Neuroscience 2. A minimum of five years of experimental experience, beyond Ph.D. training. 3. Demonstrated ability to communicate research findings clearly to audiences through oral presentations and written work. Preferred Qualifications 1. Expertise in interdisciplinary research , such as: Neuroscience and brain plasticity. Computational approaches and AI/ML for behavioral analysis. C. elegans genetics, and its use as a model organism for neurodegeneration Advanced imaging techniques (organellar or single-synapse resolution). Strong experimental expertise in worm models of sleep, learning and memory The following materials are required: Cover letter; CV; Statement of research interests and future plans; Three confidential letters of recommendation. Applications must be submitted at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/180073 Review of applications will begin March 5, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176075": {"title": "General Neurologist Faculty #NEU063", "description": "General Neurologist Faculty #NEU063. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 2, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology is searching for full-time General Neurology faculty members at any rank. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a growing neurosciences service line, which includes the opportunity to practice at University of Chicago\u2019s expanding clinical locations both in and outside of our main campus in Hyde Park. Additionally, appointees will participate in clinical, educational and research activities, including outpatient clinic, inpatient service, and involvement with fellow, resident, and medical student education. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME accredited residency in neurology, and 4) be board certified or board eligible in neurology and with subspecialty certification if applicable. We especially welcome applicants with clinical interest or subspecialty training in headache and sleep medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179901 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175743": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Pediatric Critical Care #PED225 ", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Pediatric Critical Care #PED225 . Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 2, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Critical Care, is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank with expertise and interest in cardiac critical care. Appointees will join a thriving academic and clinical environment, providing care for critically ill infants, children, and adolescents in the Pediatric Intensive Care Unit (PICU) and participating in the Pediatric Sedation Service at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital. Other duties will include scholarly activity, and the training and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows. Appointees will be part of a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty and learners at UChicago Medicine (UCM), the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and across the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The Section of Pediatric Critical Care currently includes 16 full-time faculty members, supports a fully accredited fellowship program, and oversees a 30-bed PICU, which includes a growing population of patients with cardiac disease, congenital heart surgery, pulmonary hypertension, and advanced mechanical circulatory support. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is a designated Level I Pediatric Trauma Center, a Magnet hospital recognized for nursing excellence, and an ELSO Gold Level Center of Excellence for extracorporeal life support. Beyond the University of Chicago, the appointees will also make important contributions to the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA) Pediatric Cardiology Program, a multidisciplinary initiative comprising faculty from the Sections of Cardiology and Critical Care in the Department of Pediatrics, the Department of Cardiothoracic Surgery, the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care, and other collaborators across the University of Chicago Medicine as well as affiliated institutions Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital and Endeavor Health. This collaborative group is responsible for the diagnosis, management, and longitudinal care of children with congenital and acquired heart disease within the largest pediatrics network in Illinois. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have 1) a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Critical Care Medicine, and 4) subspecialty training or significant experience in pediatric cardiology or cardiac critical care. We especially welcome individuals with additional training or experience in Cardiac Critical Care and academic interests in cardiac intensive care, mechanical circulatory support, resuscitation and simulation, informatics, quality improvement, medical education, and health policy. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179601 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instruction on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176856": {"title": "Academic Physician \u2013 Medical Director, Transplant Hepatology #MED314a", "description": "Academic Physician \u2013 Medical Director, Transplant Hepatology #MED314a. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 2, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition is searching for a full-time Associate Professor or Professor to serve as Medical Director of Adult Liver Transplantation. The appointee will join an established group of nationally known physicians and surgeons and serve as the UNOS (United Network for Organ Sharing) administrator. In collaboration with a team of colleagues from the Transplant Center, the appointee will also be responsible for expanding a preeminent program for academic discovery and clinical care. The program is currently ACGME-accredited to train a minimum of one ACGME-accredited advanced hepatology fellow each year. Clinical responsibilities, including general hepatology and liver transplant service, will be based at the University of Chicago\u2019s main campus, with consultative services also provided at one or more sites in the Chicago area. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be BC/BE in gastroenterology, and 4) have advanced training in transplant hepatology (or equivalent experience). We welcome applicants with research interests including liver metabolism, alcohol-related liver disease, hepatobiliary neoplasia, or liver transplantation. We also welcome applicants with leadership experience in managing a complex clinical operation. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180585 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176332": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Hospice and Palliative Medicine #MED412", "description": "Clinical Associates - Hospice and Palliative Medicine #MED412. Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Feb 2, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates in Palliative Medicine for renewable terms of up to three years in our expanding network. Clinical responsibilities will include seeing patients at UChicago Medicine's Ingalls Memorial Hospital inpatient and outpatient palliative care services. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information on benefit offerings is available in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Hospice and Palliative Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180136 . Applicants must uploadd a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176795": {"title": "Professor, Catholic Studies", "description": "Professor, Catholic Studies. Divinity School. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Jan 27, 2026. The University of Chicago Divinity School invites applications for full professor position(s) in Catholic Studies, for an appointment date of July 1, 2027, or some date thereafter. The successful candidate(s) will have deep familiarity with the Catholic theological tradition. Disciplinary approach is open. Salary Range: $150,000 - $225,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Divinity School is the graduate professional school for the academic study of religion at the University of Chicago, a private research university. Faculty in the School teach graduate students in its Ph.D., M.A., M.Div., and A.M.R.S. programs as well as undergraduates in the College. Many members of the faculty hold associate appointments in humanities or social science departments and professional schools of the University. A normal teaching load is four courses per year on a quarter system. The successful candidate(s) will be an established leader in their field, with a distinguished record of publication and a demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching. Applications must be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/180537 . Review of applications will begin March 13, 2026, and will continue until the position(s) is/are filled or the search is closed. Each application must include: 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vita, 3) a teaching statement, and 4) a statement articulating future research agenda. If you have any questions, contact Sara Bigger at sfbigger@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176891": {"title": "Biological Sciences Division Pathways to Independence Instructor Appointments BSD006f", "description": "Biological Sciences Division Pathways to Independence Instructor Appointments BSD006f. Biological Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Biological Sciences Division (BSD) invites applications from PhDs and MDs (or foreign equivalents) who aspire to conduct independent research in an academic faculty position. Each Pathways to Independence Instructor will conduct a program of mentored research based on qualifications, training, academic interests, and choice of mentor. Appointment will be made at the faculty rank of Instructor for up to two years for the initial appointment and for no more than four years total in an appropriate academic department of the BSD. Appointees are eligible to apply for support by institutional career development awards or other similar funding opportunities and will be encouraged to apply for national career development awards. We especially welcome applicants whose research goals match well to those of our faculty. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . By the start of appointment, basic scientists must have completed postdoctoral training. By the start of appointment, physician-scientists must have completed clinical training, be board eligible/board certified in their field, hold a permanent Illinois medical license, and have been granted University of Chicago Medical Center medical staff privileges. Physician-scientists typically will have at least 75% protected time for research for the duration of the appointment. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180618 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, research statement (suggested length, 1-3 pages), and the names and contact information of three references. Review of applications will continue until all slots are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175959": {"title": "Clinical Associates (60% effort, 80% effort, and 100% effort) FMD019", "description": "Clinical Associates (60% effort, 80% effort, and 100% effort) FMD019. Department of Family Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Family Medicine seeks full and part-time (60% effort, 80% effort, and 100% effort) family physicians to join our thriving Cottage Grove practice. These roles focus on meeting the primary care needs of our diverse community, including University employees and families, Hyde Park, and the surrounding neighborhoods. Our practice provides all-in-one convenient care within walking distance from campus, emphasizing care across the lifespan. Physicians have the option of providing reproductive health services, osteopathic treatment, and other office-based procedures. Duties include outpatient clinical practice. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Competitive candidates will demonstrate strong teamwork and commitment to high-quality patient care. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Family Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications https://apply.interfolio.com/179790 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175765": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Pediatric Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiology #PED228", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Pediatric Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiology #PED228. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Cardiology, and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank to serve as Pediatric Heart Failure and Transplant Cardiologists. Appointees will join a thriving academic and clinical environment, with responsibilities including outpatient clinics, inpatient consultations, procedures, and cardiovascular diagnostic testing, as applicable to the specialty field. Other duties will include scholarly activity and training and supervision of trainees and students. Appointees will be part of a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty and learners at UChicago Medicine (UCM), the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and across the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The appointee will join the multi-disciplinary University of Chicago Pediatric Advanced Therapy Program, which currently has two faculty members, three dedicated Advanced Practice Providers (APPs), two nurse coordinators, a dietician, a psychologist, and a social worker. Appointees will have an opportunity to participate in all aspects of program development, clinical research, and serve on multiple national committees. Beyond the University of Chicago, the appointee will make important contributions to the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA) Cardiovascular service line. The CCHA is a partnership between Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital, and Endeavor Health. The CCHA Heart Program is among the nation\u2019s top congenital heart disease programs in the country. The CCHA Heart Program includes 30 attending cardiologists, 10 categorical and advanced cardiology fellows, three pediatric cardio-thoracic surgeons, and a dedicated cardiac anesthesia service. CCHA cardiac surgeons perform over 500 cardiac operations per year and achieve outcomes that rank among the best in the nation (STS-CHS-Database). The program has one of the highest ambulatory clinic volumes in the country, with clinic sites throughout Chicago, the Chicago suburbs, Northern Illinois, and Northern Indiana. The cardiac program is one of the largest in the Midwest and provides expertise in echocardiography, advanced cardiac imaging CT/MRI, electrophysiology, interventional cardiac catheterization, fetal cardiology, adult congenital heart disease, Marfan Syndrome, Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy, cardiac critical care, preventative cardiology, cardio-oncology, pulmonary hypertension, and advanced therapies for heart failure. The Heart Failure team in the CCHA Cardiovascular service line consists of two dedicated attendings, three APPs, and two nurses. The proposed rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have 1) a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited fellowship in pediatric cardiology, 4) have completed a fourth year of additional training in pediatric heart failure and transplant or have significant experience in transplant, and 5) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Cardiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179621 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175949": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED223", "description": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED223. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, seeks Clinical Associates at full-time effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide neonatal care primarily at Community Hospital in Munster, IN, with the possibility of additional off-site community hospitals. Responsibilities include attendance at deliveries, resuscitations, codes, stabilization for transport, and care for all enhanced Level 2 and tertiary level NICU patients. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant community of faculty and clinical associates who are committed to delivering superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. The Department of Pediatrics strives to provide an educational and training environment that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. Our department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Indiana and, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. We especially welcome applicants who have cared for newborns requiring intensive care within the past five years. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179782 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175766": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED224", "description": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED224. Section of Pediatric Neonatology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, seeks Clinical Associates at full-time effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide neonatal care primarily at Ingalls Memorial Hospital, with the possibility of additional off-site community hospitals. Responsibilities include attendance at deliveries, resuscitations, codes, stabilization for transport, and care for all enhanced Level 2 and tertiary level NICU patients. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant community of faculty and clinical associates who are committed to delivering superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. The Department of Pediatrics strives to provide an educational and training environment that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. Our department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. We especially welcome applicants who have cared for newborns requiring intensive care within the past five years. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179622 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175724": {"title": "Cardiac Surgeons, Endeavor Health & The University of Chicago: Section of Cardiac Surgery #SUR187", "description": "Cardiac Surgeons, Endeavor Health & The University of Chicago: Section of Cardiac Surgery #SUR187. Section of Cardiac Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Cardiac Surgery, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who will serve as Cardiac Surgeons at Endeavor Health as a part of the Endeavor Health Cardiovascular Institute (CVI). The appointees will participate in all clinical activities required of a high-performing surgical division across six Endeavor Health hospitals\u2014Glenbrook Hospital (primary CVI site), Skokie Hospital, Evanston Hospital, Swedish Hospital, Highland Park Hospital, and Northwest Community Hospital. The appointees' clinical responsibilities will include performing high-complexity cases (e.g., multi-valve, re-operations, valve/CABG, aortic surgery) as well as providing surgical support for structural heart (e.g., TAVR, mitral clip), electrophysiology (e.g., lead extraction), and advanced heart failure. Compensation includes a generous fringe benefits package. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The successful candidates will be expected to support a highly ranked, high-performing cardiovascular surgery program and to help maintain a strong working relationship between the University of Chicago and Endeavor Health. The key responsibilities for these leadership opportunities include: \u00b7 Supporting and participating in all cardiovascular surgery services at six Endeavor Health hospitals, including oversight of the Glenbrook CVI, as well as building and growing specialized programs (e.g., minimally invasive valve, ECMO, aortic surgery, arrhythmia surgery, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, robotic surgery). \u00b7 Supporting strong working relationships with Vascular and Thoracic Surgery to ensure the provision of tertiary services at Evanston Hospital, including support for the Women\u2019s Hospital, as well as partnering with Cardiology. \u00b7 Participating in recruitment and retention of a strong clinical workforce, including junior surgeons, APPs, perfusion, OR staff, and ICU teams. \u00b7 Mentoring UChicago surgical fellows and other trainees rotating at Endeavor Health hospitals. \u00b7 Working with Cardiovascular Surgical Associates and other external clinical partners. \u00b7 Participating in a collaborative environment that supports clinical trials, outcomes research, and scholarly activity. \u00b7 Actively participating in philanthropic activities to support the CVI and the Division of Cardiovascular Surgery is expected. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be BC/BE by the American Board of Thoracic Surgery or equivalent; 4) have completed an ACGME accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in cardiovascular/thoracic (CVT) surgery. Preferred qualifications include: 1) accredited fellowship training with a minimum of 8-10 years post-fellowship clinical experience; 2) demonstrable leadership in program building and recognition regionally or nationally for surgical expertise; 3) familiarity with, or prior leadership in, STS 3-star programs and with experience with ECMO, short-term mechanical support (e.g., Impella), and/or LVAD care; and 4) clinical leadership experience in a multi-hospital system and/or experience navigating complex health systems. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179589. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/176252": {"title": "Clinical Associates, Endeavor Health & The University of Chicago: Section of Cardiac Surgery #SUR187", "description": "Clinical Associates, Endeavor Health & The University of Chicago: Section of Cardiac Surgery #SUR187. Section of Cardiac Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jan 26, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Cardiac Surgery, is searching for full-time Clinical Associates who will serve as Cardiac Surgeons at Endeavor Health as a part of the Endeavor Health Cardiovascular Institute (CVI). The appointees will participate in all clinical activities required of a high-performing surgical division across six Endeavor Health hospitals\u2014Glenbrook Hospital (primary CVI site), Skokie Hospital, Evanston Hospital, Swedish Hospital, Highland Park Hospital, and Northwest Community Hospital. The appointees' clinical responsibilities will include performing high-complexity cases (e.g., multi-valve, re-operations, valve/CABG, aortic surgery) as well as providing surgical support for structural heart (e.g., TAVR, mitral clip), electrophysiology (e.g., lead extraction), and advanced heart failure. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The successful candidates will be expected to support a highly-ranked, high-performing cardiovascular surgery program and aid in maintaining a strong working relationship between the University of Chicago and Endeavor Health. The key responsibilities for these opportunities include: \u00b7 Supporting and participating in all cardiovascular surgery services at six Endeavor Health hospitals, including oversight of the Glenbrook CVI, as well as building and growing specialized programs (e.g., minimally invasive valve, ECMO, aortic surgery, arrhythmia surgery, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, robotic surgery). \u00b7 Supporting strong working relationships with Vascular and Thoracic Surgery to ensure the provision of tertiary services at Evanston Hospital, including support for the Women\u2019s Hospital, as well as partnering with Cardiology. \u00b7 Participating in recruitment and retention of a strong clinical workforce, including junior surgeons, APPs, perfusion, OR staff, and ICU teams. \u00b7 Working with Cardiovascular Surgical Associates and other external clinical partners. \u00b7 Participating in a collaborative environment that supports clinical trials, outcomes research, and scholarly activity. \u00b7 Actively participating in philanthropic activities to support the CVI and the Division of Cardiovascular Surgery is expected. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be BC/BE by the American Board of Thoracic Surgery (or equivalent); 4) have completed an ACGME accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in cardiovascular/thoracic (CVT) surgery Preferred qualifications include: 1) accredited fellowship training with a minimum of 8-10 years post-fellowship clinical experience; 2) demonstrable leadership in program building and recognition regionally or nationally for surgical expertise; 3) familiarity with, or prior leadership in, STS 3-star programs and with experience with ECMO, short-term mechanical support (e.g., Impella), and/or LVAD care; and 4) clinical leadership experience in a multi-hospital system and/or experience navigating complex health systems. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/180065 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175999": {"title": "Lecturer, Part-time ", "description": "Lecturer, Part-time . Crown Family School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jan 7, 2026. The Crown Family School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice at the University of Chicago seeks qualified applicants for part-time lecturers to teach in a variety of areas related to direct social work practice with individuals, family and groups, social policy practice, social work community practice, and social service administration. We are especially interested in applicants who can teach in our daytime program. The position is part of the Service Employees International Union Local 73. The position is not benefits-eligible. The compensation for this position is $9,193 per course taught. The School offers master\u2019s degrees in social work and social sector leadership, a Ph.D., and leads an undergraduate minor in social inequality, social problems, and social change. All candidates must possess a graduate level degree and experience relevant to the teaching area. Each candidate should submit a cover letter, a curriculum vitae or resume, and the names of at least two references."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173312": {"title": "Academic Anesthesiologists #ACC074a", "description": "Academic Anesthesiologists #ACC074a. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 2, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia; Critical Care seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including but not limited to Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and our main campus in Hyde Park. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be a BC/BE eligible anesthesiologist. We especially seek applicants with fellowship training or special interest in cardiac, trauma, neuro, liver transplant, and regional anesthesia. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177413 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175636": {"title": "Breast Surgical Oncology, Clinical Associates (50% effort) #SUR186", "description": "Breast Surgical Oncology, Clinical Associates (50% effort) #SUR186. Section of General Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Dec 30, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Surgery, Section of General Surgery, seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 50% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide care for breast surgical oncology patients in both the inpatient and outpatient settings. Primary practice locations include River East, Hyde Park, and affiliated offsite locations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine, totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. The new cancer pavilion, opening in the spring of 2027, will be the only freestanding inpatient and outpatient Cancer Center in the Chicagoland area and will provide a unique opportunity to reimagine the future of cancer care and deliver true multidisciplinary, patient-centered care. New ambulatory facilities are in Orland Park, River East, Northwest Indiana, and Ingalls Memorial Hospital. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or board eligible in General Surgery or equivalent, and 4) be fellowship trained in Breast Surgery. We especially welcome applicants with experience building a robust practice in a competitive environment. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179509 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175542": {"title": "Gynecologic Oncology Faculty \u2013 #OBG095", "description": "Gynecologic Oncology Faculty \u2013 #OBG095. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 28, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Section of Gynecologic Oncology seeks full-time faculty members at the associate or assistant professor rank. The appointee will be responsible for comprehensive clinical care to gynecologic oncology patients, including advanced minimally invasive and open surgical techniques as well as medical therapies, including standard of care and therapeutic clinical trials. Appointee\u2019s clinical effort, including call duties, will be dedicated to supporting the section\u2019s practices in New Lenox and UChicago/Advent Health in Hinsdale, Illinois. For procedures and patients suitable for tertiary support, the appointee is encouraged to operate at our main campus in Hyde Park, Chicago. The University of Chicago Comprehensive Cancer Center clinical trials network currently offers a robust portfolio of Phase I-III trials at the above locations. With requisite experience, the appointee will have the opportunity to assume a leadership role in our clinical trials program. Other duties will include teaching and supervising trainees and students, as well as scholarly activity. Graduate Medical Education (GME) teaching opportunities are available at our main campus in Hyde Park, Chicago, as well as UChicago/Advent Health Hinsdale. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on academic rank and qualifications. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for faculty physicians, including health, retirement, educational benefits for family members, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified individuals must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology, and 4) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Gynecologic Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179425 Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and a cover letter. The review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175473": {"title": "Academic Neuro Ophthalmologists- #OVS026", "description": "Academic Neuro Ophthalmologists- #OVS026. Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 28, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide patient care in the area of neuro-ophthalmology. Candidates with an interest in practicing comprehensive ophthalmology, including cataract surgery, alongside a neuro-ophthalmology component of their practice, will also be considered. This will include office visits at the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine in Hyde Park, and/or other locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervising ophthalmology residents and medical students, as well as scholarly activity. We also welcome applicants with additional subspecialty training. Academic rank and compensation depend upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will contribute to the management of neurophthalmology patients, medical education, scholarship, and join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates and off-site locations. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) have completed an ophthalmology residency or its equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/179361 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175586": {"title": "Academic Pediatric Orthopaedic Surgeons #ORT044", "description": "Academic Pediatric Orthopaedic Surgeons #ORT044. Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 24, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Medicine is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. We welcome applications from individuals with expertise in Pediatric Orthopaedic Surgery and those who have demonstrated leadership as mid-career or senior orthopaedic surgeons, with the goal of identifying a leader for our section of pediatric orthopaedic surgery. These positions will encompass clinical and surgical pediatric orthopaedic care, as well as teaching and supervision of trainees and students and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits- eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Orthopaedic Surgery, 4) have completed an ACGME-approved orthopaedic surgery residency or its equivalent, and 5) have completed an ACGME-approved fellowship or its equivalent in an area directly relevant to the specialization. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179463 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/175443": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Hem/Onc   #MED210c", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Hem/Onc #MED210c. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Dec 18, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, seeks full-time oncologists for appointment as Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include seeing outpatients at the Ascension Saint Joseph Chicago location as well as inpatient coverage at the Ascension Saint Joseph and Saint Mary Chicago locations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/179338 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/174367": {"title": "Faculty - General Neurosurgeons #NSU015a", "description": "Faculty - General Neurosurgeons #NSU015a. Department of Neurological Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 18, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Neurological Surgery is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. This is an exciting opportunity to be a member of a growing neurosurgical program. These positions will primarily be located at our UChicago Medicine Hyde Park location with additional opportunities to practice at off-site locations . Appointees will provide comprehensive inpatient and outpatient neurosurgical care, participate in call coverage rotation, and collaborate with a team of neurosurgeons, neurologists, residents, and APPs. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University also has a robust Neuroscience Institute, which brings together more than 90 neuroscientists based in academic and clinical departments across the campus into a cohesive program. The Department of Neurological Surgery currently has 12 full-time attending neurosurgeons with plans to expand in the coming years. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) have or be eligible for permanent medical licensure in the State of Illinois; and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible by The American Board of Neurological Surgery. Additional fellowship training in neurological surgery or equivalent experience is preferred but not required. We especially welcome applicants with an academic interest and/or potential for research-based program in a field relevant to clinical practice, medical education, and/or quality improvement. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/178379 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/167103": {"title": "Research Focused Faculty Positions - Department of Medicine #MED194c", "description": "Research Focused Faculty Positions - Department of Medicine #MED194c. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Dec 11, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine in the Biological Sciences Division is searching for tenured or tenure-track faculty members at any rank. We seek applicants pursuing research on Genetics, Genomics, Data Science, Cancer Biology, Metabolism, Diabetes, Microbiome, Immunology, and related areas. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Primary appointments will be in the Department of Medicine, although we anticipate that appointees will develop close interactions with other departments, Divisions, Schools, and institutes at the University of Chicago, Argonne National Laboratory, and/or the Marine Biological Laboratory to strengthen interdisciplinary scholarship that is interested in major questions relevant to the advancement of human health. Appointees are expected to teach at the undergraduate or graduate level. The University of Chicago is a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation, and opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration are abundant, both within and outside the Biological Sciences Division. The Division, other academic units of the University, and the University of Chicago Medical Center, are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park. Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art core facilities and to outstanding colleagues and graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs within and outside the biological sciences. Competitive research space and start-up funding will be available, as will the potential for interactions with our affiliates, Argonne National Laboratory, and the Marine Biological Laboratory. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/171879 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, cover letter, and research statement. Review of complete applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/174437": {"title": "Molecular Pathologists #PAT056", "description": "Molecular Pathologists #PAT056. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 5, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pathology is searching for full-time faculty members at the rank of Assistant Professor at the Hyde Park campus. Appointees will possess the vision and expertise necessary to integrate cutting-edge diagnostic/clinical service with an innovative program in research and education. They will be involved in sign-out of a broad scope of molecular diagnostics tests and have time and resources to participate in new test development and translational research. Appointees will report to the Vice Chair for Clinical Genomics. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Pathology at the University of Chicago has a long history of excellence. Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners. They will also have the opportunity to collaborate widely across the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), UChicago Medicine, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. We pride ourselves on career development, providing resources, and mentoring support to ensure the success of our faculty members. The Molecular Diagnostics Section is CLIA includes state-of-the-art diagnostics utilizing next-generation sequencing (NGS) and other genomics platforms to support personalized clinical diagnostics in multiple areas of pathology, with a dedicated focus on oncology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be eligible for the Anatomic and/or Clinical Pathology and Molecular Genetic Pathology board examinations. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/178442 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173931": {"title": "Section Chief \u2013 Surgical Oncology #SUR185", "description": "Section Chief \u2013 Surgical Oncology #SUR185. Section of General Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 4, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Surgery is searching for a full-time associate or full professor to serve as the Section Chief, Surgical Oncology. Practice locations include both the main Hyde Park campus as well as our off-site affiliated locations throughout the UChicago Medicine Health System. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell hospital for adult inpatient care and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. The new cancer pavilion, opening in the spring of 2027, will be the only freestanding inpatient and outpatient Cancer Center in the Chicagoland area and will provide a unique opportunity to reimagine the cancer care model of the future and deliver true multidisciplinary-patient centered care. New ambulatory facilities are in Orland Park, River East, Northwest Indiana and Ingalls Memorial Hospital. The University of Chicago's Surgical Oncology program is renowned for its cutting-edge treatment of cancer through surgical interventions. The multidisciplinary team at UChicago Medicine combines expertise in surgery, oncology, genetics, and advanced imaging to deliver personalized care for complex cancer cases. Surgeons are leaders in minimally invasive and robotic techniques, striving to improve outcomes while minimizing recovery time. The program is also dedicated to pioneering research and clinical trials, offering patients access to innovative therapies and contributing to advancements in cancer care worldwide. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or board eligible in General Surgery and Complex General Surgical Oncology, and 4) be fellowship trained in Complex General Surgical Oncology. The appointee will have expertise in the management and surgical treatment, with proficiency in both open and minimally invasive reproaches, of complex upper gastrointestinal tract and hepato-pancreato-biliary cancers in both the inpatient and outpatient settings. We are seeking applicants with a demonstrated track record of progressive leadership roles and a portfolio with productive and impactful clinical, translational fundamental basic science research. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177966 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/174457": {"title": "Ultrasound Academic Physicians - Emergency Medicine #MED409", "description": "Ultrasound Academic Physicians - Emergency Medicine #MED409. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 3, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank to serve within our Ultrasound program. Responsibilities include delivering exceptional patient care, teaching and mentoring trainees in point-of-care ultrasound, and engaging in the Section\u2019s ongoing clinical and scholarly activities. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. The University of Chicago Emergency Medicine residency program, established in 1972, has a rich history as one of the oldest emergency medicine programs in the country. Appointees will join an academic faculty who are active in research, medical education, simulation, international medicine, EMS/prehospital medicine and air-medical transport. The University of Chicago Medicine is a STEMI receiving hospital, a comprehensive Stroke Center, a Burn Center, an Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center, and one of the Chicago South EMS regional resource hospitals. The Adult ED has an annual volume of over 65,000 patients, and our Pediatric ED has a volume of 30,000 patients per year. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine, and 4) be ultrasound fellowship trained. We welcome applicants who possess leadership skills and a commitment to excellence in patient care, clinical operations, and education. These positions provide the opportunity to join committed faculty members and outstanding residents who are dedicated to academic excellence. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/178464 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173595": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED221", "description": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED221. Section of Pediatric Neonatology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Dec 1, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, is seeking full-time clinicians to serve as Hospitalists/Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will be primarily based at Advent Bolingbrook Hospital. Appointees will join our multidisciplinary care team and will provide direct clinical management of neonatal patients who present with a full spectrum of medical conditions at Advent Bolingbrook Hospital. We especially welcome applicants with Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) experience. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant community of faculty and clinical associates who are committed to delivering superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. The Department of Pediatrics strives to provide an educational and training environment that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. Our department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. UChicago Medicine Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is a free-standing, 165-bed children\u2019s hospital offering a full complement of pediatric services. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board-certified or board eligible in either neonatology or general pediatrics, and 4) have neonatal care experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177670 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173634": {"title": "Part-time Pathology Clinical Associates, UChicago Medicine Community Practice #PAT057", "description": "Part-time Pathology Clinical Associates, UChicago Medicine Community Practice #PAT057. Department of Pathology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Nov 25, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pathology is searching for part-time Clinical Associates at 50% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will be based at UChicago Medicine at Ingalls and will have clinical responsibilities in anatomic and clinical pathology. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Pathology at the University of Chicago has a long history of excellence. Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty, physicians, and learners and will have the opportunity to collaborate widely across the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), UChicago Medicine, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible to hold a medical license in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) have board certification in Anatomic Pathology/Clinical Pathology (with or without an additional subspecialty) through the American Board of Pathology, and 4) have the ability to carry and maintain CLIA licensure. Applicants with experience in administration, quality improvement, and lab operations are preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177704. Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173602": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED222", "description": "Clinical Associates - Neonatology #PED222. Section of Pediatric Neonatology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Nov 25, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, is seeking full-time general pediatricians to join our multidisciplinary care team at the University of Chicago Comer Children\u2019s Hospital NICU as Clinical Associates with renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide neonatal services at one or more level II nurseries in the University of Chicago Medicine system. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will collaborate with a dynamic team of neonatologists, neonatal nurse practitioners, pediatric residents, nutritionists, and neonatal pharmacists, providing care under the supervision of the attending neonatologist. Responsibilities include daily patient care activities such as pre-rounding, presenting patients, order writing, addressing clinical problems, and communicating with parents and consultants. Following skills evaluation and, if necessary, additional training and support, appointees will perform standard neonatal procedures, attend high-risk deliveries, and perform neonatal resuscitations. At our level II nurseries, appointees will attend deliveries, perform neonatal resuscitations, stabilize infants for transport, and manage infants whose acuity allows them to remain at their birth hospital. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a MD degree or equivalent, 2) have completed an ACGME-approved pediatric residency, 3) be board certified/eligible in general pediatrics, and 4) have or be eligible for permanent State of Illinois medical licensure. We especially welcome applicants with experience equivalent to one year of post-graduate work in A Level II NICU or one of higher acuity. To be considered, interested individuals must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177681 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173594": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Neonatology #PED220", "description": "Academic Physicians - Neonatology #PED220. Section of Pediatric Neonatology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, is seeking full-time faculty members at the rank of Assistant Professor. Appointees will provide direct patient care in the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) at University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital and at contracted off-site locations in both Illinois and Northwest Indiana. Appointees will also contribute to the broader mission of the Neonatology program, including teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity in basic, translational, or clinical research. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty and learners in the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, UChicago Medicine (UCM), and the University of Chicago. The Section of Neonatology comprises 15 full-time faculty members and academic staff, who attend at the 71-bed unit in Comer Children\u2019s Hospital as well as two community hospitals to provide compassionate, evidence-based treatments in nurturing, family-centered environments with state-of-the-art equipment. In partnership with the Maternal-Fetal Service, the Section operates the University of Chicago Perinatal Network with more than 10 affiliated community hospitals. Neonatology has a fully accredited fellowship program (10 trainees) and a robust neonatal nurse practitioner program. Faculty within the Section are actively involved in research supported by federal and other extramural funds. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. We especially welcome applicants with recent experience in the intensive care of newborns. Assessment of applications will consider experience administering/directing a level II, III, IV neonatal intensive care nurseries. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177668 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173585": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology #PED219", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology #PED219. Section of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation, is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank with a focus on benign hematology and sickle cell disease. Appointees will provide direct patient care to children, adolescents, and young adults with malignant and non-malignant hematologic disorders, including leukemia and lymphoma, sickle cell disease, thalassemia, and bleeding disorders. Appointees will also contribute to the broader mission of the Hematology/Oncology program, including teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity in basic, translational, or clinical research. Academic rannk and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty and learners in the Biological Sciences Division, Pritzker School of Medicine, UChicago Medicine (UCM), and the University of Chicago. The Chicago Hematologic Malignancies Program is a multidisciplinary team comprising faculty from the Section of Hematology/Oncology in Pediatrics, the Adolescent and Young Adult Oncology Program in Pediatrics and Medicine, and the Department of Pathology. The Program is an integral component of the Cancer Service Line of the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA), a partnership between UCM, Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital, and NorthShore University Health System. The CCHA is responsible for the care of the largest population of children in Illinois, with multiple sites across the region. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Hematology and/or Oncology. We are particularly interested in candidates who demonstrate clinical and academic leadership and program-building expertise, with proven abilities in clinical trials, translational science, collaboration, and mentorship. Opportunities exist for academic and clinical program leadership for appropriately qualified candidates. A competitive package will be offered, commensurate with experience and investigative focus. To be considered, interested individuals must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177662 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/173688": {"title": "Burn Surgeons #SUR130A", "description": "Burn Surgeons #SUR130A. Section of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Plastic & Reconstructive Surgery, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide burn coverage at The University of Chicago with expertise in general plastic and/or trauma reconstruction. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1. 4 billion. Plans are underway for a new trauma program and an additional 150-bed patient tower. New ambulatory facilities are located in Orland Park and the South Loop. The Ingalls Health System has recently integrated into the network. The Section of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery is committed to excellence in education, research, patient care, and community service. It has 12 full-time faculty members who attend UChicago facilities at Hyde Park, River East, Silver Cross Hospital, Ingalls Memorial Hospital, Salt Creek, and Orland Park. The Section runs the University of Chicago Burn and Complex Wound Center, which has been verified by the American Burn Association and the Committee of Trauma of the American College of Surgeons every three years for the past 15 years. Burn care at The University of Chicago is multidisciplinary - the team includes plastic surgeons, critical care physicians, physical and occupational therapists, nurses, nutritionists, pharmacist, social workers, clergy, and a nurse practitioner . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible in plastic and reconstructive surgery or general surgery, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship or equivalent training in burn reconstruction. We especially welcome applicants with active engagement in scholarly activity and teaching, as well as some experience in a level one trauma center. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses lnterfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/177755 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170746": {"title": "Academic Physician and Section Chief - Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics #PED166a", "description": "Academic Physician and Section Chief - Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics #PED166a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 3, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of associate professor or professor to serve as an academic physician and as Section Chief of Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics. In this role, the Section Chief will be responsible for enhancing the Section\u2019s clinical and academic excellence through program building, administrative leadership, and mentorship of the faculty and staff of the Section and the ambulatory clinics. The appointee will provide clinical care to pediatric patients and as Section Chief advance high-quality equitable access and strengthen institutional and community partnerships. In addition, the appointee will be expected to undertake research and scholarly activity in any area of basic, translational, or clinical research including but not limited to health outcomes, clinical trials and health interventions, health economics, population health, and/or informatics, while supporting faculty development and extramural funding. Other responsibilities include mentoring faculty, supervising students, residents, and fellows, and advancing innovative training activities. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The appointee will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago. A tradition of excellence in patient care, clinical and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions provides the strong foundation for a career as an academic physician leader. University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) and a growing health care system across the region. The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. Our department includes more than 200 faculty physicians, scientists and clinical associates. In addition to teaching medical students, residents and fellows, our faculty conduct cutting-edge translational and clinical research with strong supporting core services. The Section of Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics is dedicated to managing the unique needs of children who present with a wide range of developmental and behavioral conditions. The scope of its clinical service covers ambulatory sites located in Comer Children's Hospital and Hyde Park, and a growing number of regional sites. In addition, it has a strong relationship with Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital and with La Rabida Children\u2019s Hospital. The Section has a well-established fellowship program and a growing research program. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) have or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified in Pediatrics, and 4) be board certified in Developmental-Behavioral Pediatrics, Neurodevelopmental Disabilities, or a similar area. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/175118 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications will end when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/172769": {"title": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Quantum Science & Engineering (Experimental)", "description": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Quantum Science & Engineering (Experimental). Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 30, 2025. The University of Chicago's Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) seeks applications for a junior faculty position in the area of experimental quantum information, science and engineering. Topics of interest include, but are not limited to, quantum sensing and metrology, quantum computing, quantum communication, and materials for quantum information. The PME currently has eleven faculty members engaged in both fundamental and applied aspects of quantum science and engineering, in both theory and experiment, as well as faculty in other cutting-edge areas of science and engineering. The PME is affiliated with Argonne National Laboratory and the Center for Molecular Engineering at Argonne, and joint appointments are encouraged when appropriate. All successful candidates will be expected to establish and maintain a robust research program and teach at the graduate and undergraduate levels. This position is part of a cluster hire in quantum science at the University of Chicago; the PME also invites applications for a junior faculty position in theoretical quantum information, science and engineering. For more information, please see: https://apply.interfolio.com/176924 . This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Before the start of employment, qualified applicants must hold a doctoral degree or equivalent focused on the areas of experimental quantum computing, quantum information or quantum science, or a closely-related topic. Candidates should apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website: https://apply.interfolio.com/176923 . The following materials are required: Cover letter Curriculum vitae including bibliography Research statement (a statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans) Teaching statement (a description of teaching philosophy and experience) Applicants should request three confidential letters of recommendation, to be submitted via Interfolio. The following materials are optional: Up to three published or unpublished research papers Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact Karen Jackson at kjackson10@uchicago.edu with any questions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/172770": {"title": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Quantum Science & Engineering (Theoretical)", "description": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Quantum Science & Engineering (Theoretical). Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 30, 2025. The University of Chicago's Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) seeks applications for a junior faculty position in the area of theoretical quantum information, science and engineering. Topics of interest include, but are not limited to, quantum information, quantum communication, quantum sensing, quantum error correction, quantum mechanical materials modelling, quantum optics and quantum simulation. The PME currently has eleven faculty engaged in both fundamental and applied aspects of quantum science and engineering, in both theory and experiment, as well as faculty in other cutting-edge areas of science and engineering. The PME is affiliated with Argonne National Laboratory and the Center for Molecular Engineering at Argonne, and joint appointments are encouraged when appropriate. All successful candidates will be expected to establish and maintain a robust research program and teach at the graduate and undergraduate levels. This position is part of a cluster hire in quantum science at the University of Chicago; the PME also invites applications for a junior faculty position in experimental quantum information, science and engineering. For more information, please see: https://apply.interfolio.com/176923 . This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Before the start of employment, qualified applicants must hold a doctoral degree or equivalent in a field related to theoretical and/or computational quantum science and engineering. Candidates should apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website: https://apply.interfolio.com/176924 . The following materials are required: Cover letter Curriculum vitae including bibliography Research statement (a statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans) Teaching statement (a description of teaching philosophy and experience) Applicants should request three confidential letters of recommendation, to be submitted via Interfolio. The following materials are optional: Up to three published or unpublished research papers Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact Karen Jackson at kjackson10@uchicago.edu with any questions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/172618": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pulmonary and Critical Care #MED406a", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pulmonary and Critical Care #MED406a. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 30, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will be primarily based at Ingalls Memorial Hospital in Harvey, IL, with potential expansion of coverage to affiliated sites in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL, Orland Park, IL, and Calumet City, IL, providing inpatient clinical care during nocturnal shifts. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) hold a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Internal Medicine. Preferred qualifications include past experience in the ICU as well as Board Eligible or Board Certified in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/176782 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/172307": {"title": "Academic Physicians - General Internal Medicine #MED404", "description": "Academic Physicians - General Internal Medicine #MED404. Section of General Internal Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 29, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of General Internal Medicine, is seeking full-time faculty at the rank of assistant professor or associate professor. Clinical responsibilities include seeing patients in our academic primary care clinic and/or affiliated sites, as well as opportunities to attend on the inpatient general internal medicine teaching service, consult services, and/or other clinical programs. Educational responsibilities will include teaching students from the University of Chicago Pritzker School of Medicine and residents from the University of Chicago Internal Medicine and Med-Peds residency programs. Scholarly activity can be either self-identified or aligned with established section and departmental interests and priorities. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or eligible in Internal Medicine or Internal Medicine and Pediatrics (Med/Peds). To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/176503 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/171070": {"title": "Faculty in Pediatric Genetics and Genomics #PED217", "description": "Faculty in Pediatric Genetics and Genomics #PED217. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 27, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics seeks tenure track/tenured faculty at any rank to develop an independent research program that complements the Department\u2019s growing research portfolio in genetics & genomics. The Department of Pediatrics encompasses a vibrant community of researchers committed to improving child health and well-being through discovery and innovation. Appointees will have the opportunity to collaborate with investigators across the BSD, including faculty in the Institute for Population and Precision Health, the Duchossois Family Institute, the University of Chicago Comprehensive Cancer Center, as well as with faculty at affiliates such as Argonne National Laboratory and the Marine Biological Laboratory. We especially welcome applicants with an interest in neurogenetics to compliment ongoing research in the Department. The appointees will teach undergraduate and/or graduate students and may have opportunities to teach medical students. Academic rank, tenure status, and compensation are dependent upon qualifications These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . . Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art core facilities and to outstanding colleagues and graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs within the BSD. Faculty in the Department and the Biological Sciences Division collaborate widely across their respective Departments, with other Divisions, Schools, and Institutes at the University of Chicago. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) hold a doctoral degree (PhD, MD, or equivalent) and 2) have completed postdoctoral training. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/175410 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, cover letter, research statement and teaching statement. Applications will be reviewed until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/172271": {"title": "Assistant Professor, Climate Systems Engineering initiative (CSEi)", "description": "Assistant Professor, Climate Systems Engineering initiative (CSEi). Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 24, 2025. The University of Chicago invites applications from scholars who will make significant contributions to Climate Systems Engineering. The University is building a world-leading Climate Systems Engineering initiative (CSEi) with multiple faculty hires addressing solar geoengineering, open-system carbon removal such as enhanced weathering, and interventions to limit loss of glacial ice. We seek individuals who will lead research programs that will produce significant contributions to their field and to this research initiative. Applications are welcome from all relevant academic disciplines including the physical and biological sciences, social sciences, engineering, and humanities at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor. Applications are welcome from scholars who have not previously worked on climate systems engineering and from scholars who focus on the physical or social risks of Climate Systems Engineering. Appointments will be made in an appropriate primary department based on field, with secondary appointment(s) in another unit possible. Research on Climate Systems Engineering is inherently interdisciplinary, so we seek individuals with a track record of collaboration beyond their discipline; the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a doctoral degree is required prior to appointment. The following materials are required: A cover letter, A curriculum vitae, A statement of research (up to 5 pages) that describes how you plan to integrate research on climate systems engineering into your overall research trajectory, A statement of teaching philosophy and experience, Applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation via Interfolio. The following additional materials may be requested: Up to three sample publications. Applications must be submitted through the University of Chicago academic careers website: https://apply.interfolio.com/176469 . Review of applications will begin on November 24, 2025, and will continue until all positions are filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/172203": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pulmonary and Critical Care #MED406", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pulmonary and Critical Care #MED406. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 24, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will be primarily based at Ingalls Memorial Hospital in Harvey, IL, with potential expansion of coverage to affiliated sites in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL, Orland Park, IL, and Calumet City, IL, providing inpatient and outpatient clinical care during daytime or nocturnal shifts. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) hold a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/176412 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/171604": {"title": "Senior Academic Breast Surgeon / Director, Breast Surgical Oncology Program  #SUR172", "description": "Senior Academic Breast Surgeon / Director, Breast Surgical Oncology Program #SUR172. Section of General Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 24, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of General Surgery, is searching for a full-time associate professor or professor to serve as a senior academic breast surgeon and as Director, Breast Surgical Oncology Program. The appointee will provide longitudinal inpatient and outpatient care to patients seeking breast care. Direct patient care responsibilities include managing patients with complex surgical oncological needs and limited off-hours and weekend coverage. As Director of the Breast Surgical Oncology Program, the appointee will chart the future direction and growth of Breast Surgical Oncology from an academic, clinical, and educational perspective. They will provide and oversee advanced clinical care for patients with breast diseases, including both malignant and benign conditions. They will ensure consistent and high-quality multidisciplinary patient management, working with medical oncology, radiation oncology, pathology, radiology, and plastic surgery. The appointee will lead patient evaluation, including risk assessment, and pre-surgical and post-operative care and participate in weekly multidisciplinary breast tumor boards to develop collaborative treatment plans. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 800 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. New ambulatory facilities are located in Orland Park and River East, and the Ingalls Health System is also integrated into the network. The free-standing, new Cancer Pavilion will open in the spring of 2027 and will house under one roof outpatient and inpatient activity for cancer patients and further integrate research into routine cancer patient care. The Breast Surgical Oncology Program at the University of Chicago is a nationally recognized leader in the treatment of breast cancer. The program offers comprehensive, multidisciplinary care, integrating advanced surgical techniques with the latest research and clinical trials. Faculty members are highly experienced and renowned for their expertise in breast surgery, reconstruction, and survivorship. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Surgery, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship or equivalent training in surgical oncology or breast surgical oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/176255 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/171506": {"title": " Complex Family Planning Faculty - #OBG092", "description": " Complex Family Planning Faculty - #OBG092. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank to join our team in the Section of Complex Family Planning. Faculty will provide complex abortion care and gynecologic care at our Hyde Park Campus and Ingalls Hospital. There will also be opportunities to attend the inpatient gynecology service or work on labor and delivery. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant community within UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology and the Section of Complex Family Planning foster a culture of support through mentorship and multidisciplinary collaborations. Areas of expertise within the section include Complex Family Planning, including contraception, abortion, and early pregnancy management, as well as minimally invasive surgery and adolescent gynecology. The Section runs a Complex Family Planning fellowship, and OB/GYN residents rotate through Complex Family Planning. Faculty and trainees contribute to our research mission. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board eligible or board certified in Obstetrics & Gynecology, 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited residency in Obstetrics & Gynecology, and 5) have completed an ACGME-accredited fellowship in Complex Family Planning. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/175793 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/171286": {"title": "Part-Time Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology #PED218", "description": "Part-Time Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology #PED218. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 14, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology, seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort to join our expanding Pediatric Hematology/Oncology group at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital in serving the children of the South Side of Chicago. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications and does not include benefits. This position is not benefits eligible. The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior and compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide an education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. The department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. UChicago Medicine Comer Children's Hospital is a free standing 165 bed children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services. Appointees will join the Section of Hematology/Oncology, a multidisciplinary program which collaborates with The Department of Surgery, the Department of Radiation Oncology, and the University of Chicago Comprehensive Cancer Center and affiliated sites: Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital and NorthShore University Health System. The group is responsible for the diagnosis and management of children in the largest pediatrics program in the state of Illinois, the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA). Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: 1) a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Pediatric Hematology/Oncology. We welcome applicants dedicated to working as part of a dynamic and exceptional team of pediatric specialists. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/175601 . Applicants must upload CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/171451": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry", "description": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry. Department of Chemistry. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 14, 2025. The Department of Chemistry at The University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track Assistant Professor of Chemistry positions in the research areas of Origins of Life or Inorganic Chemistry , broadly defined. Joint appointments with other departments are possible. Review of applications will begin November 15th, 2025, and will continue until all positions are filled or the search is closed. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . At the time of hire the successful candidate must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D., or the foreign equivalent, in chemistry or a related field. Applicants must apply online at https://apply.interfolio.com/175745 and upload the following required materials: a cover letter a curriculum vitae with a list of publications a succinct outline of research plans a one-page teaching statement applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation through Interfolio"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170362": {"title": "Ingalls Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED400", "description": "Ingalls Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED400. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 10, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will be primarily based at Ingalls Hospital Emergency Department in Harvey, IL, with potential expansion of coverage to affiliated Urgent Aid sites in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL, and Calumet City, IL. These positions are focused on direct patient care and do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) hold a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/174769 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170762": {"title": "Academic Physician and Section Chief - Academic Pediatrics #PED160b", "description": "Academic Physician and Section Chief - Academic Pediatrics #PED160b. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 9, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Academic Pediatrics, seeks a full-time faculty member at the rank of associate professor or full professor to serve as the Section Chief of Academic Pediatrics. In this role, the Section Chief will be responsible for enhancing the Section\u2019s clinical and academic excellence through program building, administrative leadership, and mentorship of the faculty and staff of the Section and the ambulatory clinics. The appointee will provide clinical care to pediatric patients and advancing equitable access to care at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital as well as ambulatory locations on our main campus in Hyde Park and a growing number of regional sites. In addition, the appointee will be expected to undertake research and scholarly activity in any area of basic, translational, or clinical research including but not limited to health outcomes, clinical trials and health interventions, health economics, population health, and/or informatics. Other responsibilities include teaching, mentoring, and supervising medical students, residents, and fellows as well as fostering a culture of inquiry and excellence in clinical education. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The appointee will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. A tradition of excellence in patient care, clinical and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions provides the strong foundation for a career as an academic physician leader. University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. The Section of Academic Pediatrics is dedicated to managing the unique primary care needs of newborns, children, and adolescents on the South Side of Chicago. The scope of its clinical service covers both ambulatory sites located in Comer Children's Hospital and Hyde Park, service to a FQHC, and a growing number of regional sites. Its clinical programs include pediatric primary care, Child Advocacy and Protective Services, adolescent medicine, and the Comer Mobile Community Unit. We anticipate significant growth in the Section\u2019s academic faculty in the coming years. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified in Pediatrics. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/175130 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications will end when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170369": {"title": "Urgent Aid Centers Academic Physicians \u2013 Emergency Medicine #MED403", "description": "Urgent Aid Centers Academic Physicians \u2013 Emergency Medicine #MED403. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 7, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will be primarily based at Ingalls Hospital Emergency Department in Harvey, IL, with potential expansion of coverage to affiliated Urgent Aid sites in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL, and Calumet City, IL. Duties include evaluating and treating patients who present to the ED and/or urgent aid sites, teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation will be dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a well-established program and develop an academic niche that builds upon our faculty expertise in basic and translational research, health equity and bioethics research, geriatric emergency care, global emergency medicine, medical education, prehospital medicine, aero-medical transport, and ultrasound. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board certified or board eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174773 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170786": {"title": "Clinical Associates, Off-site \u2013 Hem/Onc #MED270c", "description": "Clinical Associates, Off-site \u2013 Hem/Onc #MED270c. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 7, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, seeks full-time oncologists for appointments as Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include seeing outpatients at the University of Chicago\u2019s new state-of-the-art cancer center and micro hospital in northwest Indiana and affiliated satellite locations in northwest Indiana. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/175148 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170360": {"title": "Ingalls Medical Director \u2013 Emergency Medicine #MED399", "description": "Ingalls Medical Director \u2013 Emergency Medicine #MED399. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 6, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking a full-time faculty member at any rank to serve as Medical Director for the Emergency Department, located at Ingalls Hospital in Harvey, IL. The Medical Director provides strategic and operational leadership for the Emergency Department at Ingalls Memorial Hospital, including oversight of clinical quality, patient safety, staffing models, and faculty engagement. The Director collaborates closely with hospital and departmental leadership to advance service line growth, operational efficiency, and alignment with institutional goals. The appointee will maintain an active clinical practice within the Emergency Department, providing high-quality, patient-centered care while modeling best practices for faculty, advanced practice providers, and trainees. Other activities include teaching of trainees and students, as well as scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The appointee will join an academic faculty who are active in research, medical education, simulation, international medicine, EMS/prehospital medicine, and air-medical transport. At Ingalls Memorial Hospital, faculty also gain experience in a high-volume, community-based emergency department that emphasizes operational efficiency, population health, and close collaboration with hospitalists and specialty colleagues, complementing the academic environment at Hyde Park. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/174767 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170364": {"title": "Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Medical Director & Assistant Medical Director \u2013 Emergency Medicine #MED401", "description": "Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Medical Director & Assistant Medical Director \u2013 Emergency Medicine #MED401. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 6, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank to serve as Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Medical Director and Assistant EMS Medical Director for Region 7, which is the Illinois Department of Public Health\u2013designated EMS region covering suburban and south suburban Cook County, Will County, and surrounding areas. The appointees will join an academic faculty who are active in research, medical education, simulation, international medicine, EMS/prehospital medicine, and air-medical transport. The EMS Medical Director for Region 7 will help to oversee the region\u2019s EMS system to ensure consistent quality of care and patient safety. The role is responsible for developing and updating medical protocols, leading Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) initiatives, managing the credentialing of EMS providers and dispatchers, supervising a physician field-response program, and participating in the Section\u2019s EMS Fellowship Program. The Medical Director also directs training and education programs, including simulation-based instruction, and works closely with the Illinois Department of Public Health (IDPH) and other stakeholders to ensure compliance with state and national standards. This position plays a key role in policy development, quality assurance, and maintaining a highly skilled EMS workforce across the region. The Assistant EMS Medical Director is expected to assist the EMS Medical Director in all aspects of region 7 EMS operations and quality oversight, and work with other medical center clinical operations directors to promote an outstanding patient experience. The appointees will have clinical responsibilities at Ingalls Emergency Room, located at Ingalls Hospital in Harvey, IL, with potential expansion of coverage to affiliated Urgent Aid sites in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL, and Calumet City, IL. Clinical responsibilities include evaluating and treating patients who present to the ED. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation will be dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine; 4) complete an accredited EMS fellowship or meet specific practice pathways and pass the EMS subspecialty exam. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/174771 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/170367": {"title": "Urgent Aid Centers Medical Directors - Emergency Medicine #MED402", "description": "Urgent Aid Centers Medical Directors - Emergency Medicine #MED402. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 6, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank to serve as Medical Directors for three Urgent Aid Centers in Tinley Park, IL, Flossmoor, IL and Calumet City, IL. The Medical Directors provides strategic and operational leadership for the Urgent Aid Centers, including oversight of clinical quality, patient safety, staffing models, and provider engagement. They work collaboratively with hospital and departmental leadership to ensure seamless integration of urgent aid services within the broader emergency medicine enterprise, advancing service line growth, operational efficiency, and alignment with institutional goals. The appointees will maintain an active clinical practice within the Urgent Aid Centers, delivering high-quality, patient-centered care for a diverse patient population. The appointees model best practices for advanced practice providers and faculty physicians in the urgent care setting, emphasizing efficiency, safety, and patient experience. Other activities include teaching of trainees and students, as well as scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation will be dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Urgent Aid Centers serve as a high-access, community-based entry point to the health system, providing timely evaluation and treatment for lower-acuity conditions while ensuring seamless referral to emergency or specialty care when needed. These centers strengthen patient access, enhance care coordination, and reduce unnecessary emergency department utilization. They also offer opportunities for innovation in outpatient emergency medicine, quality improvement, and the development of best practices in urgent care delivery. The appointees will join an academic faculty who are active in research, medical education, simulation, international medicine, EMS/prehospital medicine and air-medical transport. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board certified or eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/174772 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169944": {"title": "Faculty in the Department of Microbiology  - MIC010", "description": "Faculty in the Department of Microbiology - MIC010. Department of Microbiology. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 3, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Microbiology is searching for tenured or tenure-track faculty members at all ranks. We seek applicants pursuing research in any area of microbiology or related areas, but we are particularly interested in scientists whose research programs focus on host-pathogens interactions, viral and bacterial pathogenesis, and emerging infectious diseases. We encourage applications from investigators whose research programs align with the scope and use of the Howard T. Ricketts Laboratory (HTRL) located on the Argonne National Laboratory ( https://htrl.uchicago.edu ). Other responsibilities include teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate level, with the potential of teaching in the medical school. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will have opportunities to interface with the Microbiome Center, as well as the Physical Sciences Division and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. The Department offers access to gnotobiotic animal research, flow cytometry, high-throughput genomics, and biocontainment facilities. In collaboration with the Duchossois Family Institute ( https://tinyurl.com/y739n78 ), investigators will have access to biorepositories of commensal bacterial strains, the metagenomic and metabolomic core facilities, and bioinformatic resources for large-scale data integration and analytics. The Biological Sciences Division also provides access to the state-of-the-art single-cell genomics facility with computational support. Appointees will be recommended as a member of the Committee on Microbiology and, if appropriate, of other PhD-granting committees in the Biological Sciences Division ( https://tinyurl.com/ybfczsbs ). Competitive research space and start-up funding will be available. The University of Chicago is a vibrant center of scientific discovery and innovation, and opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration are abundant, both within and outside the Biological Sciences Division. The Division, other academic units of the University, and the University of Chicago Medical Center, are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park. Prior to the start of employment, applicants must: 1) have a PhD or equivalent in microbiology , or a related field and 2) have completed postdoctoral training. To be considered, those interested must apply online at the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174404 . Applicants must upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae with bibliography, a research statement, and a teaching statement. R eview of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit https://tiny.cc/InterviolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168833": {"title": "Professor of Econometrics and Statistics", "description": "Professor of Econometrics and Statistics. Booth - Econometrics/Stats. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 1, 2025. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business at the University of Chicago is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenured positions in Econometrics and Statistics, beginning in the 2026-27 academic year. Applications are invited from individuals who have earned a PhD (or equivalent) in Econometrics and Statistics and have experience on the tenure-track faculty at a major research university. Applicants should be interested in contributing to a program that welcomes, respects, and supports a diverse community of scholars. Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment at the University. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, course evaluations from prior teaching, at least two samples of written work, a research statement, and the names of five scholars willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, research, and teaching. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169952": {"title": "Academic Physician and Director of Hematologic Malignancies \u2013 #PED140a", "description": "Academic Physician and Director of Hematologic Malignancies \u2013 #PED140a. Section of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 29, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation seeks a full-time faculty member at any rank to serve as Director of Hematologic Malignancies. In this role, the appointee will lead a Pediatric Hematologic Malignancy Program with the overarching goal of enhancing its clinical and academic excellence. The appointee will provide direct patient care to children, adolescents, and young adults with hematologic malignancies, including initial diagnosis, treatment planning, and long-term management. Other duties include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The appointee will contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship, and join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, UChicago Medicine (UCM), and the University of Chicago. The Chicago Hematologic Malignancies Program is a multidisciplinary team comprising faculty members from the Section of Hematology/Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics, the Adolescent and Young Adult Oncology Program in the Departments of Pediatrics and Medicine, the Department of Pathology, at the University of Chicago and the University of Chicago Comprehensive Cancer Center. The program is a key component of the Cancer Service Line of the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA), a partnership between UCM, Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital, and NorthShore University Health System. The CCHA, has sites of care across the region, and is responsible for the care of the largest population of children in the state of Illinois. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Hematology and/or Oncology. We are particularly interested in applicants who are accomplished program builders and leaders with proven strong clinical trials and/or translational science abilities, collaborative skills, and experienced mentors willing to pursue the strong opportunities for academic and clinical development of the program. A competitive package will be provided to the individual dependent on his/her investigative focus. Broader leadership opportunities are available for appropriately qualified candidates. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174410 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169948": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Neonatology #PED215", "description": "Academic Physicians - Neonatology #PED215. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 25, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide clinical care at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital NICU and contracted off-site locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Neonatology is committed to excellence in education, research, patient care, and community service. It has 15 full-time faculty members and other academic staff who attend at the 71-bed unit in Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and two community hospitals to provide compassionate, evidence-based treatments in nurturing, family-centered environments with state-of-the-art equipment. In collaboration with the Maternal-Fetal Service, the Section runs the University of Chicago Perinatal Network with over 10 affiliated community hospitals. The section has a fully accredited fellowship program with 10 trainees and a robust neonatal nurse practitioner program. Its faculty members are actively involved in research supported by federal and other extramural funds. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. We especially welcome applicants with experience in quality improvement, ECMO, outcomes research, or analysis of structured EMR data. Assessment of applications will also consider experience administering/directing level II, III, or IV neonatal intensive care nurseries. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174407 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169943": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [10% Effort] #ACC067b", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [10% Effort] #ACC067b. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 22, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care seeks Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical r esponsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine Hyde Park campus . These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are not benefits-eligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174403 . Applicants must upload: CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169978": {"title": "Academic Pediatric Ophthalmologists #OVS024", "description": "Academic Pediatric Ophthalmologists #OVS024. Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 22, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science seeks full-time Academic Pediatric Ophthalmologists at any faculty rank. Appointees will provide patient care in pediatric ophthalmology, including strabismus and retinopathy of prematurity; interest and experience in adult strabismus is welcome. Patient care will take place at our main ambulatory building in Hyde Park and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, and other sites as determined by the needs of the Department. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will contribute to medical and surgical management of pediatric ophthalmology patients, medical education, scholarship, and join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The Department of Ophthalmology and Visual Science has an outstanding culture of clinical care, scholarship, and collaboration, and serves the University of Chicago community, the South Side of Chicago, the Southwest Suburbs, and Northwest Indiana. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ophthalmology residency or its equivalent; and 4) have completed a pediatric ophthalmology fellowship or foreign equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174427 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169951": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Neonatology #PED048c", "description": "Academic Physicians - Neonatology #PED048c. Section of Pediatric Neonatology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 22, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neonatology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide clinical coverage at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital NICU and contracted off-site locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Neonatology is committed to excellence in education, research, patient care, and community service. It has 15 full-time faculty members and other academic staff who attend the 71-bed unit in Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and two community hospitals to provide compassionate, evidence-based treatments in nurturing, family-centered environments with state-of-the-art equipment. In collaboration with the Maternal-Fetal Service, the Section runs the University of Chicago Perinatal Network with more than 10 affiliated community hospitals. The Section has a fully accredited fellowship program with 10 trainees and a robust neonatal nurse practitioner program. Its faculty members are actively involved in research supported by federal and other extramural grants. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Neonatal-Perinatal Medicine. We especially welcome applicants with expertise and/or formal training in quality improvement, ECMO, outcomes research, or analysis of structured EMR data. Opportunities for leadership may be available to appointees with appropriate skills and experience. Assessment of applications will also consider experience administering/directing a level IV neonatal intensive care nursery in a large tertiary center. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174409 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/167628": {"title": "Neuro-Oncologist Faculty and Medical Director #NEU041a", "description": "Neuro-Oncologist Faculty and Medical Director #NEU041a. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 19, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology is searching for a full-time neuro-oncologist faculty member at any rank for the role of Medical Director of Neuro-Oncology. As Medical Director, the appointee will oversee growth in the neuro-oncology program, participate in strategic planning with the neuroscience service line leaders, and collaborate with leaders in Neurosurgery, Radiation Oncology, and Hematology/Oncology. The appointee will be responsible for maintaining and overseeing an active outpatient clinic. In collaboration with a multidisciplinary team, the appointee will draw on the environment and the mission of the Cancer Center to participate in novel research programs and lead clinical trials that will be offered to the surrounding community and beyond. Other duties include teaching and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows. It is anticipated that this position can spend time both in Hyde Park, which will be home to a new free-standing Cancer Hospital, and at other ambulatory care sites, including Northwest Indiana, south suburbs of Chicago, and downtown Chicago. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University is home to the NCI-designated Cancer Center at UChicago Medicine, the Ludwig Center for Metastasis Research, and the Neurosciences Institute and the Grossman Center for Quantitative Biology and Human Behavior. The faculty member will join the Cancer Center, and an expanding neurosciences service line, which includes the opportunity to bring neuro-oncology care to locations throughout the Chicagoland region both in and outside of our main campus in Hyde Park. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME accredited residency in neurology, 4) be board certified or board eligible in neurology, and 5) have completed a neuro-oncology fellowship and be UCNS neuro-oncology certified or eligible. We especially welcome candidates with an interest and background in neuro-oncology research to spearhead clinical and translational neuro-oncology studies, including a track record in conducting clinical trials. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/172351 . Applicants must upload a CV, cover letter, and research statement. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169705": {"title": "Clinical Associates, Robotic and Minimally Invasive Cardiac Surgery #SUR184", "description": "Clinical Associates, Robotic and Minimally Invasive Cardiac Surgery #SUR184. Section of Cardiac Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 19, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Cardiac Surgery, seeks Clinical Associates at full-time effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include primarily operating with the lead robotic surgeon and robotic cardiac team. Appointees will also be expected to take independent call for the Section and to possibly support offsite community practices, such as Northwest Indiana. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will contribute to patient care and join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. The University of Chicago Heart & Vascular Center has nationally recognized research and education programs that build on collaborations throughout the University and is active in clinical operations and quality improvement in the Medical Center. The Robotic Cardiac Surgery program at UChicago Medicine is recognized as an international leader in multispectrum and advanced robotic cardiac surgery. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Thoracic Surgery or equivalent, and 4) have completed an ACGME accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in cardiovascular/thoracic (CVT) surgery. We especially welcome applicants with cardiothoracic minimally invasive or robotic experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174196 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169533": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Transplant and Cellular Therapy #MED283a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Transplant and Cellular Therapy #MED283a. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 17, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who specialize in stem cell transplantation, cellular therapies, or cellular therapies for solid tumors, and have a robust program of clinical research in one of these areas. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and at network locations. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. We especially welcome applicants with interests in cell therapy for myeloid malignancies. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/174046 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169365": {"title": "Clinical Associates (Community) - Vascular Surgeons #SUR154A", "description": "Clinical Associates (Community) - Vascular Surgeons #SUR154A. Section of Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Sep 16, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Surgery, Section of Vascular Surgery and Endovascular Therapy, is searching for full-time Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. This position will be based primarily in community offsites (expected practice in Northwest Indiana, Mount Sinai Hospital, or other locations as needed) and may require activity at other affiliate locations. Clinical responsibilities will include a comprehensive vascular surgery clinical practice. Appointees should have a strong interest and expected career trajectory in vascular surgery and community-based practice. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Heart & Vascular Center has nationally recognized research and education programs that build on collaborations throughout the University and is active in clinical operations and quality improvement in the Medical Center. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Surgery or the foreign equivalent, and 4) have completed a fellowship in Vascular Surgery. We especially welcome applicants with expertise in endovascular therapy. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/173900 . Applicants must upload: CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169060": {"title": "Professor of Operations Management", "description": "Professor of Operations Management. Operations. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 10, 2025. The Booth School of Business at the University of Chicago is seeking to appoint an outstanding scholar to a tenured position in Operations Management, beginning in the 2026-27 academic year. Applications are invited from individuals who have a PhD (or equivalent) in Operations Management, Operations Research, Management Science, Industrial Engineering, or a related field and have experience on the tenure-track at a major research university. Successful candidates will have outstanding research abilities and will be committed to achieving excellence in teaching operations management at the MBA level. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, all publications and working papers, course evaluations from prior teaching, a research statement and the names of five scholars willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, research and teaching. We will start formally reviewing applications on December 1, 2025 and strongly encourage you to complete your application by then. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168573": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Neurohospitalists #NEU043a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Neurohospitalists #NEU043a. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 10, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who will provide direct patient care managing hospitalized patients with complex neurological sub-specialty needs. These positions will be oriented to UCM\u2019s hospitals predominantly at UChicago Medicine Hyde Park with rotations at Ingalls Memorial and UChicago Medicine Advent Health dependent on system needs and candidate preferences. These positions will also encompass academic effort, including providing education and oversight to medical students, residents, and fellows, and scholarly activity. Section leadership opportunities may be available. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Neurology or equivalent. We especially welcome applicants with training in vascular neurology, neurocritical care, or neurohospitalist fellowships. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/173189 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168706": {"title": "General Neuromuscular Faculty #NEU061", "description": "General Neuromuscular Faculty #NEU061. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 10, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Neurology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank that specialize in Electromyography (EMG) and Neuromuscular Medicine. Clinical responsibilities include outpatient clinics and EMG sessions at Hyde Park and select offsite locations in the Chicagoland area. Inpatient service opportunities are available based on interest and availability. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent on qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board certified or board eligible in Adult Neurology as well as Neuromuscular Medicine and/or Electromyography. We especially welcome applicants who are clinician educators, and those with clinical trial or research experience in neuromuscular diseases. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/173313 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/169048": {"title": "Academic Cardiologists \u2013 General Cardiology #MED256b", "description": "Academic Cardiologists \u2013 General Cardiology #MED256b. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 10, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank in the area of general and preventive cardiology. Appointees will have clinical responsibilities including outpatient clinic, reading EKGs, and inpatient ward and consultative cardiology predominantly at our main campus and, if needed, at affiliated sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Please note that this is not a cardiac imaging position, and appointees will not be expected to perform or read cardiac imaging. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, (2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the state of Illinois, and (3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Cardiology. We welcome applicants with diverse clinical backgrounds, including but not limited to women\u2019s cardiovascular health, hypertension, lipid disorders, social determinants of cardiovascular health, outcomes research, and clinical trials. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/173615 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168539": {"title": "Academic Cardiologists \u2013 Cardiac Imaging #MED227b", "description": "Academic Cardiologists \u2013 Cardiac Imaging #MED227b. MED - Section of Cardiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 8, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who specialize in Cardiac Imaging. Clinical responsibilities will include outpatient clinic and inpatient service, cardiac imaging reading, and cardiology consultation at Hyde Park locations and possible off-site locations like River East. Other duties include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified in internal medicine and cardiovascular disease, and 4) have training and experience in advanced echocardiography and/or cardiac MRI and/or cardiac CT. We especially welcome applicants with an interest in cardiac imaging research. Assessment of applications will consider training and experience in echo guidance of structural heart disease interventions and in cardiac MR/CT/PET. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses lnterfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/173159 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168532": {"title": "Professor of Finance", "description": "Professor of Finance. Finance. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Sep 3, 2025. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business at the University of Chicago is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenured positions in Finance, beginning in the 2026-27 academic year. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s) (we require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent). Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, course evaluations from prior teaching, at least two samples of written work, a research statement, and the names of five scholars willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, research, and teaching. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168430": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Hospital Medicine #PED126a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Hospital Medicine #PED126a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Sep 3, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hospital Medicine, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide patient care to hospitalized children at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and serve as collaborative physician leaders in a busy, dynamic environment. Appointees should have exceptional interprofessional and interpersonal skills to work with colleagues, patients, and patients' families. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a 165-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offers world-class medical care to children from the Chicagoland area and across the globe. Our Section of Hospital Medicine is a nationally renowned, dedicated faculty group with a strong commitment to clinical care and medical education. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Pediatrics, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited pediatric residency program. We especially welcome applicants with experience in medical education and collaborative clinical or outcomes research. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/173061 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168198": {"title": "Faculty Esophagologists - Gastroenterology #MED390", "description": "Faculty Esophagologists - Gastroenterology #MED390. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition is searching for full-time faculty at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor who are dedicated esophagologists with clinical research experience. Appointees will have the opportunity to play a key role in further developing and expanding our rapidly growing program of excellence in esophageal disorders, including close collaboration with the multidisciplinary surgical teams recognized for pioneering innovative surgical treatments. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities primarily at our Hyde Park campus and at our network and affiliate sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in gastroenterology. We especially welcome applicants with clinical research experience in advanced esophageal diagnostics, GERD, immune disorders of the esophagus (EoE and others), esophageal motility disorders, foregut physiology, Barrett's esophagus, early cancer detection, or novel technologies for esophageal disease management, particularly those that integrate multidisciplinary collaboration with surgical innovation to advance care paradigms. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/172858 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography, as well as a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168149": {"title": "Senior Uro-Oncologists #SUR183", "description": "Senior Uro-Oncologists #SUR183. Section of Urology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Urology, is searching for full-time faculty members at the rank of associate professor or professor. The appointees will have inpatient and outpatient responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Urology. The appointees will support the prostate cancer program through treating, evaluating, and providing patient care directly. They will provide urologic oncology care to patients within the Section. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Urology strives to provide the best available, cutting-edge approach while offering compassionate care to our patients with urological diseases, to generate new medical knowledge and discoveries in the laboratory and through clinical research, and to educate the next generation of leaders in the art and science of urology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board certified or eligible by the American Board of Urology or equivalent, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship or equivalent training in urologic oncology. We especially welcome applicants with a demonstrated interest in academic medicine, a desire to join a cancer focused program, and robust experience in performing robotic procedures. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/172819 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168136": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Cancer Genetics #MED318a", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Cancer Genetics #MED318a. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 27, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center invite applications for full-time faculty positions at any rank on the School of Medicine track from applicants who specialize in Cancer Genetics. Appointees will have regular clinic sessions at our Hyde Park campus and at possible network locations, and will also attend inpatient services. Appointees will collaborate with medical geneticists, genetic counselors, and other cancer specialists to provide comprehensive, precision-based care. The position will support the growth of cancer genetics services, expand patient access to this specialized expertise, and enhance clinical and/or translational research efforts. Appointees will engage in multidisciplinary collaborations with physicians and researchers across sections and departments. Other duties will include teaching and supervising trainees and students, as well as engaging in research and scholarly activities. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/172809 . Applicants must upload: CV including a bibliography, research statement, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/168066": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science, American Politics", "description": "Assistant Professor of Political Science, American Politics. Department of Political Science. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 26, 2025. The Department of Political Science at the University of Chicago invites applications for an Assistant Professor in American Politics. We welcome applications from the entire subfield of American politics. This position will begin on or after July 1, 2026. Candidates must have completed all requirements for the PhD degree prior to the start of the appointment. Applicants must apply at the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website at https://apply.interfolio.com/172740 . Applications must include a 1) cover letter, 2) CV, 3) teaching statement, 4) research statement, 5) at least one (and up to three) examples of scholarly writing, and 6) at least three letters of recommendation. Consideration of applications will begin on October 1, 2025 and continue until the position is filled or the search has ended. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/167470": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Gastroenterology/IBD #MED392a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Gastroenterology/IBD #MED392a. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 18, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition, is searching for full-time faculty at any rank on the School of Medicine track to provide clinical and inpatient care for inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) and general gastroenterology (GI). Appointees will focus on IBD, intestinal ultrasound, and general GI to support the growing patient demand at our onsite clinics in Hyde Park and at our offsite locations. Other duties include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Eligible or Board Certified in Gastroenterology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/172207 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography, as well as a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/167207": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED393", "description": "Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED393. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Aug 13, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Clinical responsibilities include evaluating and treating patients at the newly built Emergency Department at Crown Point Hospital, IN. The Crown Point Emergency Department comprises an 8-bed unit with a projected annual volume of approximately 8,000 patient encounters in its first year. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Indiana, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/171965 . Applicants must upload: CV, including bibliography. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/166721": {"title": "Professor of Economics ", "description": "Professor of Economics . Microeconomics. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 11, 2025. The Booth School of Business at the University of Chicago is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenured positions in Economics with a focus in microeconomics Applications are invited from individuals who have earned a PhD (or equivalent) in economics and have experience on the tenure-track faculty at a major research university. Members of our faculty are expected to conduct original research of exceptionally high quality, to teach effectively, and to participate in and contribute to the academic environment at the University. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, course evaluations from prior teaching, at least two samples of written work, a research statement, and the names of five scholars willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, research, and teaching. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/166262": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Child Abuse Pediatricians #PED214", "description": "Academic Physicians - Child Abuse Pediatricians #PED214. Section of General Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Aug 6, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Academic Pediatrics, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide consultation within an established interdisciplinary process and participate in court-related activities as needed; support the Multidisciplinary Pediatric Education and Evaluation Consortium (MPEEC) program by providing real-time expert medical consultation to child welfare and law enforcement for a mandated population of children who are reported for serious harm; train pediatric residents during their required one-month rotation; provide lectures and grand rounds to regional hospitals and medical centers, as well as community partners such as state attorneys, law enforcement, and child welfare investigators; and participate in hospital committee efforts such as quality improvement projects and programmatic and policy development related to child abuse medicine. These activities will occur at the University of Chicago\u2019s Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and affiliated sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is designated as a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and operates a nationally recognized burn unit. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is an integrated children\u2019s hospital that offers a full complement of pediatric services with a total of 172 general pediatric, NICU, and PICU beds. The Department of Pediatrics includes over 170 full-time faculty members and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents, and fellows, our faculty conducts cutting-edge translational and clinical research. The Comer Children\u2019s Child Advocacy and Protective Services (CAPS) program, established in 1991, has a national reputation as a leader in the medical care of abused and neglected children. CAPS is primarily a hospital-based consultation service. The core CAPS team includes two board-certified physicians, a physician assistant, a clinical social worker, and full-time administrative support. More information about the CAPS program can be found at https://www.uchicagomedicine.org/comer/conditions-services/emergency-medicine/child-protective-services . MPEEC is a national landmark program that has received state support for over 18 years and is managed by CAPS staff, with case coordination supported by a full-time investigations coordinator. More information about MPEEC can be found at: https://mpeec.uchicago.edu/mpeec-medical-consortium/ . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Child Abuse Pediatrics. We especially welcome applicants with a strong commitment to advancing scholarship and clinical excellence in child abuse pediatrics. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/171144 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/166748": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Economics (Microeconomics)", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Economics (Microeconomics). Microeconomics. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Aug 5, 2025. The University of Chicago Booth School of Business is seeking to appoint outstanding scholars to tenure-track positions in Microeconomics. We invite applications from those who hold a PhD (or equivalent) in the relevant field(s) or expect to complete the requirements for a PhD in the relevant field(s) by July 1, 2027 or soon thereafter (we require all tenure track faculty to have a PhD or equivalent). (We will appoint those whose degree requirements have not been satisfied by this date as instructors, the expectation being that all PhD requirements will be satisfied soon thereafter, at which time you will be appointed as an assistant professor.) Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae, a sample of written work, and the names of at least two scholars qualified and willing to evaluate the candidate\u2019s ability, training, and potential for research and teaching. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago uses AI-assisted tools to streamline and augment some recruitment processes; however, AI is not used to make hiring decisions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164013": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Breast Clinicians #MED374", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Breast Clinicians #MED374. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Jul 29, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center seek full-time faculty members at any rank on the School of Medicine track who specialize in breast cancer treatment. Appointees will have regular clinic sessions at our Hyde Park campus and possible network locations and attend inpatient services. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, as well as clinical research and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/169108 . Applicants must upload: CV including a bibliography, research statement, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165868": {"title": "Urogynecology and Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery Faculty #OBG089", "description": "Urogynecology and Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery Faculty #OBG089. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank to join our team as academic urogynecologists. Appointees will be responsible for providing outstanding clinical and surgical care to women with pelvic floor disorders. Appointees will provide consultation and surgical services at UChicago's main campus in Hyde Park and one of the other UCM satellite URPS clinics. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of Urogynecology fellows, OB/GYN residents, and medical students, as well as scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology and the Section of Urogynecology and Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery foster a culture of supportive research through mentorship and multidisciplinary collaborations. We seek confident clinician-surgeons who have a passion for education and are supportive of and will contribute to our research mission. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board eligible or board certified in Obstetrics & Gynecology as well as Urogynecology & Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery, 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited residency in Obstetrics & Gynecology or Urology, and 5) have completed an ACGME-accredited fellowship in Urogynecology & Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/170788 . Applicants must upload: a CV including a bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/166264": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Adolescent Medicine #PED212", "description": "Academic Physicians - Adolescent Medicine #PED212. Section of General Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Academic Pediatrics, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide primary outpatient clinical care for adolescents with a wide variety of healthcare needs in an academic medical center and community settings, as well as consultative inpatient adolescent care. Clinical responsibilities will occur at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and the section\u2019s off-site clinical locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is designated as a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and operates a nationally recognized burn unit. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is an integrated children\u2019s hospital offering a full complement of pediatric services with a total of 172 general pediatric, NICU, and PICU beds. Our pediatric faculty includes 170 full-time members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents, and fellows, our faculty conducts cutting-edge translational and clinical research. A tradition of excellence in patient care, basic, clinical, and translational sciences, combined with the strength of one of the world\u2019s top academic institutions, provides the foundation for a career as an academic physician. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Adolescent Medicine. We especially welcome applicants with an academic interest and/or potential for building adolescent health programs and scholarship. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/171145 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/166259": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Rheumatology #PED213", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Rheumatology #PED213. Section of Pediatric Rheumatology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pediatric Rheumatology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will provide primary outpatient clinical care for pediatric patients with rheumatologic illnesses in academic medical center and community settings, as well as consultative inpatient care. Appointees will also contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship, and join a vibrant, diverse, and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medical Center is designated as a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center and operates a nationally recognized burn unit. Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is an integrated children\u2019s hospital that offers a full complement of pediatric services with a total of 172 general pediatric, NICU, and PICU beds. The Department of Pediatrics includes more than 170 full-time faculty, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents, and fellows, faculty conduct cutting-edge translational and clinical research. A tradition of excellence in patient care and research, combined with the strength of one of the world\u2019s top academic institutions, provides the foundation for a career as an academic physician. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in pediatric rheumatology. We especially welcome applicants with a commitment to providing equitable care in diverse clinical settings. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/171142 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/166295": {"title": "Chief, Cardiovascular Surgery #SUR180A", "description": "Chief, Cardiovascular Surgery #SUR180A. Section of Cardiac Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Cardiac Surgery, is searching for a full-time faculty member at any rank, who will serve as Chief of Cardiovascular Surgery for Endeavor Health and lead the Endeavor Health Cardiovascular Institute (CVI). The appointee will lead the clinical, academic, and strategic growth of a high-performing surgical division across six Endeavor Health hospitals\u2014Glenbrook Hospital (primary CVI site), Skokie Hospital, Evanston Hospital, Swedish Hospital, Highland Park Hospital, and Northwest Community Hospital. The Chief will oversee surgical quality, program development, and collaborative integration with structural heart, advanced heart failure, electrophysiology, and imaging teams across the system and is responsible for creating a unified surgical program that addresses the needs of all constituent hospitals. The appointee\u2019s own clinical responsibilities will include performing high-complexity cases (e.g., multi-valve, re-operations, valve/CABG, aortic surgery) as well as providing surgical support for structural heart (e.g., TAVR, mitral clip), electrophysiology (e.g., lead extraction), and advanced heart failure. Other duties include teaching of residents, fellows, and medical students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The successful candidate will be expected to develop a roadmap for a highly-ranked, high-performing cardiovascular surgery program and serve as the lead surgical voice in maintaining a strong working relationship between the University of Chicago and Endeavor Health. The key responsibilities for this leadership opportunity include: Advance comprehensive CVI strategy and direct the cardiovascular surgery service at six Endeavor Health hospitals, including oversight of the Glenbrook CVI, as well as build and grow specialized programs (e.g., minimally invasive valve, ECMO, aortic surgery, arrhythmia surgery, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, robotic surgery). Build strong working relationships with Vascular and Thoracic Surgery and will ensure the provision of tertiary services at Evanston Hospital, including support for the Woman\u2019s Hospital, as well as partner with Cardiology to develop distributed ECMO capabilities (e.g., cath lab cannulation, ICU readiness at multiple sites). Lead recruitment, retention, and advancement efforts and serve as clinical leader of a surgical workforce including junior surgeons, APPs, perfusion, OR staff, and ICU teams. Mentoring UChicago surgical fellows and other trainees rotating at Endeavor Health hospitals. Act as liaison with Cardiovascular Surgical Associates and other external clinical partners. Foster a collaborative environment that supports clinical trials, outcomes research, and scholarly activity. Actively participate in philanthropic activities to support the CVI and the Division of Cardiovascular Surgery is expected. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be BC/BE by the American Board of Thoracic Surgery (or equivalent); 4) have completed an ACGME accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in cardiovascular / thoracic (CVT) surgery; and 5) have a track record of building or significantly expanding a service line. Preferred qualifications include: 1) accredited fellowship training with a minimum of 8-10 years post-fellowship clinical experience; 2) demonstrable leadership in program building and recognition regionally or nationally for surgical expertise; 3) familiarity with, or prior leadership in, STS 3-star programs and with experience with ECMO, short-term mechanical support (e.g., Impella), and/or LVAD care; and 4) clinical leadership experience in a multi-hospital system and/or experience navigating complex health systems. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/171176 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165692": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Transplant Hepatology #MED391", "description": "Clinical Associates - Transplant Hepatology #MED391. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 22, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition is seeking full-time Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to 3 years at a time to join the thriving Center for Liver Diseases. The appointees will primarily assume clinical responsibilities at off-site locations in the Chicagoland area, focused on the western/southwest suburbs, with specific locations to be determined. Additionally, there will be opportunities for inpatient consultation and transplant clinic at our main campus located in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Eligible or Board Certified in Gastroenterology and Hepatology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/170637 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography, as well as a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165876": {"title": "Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) - Polymer Science", "description": "Senior Instructional Professor (open rank) - Polymer Science. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 21, 2025. The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering at the University of Chicago invites applications for Senior Instructional Professor (open rank). This is a full-time, career-track position beginning October 2025, and is renewable with the opportunity for progression. The selected candidate will be appointed as Assistant Senior Instructional Professor, Associate Senior Instructional Professor, or Senior Instructional Professor, with rank determined by qualifications. Depending on the rank, the appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable with the possibility for promotion. The selected candidate will be responsible for leading and providing instructional oversight of the Soft Matter Engineering track of the Master of Engineering program. Instructional responsibilities will include the delivery of three courses during the academic year, which may include courses having the primary formats of lectures, laboratories, or engineering design projects; at least one quarter of the MENG 25130/35130 Soft Matter Characterization Laboratory course; and the development and delivery of new courses, depending on programmatic needs for the MEng, undergraduate, and/or PhD programs. The candidate will also be expected to keep regular office hours, contribute to non-classroom instructional or service duties, and ongoing professional development. The candidate will manage the Soft Matter Characterization Facility (SMCF), a core facility of the PME that maintains a suite of specialized instruments dedicated to studying the structure, properties, and dynamics of soft materials. The candidate will provide ongoing support to both internal and external researchers and users of the SMCF. Additional responsibilities beyond the classroom will include, but not be limited to: advising students on the Soft Matter Engineering track of the Master of Engineering program; advising College students majoring and minoring in Molecular Engineering; engaging in recruitment activities for the PME academic programs; developing, participating in, and contributing to program operations, admissions, student recruitment, alumni engagement; and/or delivering summer programming for the PME. A PhD in Physics, Engineering or related field is required by the start of appointment. The following materials must be submitted through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/170796 : cover letter, outlining the applicant\u2019s prior experience in their discipline, their prior teaching or mentoring experience, and experience managing research laboratories/facilities; curriculum vitae; at least one course syllabus of a course designed and taught; course evaluations or other evidence of teaching effectiveness; evidence of successful management of programs (or evidence of potential to do so); teaching statement; and contact information for at least three references. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165098": {"title": "Internal Medicine-Psychiatry Faculty Psychiatrists  #PBN074", "description": "Internal Medicine-Psychiatry Faculty Psychiatrists #PBN074. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 21, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who have training and experience in the fields of psychiatry and internal medicine. The appointees will have inpatient, consultation-liaison and outpatient responsibilities providing psychiatric assessment, treatment and consultation services for patients and their families. Appointees will also provide diagnosis and treatment of patient\u2019s primary care and internal medicine clinical needs. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Of note, the department is seeking to expand its residency training program to include the education and training of Internal Medicine and Psychiatry residents to enhance the collaborative opportunities between physical health and holistic health. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed residency training in Adult Psychiatry and Internal Medicine; and 4) be Board certified or eligible with the American Board of Internal Medicine (ABIM) and the American Board of Psychiatry and Neurology (ABPN). We especially welcome applicants familiar with being an integral part of a multidisciplinary care team and who have experience contributing to the growth and development of Internal Medicine-Psychiatry joint residency training programs at academic medical centers. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/170104 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165695": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Dermatology #MED382a", "description": "Clinical Associates - Dermatology #MED382a. Section of Dermatology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 21, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Dermatology, is seeking part-time clinical dermatologists for appointments as 10% Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Clinical responsibilities will include Mohs micrographic surgery and procedural dermatology onsite at the University of Chicago Medicine. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. These positions are not benefits eligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; (2) hold or be eligible for full medical licensure in the State of Illinois; (3) be board eligible or board certified in Dermatology and Micrographic Dermatologic Surgery or equivalent; and (4) have completed an ACGME-accredited fellowship in Procedural Dermatology or Micrographic Surgery or equivalent training approved by the American College of Mohs Surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/170641 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165624": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics #PED210", "description": "Clinical Associates - Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics #PED210. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 18, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics seeks experienced board certified developmental-behavioral pediatricians and general pediatricians to join our multidisciplinary care team at the University of Chicago campus and at UChicago Medicine Crown Point as Clinical Associates at 100% effort with renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will manage the care of pediatric patients who may present with a full range of developmental and behavioral conditions. Clinics are held on the University of Chicago campus at the Comer Children's Hospital clinic and at the UChicago Crown Point clinic located in Crown Point, IN. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. Our department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents and fellows, our faculty conduct cutting-edge translational and clinical research. UChicago Medicine Comer Children's Hospital is a free standing 165 bed children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: 1) a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) have or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be either BC/BE in Pediatrics, or BC/BE in Developmental-Behavioral Pediatrics or Neurodevelopmental Disabilities. Preference given to applicants with an interest in developmental diagnostics for autism, intellectual disability, sequelae of prematurity, critical illness, learning disorders, and ADHD. We also welcome applicants who have a graduate degree in public health, health informatics, or a related field. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/170577 . Applicants must upload a cover letter and CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165625": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology #PED211", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology #PED211. Section of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 17, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hematology/Oncology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will focus on the care of children, adolescents, and young adults undergoing stem cell transplant with an interest in gene therapy, cellular therapy, and leading curative options for patients with non-malignant and immune disorders at both Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and the section\u2019s off-site clinical locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . We anticipate that one of the appointees from this search will also serve in the role of Medical Director for Pediatric Stem Cell and Cellular Therapy Program Service Line of the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA) and will partner with the Director of the Pediatric Stem Cell and Cellular Therapy Program to operate a highly efficient program. In their role as Medical Director, the appropriate appointee will provide direction, leadership, and day-to-day management of clinical activities for all transplant program staff, championing organizational and departmental operational initiatives in unit while providing guidance and leadership to achieve clinical performance guidelines. Development of translational or clinical protocols in transplantation for either malignant or benign hematological diseases is expected. The Section of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology/Stem Cell Transplantation at the University of Chicago has recently formed a partnership with the Advocate Children's Hospital\u2019s Division of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology. The Children's Health Alliance (CCHA) reflects a collaboration among the University of Chicago Chicagoland Medicine Comer Children's Hospital, Advocate Children's Hospital and Pediatrics at NorthShore University Health System. There has been a significant expansion of the Stem Cell Transplant and Cellular Therapy Program due to this recent partnership. The three organizations are committed to seamless, multidisciplinary teams who are passionate about providing the best possible care for children and their families. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be BC/BE in pediatric hematology/oncology, and 4) have completed a pediatric hematology/oncology fellowship. Applicants with expertise in immunotherapy, stem cell therapeutics, or clinical trial design are encouraged to apply. However, we welcome applications from individuals with other transplantation experience with appropriate clinical experience. In addition, we especially welcome applicants with at least 3 years of experience in the management of patients undergoing stem cell transplantation and cellular therapies. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses lnterfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/170579 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165416": {"title": "Head and Neck Surgical Oncologists #SUR181 ", "description": "Head and Neck Surgical Oncologists #SUR181 . Section of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 15, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Otolaryngology \u2013 Head and Neck Surgery, seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will specialize in the treatment of benign and malignant tumors of the head and neck with responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery at the Hyde Park location and affiliated satellites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery has a rich history, matched only by our ongoing commitment to excellence in medical education, research, patient care, and service to our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, 3) be board certified or eligible by the American Board of Otolaryngology or equivalent, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship or equivalent training in Head and Neck Surgical Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/170385 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/165194": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Geriatric Oncology #MED375", "description": "Academic Physicians - Geriatric Oncology #MED375. Section of Hematology/Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 11, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center invite applications for full-time faculty positions at any rank on the School of Medicine track from applicants who specialize in Geriatric Oncology. Appointees will have regular clinic sessions at our Hyde Park campus and possible network locations and attend inpatient services. Appointees will collaborate with geriatricians and palliative care providers to deliver comprehensive, age-sensitive treatment plans. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, as well as research and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/170185 . Applicants must upload: CV including a bibliography, research statement, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164964": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Lung Transplant Pulmonologists #MED388", "description": "Clinical Associates - Lung Transplant Pulmonologists #MED388. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jul 7, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary/Critical Care Medicine, is searching for part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to three years to join an established group of nationally known pulmonologists. Appointees will focus on patient care and clinical operations in lung transplantation and interstitial lung disease (ILD) at UChicago Medicine AdventHealth on the Hinsdale campus in the inpatient and/or outpatient setting. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly work and are not benefits eligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Pulmonary/Critical Care Medicine; 4) have lung transplant fellowship training or experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/169983 . Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164786": {"title": " Academic Physicians - Pulmonary and Critical Care/Lung Transplantation #MED380", "description": " Academic Physicians - Pulmonary and Critical Care/Lung Transplantation #MED380. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 2, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine is seeking full-time faculty members at any rank with a focus on lung transplantation. Appointees will have clinical responsibilities primarily at our Hyde Park campus as well as our network and affiliate sites. Clinical responsibilities will include both inpatient and outpatient responsibilities in lung transplantation. Appointees will also have the opportunity to provide inpatient coverage on the pulmonary consult service or MICU. Other duties include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Pulmonary Disease and Critical Care Medicine, and 4) have training in lung transplantation. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/169815 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164472": {"title": "Academic Epileptologists #NEU037a", "description": "Academic Epileptologists #NEU037a. Department of Neurology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jul 1, 2025. The University of Chicago's Adult Epilepsy Center in the Department of Neurology is seeking board certified/board eligible academic epileptologists to join its faculty as the Neuroscience program continues to expand. Appointees will participate in activities of the Adult Epilepsy Center, which include an inpatient epilepsy monitoring unit, intraoperative monitoring, and outpatient and inpatient EEG. Other duties include teaching of fellows, residents, and medical students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join the Center's existing group of epileptologists, neurosurgeons, and neuroscientists at University of Chicago Medicine, which includes a growing neurosciences service line with the opportunity to practice at locations both on and outside of our main campus in Hyde Park. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) have completed a residency program in neurology accredited by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education, 3) be fellowship trained in Epilepsy and/or Clinical Neurophysiology, including advanced EEG monitoring and surgical evaluation, 4) be board certified or eligible in neurology, and 5) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois. We especially welcome applicants with clinical research experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/169545 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164637": {"title": "Abdominal Imaging Radiologists #RAD086", "description": "Abdominal Imaging Radiologists #RAD086. Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 30, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Radiology is expanding and searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will have responsibilities in all aspects of abdominal imaging including interpretation of CT, MRI, US, performance of abdomen/pelvis fluoroscopy and image guided biopsies. The appointees will also participate in a variety of interdisciplinary conferences in order to build collaborative relationships across campus. Other duties will include training and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows. This position is based at the University of Chicago Medicine\u2019s Hyde Park campus, where the appointee will spend most of their time. This role involves patient care delivery across other University of Chicago Medicine (UCM) sites in downtown Chicago and Northwest Indiana and may require occasional travel. Remote days are included weekly. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship, and join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago and its affiliates. There are numerous opportunities for collaboration available within the University and with community partners. The Department of Radiology includes more than 80 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents and fellows, our faculty conduct cutting-edge translational and clinical research. A tradition of excellence in patient care, basic, clinical, and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions provides the foundation for a career as an academic radiologist. A more complete overview of the department is available at http://radiology.uchicago.edu/ . The medical center complex is located on the main campus of the University of Chicago, south of downtown along the shore of Lake Michigan. The local Hyde Park community is a vibrant center with restaurants and entertainment. Chicago itself is a world class city. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Radiology, and 4) have completed a Fellowship in Abdominal Imaging or have equivalent experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/169691 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164680": {"title": "Part-time Clinical Associates- Radiologists #RAD087", "description": "Part-time Clinical Associates- Radiologists #RAD087. Department of Radiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 30, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Radiology seeks Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to three-years. Appointees will provide interpretation of all aspects of General Radiology including conventional radiology, CT, MRI, and US. Remote days are included weekly. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. These positions are not benefits eligible. The University of Chicago Medicine is a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center. The Department of Radiology includes more than 80 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. A more complete overview of the department is available at http://radiology.uchicago.edu . The medical center complex is located on the main campus of the University of Chicago, south of downtown along the shore of Lake Michigan. The local Hyde Park community is a vibrant center with restaurants and entertainment. Chicago itself is a world class city. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Radiology. We especially welcome applicants with fellowship training or demonstrated clinical expertise in Abdominal Imaging, Chest Imaging, and/or Musculoskeletal Imaging. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/169724 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164502": {"title": "Faculty Radiation Oncologists #RON033", "description": "Faculty Radiation Oncologists #RON033. Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jun 27, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will provide radiation oncology services at department locations and may lead a practice comprised of one or two radiation oncologists as well as nursing, physics, dosimetry, and therapy staff. The clinical work will primarily be based at Crown Point, with Hyde Park as a secondary location and other Chicago-area network sites as needed. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be Board certified or eligible in radiation oncology, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited residency or equivalent in radiation oncology. We especially welcome applicants who have formerly served as an attending radiation oncologist in an academic institution, as well as those who have experience participating in clinical trials. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/169586 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163136": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology Hospitalists #PED206", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hematology/Oncology Hospitalists #PED206. Section of Pediatric Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 24, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hematology/Oncology and Stem Cell Transplantation seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years to join our expanding Pediatric Hematology/Oncology practice. Appointees will manage the inpatient care of children with cancer and blood disorders at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, working closely with a multidisciplinary team to ensure comprehensive care, including chemotherapy administration, procedures, and resident/trainee supervision. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior and compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide an education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. The department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. UChicago Medicine Comer Children's Hospital is a free standing 165 bed children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services. Appointees will join the Section of Hematology/Oncology, a multidisciplinary program which collaborates with the Department of Surgery, the Department of Radiation Oncology, and the University of Chicago Comprehensive Cancer Center and affiliated sites: Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital and NorthShore University Health System. The group is responsible for the diagnosis and management of children in the largest pediatrics program in the state of Illinois, the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA). Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatrics, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited pediatric residency program. Applicants with prior pediatric hematology/oncology experience are particularly encouraged to apply. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/168309 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164143": {"title": "Cardiac Surgeons, Clinical Associates #SUR179", "description": "Cardiac Surgeons, Clinical Associates #SUR179. Section of Cardiac Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 24, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Cardiac Surgery, seeks Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities include independent call, inpatient rounding on LVAD patients, and supporting all Cardiac Surgery programs at the Hyde Park campus and affiliated offsite community practices and partner locations. Programs include CT Transplant, Procurement, and ECMO. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will contribute to patient care and join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. The University of Chicago Heart & Vascular Center has nationally recognized research and education programs that build on collaborations throughout the University and is active in clinical operations and quality improvement in the Medical Center. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Thoracic Surgery or equivalent, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in cardiovascular/thoracic (CVT) surgery. We especially welcome applicants with at least three years of directly related experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/169226 . Applicants must upload: CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after July 4, 2025. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163864": {"title": "Clinical Associates - General Cardiology #MED383", "description": "Clinical Associates - General Cardiology #MED383. MED - Section of Cardiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 23, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is seeking part-time clinical cardiologists for appointments as 10% Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. These positions will be primarily focused on outpatient cardiology practice and consultation at our various Chicagoland locations. Appointees will read EKGs, perform exercise stress tests, and read/interpret echocardiograms. There will also be opportunities for inpatient consultation at our main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. These positions are not benefits eligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Cardiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses lnterfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/168974 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications will take place until positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/164191": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Dermatology #MED384", "description": "Clinical Associates - Dermatology #MED384. Section of Dermatology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 23, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Dermatology, seeks full-time dermatologists for appointments as Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Clinical responsibilities will include seeing outpatients focused on general, cosmetic, and procedural dermatology at onsite and/or off-site locations (in general dermatology clinics), including our new state of the art Centers for Advanced Care in Orland Park, River East, South Loop, Crown Point, and/or other University of Chicago Medicine-contracted locations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate (MD) or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for full medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be board eligible/board certified in Dermatology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/169274. Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163975": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Adult Emergency Medicine #MED378", "description": "Clinical Associates - Adult Emergency Medicine #MED378. Section of Emergency Medicine. Position type: Faculty. Posted: Jun 23, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will be responsible for patient care and clinical operations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine is a STEMI receiving hospital, a Comprehensive Stroke Center, a Burn Center, and a regional resource hospital for Chicago South EMS. The Adult ED has an annual volume of 75,000, and we are a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or board-eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, interested individuals must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which utilizes Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/169076 . Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163933": {"title": "Tenure Track/Tenured Faculty \u2013 Ben May Department for Cancer Research #BMD009", "description": "Tenure Track/Tenured Faculty \u2013 Ben May Department for Cancer Research #BMD009. Ben May Department for Cancer Research. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Jun 12, 2025. The University of Chicago's Ben May Department for Cancer Research and Ludwig Center of Chicago invites applications for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees are expected to establish independent cutting-edge research programs in cancer biology that complement and extend the research conducted by Department and Ludwig Center faculty in the areas of the tumor microenvironment, cancer cell signaling, cancer systems biology, immunology, the microbiome and cancer, systemic effects of cancer, and the effects of diet and obesity on cancer prognosis. Appointees will teach at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Ben May Department of Cancer Research has an illustrious history of discovery in cancer biology in a dynamic academic setting. Research in the department is focused on mechanistic insights into tumor biology with an emphasis on translating scientific discovery into the clinic. The Ludwig Center of Chicago includes a range of researchers with strengths in tumor metabolism, metastasis, and microenvironment. Appointees will also be proposed for appointments in the NCI-designated University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center (UCCCC) ( http://cancer.uchicago.edu ) and collaborate in multi-disciplinary research with colleagues across the Biological Sciences and Physical Sciences Divisions, the University of Chicago Medical Center and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. The University of Chicago offers state-of-the-art core facilities and outstanding graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs in the biological and physical sciences. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/169039 Applicants must upload: 1) a cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae with bibliography, 3) a research statement, 4) a teaching statement, and 5) contact information for three references. Review of applications is expected to begin on September 2, 2025. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163537": {"title": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in General Biology", "description": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in General Biology. Biological Sciences Collegiate Division. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jun 6, 2025. The Biological Sciences Collegiate Division at the University of Chicago is accepting applications for three open-rank Instructional Professor positions in general biology . These are full-time teaching positions beginning on September 1, 2025, or as soon as possible thereafter. The appointment will be for an initial term of at least two years, with reappointment or progression possible following review. Appointment at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Instructional Professor will be considered based on the candidate\u2019s experience. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses, some designed for majors and some for non-majors, in the areas of general biology, cell biology, genetics, and/or developmental biology. Some courses include both lecture and lab components, and some are taught in partnership with university faculty. Therefore, the Instructional Professor must be able to teach a course independently and to function well as a member of a teaching team in both a lecture and laboratory teaching setting. Additional duties include hiring, training, and supervising teaching assistants. Instructional Professors of all ranks are required to engage in regular professional development. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant: $83,379-$90,049; Associate: $90,050-$97,218; Full: $97,219-$104,000 Applicants must have a PhD in Biology or a related field in hand before the start date and at least one year of teaching university-level lecture and/or laboratory courses. To apply for this position, candidates must submit their application through the University of Chicago's Interfolio job board at apply.interfolio.com/168680 and upload the following: 1) a cover letter that addresses professional and teaching experience; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a teaching statement which should include information about the candidate\u2019s teaching philosophy and goals; 4) syllabi of courses previously taught or sample syllabus of a proposed course; 5) course evaluations as the instructor of record or evidence of past teaching performance; 6) the names and contact information (including email addresses) for three references whose letters of recommendation may be solicited Review of applications will begin on July 9, 2025, and will continue until the positions are filled or the search is closed. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163414": {"title": "Part-Time (Pay-Per Shift) Clinical Associates - Pediatric General Surgery - #SUR137B", "description": "Part-Time (Pay-Per Shift) Clinical Associates - Pediatric General Surgery - #SUR137B. Section of Pediatric Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jun 3, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Pediatric Surgery, is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 30% effort for renewable terms of up to 3 years. Appointees will contribute pediatric inpatient consultations and trauma call coverage responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Pediatric Surgery at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and affiliated offsite locations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per shift and does not include benefits. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board-certified or Board-eligible by the American Board of Surgery (or equivalent) in Pediatric Surgery, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in Pediatric Surgery. We especially welcome applicants who, in addition to satisfying these requirements, are Board-certified or Board-eligible in Surgical Critical Care, or eligible for fellowship training in Surgical Critical Care. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/168561 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications will begin after June 13, 2025. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/162947": {"title": "Vascular Interventional Radiologists - Open Rank #RAD085 ", "description": "Vascular Interventional Radiologists - Open Rank #RAD085 . Department of Radiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 30, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Radiology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank for renewable terms to add to our growing section of Vascular and Interventional Radiology. Responsibilities will include the full spectrum of interventional radiology procedures with strong oncologic, hepatobiliary, transplant, trauma, vascular including CTA imaging, dialysis, non-vascular, portal venous, women\u2019s, and pediatric interventional radiology services. Other duties will include training and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows. Remote academic days are included weekly. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners at UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, the University of Chicago, and its affiliates. They will also participate in a variety of interdisciplinary conferences and building collaborative relationships across campus and with community partners. The Department of Radiology itself includes more than 80 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents and fellows, our faculty conduct cutting-edge translational and clinical research. A tradition of excellence in patient care, basic, clinical, and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions provides the foundation for a career as an academic radiologist. A more complete overview of the department is available at http://radiology.uchicago.edu/ . The University of Chicago is located south of downtown along the shore of Lake Michigan. The local Hyde Park community is a vibrant center with restaurants and entertainment. Chicago itself is a world class city. Prior to the start of employment qualified individuals must: 1) be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible, 2) have an active medical license in the states of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) have completed a vascular/interventional radiology fellowship or IR/DR residency. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/168145 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/161992": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Adult Emergency Medicine #MED376", "description": "Clinical Associates - Adult Emergency Medicine #MED376. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 29, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, is seeking Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable two-year terms. Appointees will have responsibilities in patient care and clinical operations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly work and are not benefits eligible. Pay is at a standard hourly rate. The University of Chicago Medicine is a STEMI receiving hospital, a Comprehensive Stroke Center, a Burn Center, and a Chicago South EMS regional resource hospital. The Adult ED has an annual volume of 75,000 and we are a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and 3) be Board certified or be Board eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/167312 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/163278": {"title": "Research Focused Faculty Positions - Department of Medicine #MED371a", "description": "Research Focused Faculty Positions - Department of Medicine #MED371a. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: May 29, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine seeks tenure-track or tenured faculty members who are pursuing research in the fields of population and public health, social science, clinical and outcomes research, quantitative methodology, clinical informatics or related areas. Appointees are expected to teach at the undergraduate and graduate level. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago has a tradition of innovative interdisciplinary scholarship in the social sciences applied to health and health care. Discoveries at the interface of health and the social sciences address some of health care\u2019s most critical problems. These include the relationship of individual and population health to social capital and human capital and neighborhood contexts, to the economics, organization and management of social institutions and policies. The primary appointments will be in the Department of Medicine, although we anticipate that appointees will develop close interactions with other departments, divisions, institutes and centers at the University of Chicago, Argonne National Laboratory and the National Opinion Research Center. The Biological Sciences Division, other academic units of the University, and the University of Chicago Medical Center, are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park. Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art facilities and to outstanding colleagues and graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs across the university. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/168432 . Applicants must upload: 1) CV including bibliography; 2) a cover letter; 3) a research statement; and 4) a list of three references. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/162847": {"title": "General Plastic and Reconstructive Surgeons, Clinical Associates (60% Effort) #SUR176 ", "description": "General Plastic and Reconstructive Surgeons, Clinical Associates (60% Effort) #SUR176 . Section of Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 22, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Plastic & Reconstructive Surgery seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 60% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide care for general plastic, body contour, and reconstructive surgery patients in both the inpatient and outpatient settings at our UChicago Medicine AdventHealth locations in the western suburbs of Chicago. We especially welcome applicants with an interest in weight loss and post-bariatric care. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by the American Board of Plastic Surgery or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/168065 . Applicants must upload: CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after June 1, 2025. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/162438": {"title": "Academic Obstetrics & Gynecology Faculty \u2013 #OBG086", "description": "Academic Obstetrics & Gynecology Faculty \u2013 #OBG086. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will be responsible for outpatient clinical care at our Crown Point, Indiana location and inpatient clinical care at our main campus in Hyde Park. Appointees will be members of the obstetrics call group and will also participate in gynecology service. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. We welcome applicants with training and experience in gynecology (office gynecology, minimally invasive surgery, or family planning/complex contraception), obstetrics (vaginal delivery of twins, higher order cesarean sections, and operative vaginal deliveries), and resident or medical student teaching. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/167714 . Applicants must upload: 1) CV including bibliography and 2) cover letter. Review of complete applications will continue until positions are filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/161819": {"title": "Clinical Veterinarians #SUR173", "description": "Clinical Veterinarians #SUR173. Animal Resources Center. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: May 19, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Surgery, Animal Resources Center (ARC), seeks full-time Clinical Veterinarians at any faculty rank on the School of Medicine Track. Appointees will share responsibilities of clinical care and oversight of multiple species; assist in the ARC training program, including the ACLAM-recognized veterinary residency training program; participate in IACUC protocol review, and provide investigator consultation and research support of research protocols. Other duties include mentoring and teaching trainees and students and pursuing scholarly activity. Academic rank and c ompensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a DVM or VMD from an AVMA-accredited institution, 2) hold or be eligible for veterinary licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) have completed a laboratory animal medicine residency or training program. We especially welcome applicants with ACLAM board certification or those preparing for the ACLAM board certifying exam. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/167164 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/162420": {"title": "Radiologists - Clinical Associates #RAD084", "description": "Radiologists - Clinical Associates #RAD084. Department of Radiology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 15, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Radiology seeks Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to two-years. Appointees will provide interpretation of all aspects of General Radiology including conventional radiology, CT, MRI, and US. Remote days are included weekly. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine is a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center. A more complete overview of the department is available at http://radiology.uchicago.edu . The medical center complex is located on the main campus of the University of Chicago, south of downtown along the shore of Lake Michigan. The local Hyde Park community is a vibrant center with restaurants and entertainment. Chicago itself is a world class city. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Radiology. We especially welcome applicants with fellowship training or demonstrated clinical expertise in Abdominal Imaging, Chest Imaging, and/or Musculoskeletal Imaging. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/167703 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/161466": {"title": "Part-time (Pay-per Shift) Clinical Associates in General Obstetrics and Gynecology  #OBG090", "description": "Part-time (Pay-per Shift) Clinical Associates in General Obstetrics and Gynecology #OBG090. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: May 9, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of General Obstetrics & Gynecology is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 30% effort for one-year renewable terms. The appointees will contribute to inpatient service coverage, which may be done on Labor & Delivery and/or inpatient GYN consultation service. Appointees may elect to participate in one or both options. When assigned to Labor and Delivery shift coverage, the appointees will cover shifts in-house at the Family Birth Center on the Hyde Park campus. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity, and are not benefits-eligible. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Obstetrics and Gynecology, and 4) have completed an ACGME accredited residency in Obstetrics & Gynecology. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166831 . Applicants must upload a CV, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/161248": {"title": "Chief - Section of Maternal Fetal Medicine #OBG088", "description": "Chief - Section of Maternal Fetal Medicine #OBG088. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 28, 2025. The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology at The University of Chicago is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor on the School of Medicine track to serve as Section Chief for Maternal Fetal Medicine. The appointee will participate in the maintenance and further development of the perinatal referral network and will be expected to maintain good working relationships with community-based physicians, referring physicians, and hospitals, and participate in the outreach efforts of the University of Chicago Medical Center. The Section Chief will also be responsible for further developing innovative research programs that leverage collaboration with existing university and community partners and support the growth of the Biological Sciences Division\u2019s grant portfolio. Additional duties will include, but are not limited to, strategy, budget, and planning oversight of the Section\u2019s revenues, expenses, and faculty compensation plan, as well as management of annual performance metrics. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows, and scholarly activity. In summary, the Section Chief will provide strategic, innovative, and operational leadership and will be responsible for the Section's clinical, educational, and research performance. Clinical responsibilities include reading ultrasound, attending on the clinical MFM service, consults, and outpatient clinic. The candidate may choose to participate in the coverage of Labor & Delivery. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Maternal Fetal Medicine (MFM) Section is situated in state-of-the-art facilities on the University of Chicago medical campus in the Hyde Park neighborhood. The Section leads the Perinatal Program for 15 regional hospitals and provides substantial academic and clinical services to the area. The Section strategically enhances our High-Risk Pregnancy Program, actively collaborating with the Section of General Obstetrics and Gynecology and our Midwives, as well as other departments, including Anesthesiology, Pediatrics (Neonatology, Pediatric Cardiology), Endocrinology, and Cardiology. The Section Chief supports a collaborative culture that allows a comprehensive, patient-oriented approach to the care we provide. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited fellowship in MFM, or have equivalent experience, and 4) be Board certified, or eligible for Board certification, in MFM. We especially welcome candidates who have experience managing clinical operations and have the leadership skills needed to support and enhance the Section's clinilcal, research, outreach, and educational efforts. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166634. Applicants must upload: a CV including a bibliography as well as a cover letter. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159003": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Gastrointestinal (GI) Oncology Program Leader #MED366", "description": "Academic Physicians - Gastrointestinal (GI) Oncology Program Leader #MED366. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 24, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor on the School of Medicine track to serve as the Gastrointestinal Cancer Program Leader . The appointee will provide visionary leadership and strategic direction in close partnership with institutional leadership to advance clinical excellence, research, innovation, programmatic growth, and multidisciplinary collaboration in gastrointestinal oncology. The appointee will have weekly clinic sessions focused on GI oncology and attend on inpatient services at our Hyde Park campus. The appointee will be expected to have a strong record of scholarly research, with an emphasis on clinical and translational investigation, including the development and leadership of clinical trials and other activities that contribute to advancements in the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of gastrointestinal malignancies. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity . Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. We especially welcome applicants with past and/or current leadership experience in gastrointestinal clinical and research programs, as well as experience directing young adult cancer programs. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/164622 . Applicants must upload: CV including a bibliography, research statement, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/161111": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hospitalists #PED138b", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hospitalists #PED138b. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 21, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hospital Medicine, seeks Clinical Associates at full-time effort for a renewable term of up to two years to join our expanding Pediatric Hospital Medicine group. Clinical responsibilities will include pediatric medicine, newborn care, and ED consultation. Appointees may have the opportunity to participate in quality improvement efforts in the Section of Pediatric Hospital Medicine. These positions do not require teaching of residents or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior and compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide an education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. The department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. UChicago Medicine Comer Children's Hospital is a free standing 165 bed children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Pediatrics, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited pediatric residency program. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/166516 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications end when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/161117": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Lung Transplantation #SUR174", "description": "Clinical Associates - Lung Transplantation #SUR174. Section of Thoracic Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 21, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Sections of Transplant and Thoracic Surgery, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will assist with various clinical responsibilities within the lung transplant programs. They will be responsible for lung organ procurement, assisting in the operating room during lung transplants and related surgical procedures, and providing patient care across inpatient, outpatient, and ambulatory settings. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be board-certified or board-eligible by The American Board of Thoracic Surgery or the foreign equivalent, and 4) have completed a lung transplant fellowship or equivalent training. We especially welcome applicants with experience in thoracic surgery and lung transplantation with successful completion of a lung transplant fellowship. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166521 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter . Review of applications will end with the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160651": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia #ACC076", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia #ACC076. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 17, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care seeks Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including, but not limited to Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and or main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Clinical Associates will have access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care resources including, but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. Separate searches are underway for similar appointees but at different levels of effort; we encourage applications to one, some, or all positions: 80% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166107 60% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166110 50% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166111 Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications at apply.interfolio.com/166106 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160654": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [80% Effort] #ACC077", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [80% Effort] #ACC077. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 17, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care seeks Clinical Associates at 80% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including, but not limited to, Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and/or main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Clinical Associates will have access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care resources, including, but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. Separate searches are underway for similar appointees but at different levels of effort; we encourage applications to one, some, or all positions: 100% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166106 60% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166110 50% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166111 Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166107 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160655": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [60% Effort] #ACC078", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [60% Effort] #ACC078. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 17, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care seeks Clinical Associates at 60% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including, but not limited to, Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and or main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Clinical Associates will have access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care resources, including but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. Separate searches are underway for similar appointees but at different levels of effort; we encourage applications to one, some, or all positions: 100% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166106 80% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166107 50% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166111 Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166110 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160657": {"title": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [50% Effort] #ACC079", "description": "Clinical Associates in Anesthesia [50% Effort] #ACC079. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 17, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care seeks Clinical Associates at 50% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including but not limited to Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and or main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Clinical Associates will have access to the University of Chicago and the Department of Anesthesia & Critical Care resources, including but not limited to, educational conferences and CME activities as the clinical schedule allows. Separate searches are underway for similar appointees but at different levels of effort; we encourage applications to one, some, or all positions: 100% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166106 80% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166107 60% effort Clinical Associates: apply.interfolio.com/166110 Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, and 3) be BC/BE or equivalent in anesthesiology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166111 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160907": {"title": "Academic Physician - Co-Director, Transplant Institute #MED337a", "description": "Academic Physician - Co-Director, Transplant Institute #MED337a. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 14, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition is searching for a full-time Associate Professor or Professor to serve as Co-Director, Transplant Institute. Clinical responsibilities, including general hepatology and liver transplant service, will be based at the University of Chicago\u2019s main campus, with consultative services also provided at one or more sites in the Chicago area. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be BC/BE in gastroenterology, and 4) have advanced training in transplant hepatology (or equivalent experience). We welcome applicants with research interests including liver metabolism, alcohol-related liver disease, hepatobiliary neoplasia, or liver transplantation. We also welcome applicants with leadership experience in managing a complex clinical operation. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/166336 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the available position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160438": {"title": "Pathology Clinical Associates, UChicago Medicine Community Practice #PAT051", "description": "Pathology Clinical Associates, UChicago Medicine Community Practice #PAT051. Department of Pathology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 11, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pathology is searching for Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. The appointee will be based at one or more University of Chicago community hospitals and will have clinical responsibilities in anatomic and clinical pathology. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Department of Pathology at the University of Chicago has a long history of excellence. Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty, physicians, and learners and will have the opportunity to collaborate widely across the Biological Sciences Division (BSD), UChicago Medicine, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible to hold a medical license in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) have board certification in Anatomic Pathology/Clinical Pathology (with or without an additional subspecialty) through the American Board of Pathology, and 4) have the ability to carry and maintain CLIA licensure. Applicants with experience in administration, quality improvement, and lab operations are preferred. The University of Chicago has retained Sante Consulting, LLC ( http://www.santellc.com ) to support this recruitment. Rich Cornell, President of Sante Consulting is leading the search. Inquiries, applications, and nominations must be sent by email to Rich Cornell at rcornell@santellc.com . Please note all job seekers wishing to be considered for the position must also submit their materials through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/165905. Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160691": {"title": "Transfusion Medicine and Coagulation Faculty Position #PAT053", "description": "Transfusion Medicine and Coagulation Faculty Position #PAT053. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 10, 2025. The University of Chicago Department of Pathology is recruiting a full-time open rank faculty position in the Section of Transfusion, Hemostasis, and Apheresis Medicine (THAM). The successful applicant will have clinical responsibilities in transfusion medicine (blood bank and donor collection activities), apheresis, and coagulation. The appointee will also participate in on-call service as well as section quality improvement activities, blood utilization oversight, and regulatory compliance. Other duties will include supervision and teaching of medical trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . UChicago Medicine (UCM), an 811-bed adult and pediatric academic medical center, seeks a dedicated professional to contribute to its laboratory operations supporting a wide range of patient care needs. The Blood Bank issues over 41,000 blood products annually, providing critical support for adult and pediatric Level 1 Trauma, complex cardiac and transplant surgeries, high-risk obstetrics, a Level IV NICU, and cutting-edge oncology and cell therapy services. The Apheresis service performs over 1200 procedures annually, including more than 790 therapeutic procedures such as plasma exchange, red cell exchange, photopheresis, and depletions, as well as stem cell and cell therapy harvests. The hospital-based Blood Donation Center collects approximately 1600 whole blood and plateletpheresis products annually, all of which are used to support UCM patients. The stand-alone Coagulation Lab delivers robust routine, stat, and esoteric coagulation testing, with a specialized focus on cutting-edge platelet function analysis. This position offers the opportunity to contribute significantly to the care of massively hemorrhaging trauma patients, hematology patients receiving Immunotherapeutics, and patients with hemolytic disease of the fetus/newborn, working at the forefront of transfusion medicine in a dynamic academic environment. The section directs an ACGME-accredited fellowship in blood banking/transfusion medicine. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible to hold a medical license in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) board certification or board eligibility in Blood Banking/Transfusion Medicine through the American Board of Pathology. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/166146 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160739": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Thoracic Cancer #MED370", "description": "Academic Physicians - Thoracic Cancer #MED370. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Apr 9, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who specializes in the treatment of thoracic malignancies and related clinical research. The appointees will have weekly clinic sessions at our Hyde Park campus and network locations and attend on inpatient services. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/166181 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160440": {"title": "Faculty in Synthetic Biology #BSD036", "description": "Faculty in Synthetic Biology #BSD036. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Apr 7, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Biological Sciences Division (BSD), the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center (UCCCC), the University of Chicago Department of Medicine (DOM), and the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) seek tenure track/tenured faculty members at any rank in the field of synthetic biology. The primary department of each appointee will be dependent on research interests. Appointees will work at the interface of immunology, molecular biology, chemistry and bioengineering to develop approaches to engineer cellular, regulatory, and metabolic networks for cancer immunotherapy. Examples of areas of research interest include, but are not limited to, design of synthetic circuits to engineer self-regulated T cells, engineering immunological cellular ecosystems, genome-scale engineering, and engineering cells for therapeutic applications. Other duties will include teaching of students at the graduate and/or undergraduate levels and supervision of trainees. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The BSD, UCCCC, DOM, and PME bring together world renowned clinicians, scientists, and bioengineers who are focused on conquering cancer and related diseases. Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art core facilities and to outstanding colleagues and graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs within the BSD and the PME. Faculty in the BSD and the PME collaborate widely across their respective Departments, with other Divisions, Schools, and Institutes at the University of Chicago, and with researchers at the Argonne National Laboratory and the Marine Biological Laboratory. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must hold a doctoral degree or equivalent in a field related to biomedicine, cell or molecular biology, synthetic biology, bioengineering, or related discipline. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/165908 . Applicants must upload: 1) CV with bibliography; 2) cover letter, 3) research statement; 4) teaching statement; 5) up to five selected publications; 6) and contact information for three references. Review of complete applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/160151": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine #PED205", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine #PED205. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Apr 3, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine, seeks part-time Clinical Associates at 10% effort for renewable terms of up to one year. Clinical responsibilities will focus on care of children in inpatient and ambulatory settings at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital ambulatory clinics and other offsite ambulatory locations affiliated with the University of Chicago. This position will not have teaching duties and is not benefits eligible. The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a 155-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offers world-class medical care to children from around the Chicagoland area and across the globe. The Pediatric Pulmonary and Sleep Medicine program at the University of Chicago works closely with Pediatric Otolaryngology and Maxillofacial surgery to care for children with complex neonatal craniofacial deformities. Appointees will attend in the Pediatric Sleep outpatient clinics, interpret pediatric polysomnograms (from neonates to adolescents) and provide clinical coverage for inpatient sleep consultation services. The institution has an ACGME-accredited residency program as well as a Sleep Medicine Fellowship with 3-4 clinical fellows annually. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must 1) have a MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent degree, 2) have completed fellowship training in Sleep Medicine, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Sleep Medicine, and 4) have or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois. In addition to Sleep Medicine, expertise in Pediatric Pulmonology, Pediatric Critical Care, Pediatric Neurology or other subspecialties complementary to Sleep Medicine are preferred. Additionally, expertise in nocturnal ventilation management and caring for complex ventilatory disorders is preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/165653 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159839": {"title": "Sleep Psychologists  #PBN062", "description": "Sleep Psychologists #PBN062. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 31, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank for the Sleep Psychology program. Appointees will provide assessments and evidence-based interventions for patients presenting with sleep disorders, interact with multidisciplinary team members (e.g., pulmonology, neurology, otolaryngology) to inform shared treatment plans, and provide clinical and consultative services to sleep medicine providers. They will support clinics including adult and pediatric sleep disorders; and join a multidisciplinary team aiming to expand the reach and effectiveness of the integrated behavioral health program in sleep medicine. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a PhD or PsyD in Clinical Psychology or Counseling Psychology from an APA-accredited university-based doctoral program, 2) hold or be eligible for an Illinois license in Clinical Psychology, and 3) have completed an internship at an APA-accredited site. We especially welcome applicants with training and experience in adult and pediatric psychology (with an emphasis in sleep psychology preferred), behavioral medicine principles, evidence-based treatment for medical and psychiatric conditions, and/or competency in cognitive-behavior therapy, including third wave treatments and motivational enhancement. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/165385 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159765": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Pulmonologists #PED179", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Pulmonologists #PED179. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Pulmonology and Sleep Medicine, seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will focus on care of children in inpatient and ambulatory settings at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital ambulatory clinics and other offsite ambulatory locations affiliated with the University of Chicago. Opportunities for leadership roles may be available to candidates with appropriate skills or experience. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a 155-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offers world-class medical care to children from around the Chicagoland area and across the globe. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and the State of Indiana, 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited Pediatric Pulmonology and/or Sleep Medicine training program, 4) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Pulmonology, and 5) have bronchoscopy experience. We especially welcome applicants with experience as clinical educators and/or conducting research in the field of Pediatric Pulmonology and Sleep Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/165308 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159717": {"title": " Academic Anesthesiologists #ACC075", "description": " Academic Anesthesiologists #ACC075. Department of Anesthesia and Critical Care. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 26, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Anesthesia; Critical Care seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will include general anesthesia and non-operating room anesthesia environments at the University of Chicago Medicine including but not limited to Ingalls Memorial, Crown Point Indiana, and or main campus in Hyde Park. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be a BC/BE eligible anesthesiologist. We especially seek applicants with fellowship training or special interest in cardiac, trauma, neuro, liver transplant, and regional anesthesia. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/165274. Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159422": {"title": "Research-Focused Faculty Positions - Department of Medicine #MED362", "description": "Research-Focused Faculty Positions - Department of Medicine #MED362. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 21, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hospital Medicine, seeks faculty members at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor on the School of Medicine track. Appointees will conduct research in one or more areas of human health, including population and public health, social science, clinical and outcomes research, quantitative methodology, clinical informatics and related areas. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees, medical students and scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Primary appointments will be in the Department of Medicine, although we anticipate that appointees will develop close interactions with other departments, divisions, institutes and centers at the University of Chicago, Argonne National Laboratory and the National Opinion Research Center as part of an effort to strengthen and diversify a collegial group that is interested in major questions relevant to the advancement of human health. The University of Chicago has a tradition of innovative interdisciplinary scholarship in the social sciences applied to health and health care. Discoveries at the interface of health and the social sciences address some of health care\u2019s most critical problems. These include the relationship of individual and population health to social capital and human capital and neighborhood contexts, to the economics, organization and management of social institutions and policies. The Biological Sciences Division, other academic units of the University, and the University of Chicago Medical Center, are all contained within one compact campus in Chicago\u2019s Hyde Park. Appointees will have access to state-of-the-art facilities and to outstanding colleagues and graduate students affiliated with numerous degree-granting programs across the university. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/165001 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, a cover letter, and a research statement. Review of complete applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159173": {"title": " Director, REI / In Vitro Fertilization Laboratory - #OBG085", "description": " Director, REI / In Vitro Fertilization Laboratory - #OBG085. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility is searching for a full-time faculty member at any rank to serve as Director of the In Vitro Fertilization Laboratory (IVF Lab). The appointee will assist with the planning and opening of the new IVF lab at our South Loop Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility (REI) practice location. Primary duties include directing the IVF laboratory, establishing, and developing cutting-edge technology, and supporting the rapidly expanding the IVF/REI laboratory. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a PhD in embryology, clinical science, reproductive biology, or related field, 2) be board certified by the American Board of Bioanalysis (ABB) or equivalent (ELD/HCLD certification), and 3) have a minimum of 5 years of experience working in a human IVF laboratory, with at least 2 years in a leadership role. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/164777 . Applicants must upload a CV, statement of research interest, and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159195": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Endocrinology #MED369", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Endocrinology #MED369. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Mar 18, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Adult and Pediatric Endocrinology, Diabetes, and Metabolism, is searching for full-time Clinical Associates to join an established group of nationally-known endocrinologists. The appointees will specifically focus on inpatient endocrinology and will have inpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus. Inpatient activities will be on the endocrinology consultation service. Compensation will be dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible - for information on benefits, please consult the University of Chicago Benefits Guide: https://mybenefits.nfp.com/UChicago/benefits-guide/ . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Endocrinology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/164791 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159084": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Neurologists #PED191", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Neurologists #PED191. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 14, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neurology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will be assigned in the outpatient, inpatient, and procedural settings at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital and other practice plan sites, to be focused on pediatric patients with neurological disorders. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. A tradition of excellence in patient care, basic, clinical, and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions, provides the foundation for a career as an academic physician. The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a busy urban teaching hospital with a diverse patient population and is located on the same campus as the medical school and the University. The University also has a robust Neuroscience Institute, which brings together more than 90 neuroscientists based in academic and clinical departments across the campus into a cohesive program. The Section of Pediatric Neurology currently has 9 full-time child neurology attendings and aims to expand in the coming years. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana and 3) be board certified or board eligible in Neurology with Special Qualification in Child Neurology. We especially welcome applicants who have Pediatric Neurology Fellowship training. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/164695 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/159134": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Allergy and Immunology #PED133a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Allergy and Immunology #PED133a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 14, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Allergy, Immunology and Pediatric Pulmonology seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will focus on clinical activity for both children and adults in ambulatory settings including the University of Chicago campus in Hyde Park and offsite ambulatory locations. Appointees will be collaborative physician leaders positioned to succeed in a busy, dynamic environment and advance the clinical, educational, and scholarly goals of the Section of Allergy, Immunology and Pediatric Pulmonology. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . UChicago Medicine is an 811-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offers world-class medical care to adults and children from around the Chicagoland area and across the globe. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics has recently formed the Advocate NorthShore Pediatric Partnership. Together, this partnership cares for approximately one million children across three states. The Food Allergy program at the University of Chicago leads a robust portfolio of basic and clinical/translational research benefiting both children and adults. Appointees will take a prominent role in the translational research program. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) be certified or eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited Allergy/Immunology training program and be Board certified or Board eligible in Allergy/Immunology. We especially welcome applicants with a clinical and research interest in the field of Asthma. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https:// apply.interfolio.com/164742 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, as well as cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/158875": {"title": "Academic Cardiologists \u2013 General Cardiology/Cardio-Oncology #MED365", "description": "Academic Cardiologists \u2013 General Cardiology/Cardio-Oncology #MED365. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 5, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank in the area of general and preventive cardiology, with experience or interest in Cardio-Oncology. Appointees will have clinical responsibilities including outpatient clinic with an emphasis on Cardio-Oncology, reading EKGs, and general cardiology inpatient ward and consultative cardiology predominantly at our main campus and, if needed, at affiliated sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, (2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the state of Illinois, and (3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Cardiology. We additionally welcome applicants with a background in pharmacology, translational or outcomes research and/or clinical trials and registries. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/164506 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/158109": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Palliative Care #PED118a ", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Palliative Care #PED118a . Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 3, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Critical Care Medicine, seeks full-time faculty members at any rank for its growing Palliative Care Service. Appointees will provide consultation on inpatient and select outpatient areas with a focus on care of children with life-limiting illness. Other duties include active contribution to teaching and supervision of medical students, residents and fellows, nursing staff, and faculty, as well as scholarly activity. Opportunities for leadership roles may be available to candidates with appropriate skills or experience. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Palliative Care Team is housed in the Section of Pediatric Critical Care, which has 16 full-time faculty members, a fully accredited fellowship program, and a 30 bed PICU. The Section also runs the Pediatric Sedation and Palliative Care Services. Faculty members are actively involved in research, quality improvement, and medical education initiatives. The PICU has approximately 1600 annual admissions with continued annual increase in volume over the past 5 years. We are the only level I Pediatric Trauma Center on the South Side of Chicago and the hospital is designated a Magnet hospital by the American Nurses Credentialing Center, a national honor that recognizes nursing excellence. Additionally, we are an ELSO Center of Excellence at the Gold Level. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a MD, MD/PhD, or equivalent degree(s); 2) have or be eligible for permanent medical licensure in the State of Illinois; and 3) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Palliative Medicine We especially welcome applicants with an academic interest and/or potential for research-based program in a field relevant to clinical practice, medical education, quality improvement, and/or policy-based interventions. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/163852 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/158447": {"title": "Supportive Oncology Psychiatrists #PBN059", "description": "Supportive Oncology Psychiatrists #PBN059. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Mar 3, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will contribute to psychiatric care of the Supportive Oncology program, including inpatient and outpatient care, providing assessments and consultations for hematology and oncology patients and their families. They will also contribute to the continued growth of the Supportive Oncology Psychiatry program. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Psychiatry, and 4) have residency training in Adult Psychiatry. We especially welcome applicants with leadership experience who are also familiar with being an integral part of a multidisciplinary care team who have experience contributing to the growth and development of the Psychosocial Oncology Program. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/164115 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/158318": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Neurologists #PED201", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Neurologists #PED201. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 27, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Neurology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Clinical responsibilities will be assigned in the outpatient, inpatient, and procedural settings at the University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital and other practice plan sites, to be focused on pediatric patients with neurological disorders. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will join a vibrant and distinguished community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. A tradition of excellence in patient care, basic, clinical, and translational sciences, combined with the strengths of one of the world's top academic institutions, provides the foundation for a career as an academic physician. The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital is a busy urban teaching hospital with a diverse patient population and is located on the same campus as the medical school and the University. The University also has a robust Neuroscience Institute, which brings together more than 90 neuroscientists based in academic and clinical departments across the campus into a cohesive program. The Section of Pediatric Neurology currently has 9 full-time child neurology attendings and aims to expand in the coming years, with a focus on neurofibromatosis and neuromuscular disorders. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana and 3) be board certified or board eligible in Neurology with Special Qualification in Child Neurology. We especially welcome applicants who have Fellowship training as well as candidates with expertise in neurofibromatosis and/or neuromuscular disorders. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/163993 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157277": {"title": "Director of Supportive Oncology #MED359", "description": "Director of Supportive Oncology #MED359. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 27, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Geriatrics and Palliative Medicine, is searching for a full-time Associate Professor or Professor on the School of Medicine track to serve as Director of Supportive Oncology. The appointee will be responsible for the development, expansion, management, and alignment of services reflected in a patient\u2019s cancer journey as we further broaden our cancer service line, including additional network locations and the new cancer center building in Hyde Park. The appointee will further develop integrative medicine capabilities to ensure that our patients receive comprehensive cancer care. These include coordination with social services, therapy services, nutrition, medical oncology, radiation oncology, integrative medicine, lifestyle medicine, surgical oncology, psychiatry, psychology, case management, palliative and hospice care, survivorship, and community-based support services. This position will also support the addition of ambulatory and inpatient capacity in the Section of Palliative Medicine, which intends to grow as oncology broadens its footprint across the health system. The appointee will provide inpatient and outpatient palliative medicine consultation. Other duties include leading quality improvement initiatives and contributing to scholarship as well as participating in the education of trainees. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board-Certified or Board-Eligible in Hospice and Palliative Medicine. We especially welcome applicants with experience in program development, operations and growth of supportive oncology programs and/or with previous leadership experience over such programs. Those with qualifications in integrative or lifestyle medicine are also welcome to apply. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/163026 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will end after the position has been filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/158156": {"title": "Pediatric Otolaryngologists #SUR171", "description": "Pediatric Otolaryngologists #SUR171. Section of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 20, 2025. The University of Chicago's Section of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The successful appointees will provide Pediatric Otolaryngology care. Appointees will have responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Otolaryngology- Head and Neck Surgery at the Hyde Park location and affiliated off-sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery is committed to medical education, research, patient care, and serving our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or are eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and Indiana 3) are Board certified or eligible in Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery or equivalent and 4) have completed an accredited Residency in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery and an accredited Pediatric Otolaryngology fellowship or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/163834. Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157633": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Non-Malignant Hematology #MED280b", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Non-Malignant Hematology #MED280b. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 19, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for full-time faculty members at any rank who specialize in the treatment of non-malignant hematology. The appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and possibly at our network locations. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Hematology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/163351 . Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/158167": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Solid Tumor Cancers #MED252a", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Solid Tumor Cancers #MED252a. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 19, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, and the University of Chicago Medicine Comprehensive Cancer Center are searching for highly clinical full-time faculty members at the Assistant Professor rank who specialize in the treatment of solid tumor cancers. The appointees will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities at our Hyde Park campus and at network locations. Other duties will include the teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Oncology. T o be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/163850 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157957": {"title": "Adult Congenital Heart Disease Cardiologists #PED203", "description": "Adult Congenital Heart Disease Cardiologists #PED203. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 18, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Cardiology, and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is searching for full-time Adult Congenital Heart Disease specialists at any rank. The appointees will have a primary academic appointment in the Department of Pediatrics with a secondary academic appointment in the Department of Medicine. Responsibilities include outpatient clinics, inpatient consultations, procedures, and reading cardio-diagnostic testing as applies to the specialty field. Other duties will include scholarly activity, and training and supervision of trainees and students throughout the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance Cardiovascular service line. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance (CCHA) is a partnership between Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital, and Endeavor Health. CCHA supports patients\u2019 and families\u2019 needs for the highest level of care with easier access to our network of adult congenital heart disease specialists throughout Chicago and the surrounding Chicagoland area. The multidisciplinary program includes faculty members in the departments of medicine, pediatrics, and surgery. The Chicago Adult Congenital Heart Disease Alliance is one of the largest programs in the state of Illinois, seeing over 2800 patients annually with clinics throughout Chicago, the suburbs, and Northern Indiana. It is a pillar of the overarching Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance Heart Program, which is among the nation\u2019s top Children\u2019s heart programs in the country. This larger program includes 30 attending cardiologists, 6 categorical cardiology fellows, and several additional advanced fellows, four pediatric cardio-thoracic surgeons, and a dedicated cardiac anesthesia service. Our cardiac surgeons perform over 400 cardiac operations per year and achieve outcomes that rank among the best in the nation (STS-CHS-Database). We also have one of the highest ambulatory clinic volumes in the country with clinic sites throughout Chicago, the Chicago suburbs, Northern Illinois, and Northern Indiana. Our cardiac program is one of the largest in the Midwest and provides expertise in echocardiography, advanced cardiac imaging CT/MRI, electrophysiology, interventional cardiac catheterization, fetal cardiology, adult congenital heart disease, Marfan Syndrome, Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy, cardiac critical care, and preventative and rehabilitative cardiology. Prior to the start of employment, an individual must possess the following qualifications: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, (2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the state of Illinois (3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Adult Congenital Heart Disease. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/163657 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157198": {"title": "Faculty Scientist \u2013 The David and Etta Jonas Center for Cellular Therapy #MED350", "description": "Faculty Scientist \u2013 The David and Etta Jonas Center for Cellular Therapy #MED350. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 14, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hematology/Oncology, is searching for a faculty scientist at any rank, on the School of Medicine track, who will lead research contributing to the mission of The David and Etta Jonas Center for Cellular Therapy. In collaboration with other investigators in the Jonas Center, the appointee will apply their scientific background in basic and translational research to the development of therapeutic T cell receptors (TCR) against neoantigens, especially non-synonymous Single Nucleotide Variants expressed in cancers, particularly those of the pancreas and the ovary. The appointee should be conversant with methods of T cell cloning, transduction, and transfection, as well as related techniques. In addition, the appointee will serve as the Scientific Director of the Jonas Center Tumor Biobank. The goal of this research is to develop personalized, cancer-specific therapy, involving the selection and characterization of therapeutic TCRs for cancer-specific adoptive transfer. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent and have completed at least one year of postdoctoral training. Experience utilizing human tissue samples is required. We especially welcome applicants with experience in the identification, analysis, and selection of TCRs against neoantigens. Experience with murine tumor models is encouraged. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/162961 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, a cover letter, and a research statement. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157269": {"title": "Team Scientists -- Translational Data Science #MED354", "description": "Team Scientists -- Translational Data Science #MED354. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 14, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine is searching for full-time faculty members on the School of Medicine track, at any rank, to join the Center for Translational Data Science, which focuses on the application of data science to research problems in biology, medicine and healthcare). We seek team scientists who will lead research focusing on: a) translational data science; b) the development of data commons, data ecosystem and other cloud computing platforms, systems and applications to support translational data science; and/or c) the development of machine learning, statistical, bioinformatics and AI algorithms to support translational data science. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a doctoral degree or equivalent, in the following fields: Biomedical Data Science, Computer Science, Data Science, Informational Technology, Biomedical Informatics, or a related field. We especially welcome applicants whose research is broadly focused in the areas of computer science, bioinformatics, data science, mathematics, information technology, genetics, genomics, and/or systems biology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/163014 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography, a cover letter, and a research statement. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157635": {"title": "Breast Pathologist #PAT050", "description": "Breast Pathologist #PAT050. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 13, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Pathology is searching for a full-time faculty member at any rank to serve as a Breast Pathologist. Limited involvement in another field of surgical pathology or in molecular pathology can be accommodated if the candidate has the appropriate training or experience. The successful candidate will sign out on the in-house and consult breast services and will also participate on the general frozen section service. The other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Opportunities for translational research are available. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) possess an MD degree or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible to hold a medical license in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) have board certification or eligibility in Anatomic Pathology or Anatomic Pathology/Clinical Pathology. We especially welcome applicants with fellowship training or previous experience in Breast Pathology. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/163353. Applicants must upload: CV including bibliography, cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157244": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED358", "description": "Clinical Associates - Emergency Medicine #MED358. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Feb 13, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks Clinical Associates at 50% effort for a renewable term of up to three years. The appointees will have responsibilities in patient care and clinical operations. The University of Chicago Medicine is a STEMI-receiving hospital, a Comprehensive Stroke Center, a Burn Center, and a Chicago South EMS regional resource hospital. The Adult ED has an annual volume of 75,000 and we are a Level 1 Adult and Pediatric Trauma Center. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board-certified or board-eligible in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/162995 . Applicants must upload: CV with bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will begin after February 20, 2025 and ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/157204": {"title": "Surgical Oncologists #SUR169", "description": "Surgical Oncologists #SUR169. Section of General Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Feb 12, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of General Surgery, is seeking full-time Clinical Associates at 100% effort for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will provide care for breast, endocrine, and melanoma surgery patients in both the inpatient and outpatient setting. Practice location is Ascension St. Joseph Hospital - Chicago. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 800 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. New ambulatory facilities are located in Orland Park and River East, and the Ingalls Health System has recently integrated into the network. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Surgery, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship in General Surgical Oncology or equivalent training. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/162966 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends on [OAA to insert date]. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/156654": {"title": "Part-Time (Pay-Per Shift) Clinical Associates - Pediatric General Surgery #SUR137A", "description": "Part-Time (Pay-Per Shift) Clinical Associates - Pediatric General Surgery #SUR137A. Section of Pediatric Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Feb 11, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Pediatric Surgery, is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 30% effort for terms of up to one year. Appointees will contribute pediatric inpatient consultations and trauma call coverage responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Pediatric Surgery at Comer Children\u2019s Hospital and affiliated offsite locations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is paid per clinic and does not include benefits. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board-certified or Board-eligible by the American Board of Surgery (or equivalent) in Pediatric Surgery, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship and/or equivalent training in Pediatric Surgery. We especially welcome applicants who, in addition to satisfying these requirements, are Board-certified or Board-eligible in Surgical Critical Care, or eligible for fellowship training in Surgical Critical Care. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/162484. Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications will begin after February 24, 2025. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/156457": {"title": "Adult Psychiatrists #PBN058", "description": "Adult Psychiatrists #PBN058. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Feb 4, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will have outpatient responsibilities for the care of a range of psychiatric disorders, such as mood, anxiety and psychotic disorders. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Psychiatry, and 4) have residency training in Adult Psychiatry. We especially welcome applicants familiar with outpatient clinical work and open to developing intensive outpatient programs as well as interventional psychiatry and/or forensic experience. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http:// apply.interfolio.com/162303 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/156927": {"title": "Director, Adult Director of the Adult Cardiac Magnetic Resonance/Computed Tomography Programs #MED352a", "description": "Director, Adult Director of the Adult Cardiac Magnetic Resonance/Computed Tomography Programs #MED352a. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 31, 2025. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is searching for a full-time Associate Professor or Professor to serve as the Director of the Adult Cardiac Magnetic Resonance/Computed Tomography (CMR/CCT) programs. The appointee will join and lead an established group of nationally and internationally renowned physicians, researchers and educators in the field of cardiac CMR/CCT imaging. In conjunction with the Section of Cardiology, other leaders in the Multimodality Cardiac Imaging Laboratory, and the Heart and Vascular Center, the Director is expected to implement a plan that will expand clinical and translational research, develop strategies for increasing study volumes, and oversee the expansion of advanced cardiac imaging to multiple satellite cardiac imaging sites. Clinical responsibilities will include interpretation of CMR/CCT studies, inpatient and ambulatory cardiology clinical services, and supervision of satellite cardiac imaging laboratories. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of fellows, residents and students, as well as scholarly activities. Academic rank is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Multimodality Cardiac Imaging Group at the University of Chicago has a long-standing tradition of excellence in a full range of research activities as well as diagnostic and therapeutic procedures. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate (MD) or equivalent degree, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be board certified in Cardiology. We especially welcome applicants with board certification in MRI and CT. We also welcome applicants with leadership experience in managing complex clinical operations with a track record of clinical and research success in cardiac imaging. Additionally, we welcome applicants with experience and understanding of other cardiac imaging modalities, namely Cardiac Ultrasound and Nuclear Cardiology is preferable. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/162720 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/156148": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Rheumatology #MED360", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Rheumatology #MED360. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Jan 29, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Rheumatology, is seeking full-time rheumatologists for appointments as Clinical Associate for renewable terms of up to two years. Appointees will primarily see outpatients in the general rheumatology clinic at our Harvey, IL and/or Tinley, IL site and have a half-day clinic onsite in the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM). Occasionally, appointees might be asked to serve on the inpatient consult service at our main campus in Hyde Park. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in rheumatology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/162015 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after February 8, 2025 and ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/156493": {"title": "Neurotologists #SUR168", "description": "Neurotologists #SUR168. Section of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Jan 28, 2025. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Otolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will provide Otology, Neurotology, and Lateral Skull Base care. Appointees will have responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in Otolaryngology at the Hyde Park location and affiliated off-sites. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. T his position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Section of Otolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery is committed to medical education, research, patient care, and serving our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the States of Illinois and Indiana, and 3) be board certified or eligible by the American Board of Otolaryngology or equivalent. We especially welcome applicants who have completed an accredited residency in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery and accredited Neurotology Fellowship or equivalent. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/162337 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/155199": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Allergy/Immunologists #PED194 ", "description": "Academic Physicians - Allergy/Immunologists #PED194 . Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 20, 2024. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Allergy, Immunology and Pediatric Pulmonology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will serve as Pediatric and Adult Allergy/Immunology physicians and will lead our Primary Immunodeficiency Program and focus on the clinical activity of both children and adults in ambulatory locations affiliated with the University of Chicago Medical Center, Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, and our partnership with Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital. Appointees will work with the members of the Hematology/Oncology service line to manage the significant immunodeficiency population across the combined partnership enterprise. In addition, they will develop an academic niche that builds on our faculty expertise in basic and translational immunology, host defense, novel therapies or other areas that relate to the mission of the Department of Pediatrics. Appointees will be collaborative physician leaders positioned to succeed in a busy, dynamic environment and advance the clinical, educational and scholarly goals of the Section of Allergy, Immunology & Pediatric Pulmonology. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. The University of Chicago Comer Children\u2019s Hospital is a 165-bed facility with state-of-the-art technology and amenities that offer world-class medical care to children from around the Chicagoland area and across the globe. The University of Chicago Department of Pediatrics has recently formed the Advocate NorthShore Pediatric Partnership for Hematology/Oncology and Cardiology service lines. Together, this partnership cares for approximately one million children across three states. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an ACGME-accredited Allergy/Immunology training program, and 4) be Board certified or Board eligible in Allergy/Immunology. We especially welcome applicants with experience as a clinical educator and/or conducting research in the field of Allergy/Immunology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/161151 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/155145": {"title": "Maternal Fetal Medicine Physicians \u2013 #OBG084", "description": "Maternal Fetal Medicine Physicians \u2013 #OBG084. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 19, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will contribute to all clinical aspects of the multidisciplinary Obgyn Ultrasound Unit, Obgyn Genetics program, and the Fetal Neonatal Care Center, including the evaluation and management of prenatal patients with fetal anomalies, inpatient, and outpatient care, and a productive clinical portfolio. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. We are an academic section with a high outpatient obstetrical volume, consultation service, and a large ultrasound unit with a fetal center, in addition to being a tertiary referral center for surrounding hospitals through our perinatal network. Other features of our program include strong Obgyn ultrasound and fetal care research, a placenta center, obstetrical quality, an OB anesthesia program, as well as our state of art Labor and Delivery unit. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited residency in OB/GYN, 4) have completed an accredited fellowship in MFM, or have equivalent experience, and 5) be eligible or board certified in MFM by ABOG or medical genetics by ABMGG. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/161102 . Applicants must upload a CV with bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/155149": {"title": "Maternal Fetal Medicine Physicians - #OBG083", "description": "Maternal Fetal Medicine Physicians - #OBG083. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 19, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will contribute to all clinical aspects of the multidisciplinary Maternal-Fetal Medicine (MFM) program, including the evaluation and management of high-risk pregnancy patients, inpatient and outpatient care, and a productive clinical portfolio. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent on qualifications. We are an academic section with a high outpatient obstetrical volume, consultation service, and a large ultrasound unit with a fetal center, in addition to being a tertiary referral center for surrounding hospitals through our perinatal network. Other features of our program include strong preeclampsia research, a placenta center, obstetrical quality, an OB anesthesia program, as well as our exemplary Labor and Delivery unit. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited fellowship in MFM or have equivalent experience, and 4) be Board certified or eligible in MFM. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/161104 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/154764": {"title": "Gynecologic Oncology Faculty \u2013 #OBG082", "description": "Gynecologic Oncology Faculty \u2013 #OBG082. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 13, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Gynecologic Oncology, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will be responsible for the comprehensive clinical care of patients with gynecologic cancers including outpatient assessment, inpatient care, and major operative procedures. The majority of the appointees\u2019 clinical time will be dedicated to supporting practice in Crown Point and Orland Park. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) have completed a fellowship in Gynecologic Oncology, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology, and 4) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/160750 . Applicants must upload a CV including a bibliography and a cover letter. The review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/154264": {"title": "Abdominal Transplant Surgeons #SUR163A", "description": "Abdominal Transplant Surgeons #SUR163A. Section of Transplant Surgery. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Dec 6, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Transplant Surgery, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. These positions are oriented toward multi-organ practice with primary focus on liver transplantation. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. The Transplant Institute has nationally recognized research and education programs that build on collaborations throughout the University and is active in clinical operations and quality improvement in the Medical Center. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible by the American Board of Surgery or the foreign equivalent. We especially welcome applicants who have completed an ASTS fellowship or equivalent and have at least five years of practice as faculty at the Assistant/Associate Professor rank. Additionally, we welcome applicants with strong translational research backgrounds and high potential to achieve independent funding. Experience in living and deceased donor liver transplantation, organ perfusion, and other surgical and transplant procedures preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/160290 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/153988": {"title": "Emergency Medicine - Clinical Associates  #MED306a", "description": "Emergency Medicine - Clinical Associates #MED306a. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Nov 27, 2024. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. Clinical responsibilities include evaluating and treating patients at the newly built Emergency Department at Crown Point Hospital, IN. The Crown Point Emergency Department comprises an 8-bed unit with a projected annual volume of approximately 8,000 patient encounters in its first year. These positions offer opportunities to treat patients at our main campus in Chicago, IL, if desired. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent upon qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Indiana and Illinois, and 3) be BC/BE in Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/160047 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after December 7th, 2024 and ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/153867": {"title": "Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility Physicians - OBG081", "description": "Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility Physicians - OBG081. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 26, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Section of Reproductive Endocrinology Infertility (REI), is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will be responsible for outpatient clinical care at satellite locations, including in vitro fertilization (IVF) coverage and availability to perform reproductive surgeries. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. We deliver high-quality fertility services to diverse populations. In addition to providing the full breadth of reproductive medicine and assisted reproductive technology (ART) services, we encourage and support faculty members to develop a niche expertise within the REI field. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited fellowship in REI or have equivalent experience, and 4) be Board certified or eligible in REI. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: apply.interfolio.com/159933 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the lnterfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/lnterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/153151": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Emergency Medicine #PED105b", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Emergency Medicine #PED105b. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Nov 21, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Emergency Medicine, seeks part-time Clinical Associates for renewable one-year terms as Primary Attending at our Pediatric Level I Trauma accredited Emergency Room at Comer Children's Hospital. These positions require collaborative physicians who will be positioned to succeed in a busy, dynamic environment. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on clinical shifts worked. UChicago Medicine is located on the main University campus in Chicago's Hyde Park neighborhood. Comer Children's Hospital is a state of art free standing children's hospital within the medical complex. The Pediatric Emergency Room treats more than 35,000 patients per year and is the only Level I Pediatric Trauma Center on the south side of Chicago. It is a newly renovated facility with 22 general exam rooms, two procedure rooms, two triage rooms and two trauma/resuscitation rooms. The Section of Pediatric Emergency Medicine is committed to medical education, clinical research, patient care, and community service. The section has a full accredited fellowship program and a nurse practitioner program. Its faculty members are actively involved in scholarship and training. The program has active pediatrics, med/peds and emergency medicine residency programs, and precepts University of Chicago Pritzker School of Medicine medical students. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and 3) be Board certified in Pediatrics and/or be Board certified or Board eligible in Pediatric Emergency Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/159272 . Applicants must upload a CV. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/153247": {"title": "Pediatric Urologists #SUR164", "description": "Pediatric Urologists #SUR164. Section of Urology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 21, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Surgery, Section of Urology, seeks full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will have inpatient and outpatient responsibilities to support and complement the faculty in the Section of Urology. Responsibilities will include patient care and teaching at The University of Chicago - Comer Children's Hospital and at affiliated off-site locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine totaling more than 550 beds in service. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and Indiana, 3) be board certified or eligible by the American Board of Urology or equivalent, and 4) have completed an accredited fellowship or equivalent training in Pediatric Urology. We especially welcome applicants with an interest in academic medicine and with experience in laparoscopic and robotic surgery. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/159353 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/153331": {"title": "Maternal Fetal Medicine Physicians - #OBG080", "description": "Maternal Fetal Medicine Physicians - #OBG080. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 20, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will contribute to all clinical aspects of the multidisciplinary Maternal-Fetal Medicine (MFM) program at Ingalls Memorial Hospital and the Hyde Park campus, including the evaluation and management of high-risk pregnancy patients, inpatient and outpatient care, and a productive clinical portfolio. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees, medical students and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent on qualifications. We are an academic section with a high outpatient obstetrical volume, consultation service, and a large ultrasound unit with a fetal center, in addition to being a tertiary referral center for surrounding hospitals through our perinatal network. Other features of our program include strong preeclampsia research, a placenta center, obstetrical quality, an OB anesthesia program, as well as our exemplary Labor and Delivery unit. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed an accredited fellowship in MFM or have equivalent experience, and 4) be Board certified or eligible in MFM. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/159426 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152743": {"title": "Gastrointestinal Pathologists #PAT046", "description": "Gastrointestinal Pathologists #PAT046. Department of Pathology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 12, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Pathology invites applications for full-time Gastrointestinal Pathologists at any rank. Appointees will participate on the gastrointestinal pathology service and contribute to the frozen section service and autopsy service. Participation on the Molecular Pathology or Cytopathology services may also be accommodated for candidates with fellowship training in either of these two areas. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of residents, GI pathology fellows, and medical students. Opportunities for translational research are available. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) possess an MD degree or equivalent, 2) have board certification or eligibility in Anatomic Pathology, and 3) hold or be eligible to hold a medical license in the States of Illinois and Indiana. We especially welcome applicants with fellowship training in GI pathology. To be considered, those interested must apply through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158899 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/150661": {"title": "Associate Director - Cardiac Arrhythmia Center (Electrophysiology) #MED344", "description": "Associate Director - Cardiac Arrhythmia Center (Electrophysiology) #MED344. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 12, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is seeking a full-time faculty member at any rank to join an established group of nationally known physicians and surgeons in the Cardiac Arrhythmia Center (Electrophysiology) within the Heart and Vascular Center. The appointee will have clinical responsibilities predominantly at our main campus in Hyde Park and, if needed, at affiliated off-sites. Clinical responsibilities include performing cardiac electrophysiology procedures and doing outpatient cardiology clinic, inpatient service, and consultation. Additionally, the Associate Director will assist the Director of Electrophysiology in creating a strategy for growth of the cardiac electrophysiology subsection. They will also work with the Director to oversee the day-to-day management of the cardiac electrophysiology labs and the outpatient and inpatient cardiac electrophysiology services. The appointee will work to grow the research footprint of the subsection by increasing participation and enrolment in clinical trials, as well as close interaction with the basic scientists involved in cardiac EP research. The appointee will work collaboratively with UCM offsite facilities (e.g. Crown Point, Orland Park, River East) and our network partners (e.g. Advent Health) to offsite growth of the cardiac electrophysiology practice. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. Cardiac electrophysiology at the University of Chicago has a long tradition of clinical and academic excellence. A full range of diagnostic and therapeutic procedures are provided as well as two new state-of-the-art , dedicated clinical electrophysiology laboratories. The program is currently accredited to train up to 3 ACGME-accredited clinical cardiac electrophysiology fellows each year. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be ABIM Certified in Cardiovascular Disease and Clinical Cardiac Electrophysiology. We especially welcome applicants who specialize in therapeutic approaches for atrial and ventricular arrhythmias, device implantations, and lead management, and have experience with outreach patient referral activities. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/156982 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152726": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Critical Care #PED103a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Critical Care #PED103a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 10, 2024. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Pediatrics, Section of Critical Care is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will provide clinical coverage for the Comer Children's Hospital PICU and Pediatric Sedation Service. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. The Section of Pediatric Critical Care has 16 full-time faculty members, a fully accredited fellowship program, and a 30 bed PICU. The Section also runs the Pediatric Sedation and Palliative Care Services. Faculty members are actively involved in research, quality improvement, and medical education initiatives. The PICU has approximately 1,600 annual admissions with continued annual increase in volume over the past 5 years. The University of Chicago is the only Level I Pediatric Trauma Center on the South Side of Chicago, and the hospital is designated a Magnet hospital by the American Nurses Credentialing Center, a national honor that recognizes nursing excellence. Additionally, we are an Extracorporeal Life Support Organization (ELSO) Center of Excellence at the Gold Level. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Pediatric Critical Care Medicine, 3) be eligible for permanent medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 4) have cared for complex and critically ill infants, children, and adolescents during the past five years. We especially welcome applicants with an academic interest and/or potential to conduct research-based programs in a field relevant to neurocritical care, resuscitation and simulation, informatics, quality improvement science and scholarship, and/or policy-based interventions. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158887 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152675": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Emergency Medicine #PED114a", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Emergency Medicine #PED114a. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 8, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Emergency Medicine, in the Biological Sciences Division is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. Appointees will serve as Pediatric Emergency Medicine Physicians in our Pediatric Level I Trauma-accredited emergency room at The University of Chicago Comer Children's Hospital. In addition, they will develop an academic niche that builds on our faculty expertise in medical education, simulation, quality improvement, health equity and bioethics research, health outcomes, disaster preparedness, child abuse, trauma, or other areas that relate to the mission of the Department of Pediatrics and Comer Children's Hospital. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. The University of Chicago cares for many children, adolescents and young adults with cancer and non-malignant blood diseases as part of the CCHA. Care is provided at Comer Children's Hospital, our outpatient clinic in Hyde Park, and at CCHA satellite clinics across the Chicagoland region. The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. Located in historic Hyde Park, the primary medical campus includes the Center for Care and Discovery, Comer Children's Hospital, Bernard A. Mitchell Hospital for adult inpatient care, and the Duchossois Center for Advanced Medicine (DCAM) totaling more than 550 beds in service and net patient care revenues over $1.4 billion. New ambulatory facilities are located in Orland Park and the South Loop, and the Ingalls Health System has recently integrated into the network. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or Board eligible in Pediatric Emergency Medicine. We especially welcome applicants with experience in Level I trauma, medical education, simulation, and health outcomes research. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158840 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152671": {"title": "Faculty (Open Rank) - AI/Computation   Energy", "description": "Faculty (Open Rank) - AI/Computation Energy. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Nov 7, 2024. The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) is a unique interdisciplinary school launched by the University of Chicago in 2011 with the aim of translating molecular and quantum science into technological solutions with societal impact in health and immunology, energy/water/materials, environmental sustainability, and information processing. PME is also closely affiliated with Argonne National Laboratory, and joint appointment is encouraged when appropriate. The University of Chicago\u2019s Institute for Climate and Sustainable Growth (ICSG) is a groundbreaking effort uniting faculty from across the university to advance society\u2019s understanding of our energy and climate future. The Institute combines faculty insights in energy markets and policy, climate systems engineering, and energy technology to produce world-changing ideas while also investing in the next generation of energy and climate thinkers, leaders, and innovators through novel educational programs. The University of Chicago\u2019s Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering and the Institute for Climate and Sustainable Growth seek applicants for multiple open rank \u2013 tenure-track assistant professor or tenured associate or full professor \u2013 appointments at the intersection of artificial intelligence/computation, materials and energy. Tenure lines will be housed within the PME, or jointly between PME and another unit. Successful candidates will have the opportunity to secure an affiliation with the UChicago Institute for Climate and Sustainable Growth and the Data Science Institute (DSI). The hire is part of university wide multi-disciplinary strategic initiatives in the areas of climate and sustainable growth, AI and data science, and materials for energy and sustainability. Successful candidates\u2019 primary research focus is expected to be computational and theoretical in nature, and one that combines AI and science-based approaches across a variety of disciplines, although applicants possessing experimental components to their research program will be considered. The PME is a collaborative and interdisciplinary environment that promotes deep interaction opportunities between theoretical/computational and experimental research groups. Candidates with interest in topics that span across the stack--from materials to systems and their integration \u2013are encouraged to apply. Topics may include, but are not restricted to: AI emulators and foundational models for physical systems relevant to energy; quantum and classical simulations of material and molecular properties; battery and electrochemical science & engineering; processes , systems and economic mechanisms leading to energy efficient performance and green manufacturing; statistical/machine learning/econometric methods for assessing the impact of new technologies; energy efficient information processing (including bio-inspired, classical, quantum and sensor network based); machine learning methods for solving analyzing integrative models; and carbon conversion and related technologies. Before the start of employment, qualified applicants must hold a doctoral degree in a relevant field of study and have a strong record of independent research. Candidates for Associate or Full Professor must have evidence of leadership in their field and an outstanding track record of independent research, allowing them to qualify for appointment as a tenured professor in the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (or jointly with another department in the University). All successful candidates will be expected to establish and maintain a robust research program and teach at the graduate and undergraduate levels. Candidates should apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website: http://apply.interfolio.com/158838 . Applicants must upload: Cover letter CV including bibliography Research statement (a statement describing past and current research accomplishments and outlining future research plans) Teaching statement. This statement may also include the candidate's experience in teaching diverse students. The University's Diversity Statement can be found at https://provost.uchicago.edu/statements-diversity . Contact information for three references who can provide confidential letters of evaluation The following materials are optional: Up to three published or unpublished research papers Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. Please contact Daphne Gavins at ddgavins@uchicago.edu with any questions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152397": {"title": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates \u2013 Hospital Medicine #MED162d", "description": "Pay-Per-Shift Clinical Associates \u2013 Hospital Medicine #MED162d. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Nov 6, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hospital Medicine, seeks Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years at up to 10% effort. The appointees will provide direct patient care, including managing patients with complex sub-specialty needs, which will involve night and weekend coverage. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent on qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Internal or Hospital Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158579 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after November 16, 2024. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152139": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Interventional Cardiologists  #MED349", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Interventional Cardiologists #MED349. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 5, 2024. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is seeking full-time faculty members for appointment at any rank to join our nationally recognized adult Cardiac Catheterization Laboratory. The appointees will participate in the growth of the Heart and Vascular Institute and have access to state-of-the-art technology and a programmatic approach to revascularization which includes close collaboration with vascular and cardiothoracic surgery. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. Appointees will share responsibilities across a broad range of diagnostic and therapeutic procedures, in addition to staffing the Cardiac Intensive Care Unit and providing outpatient clinical care on our main campus, Orland Park, River East and other off sites. Other duties will include training and supervision of trainees and students and scholarly activity. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: (1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, (2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the state of Illinois, (3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Cardiology, (4) have completed an ACGME-accredited Interventional Cardiology Fellowship, and (5) be eligible or have passed the Added Qualification Examination in Interventional Cardiology. We especially welcome applicants with experience and/or training in peripheral vascular intervention, complex coronary revascularization and mechanical circulatory/cardiopulmonary support. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158335 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152252": {"title": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Hospital Medicine    #MED273a", "description": "Academic Physicians \u2013 Hospital Medicine #MED273a. Department of Medicine. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Nov 5, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hospital Medicine, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will direct patient care including managing hospitalized patients with complex sub-specialty needs. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. The Section of Hospital Medicine has nationally recognized research and education programs that build on collaborations throughout the University and is active in clinical operations and quality improvement in the Medical Center. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in Internal or Hospital Medicine, and 4) have cared for hospitalized general medical patients with multiple and complex co-morbidities. We especially welcome applicants with advanced training in research methodology and who have conducted research relevant to hospital medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158438 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152247": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Hospital Medicine #MED159d", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Hospital Medicine #MED159d. Department of Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Nov 4, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Hospital Medicine, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years. The appointees will provide direct patient care, including managing patients with complex sub-specialty needs, which will involve night and weekend coverage. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) is dependent on qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Internal or Hospital Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/158436 . Applicants must upload a curriculum vitae and a cover letter. Review of applications will begin after November 14, 2024. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/152259": {"title": "Director of the Becker Friedman Institute of Economics", "description": "Director of the Becker Friedman Institute of Economics. The University of Chicago. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Nov 1, 2024. The Becker Friedman Institute for Economics (BFI) is a leading center that brings together the entire University of Chicago economics community to foster novel insights on the world\u2019s most difficult economic problems. A collaboration between the University of Chicago Booth School of Business, the Kenneth C. Griffin Department of Economics, the Harris School of Public Policy, and the Law School, BFI brings together more than 200 affiliated economists and scholars to foster frontier research and achieve global impact. The BFI achieves these aims through conferences, workshops, initiatives, public communications, government relations development, an active visiting scholars program that brings nearly 100 scholars to campus per year, a postdoctoral program, and support for more than 40 predoctoral students a year. BFI supports a wide range of initiatives that spur research and policy change around common thematic areas including price theory, labor economics, big data, health economics, and macroeconomics; as well as programs focused on specific geographical areas such as Latin America, China, and India. BFI is headed by a faculty member of the University who is appointed Director by the Provost of the University. It is the Director\u2019s responsibility to oversee all BFI activities, to foster and promote the work of researchers affiliated with BFI, to be active in fundraising, and to manage BFI\u2019s operating budget for impact. Reporting to the Provost of the University of Chicago, the Director will develop a distinctive vision for BFI that reflects current faculty scholarship, build on UChicago\u2019s tradition as a premier intellectual destination for visiting faculty and promising early career scholars, and enhance fundraising to grow the Institute. The Director will be a tenured member of the faculty who holds a PhD or equivalent degree in a field relevant to BFI\u2019s areas of expertise; the Director will hold a tenured appointment in an appropriate Department or School. The Director will serve for an initial five-year term, renewable following a standard review process. The Director will understand BFI\u2019s history, legacy, and core mission, and will raise BFI\u2019s profile on and off campus while maintaining its reputation as a leader in the field. Additionally, the ideal candidate will be able to: Develop BFI priorities in coordination with BFI affiliates and ensure those priorities are widely and clearly articulated to faculty, staff, and internal and external partners; Work with the Provost and University Alumni Relations and Development to set development goals to further BFI's strategic initiatives. The Director will lead all fundraising efforts including securing grant funding, stewarding current individual and institutional donors, and building a pipeline of emerging donors for the future; Lead the Institute while working closely with the University of Chicago administration. The Director will oversee the allocation of financial resources and budgeting of BFI; advocate for funding with the administration when needed; and identify and recruit a Deputy Director (a faculty member) who will be a key partner in overseeing BFI; Oversee a core team of 25 staff, more than 40 predoctoral research professionals, and more than 4 postdoctoral fellows; ensure that staff roles and responsibilities are clearly articulated; and communicate frequently with different teams within BFI to develop and optimize BFI endeavors; Coordinate 25 faculty co-directors of affiliate initiatives, programs, and centers, and facilitate engagement with more than 200 affiliated scholars across campus; Cultivate strong relationships with Advisory Committee members; collaborate with leadership and colleagues in other divisions and departments to elevate BFI as a University resource; build consensus from diverse constituencies on complex projects and issues; Advance the research projects that are crucial to the mission of BFI; facilitate projects within University administration processes as well as through government officials and processes where appropriate; Foster partnerships and collaborations with other research centers and organizations, in related subjects and internationally; Expand BFI\u2019s expertise in policy advocacy and communications in ways that align with faculty research and expand the impact of University of Chicago scholarship. Communicate the relevance of BFI in today\u2019s global environment, elevating the Institute and the University\u2019s role in a global economics conversation. Applicants are asked to submit a CV and cover letter addressed to the BFI Director Search Advisory Committee. Please note all job seekers wishing to be considered for this position must submit their materials through the University\u2019s Interfolio website: http://apply.interfolio.com/158447"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/151249": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Cardiology #PED183", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Cardiology #PED183. Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 23, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Cardiology, and Comer Children\u2019s Hospital are seeking full-time faculty members at any rank on a non-tenure eligible track in pediatric cardiology. Academic rank and compensation (including generous package of fringe benefits) will be determined by qualifications. Responsibilities include outpatient clinics, inpatient consultations, procedures, and reading cardio-diagnostic testing as applies to the specialty field. Other duties will include training and supervision of trainees and students throughout the Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance Heart Cardiovascular service line, and scholarly activity. The Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance is a partnership between Comer Children\u2019s Hospital, Advocate Children\u2019s Hospital, and NorthShore University Health System Pediatrics. The Chicagoland Children\u2019s Health Alliance\u2019s Cardiovascular service line is among the nation\u2019s top Children\u2019s heart programs in the country. Our multidisciplinary team includes cardiologists, both categorical and advance fellows, 3 board congenital heart surgeons, and a dedicated cardiac anesthesia service. Our cardiac surgeons perform over 400 cardiac operations per year and achieve outcomes that rank among the best in the nation (STS-CHS-Database). We also have one of the highest ambulatory clinic volumes in the country with clinic sites throughout Chicago, the Chicago suburbs, Northern Illinois, and Northern Indiana. Our cardiac program is one of the largest in the Midwest and provides expertise in echocardiography, advanced cardiac imaging CT/MRI, electrophysiology, interventional cardiac catheterization, fetal cardiology, adult congenital heart disease, Marfan Syndrome, Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy, cardiac critical care, and preventative and rehabilitative cardiology. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the states of Illinois and Indiana, 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pediatric Cardiology, and 4) have completed a fellowship in pediatric cardiology or pediatric cardiology subspecialty. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/157512 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/151530": {"title": "Faculty (Open Rank) - Bioengineering and Immunoengineering", "description": "Faculty (Open Rank) - Bioengineering and Immunoengineering. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 22, 2024. The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) at the University of Chicago invites applications for multiple faculty positions (open rank) in Bioengineering and Immunoengineering at both the Assistant Professor and Associate/Full Professor levels. The PME is a unique interdisciplinary school with significant strengths in immunoengineering, quantum engineering, and advanced materials engineering. We are interested in expanding our research themes into new areas in bioengineering and systems biology, broadly defined. We welcome applicants studying the immune system, cancer, cell and tissue engineering, immunotherapy, synthetic biology, development of biological methods and technologies, AI and its applications, omics, single cell methods, characterization and modeling of complex systems, multi-cellular systems and models, pathogen-host interactions, microbiome, and other topics. We especially welcome applicants who use a multi-faceted approach to biological research that integrates experiments, computation, and methods/technology development. Research programs with a translational focus are also well-suited for these positions. Applications from researchers who answer fundamental questions, as well as those focused on human health and applications of biomedical research, are both encouraged. About the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering The PME was launched by the University of Chicago in 2011 with the aim of translating molecular science into solutions with impact in health and immunology, quantum information technology, and environmental sustainability. PME aims to solve important societal problems by creating synergy and collaborations across disciplines. The PME currently has 46 faculty engaged in both fundamental and applied aspects of molecular engineering. We envision significantly expanding the number of our faculty, research and education programs over the next decade. PME is affiliated with Argonne National Laboratory and the Center for Molecular Engineering at Argonne, and joint appointment is encouraged when appropriate. PME is highly integrated into the research ecosystem in the broader Chicagoland area, with joint programs and affiliations with the new Chan-Zuckerberg Biohub Chicago, Hyde Park Labs that provides additional space for academic labs and start-up companies, and the Biological Sciences Division and Physical Sciences Division at the University of Chicago. PME has joint research programs with divisions across the University of Chicago campus in cancer, immunology, microbiology, biophysics, chemical biology, neuroscience, genomics and systems biology, and computation/AI. We invite applications at the ranks of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, and Professor. Applicants must hold a doctoral degree or equivalent in a field related to biology, bioengineering, chemical engineering, chemistry, physics, or a related discipline. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. Candidates for Associate or Full Professor must have evidence of leadership in their field and an outstanding track record of independent research, allowing them to qualify for appointment as a tenured professor in the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering or jointly with another department in the University. All candidates will be expected to establish and maintain a robust research program and teach at the graduate and undergraduate levels. Apply online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website: http://apply.interfolio.com/157759 The following materials are required: Cover letter; Curriculum Vitae including bibliography; Research statement describing past, current research accomplishments and future research plans; Teaching statement describing teaching philosophy and experience; and Contact information for three references The following materials are optional: Up to three research papers Please contact Karen Jackson at kjackson10@uchicago.edu with any questions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/99090": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition #PED123a ", "description": "Academic Physicians - Pediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition #PED123a . Department of Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 21, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition, is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank to join our rapidly growing team. Appointees will have a mix of inpatient, outpatient, and procedural clinical responsibilities, including general hepatology, liver consultation, and nutrition consultation services across multiple sites in the Chicagoland and Northwest Indiana area. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois and State of Indiana, and 3) be Board Certified or Eligible in Gastroenterology and Hepatology. We especially welcome applicants with advanced training in gastroenterology and hepatology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/156650 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/150921": {"title": "Faculty, Gynecology and Minimally Invasive Surgery \u2013 OBG075", "description": "Faculty, Gynecology and Minimally Invasive Surgery \u2013 OBG075. Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 18, 2024. The University of Chicago's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is searching for full-time faculty members at any rank. The appointees will be responsible for gynecologic clinical care and management of patients on campus and at off-site locations and will be responsible for emergency department and inpatient consultative services in gynecology. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in Obstetrics and Gynecology. We especially welcome applicants with training and expertise in the area of gynecologic surgery, interpretation of gynecologic ultrasound, and expertise in cancer genetics. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: http://apply.interfolio.com/157219 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit: http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/145188": {"title": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in Energy and Sustainability ", "description": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in Energy and Sustainability . Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Aug 12, 2024. The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering at the University of Chicago invites applications for a teaching position as an Instructional Professor (open rank) to support our undergraduate, masters, and doctoral educational programs. The selected candidate will be appointed at the rank of Assistant Instructional Professor, Associate Instructional Professor, or Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications. The appointment will be for a term of up to five years, renewable with the possibility for promotion. This is a nine-month, career-track position with competitive salary and benefits. The appointee will play a key role in designing and implementing a new laboratory course in energy storage and conversion, primarily serving masters and doctoral students in the PME. The appointee will be responsible for leading a Master of Engineering track in Energy & Sustainability, advising students in that track, and serving on the Master of Engineering admissions committee. More generally, the appointee will be expected to work with PME faculty, leadership, and staff to support its undergraduate and graduate programs. This support would include activities such as: designing and teaching new courses, teaching existing courses, academic advising of students, advising of undergraduate student Capstone teams, curricular development, and the development and delivery of other workshops and programs to support the PME. Expected start date is September 1, 2025. The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and the University. Completion of all requirements for a Ph.D. in Molecular Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Materials Science & Engineering, Chemistry, Physics, or a related field; or an MS in same with five to ten years of experience relevant to the position. At least one year of college-level teaching experience, either as an instructor of record or as a teaching assistant. Candidates should apply online through the University of Chicago's Interfolio website: http://apply.interfolio.com/151895 The following materials are required: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae, including teaching experience and publications; Syllabi of courses previously taught and/or designed by the candidate, or sample syllabi of proposed courses; Description of teaching philosophy and experience; and Three confidential letters of recommendation, at least two of which must address the candidate's teaching ability. Please contact Daphne Gavins at ddgavins@uchicago.edu with any questions."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/142165": {"title": "Lecturer, Graham School", "description": "Lecturer, Graham School. Graham School of Continuing Liberal and Professional Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Jul 2, 2024. The Graham School of Continuing Liberal Arts Education is currently seeking qualified applicants for a part-time position to teach in our Master of Liberal Arts program. Current University faculty and staff, as well as professionals working in relevant positions outside of the University, are eligible to apply. Courses are held online or in person at our Chicago campus. The program currently operates on a quarterly schedule with classes held weekly, 8 to 10 weeks of instruction per quarter. Responsibilities Develop syllabus including weekly class schedule, discussion topics, learning objectives, reading assignments, and assessment plans using grading rubrics. Develop Canvas Learning Management System course site. Develop and deliver in-class content (lectures, activities, breakout group work). Develop asynchronous content which supplements the in-class learning experience (recorded lectures, discussion board content, project, and research work). Grade assignments and return comments and grades to students in timely manner. Attend all weekly in-person and/or remote synchronous sessions. Attend instructor meetings coordinated and hosted by program staff. Work with the program director and other program instructors to ensure consistency across class sections and participate in curriculum reviews. Supplemental asynchronous content and remote synchronous instruction methods must be developed and planned in conjunction with Graham School online instructional design and/or teaching and learning teams and approved by the program. After initial review of applications, candidates may be requested to submit a course proposal. Qualified applicants must have a graduate-level degree in a relevant field, PhD preferred. Candidates with prior teaching experience and/or relevant practical experience are strongly preferred. CV or Resume Cover Letter (optional) References upon request"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/101117": {"title": "Senior Research Associate \u2013 Anomalous Small Angle Scattering (ASAXS)", "description": "Senior Research Associate \u2013 Anomalous Small Angle Scattering (ASAXS). Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Aug 15, 2022. The University of Chicago Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering is seeking a full time Senior Research Associate (SRA) to become part of NSF\u2019s ChemMatCARS. ChemMatCARS, affiliated with the Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME), the University of Chicago and funded by NSF, is a national user facility for frontier research in chemistry and materials science using synchrotron x-rays. ChemMatCARS is located at Sector 15 of the Advanced Photon Source (APS) at Argonne National Laboratory (ANL). Anomalous Small Angle Scattering (ASAXS) is one of the most powerful synchrotron X-ray techniques for element-specific structural study of soft matter systems. It is indispensable for understanding quantitative structural heterogeneities in soft matter systems and nanomaterials. The successful candidate will be expected to lead an ASAXS user program that will serve a general scientific user community. The candidate will be expected to originate and engage in collaborative efforts in ASAXS studies of a variety of soft matter systems with ChemMatCARS users. These efforts include, but are not limited to, conducting synchrotron experiments with users at ChemMatCARS, analyzing ASAXS data, contributing to manuscripts for publication, and, if the situation applies, contributing to grant proposal applications. In addition, this candidate will be expected to provide additional support for scientists conducting research at ChemMatCARS, as well as lead R&D efforts to expand the capabilities of ChemMatCARS in the use of ASAXS. Areas of investigation include, but are not limited to, aggregation and complexation of polyelectrolytes, interactions between charged colloids and macro-ions in solutions, and element distribution within functional nanomaterials at the length scales from a few to hundreds of nanometers. A Ph.D. in Physical Sciences in the study of soft matter science with synchrotron hard x-ray techniques, preferably with Anomalous Small Angle X-ray Scattering (ASAXS), a research record in soft matter science using synchrotron hard x-ray scattering techniques, and a minimum of 8 years of relevant experiences with 3 years in leading a user science program at a synchrotron x-ray ASAXS facility. Expertise in a range of synchrotron x-ray techniques, including the use of high energy x-rays (up to 70 keV), for studying soft matter is especially beneficial. Strong computer skills, including EPICS, SPEC, and Python, as well as the ability to use these to write data acquisition software, data analysis software, instrumentation control interfaces and beamline control programs, are also required. Experience with the operation of synchrotron beamlines is highly preferred. Moreover, the SRA will have the opportunity to serve in the role of Principal Investigator, writing proposals to federal and non-federal agencies to support such research and development. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio website: http://apply.interfolio.com/111717. Review of applications will continue until position is filled. The following materials are required: cover letter. curriculum vitae including a list of publications. research statement describing past and current research accomplishments contact information for three references who can provide confidential letters of evaluation."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193936": {"title": "Law and Economics Fellow", "description": "Law and Economics Fellow. Law School. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 16, 2026. The University of Chicago Law School invites applications for a full-time Law and Economics Fellow, appointed with the rank of Lecturer, for a one-year term beginning July 1, 2027. Appointment for a second one-year term might be possible. Candidates must demonstrate the promise of distinguished legal scholarship and have the ability to develop and teach a graduate level course or seminar in an appropriate subject matter. The successful applicant will teach one one-quarter course or seminar in each appointed year and will be expected to publish original scholarship and contribute to the intellectual life of the Law School. Candidates will be evaluated on the basis of grades, publications, recommendations, and experience. In assessing potential contributions to the life of the Law School, we value candidates who will contribute diverse ideas and perspectives that will enrich and improve student experiences and the Law School's intellectual culture. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . A J.D. is required, though in unusual cases a candidate who holds a Ph.D. in a related/complementary discipline or holds a J.D. and is completing a Ph.D. may be considered. Applications are accepted only at apply.interfolio.com/195391 . Cover letter, CV, writing sample, reference contact information, law school/terminal degree transcript, and research statement must be submitted electronically at the time of application. Applications accepted until the position is filled or until May 15, 2027, whichever is sooner."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194052": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor \u2013 Psychiatry & Behavioral Neuroscience #PBN087", "description": "Research Assistant Professor \u2013 Psychiatry & Behavioral Neuroscience #PBN087. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Oct 16, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry & Behavioral Neuroscience, seeks a research appointee at the rank of Research Assistant Professor to join the Innovation in Population Health Center. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Innovation in Population Health Center has a long history of developing person-centered assessment tools, decision-support models, and data-driven strategies to improve behavioral health and human service systems. Grounded in Transformational Collaborative Outcomes Management (TCOM), this work integrates research, statistical analysis, training, and implementation support to translate information about individual needs and strengths into actionable guidance for service planning, quality improvement, and outcome monitoring. The appointee will initially conduct academic research in collaboration with a faculty member or team, participating in one or more of the ongoing research projects involving the development and application of statistical and machine learning methods to Child and Adolescent Needs and Strengths (CANS) and other Transformational Collaborative Outcomes Management (TCOM) assessment data, including predictive modeling, evaluation of service outcomes, and development of decision-support algorithms, but will be expected, over time, to develop their own independent research agenda and grant portfolio. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: 1) a doctoral degree or equivalent in epidemiology, biostatistics, or related field and 2) completed postdoctoral training in biostatistics, epidemiology, statistical methodology, or a related quantitative research area. We especially welcome applicants with expertise and experience in statistical methodology development, machine learning, predictive modeling, analysis of large-scale assessment data, and scientific programming in R and Python, with applications to behavioral health or child welfare research. Assessment of applications will consider publication record, experience with collaborative research, track record, and potential to obtain extramural funding. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195491 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and research statement. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194058": {"title": "Research Assistant Professor \u2013 Psychiatry & Behavioral Neuroscience #PBN089", "description": "Research Assistant Professor \u2013 Psychiatry & Behavioral Neuroscience #PBN089. Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Neuroscience. Position type: OAA - Research Associate. Posted: Oct 16, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Psychiatry & Behavioral Neuroscience seeks a research appointee at the rank of Research Assistant Professor to join the Innovation in Population Health Center. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The Innovation in Population Health Center has a long history of developing person-centered assessment tools, decision-support models, and data-driven strategies to improve behavioral health and human service systems. Grounded in Transformational Collaborative Outcomes Management (TCOM), this work integrates research, statistical analysis, training, and implementation support to translate information about individual needs and strengths into actionable guidance for service planning, quality improvement, and outcome monitoring. The appointee will initially conduct academic research in collaboration with a faculty member or team, participating in one or more of the ongoing research projects involving the development and application of statistical methods to behavioral health assessment and service data, including longitudinal analysis of needs and outcomes, evaluation of service utilization, and validation of decision-support algorithms, but will be expected, over time, to develop their own independent research agenda and grant portfolio. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have: 1) a doctoral degree or equivalent in statistics, biostatistics, or related field and 2) completed postdoctoral training in statistics, biostatistics, epidemiology, or a related quantitative research area. We especially welcome applicants with expertise and experience in statistical methodology development, longitudinal and functional data analysis, mixed-effects modeling, Bayesian methods, and analysis of observational health data, with applications to behavioral health services and population health research. Assessment of applications will consider publication record, experience with collaborative research, track record, and potential to obtain extramural funding. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u201a Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195499 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and research statement. Review of applications ends when the position is filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194207": {"title": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Geochemistry and Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi)", "description": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Geochemistry and Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi). Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 15, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor in geochemistry, broadly defined, from scholars whose research will make significant contributions to climate systems engineering. The University is building a world-leading Climate Systems Engineering initiative (climateengineering.uchicago.edu) with multiple faculty hires spanning open-system carbon removal, sunlight reflection methods, and interventions to limit the loss of glacial ice. The successful candidate will hold a faculty appointment in the Department of the Geophysical Sciences and be a member of the Climate Systems Engineering initiative. Examples of relevant expertise include but are not limited to: mineral dissolution and weathering kinetics, carbonate chemistry and alkalinity cycling, mineral carbonation, ocean or soil biogeochemistry, trace metal and nutrient cycling, or subglacial water-rock interaction. Applications are welcomed from scholars who have not previously worked on climate systems engineering. Research on climate systems engineering is inherently interdisciplinary, so we seek individuals with a track record of collaboration beyond their discipline; the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a doctoral degree is required prior to appointment. The following materials are required: A brief cover letter, A curriculum vitae, A statement of research plans (up to 5 pages) that describes how you plan to integrate research on climate systems engineering into your overall research trajectory, A statement of teaching philosophy and experience, Applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation through Interfolio. The following additional materials may be requested: Up to three sample publications. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Job\u2019s website: apply.interfolio.com/195686 . Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled. Base salary range is $120,000 - $150,000. The base salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194280": {"title": "Associate Professor \u2013 Geochemistry and Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi)", "description": "Associate Professor \u2013 Geochemistry and Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi). Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 15, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of Associate Professor in geochemistry, broadly defined, from scholars whose research will make significant contributions to climate systems engineering. The University is building a world-leading Climate Systems Engineering initiative (climateengineering.uchicago.edu) with multiple faculty hires spanning open-system carbon removal, sunlight reflection methods, and interventions to limit the loss of glacial ice. The successful candidate will hold a faculty appointment in the Department of the Geophysical Sciences and be a member of the Climate Systems Engineering initiative. Examples of relevant expertise include but are not limited to: mineral dissolution and weathering kinetics, carbonate chemistry and alkalinity cycling, mineral carbonation, ocean or soil biogeochemistry, trace metal and nutrient cycling, or subglacial water-rock interaction. Applications are welcomed from scholars who have not previously worked on climate systems engineering. Research on climate systems engineering is inherently interdisciplinary, so we seek individuals with a track record of collaboration beyond their discipline; the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Doctoral Degree Evidence of Leadership in the field and a record of independent research. The following materials are required: A brief cover letter, A curriculum vitae, A statement of research plans (up to 5 pages) that describes how you plan to integrate research on climate systems engineering into your overall research trajectory, A statement of teaching philosophy and experience. The following additional materials may be requested: Names and contact information of three individuals who can provide confidential letters of recommendation. Up to three sample publications. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Job\u2019s website: apply.interfolio.com/195704 . Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled. Base salary range is: $130,000 - $165,000. This base salary range is for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. This position may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194282": {"title": "Professor \u2013 Geochemistry and Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi)", "description": "Professor \u2013 Geochemistry and Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi). Department of the Geophysical Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 15, 2026. The Department of the Geophysical Sciences at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenured faculty positions at the rank of Professor in geochemistry, broadly defined, from scholars whose research will make significant contributions to climate systems engineering. The University is building a world-leading Climate Systems Engineering initiative (climateengineering.uchicago.edu) with multiple faculty hires spanning open-system carbon removal, sunlight reflection methods, and interventions to limit the loss of glacial ice. The successful candidate will hold a faculty appointment in the Department of the Geophysical Sciences and be a member of the Climate Systems Engineering initiative. Examples of relevant expertise include but are not limited to: mineral dissolution and weathering kinetics, carbonate chemistry and alkalinity cycling, mineral carbonation, ocean or soil biogeochemistry, trace metal and nutrient cycling, or subglacial water-rock interaction. Applications are welcomed from scholars who have not previously worked on climate systems engineering. Research on climate systems engineering is inherently interdisciplinary, so we seek individuals with a track record of collaboration beyond their discipline; the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Doctoral Degree Evidence of leadership in the field and a record of independent research. The following materials are required: A brief cover letter, A curriculum vitae, A statement of research plans (up to 5 pages) that describes how you plan to integrate research on climate systems engineering into your overall research trajectory, A statement of teaching philosophy and experience. The following additional materials may be requested: Names and contact information of three individuals who can provide confidential letters of recommendation. Up to three sample publications. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Job\u2019s website: apply.interfolio.com/195707 . Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled. Base salary range is: $155,000 - $210,000. These base salary ranges are for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. These positions may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194150": {"title": "Interventional Pulmonologist \u2013 Pulmonary & Critical Care #MED447", "description": "Interventional Pulmonologist \u2013 Pulmonary & Critical Care #MED447. Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 15, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, is seeking full-time faculty members at the rank of Assistant Professor to join its clinical faculty with a focus in Interventional Pulmonology. These positions are intended for physicians interested in developing a clinical practice that includes interventional pulmonary procedures, ambulatory pulmonary medicine, and multidisciplinary care of patients with complex pulmonary and thoracic conditions. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will have clinical responsibilities across the University of Chicago Medicine health system, including UChicago Medicine Hyde Park and Ingalls Memorial Hospital and affiliated ambulatory locations. Clinical responsibilities will primarily include interventional pulmonary procedures and the evaluation and management of patients requiring advanced diagnostic and therapeutic pulmonary services. Procedural responsibilities include advanced bronchoscopy, including robotic bronchoscopy, endobronchial ultrasound, rigid bronchoscopy, pleural procedures, and other advanced diagnostic and therapeutic airway and pulmonary interventions based on the appointees' expertise and clinical need. Ambulatory responsibilities will include the evaluation and longitudinal management of patients referred for interventional pulmonary evaluation and procedures. Appointees will work collaboratively with Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine faculty and clinical teams across the health system, including multidisciplinary collaboration with relevant medical and surgical specialties. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students and scholarly activity appropriate to the appointee's academic interests and career goals. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195583 . Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194325": {"title": "Senior Instructional Professor (open-rank), Director of Design", "description": "Senior Instructional Professor (open-rank), Director of Design. Committee on Theater and Performance Studies. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 15, 2026. The Committee on Theater & Performance Studies at the University of Chicago invites applications for a position as an open-rank Senior Instructional Professor and Director of Design, with an expected start date of September 1, 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. Teaching normally consists of four courses across three quarters. Other duties include evaluating and supervising unionized lecturers and staff teaching design-related courses in TAPS; participating in the selection of the TAPS-sponsored productions; designing or appointing designers for these productions and supervising designers in mentoring students; advising undergraduate and graduate students on the design of their performance projects; consulting with the approximately 20 active student theater and performance groups; developing design curriculum; overseeing the training and mentorship of students in design and production; and coordinating with TAPS production staff, Logan Center staff, and other arts departments on design resource distribution for students and productions. The selected candidate will be appointed at the rank of Assistant Senior Instructional Professor, Associate Senior Instructional Professor, or Senior Instructional Professor, depending on qualifications and educational background. The initial appointment is for a minimum of three years, with longer initial terms possible depending on qualifications and initial rank. Salary Range or Pay Grade: Assistant Senior Instructional Professor: $80,000 - $95,000; Associate Senior Instructional Professor: $91,000 - $105,000; Senior Instructional Professor: $98,000 - $115,000. This base pay range is for a nine-month academic appointment paid over twelve months. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . An MFA or comparable terminal degree is required; a PhD is preferred. Applicants must have professional design experience in the arts and arts administration and must have teaching experience at the university or post-secondary level. Experience in more than one design area preferred. Experience in arts leadership and management is preferred. Applicants must upload the following materials to the University of Chicago\u2019s recruitment website at apply.interfolio.com/195746 by 10:59 PM Central Time/11:59 PM Eastern Time on November 16, 2026 : \u00b7 CV \u00b7 Cover letter of no more than three pages that addresses professional experience, inclusive of previous administrative and teaching experience, and that offers a cohesive vision for design within the TAPS performance culture \u00b7 Teaching statement \u00b7 Sample syllabus \u00b7 Arts portfolio (website portfolio is acceptable) \u00b7 Contact information for three references Applicants may be asked to provide additional materials following the initial review of applications. Questions may be directed to Vicki Walden at vwalden@uchicago.edu . This position is contingent upon budgetary approval."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194271": {"title": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hospitalists #PED242", "description": "Clinical Associates - Pediatric Hospitalists #PED242. PEDS - Section of Hospital Medicine. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 15, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Hospital Medicine, seeks full-time Clinical Associates for renewable terms of up to two years to join our expanding Pediatric Hospital Medicine group. Clinical responsibilities will include pediatric medicine, newborn care, and ED consultation. Appointees may have the opportunity to participate in quality improvement efforts in the Section of Pediatric Hospital Medicine. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior and compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide an education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. The department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates, and postdoctoral researchers. UChicago Medicine Comer Children's Hospital is a free standing 165 bed children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Pediatrics, and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited pediatric residency program. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195697 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194011": {"title": "Assistant Professor - Energy and Environmental Economics and Policy", "description": "Assistant Professor - Energy and Environmental Economics and Policy. Harris School of Public Policy Studies. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 14, 2026. The Harris School of Public Policy at the University of Chicago invites applications for tenure-track faculty positions in Energy and Environmental Economics and Policy at the Assistant Professor level. Applicants are expected to have completed a Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field by July 31, 2027 and have a demonstrated scholarly interest in energy and environmental economics and policy. To apply for this position, candidates must submit their application through the University of Chicago\u2019s Interfolio academic job board at apply.interfolio.com/195459 . Applicants are required to submit: 1) curriculum vitae, 2) two letters of recommendation, 3) one writing sample, 4) research statement, and 5) teaching statement. Applicants may also submit additional optional documents such as: additional writing samples and/or a third letter of reference. Review of applications will begin on November 13, 2026. No applications will be accepted after May 1, 2027. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Salary Range or Pay Grade: $140,000 - $180,000. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194049": {"title": "Academic Physician - Endocrinology #MED442", "description": "Academic Physician - Endocrinology #MED442. MED - Section of Adult Endocrinology, Diabetes, and Metabolism. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 14, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Adult and Pediatric Endocrinology, Diabetes, and Metabolism, is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor to join an established group of nationally known endocrinologists. The appointee will have a mix of inpatient and outpatient clinical responsibilities primarily at our Hyde Park campus and at our network and affiliate sites. Inpatient activities will be on the endocrinology consultation service and/or the General Medicine inpatient service. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Endocrinology. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago's Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195489 . Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications will begin after October 24, 2026. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194142": {"title": "General Cardiologist - Cardiology #MED441", "description": "General Cardiologist - Cardiology #MED441. MED - Section of Cardiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 14, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of Assistant Professor who specializes in General and Preventive Cardiology. The appointee will have clinical responsibilities including outpatient clinic, reading EKGs, and inpatient ward and consultative cardiology at our main campus and/or at affiliated sites (e.g. River East). Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. A limited role in echocardiography is also possible. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Cardiology. We especially welcome applicants who have trained at rigorous, academically oriented programs and with clinical strength in lipid disorders, but also with interests in hypertension, social determinants of cardiovascular health, outcomes research, and clinical trials. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195578 . Applicants must upload: a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications will begin after October 24, 2026. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194473": {"title": "AI and Information Literacy Librarian", "description": "AI and Information Literacy Librarian. The Library. Position type: OAA - University Librarian. Posted: Oct 23, 2026. Position Description The University of Chicago Library seeks a new team member to support our strategic priority to grow the scope and reach of the Library\u2019s instructional programming for information literacy, digital skills, artificial intelligence (AI), and undergraduate research. The AI and Information Literacy Librarian will create, implement, and evaluate programs and services to help undergraduate students at the University of Chicago develop information literacy skills in the age of AI. The AI and Information Literacy Librarian reports to the Director of Teaching and Academic Engagement, and will be part of a unit dedicated to supporting the undergraduate experience and empowering students with the information literacy needed for academic success and life-long learning. We are looking for an individual who thrives in a team environment, is excited about experimentation and exploration, and envisions the library at the center of the undergraduate student experience. Responsibilities: Design and implement training for undergraduate students on the appropriate use of AI, including how to critically evaluate technology platforms, tools, and AI outputs, and understand the legal and ethical implications of its use (including academic honesty). Partner with the AI Librarian and other colleagues in the Center of Digital Scholarship to create instructional programs and materials promoting awareness and understanding of AI technologies and their implications for learning and research. Collaborate and consult with librarians, teaching faculty, and instructional designers to develop information and AI literacy programs for undergraduate courses and majors. Meet with undergraduates about using GenAI and research tools for assignments, capstone projects, research experiences, or future career opportunities. Provide teaching and reference support for the College\u2019s new writing course, Inquiry, Conversation, Argument , focused on developing information literacy skills for first-year undergraduate students. Create online tutorials, research guides, and streaming videos to introduce students to the wide variety of tools and technologies available for their research and assignments. Serve on committees and workgroups focused on undergraduate teaching, instructional technology, and artificial intelligence. Stay informed about the evolving landscape of AI tools, ethics, policies, and regulations. Participate in the Library\u2019s Ask a Librarian virtual reference services. Meet one-on-one with students about assignments and research projects. Triage AI reference questions and consultation requests in collaboration with the AI Librarian and subject experts based on complexity and needs. Provide user support for citation managers (Zotero, EndNote) and AI research and productivity tools (e.g. Semantic Scholar, ResearchRabbit, Connected Papers). Support events and programming managed by Teaching and Academic Engagement staff, including the Library\u2019s annual open house, table events, study breaks, and workshops (includes some weekend and evening hours). Provide orientations and tours for students, visiting scholars, and other researchers. Assume other duties and responsibilities as assigned. Qualifications Required: Master's degree from an ALA-accredited Library or Information Science program At least one year of experience providing public service or reference support in an academic library. Experience providing information literacy instruction in libraries or a college/university setting, or completed coursework focused on teaching, pedagogy, or library instruction. Demonstrated experience using Generative AI tools and platforms including Claude.ai, ChatGPT, Gemini, and Co-Pilot in an academic setting. Demonstrated knowledge of the ethical, legal, social, and educational impacts of AI and machine learning. Preffered: Experience creating online tutorials, learning modules, or library research guides. Experience supporting undergraduate writing or first-year experience courses. Familiarity with AI features in research databases and publisher platforms. Experience developing and implementing library programming for undergraduate students. Ability to communicate complex technology concepts to non-expert audiences. Commitment to collaborating with diverse stakeholders in college or university settings. Flexibility to work collaboratively and independently in a changing environment. To Apply: Submit cover letter, curriculum vitae, and reference contact information online through the University of Chicago's Academic Recruiting website: https://apply.interfolio.com/195888 . Review of applications will begin after November 30, 2026. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. Salary and Benefits: $65,000- $75,000. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Questions: Contact the University Library Human Resources department at libraryhr@uchicago.edu."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194922": {"title": "Head of Conservation ", "description": "Head of Conservation . The Library. Position type: OAA - University Librarian. Posted: Oct 23, 2026. Summary Conservation, part of the Preservation Department, encompasses the full range of activities required to maintain and improve the physical condition of the University of Chicago Library's collections, including assessments and treatments that restore the condition and usability of library materials in their original formats. This work directly supports the library's mission to preserve and provide long-term access to its collections. The Head of Conservation will report to the Director of Preservation and lead a comprehensive conservation program and laboratory, delivering a broad spectrum of physical treatments across all University of Chicago Library collections. The program spans basic stabilization, intermediate repair, and complex conservation treatment for both general and special collections materials, all carried out in accordance with accepted professional standards. In this role, the Head of Conservation brings deep professional expertise to determine appropriate treatment courses for library and archival materials. Responsibilities include specifying treatments, overseeing laboratory operations, supervising staff and student workers involved in conservation activities, and personally executing a wide range of conservation treatments on paper-based materials. The Head of Conservation works closely and collaboratively with Collection Development librarians, the Hanna Holborn Gray Special Collections Research Center (HHGSCRC) staff, D'Angelo Law Library staff, Access Services staff, and the Kim-Park Program for the History of the Book in the Library to prioritize materials for treatment, design treatment plans, and set program policies and goals. The Head also works with other colleagues on campus such as the Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures, the Smart Museum of Art, and the Visual Resources Center. Responsibilities Planning & Management Lead the Library\u2019s Conservation Program for circulating and special collections, establishing goals, treatment priorities, and initiatives aligned with departmental and Library objectives, including grants and special projects. Direct Conservation Laboratory operations, including budgets, resource allocation, workflow efficiency, and safety protocols. Hire, supervise, and develop conservation staff, including coordinating training and mentorship opportunities for staff, interns, and fellows. Establish treatment criteria, quality standards, and documentation practices consistent with the American Institute for Conservation (AIC) Code of Ethics and Guidelines for Practice. Conduct collection condition assessments, perform complex conservation treatments, and maintain treatment records and program statistics. Evaluate and implement emerging conservation techniques, oversee outsourced treatments, and manage vendor selection and assessment. Serve as a subject matter expert on broader preservation issues, including environmental controls and storage best practices, while maintaining expertise in conservation research and professional practices. Maintain expertise in emerging conservation research, practices, and technologies through professional development, scholarly literature, and engagement with the field. Partnership & Collaboration Partner with the Director of Preservation, Preservation managers, and staff on departmental initiatives, projects, policies, and procedures. Coordinate with Collection Services, the Special Collections Research Center (SCRC), D\u2019Angelo Law Library, Maps Librarian, and other Library colleagues to establish conservation priorities and treatment goals. Work with SCRC Collection Management on needs assessments, grant opportunities, treatment planning, and preservation considerations for exhibitions and digitization projects. Support Access Services in use-driven treatment selection and appropriate materials handling practices. Provide technical guidance and consultation to Library staff on conservation-related matters. Represent the Conservation Program on Library committees and working groups, as appropriate. Additional Responsibilities Perform other duties as assigned. Required Master\u2019s degree in art or museum conservation, or an MLS with an advanced certificate in conservation, from an accredited institution. Seven years of conservation experience in a research library or archive. Experience supervising and mentoring staff and interns. Demonstrated leadership in local and national conservation organizations. Advanced conservation treatment skills across a broad range of library materials, demonstrated by a portfolio. Working knowledge of chemistry as it applies to library and archival materials and conservation techniques. In-depth knowledge of the materials and methods used to create library and archival materials, including bookbinding, papermaking, manuscript production, and printing history. Experience with instrumental analysis in materials science, including X-ray fluorescence (XRF) spectrometry and polarized light microscopy, and familiarity with other analytical techniques such as FTIR and mass spectrometry (GC-MS and LC-MS). Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Preferred Familiarity with multi-spectral imaging. Working knowledge of photography. To Apply: Submit cover letter, curriculum vitae, and reference contact information online through the University of Chicago's Academic Recruiting website: apply.interfolio.com/196292 . Review of applications will begin November 22, 2026. Screening of applications will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. Salary and Benefits: $80,000 - $90,000. The position also includes a relocation allowance for qualifying expenses. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Questions: Contact the University Library Human Resources department at libraryhr@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194604": {"title": "Community General Surgeons, Clinical Associates #SUR199a", "description": "Community General Surgeons, Clinical Associates #SUR199a. Section of Minimally Invasive and General Surgery. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 23, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Surgery, Section of Minimally Invasive and General Surgery, seeks full-time Clinical Associates practicing within a community general surgical practice for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will work alongside a group of community surgeons to provide comprehensive inpatient and outpatient general surgical care, including elective surgery, emergency general surgery, and longitudinal preoperative and postoperative care at Ingalls Memorial Hospital and other offsite locations. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Responsibilities include shared weekday and weekend call coverage, participation in group coverage arrangements and practice workflows, and collaboration with hospital staff and other clinical services to provide timely, coordinated care. The University of Chicago Medicine has been at the forefront of medical care since 1927. The University of Chicago Medicine Ingalls Memorial Hospital, located in Harvey, Illinois, serves Chicago\u2019s south suburban communities. These positions offer the opportunity to build a broad general surgery practice within an established community hospital, working closely with local referring clinicians and a multidisciplinary care team. As part of UChicago Medicine, the practice combines community-based care with access to the expertise and resources of an academic health system. The Section of Minimally Invasive and General Surgery is committed to medical education, research, patient care, and serving our community. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board certified or eligible in General Surgery or equivalent. We especially welcome applicants committed to a broad community general surgery practice, building lasting relationships with patients and referring clinicians, and working collaboratively within an established surgical group. Applicants with experience in community general surgery, a broad operative skill set, and proficiency in minimally invasive techniques are especially encouraged to apply. A demonstrated commitment to collegiality, teamwork, and serving the needs of the local community is highly valued. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195999 . Applicants must upload a CV and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194374": {"title": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Radiation and Cellular Oncology, Part-time (60% FTE) #RON040", "description": "Clinical Associates \u2013 Radiation and Cellular Oncology, Part-time (60% FTE) #RON040. Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology. Position type: OAA - Clinical Associate. Posted: Oct 22, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology is seeking part-time Clinical Associates at 60% effort for renewable terms of up to three years. Appointees will provide comprehensive clinical radiation oncology services primarily at Silver Cross Hospital, a local community hospital, working in close collaboration with existing departmental faculty and the multidisciplinary cancer care team. These positions do not require teaching or scholarly activity. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Appointees will support and complement faculty in Radiation and Cellular Oncology at The University of Chicago and affiliated satellite locations. Responsibilities will include evaluating new patients, developing and overseeing radiation treatment plans, providing on-treatment management and follow-up care, managing treatment-related symptoms, and coordinating care with referring physicians and other oncology specialists. Appointees will participate in multidisciplinary tumor boards, peer review, and departmental quality and patient safety activities. Appointees will provide clinical coverage for the community practice, including coverage during physician absences, and participate in the broader departmental on-call and clinical coverage. Clinical schedules and coverage responsibilities will be assigned consistent with the 60% appointment. Appointees will also participate in clinical trials available at the facility, including identifying potentially eligible patients and collaborating with faculty and research staff to support protocol-directed care. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent; 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois; 3) be board certified or board eligible in radiation oncology; and 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited residency or equivalent in radiation oncology. We especially welcome applicants with experience in community radiation oncology, a commitment to patient-centered care, and demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, referring physicians, and multidisciplinary clinical teams. Experience caring for patients across a broad range of cancer diagnoses and participating in clinical trials is preferred. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195792 . Applicants must upload a CV and cover letter. Review of complete applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194120": {"title": "Academic Physicians - Interventional Endoscopists #MED452", "description": "Academic Physicians - Interventional Endoscopists #MED452. Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology, and Nutrition. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 22, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Medicine, Center for Endoscopic Research and Therapeutics (CERT), and Section of Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition are searching for full-time faculty members at the ranks of Assistant or Associate Professor to join our rapidly growing interventional endoscopy team. The appointees will have a mix of outpatient and inpatient clinical responsibilities, including performing advanced endoscopy procedures in our state-of-the-art unit at our Hyde Park campus and potentially at our Chicagoland area network locations. Other duties will include teaching and supervision of trainees and students, and scholarly activity. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) hold or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, and 3) be Board Certified or Board Eligible in Gastroenterology. We especially welcome applicants with an interest in esophageal (anti-reflux) and bariatric procedures. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195559 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194812": {"title": "Postdoctoral Researchers/Instructors in Molecular Engineering", "description": "Postdoctoral Researchers/Instructors in Molecular Engineering. Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering. Position type: Faculty - Instructor/Collegiate Assistant Prof.. Posted: Oct 21, 2026. The Pritzker School of Molecular Engineering (PME) invites applications for the position of Postdoctoral Researcher/Instructor in Molecular Engineering. Appointees are provided with a structured research and teaching experience, allowing for development as both independent scholars and effective educators within a leading research-intensive academic environment. The position of Postdoctoral Researcher/Instructor is initially for a one-year term, renewable for up to three years. The anticipated start date will be on or after January 1, 2027. Postdoctoral Researchers will conduct research under the mentorship of a PME faculty advisor and teach or co-teach two required PME courses in one of the thematic areas (Immunoengineering, Materials and Sustainability, and Quantum). Effort allocation between research and teaching will vary by quarter. Through substantive experience in research, teaching, curriculum development, student mentoring, and professional development activities, they will be prepared for careers at research universities, teaching-focused institutions, and interdisciplinary academic programs. Applicants identify potential research labs at PME based on their graduate training, research interests, and specific career goals. Applicants may contact potential PME research mentors for acceptance into a research group at any time during the application process. Postdoctoral researchers have access to PME and UChicago professional development resources including: PME and UChicago professional development workshops; attend and present at a teaching-focused conference; complete Effective Mentoring training (e.g., CIMER); receive training in grant writing, proposal development, and grants management; and participate in workshops focused on academic leadership and faculty career preparation. Responsibilities Research: Conduct independent and collaborative research within a PME research group. Develop and execute a research project aligned with their scholarly interests and PME priorities. Publish findings in peer-reviewed journals. Complete training in Responsible Conduct of Research and Research Ethics. Present research at national and international conferences. Participate in research group meetings, seminars, journal clubs, scholarly activities, and contribute to the intellectual community of PME. Teaching: Teach or co-teach two required PME courses. Can design and propose a new course related to their disciplinary expertise and in alignment with PME course priorities. Participate in pedagogical training offered through the Center for Teaching and Learning. Shadow experienced instructors and engage in classroom observations. Develop course materials, assessments, and instructional activities. Mentor and advise undergraduate and graduate students. Required: Candidates must have completed their PhD within 5 years of the appointment start date in engineering, physical sciences, biological sciences, computer science, mathematics, or a closely related field. Demonstrated research excellence, scholarly achievement, and ability to work effectively in an interdisciplinary research environment. Interest in developing teaching expertise and pursuing an academic career. Strong communication and collaboration skills. Preferred: Previous teaching, instructional, and/or mentoring experience. Evidence of commitment to student success and inclusive teaching practices. Strong publication record relative to career stage. Interest in curriculum development and interdisciplinary education. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/196192 . Applicants must upload: CV; Cover letter describing interest in the Postdoctoral Researcher/Instructor position and how it will advance the applicant\u2019s career goals; Research statement; Teaching statement describing interest in teaching, including the undergraduate and/or graduate courses the applicant is qualified to teach; Identification of up to 3 potential PME research mentors or laboratories; Three confidential letters of recommendation, at least two of which must address teaching ability/interest; and Course observations and/or student evaluations (optional). Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Questions may be directed to Vipul Sharma, Director for Postdoctoral Affairs, at sharmav@uchicago.edu ."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194472": {"title": "Associate Professor / Professor (Open Rank) \u2013 Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi)", "description": "Associate Professor / Professor (Open Rank) \u2013 Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi). Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 21, 2026. The University of Chicago invites applications for tenured faculty positions from scholars who will make significant contributions to Climate Systems Engineering. The University is building a world-leading Climate Systems Engineering initiative (CSEi) with multiple faculty hires spanning open-system carbon removal, sunlight reflection methods, and interventions to limit the loss of glacial ice. We seek individuals who will lead research programs that will produce significant contributions to their field and to this research initiative. Applications are welcome from all relevant academic disciplines, including physical and biological sciences, social sciences, engineering, and humanities at the ranks of Associate Professor and Professor. We welcome applications from scholars who have not previously worked on climate systems engineering and from scholars who focus on the physical or social risks of Climate Systems Engineering. Appointments will be made in an appropriate primary department based on field, with secondary appointment(s) in another unit possible. Research on Climate Systems Engineering is inherently interdisciplinary, so we seek individuals with a track record of collaboration beyond their discipline; the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Doctoral Degree Evidence of leadership in the field and a record of independent research. The following materials are required: A brief cover letter, A curriculum vitae, A statement of research plans (up to 5 pages) that describes how you plan to integrate research on climate systems engineering into your overall research trajectory, A statement of teaching philosophy and experience. The following additional materials may be requested: \u00b7 Names and contact information of three individuals who can provide confidential letters of recommendation. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Job\u2019s website: https://apply.interfolio.com/195886 Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled. Base salary ranges, by rank: Associate Professor: $130,000 - $165,000 Professor: $155,000 - $210,000 These base salary ranges are for a nine-month academic appointment. Individuals may be eligible for summer salary. These positions may be eligible for a taxable moving allowance."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194793": {"title": "Instructional Professor of Quantitative Methods in MAPSS (Open Rank)", "description": "Instructional Professor of Quantitative Methods in MAPSS (Open Rank). Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 21, 2026. The University of Chicago Division of the Social Sciences invites applications for appointment as Instructional Professor (IP) at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, with a specialization in quantitative methods, in the Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences ( https://mapss.uchicago.edu ). We seek candidates who have the training and experience necessary to develop and teach an interdisciplinary applied statistics sequence and advise students in quantitative methods. We invite applications from candidates across the full range of disciplines and fields in the social sciences. This is a full-time, career track teaching and advising position that involves designing graduate seminars, mentoring students on research projects, and helping a diverse range of students navigate graduate school. The initial three-year appointment will begin in Academic Year 2027-28 and is renewable with opportunity for promotion. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. MAPSS is a one-year program providing students with a foundation in graduate-level interdisciplinary social science research. Students concentrate in psychology, anthropology, history, political science, sociology, or in interdisciplinary fields, and conduct research projects under the supervision of UChicago faculty or MAPSS IPs. MAPSS recruits talented students from a wide array of backgrounds, experiences, and trajectories. Some go on to doctoral study after MAPSS, but most use the degree to advance their careers with the help of an in-house Career Services team. In MAPSS, IP have an annual teaching load of 4 courses, in addition to substantial mentoring and service responsibilities. Their courses include one section of the MAPSS core course and other courses oriented toward MA and/or undergraduate students, according to program need. The specific courses IP teach in any quarter are determined by the MAPSS Faculty Director and Director. MAPSS IP perform substantive roles as preceptors, a position that involves annually advising students on quarterly course selection and independent research projects, serving as second readers on student theses, supervising capstone projects, and providing broad academic and professional guidance. They also closely advise students and alumni who are applying to PhD programs. MAPSS IP fulfill a range of service requirements. They participate in admissions, recruiting, and staff meetings, and contribute to program operations and co-curricular academic programming, including the annual student academic conference. The position includes support for professional development, including funds to attend conferences and workshops, as well as mentoring from senior members of the MAPSS instructional team. The position also offers opportunities to participate in the intellectual life of the University. Applicants must have a PhD in a social science or related discipline prior to the start date. Prior teaching in the social sciences is required. Experience mentoring students on original research projects is preferred. The following materials must be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/196176 1) a cover letter, outlining the applicant\u2019s prior training and research experience, their prior teaching and/or mentoring experience, and their suggested course offerings; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a doctoral transcript (unofficial is acceptable); 4) an article-length writing sample; 5) at least one course syllabus designed by the candidate; 6) course evaluations or other evidence of past experience teaching or mentoring; 7) a teaching statement; and 8) three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin 30 days after the position has been posed and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . This position will be part of the Service Employees International Union."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194796": {"title": "Instructional Professor of Psychology in MAPSS (Open Rank)", "description": "Instructional Professor of Psychology in MAPSS (Open Rank). Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 21, 2026. The University of Chicago Division of the Social Sciences invites applications for appointment as Instructional Professor (IP) at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, with a specialization in Psychology, in the Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences ( https://mapss.uchicago.edu ). We invite applications from candidates across the full range of fields within the discipline. We are particularly interested in candidates with the training and experience necessary to teach and advise students in experimental and/or quantitative methods. This is a full-time, career track teaching and advising position that involves designing graduate seminars, mentoring students on research projects, and helping a diverse range of students navigate graduate school. The initial three-year appointment will begin in Academic Year 2027-28 and is renewable with opportunity for promotion. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. MAPSS is a one-year program providing students with a foundation in graduate-level interdisciplinary social science research. Students concentrate in psychology, anthropology, history, political science, sociology, or in interdisciplinary fields, and conduct research projects under the supervision of UChicago faculty or MAPSS IPs. MAPSS recruits talented students from a wide array of backgrounds, experiences, and trajectories. Some go on to doctoral study after MAPSS, but most use the degree to advance their careers with the help of an in-house Career Services team. In MAPSS, IP have an annual teaching load of 4 courses, in addition to substantial mentoring and service responsibilities. Their courses include one section of the MAPSS core course and other courses oriented toward MA and/or undergraduate students, according to program need. This often includes a graduate seminar introducing an important social science research methodology. The specific courses IP teach in any quarter are determined by the MAPSS Faculty Director and Director. MAPSS IP perform substantive roles as preceptors, a position that involves annually advising students on quarterly course selection and independent research projects, serving as second readers on student theses, supervising capstone projects, and providing broad academic and professional guidance. They also closely advise students and alumni who are applying to PhD programs. MAPSS IP fulfill a range of service requirements. They participate in admissions, recruiting, and staff meetings, and contribute to program operations and co-curricular academic programming, including the annual student academic conference. The position includes support for professional development, including funds to attend conferences and workshops, as well as mentoring from senior members of the MAPSS instructional team. The position also offers opportunities to participate in the intellectual life of the University. Applicants must have a PhD in Psychology or in a related discipline prior to the start date. Prior teaching in the social sciences is required. Experience mentoring students on original research projects is preferred. The following materials must be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/196179 1) a cover letter, outlining the applicant\u2019s prior training and research experience, their prior teaching and/or mentoring experience, and their suggested course offerings; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a doctoral transcript (unofficial is acceptable); 4) an article-length writing sample; 5) at least one course syllabus designed by the candidate; 6) course evaluations or other evidence of past experience teaching or mentoring; 7) a teaching statement; and 8) three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin 30 days after the position has been posted and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . This position will be part of the Service Employees International Union."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194800": {"title": "Instructional Professor of Sociology in MAPSS (Open Rank)", "description": "Instructional Professor of Sociology in MAPSS (Open Rank). Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 21, 2026. The University of Chicago Division of the Social Sciences invites applications for appointment as Instructional Professor (IP) at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, with a specialization in Sociology, in the Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences ( https://mapss.uchicago.edu ). We invite applications from candidates across the full range of fields within the discipline. We are especially interested in candidates whose substantive work addresses questions around identity and who have expertise in either quantitative or qualitative methods. This is a full-time, career track teaching and advising position that involves designing graduate seminars, mentoring students on research projects, and helping a diverse range of students navigate graduate school. The initial three-year appointment will begin in Academic Year 2027-28 and is renewable with opportunity for promotion. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. MAPSS is a one-year program providing students with a foundation in graduate-level interdisciplinary social science research. Students concentrate in psychology, anthropology, history, political science, sociology, or in interdisciplinary fields, and conduct research projects under the supervision of UChicago faculty or MAPSS IPs. MAPSS recruits talented students from a wide array of backgrounds, experiences, and trajectories. Some go on to doctoral study after MAPSS, but most use the degree to advance their careers with the help of an in-house Career Services team. In MAPSS, IP have an annual teaching load of 4 courses, in addition to substantial mentoring and service responsibilities. Their courses include one section of the MAPSS core course and other courses oriented toward MA and/or undergraduate students, according to program need. This often includes a graduate seminar introducing an important social science research methodology. The specific courses IP teach in any quarter are determined by the MAPSS Faculty Director and Director. MAPSS IP perform substantive roles as preceptors, a position that involves annually advising students on quarterly course selection and independent research projects, serving as second readers on student theses, supervising capstone projects, and providing broad academic and professional guidance. They also closely advise students and alumni who are applying to PhD programs. MAPSS IP fulfill a range of service requirements. They participate in admissions, recruiting, and staff meetings, and contribute to program operations and co-curricular academic programming, including the annual student academic conference. The position includes support for professional development, including funds to attend conferences and workshops, as well as mentoring from senior members of the MAPSS instructional team. The position also offers opportunities to participate in the intellectual life of the University. Applicants must have a PhD in Sociology or in a related discipline prior to the start date. Prior teaching in the social sciences is required. Experience mentoring students on original research projects is preferred. The following materials must be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/196181 1) a cover letter, outlining the applicant\u2019s prior training and research experience, their prior teaching and/or mentoring experience, and their suggested course offerings; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a doctoral transcript (unofficial is acceptable); 4) an article-length writing sample; 5) at least one course syllabus designed by the candidate; 6) course evaluations or other evidence of past experience teaching or mentoring; 7) a teaching statement; and 8) three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin 30 days after the position has been posted and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . This position will be part of the Service Employees International Union."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194158": {"title": "Academic Structural Interventional Cardiologists - Cardiology #MED437", "description": "Academic Structural Interventional Cardiologists - Cardiology #MED437. MED - Section of Cardiology. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 21, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Medicine, Section of Cardiology, is seeking a full-time Structural Interventional Cardiologist at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor to join our nationally recognized Cardiac Catheterization Laboratory and growing Heart and Vascular Institute. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Responsibilities include performing structural and interventional procedures, providing inpatient and outpatient care across multiple sites, and contributing to the Cardiac Intensive Care Unit. Appointees will also support and expand clinical research programs in structural heart disease, mentor trainees, engage in clinical outreach and offsite clinical activities, and participate in scholarly activity, including publications and national presentations. Prior to the start of employment, applicants must: 1) hold an MD or equivalent, 2) be eligible for Illinois medical licensure, 3) be Board Certified/Eligible in Cardiovascular Disease, 4) have completed an ACGME-accredited Interventional Cardiology Fellowship, and 5) be eligible for or certified in Interventional Cardiology. We especially welcome candidates with advanced experience in peripheral arterial interventions, complex coronary disease, mechanical circulatory support, and leadership roles in clinical trials of novel structural heart devices. Applicants with extensive experience in structural heart interventions, including advanced transcatheter therapies for complex valvular and structural heart disease, and a demonstrated track record of participation in multiple clinical trials involving novel structural heart devices are preferred. We also welcome applicants who have a strong record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications in structural heart disease and/or interventional cardiology. We welcome candidates across the mid-career to senior spectrum, including established leaders in the field who seek a high-impact academic role without formal administrative or directorship responsibilities. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195591 . Applicants must upload a CV, including bibliography, and a cover letter. Review of applications ends when all available positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194656": {"title": "Clinical Faculty (Open Rank) \u2013 Law School Innovation Clinic", "description": "Clinical Faculty (Open Rank) \u2013 Law School Innovation Clinic. Law School. Position type: OAA - Clinical Track Professor. Posted: Oct 20, 2026. THE UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO LAW SCHOOL is seeking qualified applicants for a full-time position directing its Innovation Clinic, which is a transactional clinic providing legal services for start-up ventures in the innovation sector. Responsibilities of the position would include setting the strategic direction for the Innovation Clinic, sourcing clients and projects for the Innovation Clinic, and representing Innovation Clinic clients. In addition, the position would involve teaching and supervising law students in the Innovation Clinic and teaching a seminar related to the clinic and/or clinical skills courses. The appointment would begin July 1, 2027, and would be at the rank of Assistant Clinical Professor of Law, or if coming from a similar position at another law school, possibly as Associate Clinical Professor of Law or Clinical Professor of Law under University Statute 11.2 (Other Academic Appointees). Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Candidates must have a J.D. Candidates must have at minimum three years of relevant experience, and candidates with at least five years of relevant experience are strongly preferred. Candidates must be admitted to or eligible for admission to the Illinois bar. Candidates who teach in a law school legal clinic or who have prior experience supervising, mentoring, or teaching law students or other attorneys are preferred. Excellent writing, editing, and supervision skills are required. We value candidates who will contribute diverse perspectives that will enrich and improve student experiences and the Law School\u2019s intellectual culture. Each candidate should submit a curriculum vitae or resume, a list of references, a legal writing sample, a law school transcript, a cover letter that includes a detailed description of the candidate\u2019s relevant practice experience and teaching experience, and course evaluations, if any, from prior teaching experience. Other material relevant to the candidate\u2019s candidacy may be included as well. Candidates must apply online and upload application material at: apply.interfolio.com/196048 . Applications will be accepted until May 15, 2027, or until the position is filled, whichever comes first."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194122": {"title": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in the Center for the Study of Gender and Sexuality", "description": "Instructional Professor (Open Rank) in the Center for the Study of Gender and Sexuality. Center for the Study of Gender and Sexuality. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 20, 2026. The Social Sciences Division at the University of Chicago invites applications for appointment as Instructional Professor at any rank in the Center for Gender and Sexuality and the College. This is a full-time, career-track teaching position, with administrative, mentoring and programming responsibilities expected to begin on or after September 1, 2027. The initial appointment term will be from two to five years, dependent on rank, and renewable with opportunity for promotion within the Instructional Professor track based on a successful performance review and recommendation. Academic rank and compensation are dependent upon qualifications. Gender and Sexuality Studies is an interdisciplinary undergraduate program focused on fostering a critical understanding of gender and sexuality as key categories of political, social, and cultural analysis. The Instructional Professor will teach up to five quarter-length courses each academic year, as determined by curricular need, including: the core Gender and Sexuality in World Civilizations Sequence, required or elective courses for the GNSE major, and the B.A. thesis seminar. The appointee will also provide administrative support for the undergraduate curriculum, plan and implement programming, and academic advising for Gender and Sexuality Studies majors and minors, including linking students to relevant research, fellowship, and internship opportunities. The base salary range for this position is $77,946 \u2013 $100,000 for a nine-month academic appointment, to be paid over twelve months. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . The terms and conditions of this position are described in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Chicago and the Service Employees International Union. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess: PhD in a humanistic social science in hand by the start date of the appointment At least one year of teaching university-level courses in the humanistic social sciences We especially welcome applicants with: Demonstrated experience in interdisciplinary and/or transnational departments, centers, or programs Evidence of excellence in undergraduate teaching Evidence of excellence in advising and/or curriculum development Evidence of excellence in planning co-curricular events and programs Strong writing, organizational, communication, and collaboration skills. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195564. Applicants must upload: Cover letter describing your interest in the position, experience, and academic background in teaching, advising and/or curriculum development, planning co-curricular events or programs Curriculum vitae Teaching statement Sample syllabi of courses previously taught and/or designed (max. 3) Sample course assignments (max. 3) Candidates who advance to a second round will be asked (at a later date) for the following materials: Additional evidence of teaching excellence (might include: course evaluations, performance reviews, or other relevant assessments) Writing sample of the applicant's academic work (article-length) Three (3) letters of recommendation Review of applications will begin by December 1st and will continue until the position is filled or the search has ended."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194247": {"title": "Research Associate Professor, Chicago Center for Computational Social Science", "description": "Research Associate Professor, Chicago Center for Computational Social Science. Chicago Center for Computational Social Science. Position type: OAA - Research Track Professor. Posted: Oct 20, 2026. The University of Chicago, Division of the Social Sciences invites applications for appointment as Research Associate Professor to join the Chicago Center for Computational Social Science for an initial three-year term. The Chicago Center for Computational Social Science (C3S2) \u2014 informally known as \"the Cube\" \u2014 is a new strategic initiative within UChicago's Division of the Social Sciences. C3S2 consolidates the University's diverse CSS programs (MACSS, the CSS Ph.D. certificate, the undergraduate CSS minor, CSDS, and the CSS Workshop) into a single hub spanning 20+ departments and labs, organized around three core arms: Research, Education, and Engagement. Its dual mission is to advance social science through computation and to examine AI and computation as social phenomena in their own right. The selected appointee will be expected to lead cross-disciplinary research initiatives and make a substantial contribution to C3S2 research, education and faculty engagement. The appointee will be responsible for developing strategic partnerships with University units, industry, and civic organizations through sponsored research agreements, data-sharing MOUs, internships, and fellows-in-residence programs. Additional responsibilities include connecting the Center\u2019s research and educational missions through co-mentored student projects and support for MACSS and related degree programs. This is a full-time benefits-eligible position. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must possess a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago, Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195681 Applicants must upload: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Research Statement One sample publication Contact information for three references"}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194436": {"title": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi)", "description": "Assistant Professor \u2013 Climate Systems Engineering (CSEi). Physical Sciences Division. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 19, 2026. The University of Chicago invites applications from scholars who will make significant contributions to Climate Systems Engineering. The University is building a world-leading Climate Systems Engineering initiative (CSEi) with multiple faculty hires spanning open-system carbon removal, sunlight reflection methods, and interventions to limit the loss of glacial ice. We seek individuals who will lead research programs that will produce significant contributions to their field and to this research initiative. Applications are welcome from all relevant academic disciplines, including physical and biological sciences, social sciences, engineering, and humanities at the rank of tenure-track Assistant Professor. We welcome applications from scholars who have not previously worked on climate systems engineering and from scholars who focus on the physical or social risks of Climate Systems Engineering. Appointments will be made in an appropriate primary department based on field, with secondary appointment(s) in another unit possible. Research on Climate Systems Engineering is inherently interdisciplinary, so we seek individuals with a track record of collaboration beyond their discipline; the University\u2019s culture is highly collaborative. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Completion of all requirements for a doctoral degree is required prior to appointment. The following materials are required: A brief cover letter, A curriculum vitae, A statement of research plans (up to 5 pages) that describes how you plan to integrate research on climate systems engineering into your overall research trajectory, A statement of teaching philosophy and experience, Applicants are required to request three confidential letters of recommendation through Interfolio. Applications must be submitted online through the University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Job\u2019s website: https://apply.interfolio.com/195850 Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026 and continue until all positions are filled."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193792": {"title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor: Health Services Research PHS028", "description": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor: Health Services Research PHS028. Department of Public Health Sciences. Position type: Faculty - Tenure Track/Tenured. Posted: Oct 19, 2026. The University of Chicago\u2019s Department of Public Health Sciences is seeking full-time faculty members at the rank of assistant professor in Health Services Research. Research areas of particular interest include pharmaceutical development, regulation, and market dynamics; drug pricing, coverage, reimbursement, and access; and pharmaceutical delivery and utilization. Public Health Sciences faculty carry out independent research, collaborative research, and education of undergraduate, graduate, and/or medical students and fellows. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. These positions are benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must have a research doctoral degree or equivalent in economics, health economics, health services research, public policy, or a related discipline. To be considered, interested candidates must apply online through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment Job Board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195406 . Applicants must upload a cover letter, a cv including bibliography, a research statement, a teaching statement that describes the candidate\u2019s experience in teaching and mentoring diverse students, an exemplary writing sample or publication, and contact information for three academic references. Review of applications will commence on or around December 1, 2026, and continue until the positions are filled. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp. "}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194480": {"title": "Instructional Professor of Political Science in MAPSS (Open Rank)", "description": "Instructional Professor of Political Science in MAPSS (Open Rank). Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences. Position type: OAA - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer. Posted: Oct 19, 2026. The University of Chicago Division of the Social Sciences invites applications for appointment as Instructional Professor (IP) at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor, with a specialization in Political Science, in the Master of Arts Program in the Social Sciences ( https://mapss.uchicago.edu ). We invite applications from candidates across the full range of fields within the discipline. We are especially interested in candidates whose substantive work addresses questions around identity and who have expertise in either quantitative or qualitative methods. This is a full-time, career track teaching and advising position that involves designing graduate seminars, mentoring students on research projects, and helping a diverse range of students navigate graduate school. The initial three-year appointment will begin in Academic Year 2027-28 and is renewable with opportunity for promotion. Academic rank and compensation (including a generous package of fringe benefits) are dependent upon qualifications. MAPSS is a one-year program providing students with a foundation in graduate-level interdisciplinary social science research. Students concentrate in psychology, anthropology, history, political science, sociology, or in interdisciplinary fields, and conduct research projects under the supervision of UChicago faculty or MAPSS IPs. MAPSS recruits talented students from a wide array of backgrounds, experiences, and trajectories. Some go on to doctoral study after MAPSS, but most use the degree to advance their careers with the help of an in-house Career Services team. In MAPSS, IP have an annual teaching load of 4 courses, in addition to substantial mentoring and service responsibilities. Their courses include one section of the MAPSS core course and other courses oriented toward MA and/or undergraduate students, according to program need. This often includes a graduate seminar introducing an important social science research methodology. The specific courses IP teach in any quarter are determined by the MAPSS Faculty Director and Director. MAPSS IP perform substantive roles as preceptors, a position that involves annually advising students on quarterly course selection and independent research projects, serving as second readers on student theses, supervising capstone projects, and providing broad academic and professional guidance. They also closely advise students and alumni who are applying to PhD programs. MAPSS IP fulfill a range of service requirements. They participate in admissions, recruiting, and staff meetings, and contribute to program operations and co-curricular academic programming, including the annual student academic conference. The position includes support for professional development, including funds to attend conferences and workshops, as well as mentoring from senior members of the MAPSS instructional team. The position also offers opportunities to participate in the intellectual life of the University. Applicants must have a PhD in Political Science or in a related discipline prior to the start date. Prior teaching in the social sciences is required. Experience mentoring students on original research projects is preferred. The following materials must be submitted through Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/195892 1) a cover letter, outlining the applicant\u2019s prior training and research experience, their prior teaching and/or mentoring experience, and their suggested course offerings; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) a doctoral transcript (unofficial is acceptable); 4) an article-length writing sample; 5) at least one course syllabus designed by the candidate; 6) course evaluations or other evidence of past experience teaching or mentoring; 7) a teaching statement; and 8) three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin 30 days after the position has been posted and will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed. This position is benefits-eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook . This position will be part of the Service Employees International Union."}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/194345": {"title": "Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics Faculty Physician #PED244", "description": "Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics Faculty Physician #PED244. Section of Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics. Position type: Faculty - School of Medicine Track. Posted: Oct 19, 2026. The University of Chicago's Department of Pediatrics, Section of Developmental and Behavioral Pediatrics, is searching for a full-time faculty member at the rank of assistant professor. The appointee will contribute to patient care, medical education, and scholarship, and join a community of faculty colleagues and learners in UChicago Medicine, the Biological Sciences Division, the Pritzker School of Medicine, and the University of Chicago and its affiliates. Compensation is dependent upon qualifications. This position is benefits eligible. The University of Chicago offers a wide range of benefits programs and resources for eligible employees, including health, retirement, and paid time off. Information about the benefit offerings can be found in the Benefits Guidebook. Clinical responsibilities will include care for pediatric patients who may present with a full range of developmental and behavioral conditions. Clinics are held on the University of Chicago campus, at the Comer Children's Hospital and offsite clinics in the greater Chicagoland area. Other duties will include training and supervision of medical students, residents, and fellows. Scholarly activity is expected and may be in research, education, or clinical program development. Applicants with experience in developmental diagnostic teams for autism, intellectual disability, sequelae of prematurity, critical illness, learning disorders, and ADHD are also welcomed. The Department of Pediatrics is committed to providing superior, compassionate healthcare to all children. We strive to provide an education and training experience that fosters the next generation of national and international leaders in child health. Our department includes more than 130 full-time faculty members, clinical associates and postdoctoral researchers. In addition to teaching medical students, residents and fellows, our faculty conduct cutting-edge translational and clinical research. Comer Children's Hospital is a free-standing children's hospital with a full complement of pediatric services and a total of 161 pediatric and neonatal beds: 60 general, 30 PICU, and 71 NICU. Prior to the start of employment, qualified applicants must:: 1) have a medical doctorate or equivalent, 2) have or be eligible for medical licensure in the State of Illinois, 3) have completed/be in the process of completing an ACGME approved behavioral developmental pediatrics fellowship at an ACGME accredited institution, and 4) hold equivalent certification/credentials relevant to developmental pediatrics, such as a Master of Public Health or Master of Health Informatics, or have experience involving developmental/behavioral care coordination for children with special health care needs. To be considered, those interested must apply through The University of Chicago\u2019s Academic Recruitment job board, which uses Interfolio to accept applications: https://apply.interfolio.com/195765 . Applicants must upload a CV including bibliography and cover letter. Review of applications will begin after October 29, 2026. For instructions on the Interfolio application process, please visit http://tiny.cc/InterfolioHelp . "}}, "rows": {"https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193836": {"school_id": 382, "posting_url": "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193836", "job_title_in_post": "Assistant Professor - AI and Policy", "position_type": "Assistant_Professor", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "School of Public Policy Studies", "area_key_words": "AI and Policy", "deadline_of_application": "November 11, 2026", "posted_date": "Oct 12, 2026", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193848": {"school_id": 382, "posting_url": "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193848", "job_title_in_post": "Clinical Associates - Geriatric Medicine #MED427a", "position_type": "Lecturer", "job_term": "Full-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "posted_date": "Oct 12, 2026", "position_start_date": ""}, "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193651": {"school_id": 382, "posting_url": "https://academicjobs.uchicago.edu/positions/193651", "job_title_in_post": "Academic Nephrologist - Nephrology #MED43